  {
    "id": "new-litware-2",
    "sourceId": "litware:2",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You need to create a DAX measure to calculate the average overall satisfaction score.</p><p>How should you complete the DAX code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.</p><p>NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>Rolling 12 Overall Satisfaction =\nVAR NumberOfMonths = 12\nVAR LastCurrentDate = MAX ( &#x27;Date&#x27;[Date] )\nVAR Period = DATESINPERIOD ( &#x27;Date&#x27;[Date], LastCurrentDate, - NumberOfMonths, MONTH )\nVAR Result =\n    CALCULATE (\n        [dropdown 1],\n        [dropdown 2]\n        &#x27;Survey Question&#x27;[Question Title] = &quot;Overall Satisfaction&quot;\n    )\nRETURN\n    Result</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "AVERAGE('Survey'[Response Value]),",
          "AVERAGEA('Question'[Question Text]),",
          "AVERAGEX(VALUES('Survey'[Customer Key]),"
        ],
        "answer": "AVERAGE('Survey'[Response Value]),"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "LastCurrentDate,",
          "NumberOfMonths,",
          "Period,"
        ],
        "answer": "Period,"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p><b>First dropdown: AVERAGE(&#x27;Survey&#x27;[Response Value]),</b> The Response Value column holds the numeric survey scores, so it is the only column that can be averaged into a satisfaction score. Question Text is text and cannot be averaged, and Customer Key is an identifier, not a score.</p>\n<p><b>Second dropdown: Period,</b> Period is the table of dates returned by DATESINPERIOD for the last 12 months, and CALCULATE needs a table as its filter. NumberOfMonths is only the integer 12, and LastCurrentDate is a single date, so neither can serve as the filter.</p>\n<p>The &#x27;Survey Question&#x27;[Question Title] = &quot;Overall Satisfaction&quot; filter then restricts the average to the overall-satisfaction question, which is the third question in each survey.</p>Key verified against the case-study requirements.",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "litware:2"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Litware"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-litware-3",
    "sourceId": "litware:3",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You need to resolve the issue with the pricing group classification.</p><p>How should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.</p><p>NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>CREATE [dropdown 1] [dbo].[ProductsWithPricingGroup]\nAS\nSELECT ProductId,\n    ProductName,\n    ProductCategory,\n    ListPrice,\n    [dropdown 2]\n        WHEN ListPrice &lt;= 50 THEN &#x27;low&#x27;\n        [dropdown 3]\n        WHEN ListPrice &gt; 1000 THEN &#x27;high&#x27;\n    END AS PricingGroup\nFROM dbo.Products</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "FUNCTION",
          "PROCEDURE",
          "TABLE",
          "VIEW"
        ],
        "answer": "VIEW"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "CASE",
          "COALESCE",
          "IIF",
          "SET"
        ],
        "answer": "CASE"
      },
      {
        "id": "d3",
        "label": "d3",
        "options": [
          "WHEN (ListPrice > 50 AND ListPrice <= 1000) THEN 'medium'",
          "WHEN (ListPrice >= 50 AND ListPrice < 1000) THEN 'medium'",
          "WHEN (ListPrice BETWEEN 50 AND 1000) THEN 'medium'"
        ],
        "answer": "WHEN (ListPrice > 50 AND ListPrice <= 1000) THEN 'medium'"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p><b>First dropdown: VIEW.</b> The pricing group logic must be maintained in a single location and be available to T-SQL queries and the default semantic model. A view encapsulates the logic as a query over dbo.Products without storing data. A table would store data, and a function or a procedure is not a queryable object for the semantic model.</p>\n<p><b>Second dropdown: CASE.</b> CASE opens the conditional expression that the WHEN branches belong to. COALESCE, IIF and SET do not open a multi-branch WHEN list.</p>\n<p><b>Third dropdown: WHEN (ListPrice &gt; 50 AND ListPrice &lt;= 1000) THEN &#x27;medium&#x27;.</b> The case study defines the medium group as list prices greater than 50 and less than or equal to 1,000, so this option restates the rule exactly. The highlighted option WHEN (ListPrice &gt;= 50 AND ListPrice &lt; 1000) THEN &#x27;medium&#x27; is wrong: a price of exactly 1,000 is not less than 1,000, and it is not greater than 1,000 either, so it would fall through unclassified.</p>Key verified against the case-study requirements.",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "litware:3"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Litware"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-litware-4",
    "sourceId": "litware:4",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>What should you recommend using to ingest the customer data into the data store in the AnatyticsPOC workspace?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a stored procedure"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a pipeline that contains a KQL activity"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a Spark notebook"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "a dataflow"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p><b>D. a dataflow.</b> The case study says the data engineers will use low-code tools for data ingestion whenever possible. A dataflow is the low-code ingestion tool. A stored procedure, a pipeline that contains a KQL activity, and a Spark notebook are all code-based, so they do not meet the low-code requirement.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "litware:4"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Litware"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-litware-5",
    "sourceId": "litware:5",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Which type of data store should you recommend in the AnalyticsPOC workspace?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a data lake"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a warehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "an external Hive metaStore"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p><b>C. a lakehouse.</b> The data store must support read access by using T-SQL or Python, semi-structured and unstructured data, and row-level security for users executing T-SQL queries. A lakehouse meets all three: it stores files of any shape in OneLake, exposes a SQL analytics endpoint alongside Spark, and supports row-level security. A data lake has no SQL endpoint or row-level security, and a warehouse does not hold semi-structured and unstructured data the same way.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "litware:5"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Litware"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-mixed-1",
    "sourceId": "mixed:1",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are planning a Fabric analytics solution for the following users:</p><ul><li>2,000 Microsoft Power BI consumers without an individual Power BI Pro license.</li><li>32 Power BI modelers with an individual Power BI Pro license.</li><li>16 data scientists</li></ul><p>You need to recommend a Fabric capacity SKU. The solution must minimize costs.</p><p>What should you recommend?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "F2"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "F2048"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "F32"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "F64"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>F64 is the smallest Fabric capacity (equivalent to a P1 Power BI Premium capacity) that supports premium Fabric workspaces and does not require Power BI report consumers to have individual Power BI Pro licenses. F2 and F32 are incorrect since they require that the 2,000 employees have individual Power BI Pro licenses to consume Power BI content. F2048 is incorrect since it is not the smallest capacity that meets the stated requirements.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "mixed:1"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-mixed-2",
    "sourceId": "mixed:2",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.</p><p>You have write permissions to an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account named storage1 that contains a folder named Folder1.</p><p>You plan to delete a shortcut named Shortcut1 that points to a file named File1 stored in Folder1.</p><p>You run the delete operation on the following path.</p><p><code>Lakehouse1\\Files\\Shortcut1</code></p><p>What will occur after you run the delete operation?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Only File1 and Folder1 will be deleted."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Only File1 will be deleted."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Only Shortcut1 will be deleted."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Shortcut1, Folder1, and File1 will be deleted."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Shortcuts do not perform cascading deletes. However, if the user has write permissions to the shortcut target and specifies the full path of the target, the target will be deleted from the storage account.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "mixed:2"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-mixed-3",
    "sourceId": "mixed:3",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace that contains a Microsoft Power BI report.</p><p>You need to modify the column names in the Power BI report without changing the original names in the underlying Delta table.</p><p>Which warehouse object should you create?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "columnstore index"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "schema"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "table-valued function"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "view"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A view provides a convenient way to encapsulate additional query logic, such as renaming columns, filtering, aggregating, etc. Views contain only a query definition and do not change the underlying tables.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "mixed:3"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-mixed-4",
    "sourceId": "mixed:4",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant.</p><p>Your company has 1 TB of legacy accounting data stored in an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account. The data is queried only once a year for a few ad-hoc reports that submit very selective queries.</p><p>You plan to create a Fabric lakehouse or warehouse to store company sales data. Developers must be able to build reports from the lakehouse or warehouse based on the sales data. The developers must also be able to do ad-hoc analysis of the legacy data at the end of each year.</p><p>You need to recommend which Fabric architecture to create and the process for integrating the accounting data into Fabric. The solution must minimize administrative effort and costs.</p><p>What should you recommend?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Ingest the sales data into the Fabric lakehouse and set up a shortcut to the legacy accounting data in the storage account."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Ingest the sales data into the Fabric lakehouse and use a pipeline to move the legacy accounting data into the lakehouse."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Ingest the sales data into the Fabric warehouse and use a pipeline to move the legacy accounting data into the warehouse."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Set up a lakehouse with a shortcut to the legacy accounting data. Ingest the sales data into the Fabric warehouse and add the SQL analytics endpoint of the lakehouse to the warehouse for cross querying."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Since the legacy accounting data is only accessed once a year for a few ad-hoc queries that are highly selective, there is no need to move the data into a Fabric workspace. Shortcuts enable the querying of remote data without having to move the data. Shortcuts are only supported in a Fabric lakehouse. While you can add the SQL endpoint of a lakehouse to a warehouse for cross database querying, that is not the simplest method. The simplest method is to use a shortcut.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "mixed:4"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-mixed-5",
    "sourceId": "mixed:5",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace that contains a Microsoft Power BI report named Sales.</p><p>You plan to use Dataflow Gen2 to add an additional column to the report. The new column must be based on the unit price of a product. Any product that has a unit price that is greater than $1,000 must be labeled as High, while any product that has a unit price that is less than $1,000 must be labeled as Regular.</p><p>What should you select on the Add column tab in Power Query Editor?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Duplicate column"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Conditional column"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Index column"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Merge columns"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The Conditional column option enables adding new columns whose values will be based on one or more conditions applied to the existing table columns.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "mixed:5"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-mixed-6",
    "sourceId": "mixed:6",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.</p><p>You have a large 1 TB dataset in an external data source.</p><p>You need to recommend a method to ingest the dataset into Lakehouse1. The solution must provide the highest throughput. The solution must be suitable for developers who prefer the low-code/no-code option.</p><p>What should you recommend?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Use Dataflow Gen2 to import the files and load them to a table."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Use Fabric notebooks and PySpark to load the data to a DataFrame and write the results to a location in Lakehouse1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Use Lakehouse explorer to upload the files directly."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Use the Copy data activity of a pipeline to copy the data."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The Copy data activity provides the best performance when copying data from large datasets.</p>",
    "images": [],
