    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`VIEW` — `CASE` — `WHEN ListPrice BETWEEN 50 AND 1000) THEN &#x27;medium&#x27;`.**\n\n**The PDF&#x27;s answer-key image is wrong on two of the three blanks** (it marks `TABLE` and `&gt; 50 AND &lt; 1000`). Here is the reasoning.\n\n**Blank 1 — `VIEW`.** The requirement is *&quot;The product pricing group logic must be maintained by the analytics engineers in a **single location**. The pricing group data must be made available in the data store for T-SQL queries **and in the default semantic model**.&quot;*\n- A **view** stores the logic once and re-evaluates it on every read, so it can never drift from `dbo.Products` — that is what &quot;single location&quot; means. Views in a Fabric warehouse are queryable with T-SQL and are picked up by the default semantic model, which satisfies both halves of the requirement (the case study separately states that the semantic models *&quot;must include only tables **or views** from the dimensional model&quot;*).\n- A **TABLE** (via CTAS) materialises a **copy**. The moment a list price changes, the copy is stale, and you now maintain the logic *and* a refresh job — the opposite of a single location.\n- **FUNCTION** / **PROCEDURE** do not fit the `CREATE x [dbo].[ProductsWithPricingGroup] AS SELECT ...` shape shown, and a stored procedure is not exposed to the default semantic model.\n\n**Blank 2 — `CASE`.** The block is `WHEN ... THEN ... END AS PricingGroup`, which is a searched `CASE` expression. `COALESCE` returns the first non-null argument, `IIF` takes exactly one condition, and `SET` is not an expression.\n\n**Blank 3 — `BETWEEN`.** The spec is: low `&lt;= 50`, medium `&gt; 50 and &lt;= 1000`, high `&gt; 1000`. Check each option against **1,000**, the boundary that breaks two of them:\n\n| Option | ListPrice = 1000 | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `&gt; 50 AND &lt; 1000` | no branch matches → **NULL** | wrong |\n| `&gt;= 50 AND &lt; 1000` | no branch matches → **NULL** | wrong |\n| `BETWEEN 50 AND 1000` | `BETWEEN` is inclusive → **&#x27;medium&#x27;** | correct |\n\n`BETWEEN` also includes 50, which looks like an overlap with the low group — but it is not one, because **`CASE` evaluates its `WHEN` clauses in order and stops at the first match**. `ListPrice = 50` is caught by the preceding `WHEN ListPrice &lt;= 50 THEN &#x27;low&#x27;`, so it never reaches the `BETWEEN`. The documented behaviour: *&quot;Evaluates, in the order specified, `Boolean_expression` for each `WHEN` clause … Returns `result_expression` of the first `Boolean_expression` that evaluates to TRUE.&quot;*\n\n*Note:* the top comment (+77) makes exactly this argument. A second comment (+34) complains that all three options are wrong because none literally reads `&gt; 50 AND &lt;= 1000` — true, but it overlooks the short-circuit, which makes `BETWEEN` behave correctly. A third commenter who sat the exam reports Microsoft has since changed the live item to `WHEN(ListPrice &gt; 50 AND ListPrice &lt;= 1000)`; if you see that variant, pick it.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "CASE (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/case-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "BETWEEN (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/between-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CREATE VIEW (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-view-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
