  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q10",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q10",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Staging.Sales. Staging.Sales contains the following columns.\nYou need to write a T-SQL query that will return data for the year 2023 that displays ProductID and ProductName and has a summarized Amount\nthat is higher than 10,000.\nWhich query should you use?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "WHERE DATEPART(YEAR, SaleDate) = '2023' · GROUP BY ProductID, ProductName · HAVING SUM(Amount) > 10000"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "GROUP BY ProductID, ProductName · HAVING DATEPART(YEAR, SaleDate) = '2023' AND SUM(Amount) > 10000"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "WHERE DATEPART(YEAR, SaleDate) = '2023' AND SUM(Amount) > 10000  (no GROUP BY)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "WHERE DATEPART(YEAR, SaleDate) = '2023' · GROUP BY ProductID, ProductName · HAVING TotalAmount > 10000"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Only **A** is valid T-SQL. The rule to apply is the logical order of evaluation: `FROM → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY`. `WHERE` filters **rows** before grouping and cannot see aggregates; `HAVING` filters **groups** after aggregation and cannot see `SELECT` aliases.\n\n```sql\nSELECT ProductID, ProductName, SUM(Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Staging.Sales\nWHERE DATEPART(YEAR, SaleDate) = &#x27;2023&#x27;   -- row filter, pre-aggregation\nGROUP BY ProductID, ProductName\nHAVING SUM(Amount) &gt; 10000              -- group filter, repeats the aggregate\n```\n\n- **B** – puts the year test in `HAVING`, but `SaleDate` is neither in the `GROUP BY` list nor inside an aggregate, so it is not valid in a `HAVING` clause.\n- **C** – uses `SUM(Amount)` inside `WHERE`. Aggregates are not allowed there (`WHERE` runs before grouping), and there is no `GROUP BY` to support the aggregate in the `SELECT` list either.\n- **D** – references the alias `TotalAmount` in `HAVING`. `SELECT` aliases are only visible to `ORDER BY`; in `HAVING` you must repeat `SUM(Amount)`.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "SELECT — HAVING (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-having-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Logical processing order of the SELECT statement",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p047_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q10"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p047_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q11",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q11",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a data warehouse that contains a table named Stage.Customers. Stage.Customers contains all the customer record updates from a\ncustomer relationship management (CRM) system. There can be multiple updates per customer.\nYou need to write a T-SQL query that will return the customer ID, name. postal code, and the last updated time of the most recent row for each\ncustomer ID.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": ", x =  ______  OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY LastUpdated DESC)",
        "options": [
          "LAST_Value()",
          "NTILE()",
          "ROW_NUMBER()"
        ],
        "answer": "ROW_NUMBER()"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "SELECT ... FROM CUSTOMERBASE  ______",
        "options": [
          "Having Max(LastUpdated) = 1",
          "WHERE LastUpdated = Max(LastUpdated)",
          "WHERE X = 1"
        ],
        "answer": "WHERE X = 1"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`ROW_NUMBER()` — `WHERE X = 1`.** This is the canonical &quot;latest row per key&quot; pattern: number the rows inside each customer, newest first, then keep number 1.\n\n```sql\nWITH CUSTOMERBASE AS (\n  SELECT [CustomerID], [CustomerName], [PostalCode], [LastUpdated]\n    , x = ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY LastUpdated DESC)\n  FROM [LakehousePOC].[dbo].[CustomerChanges]\n)\nSELECT CustomerID, CustomerName, PostalCode, LastUpdated\nFROM CUSTOMERBASE\nWHERE X = 1;\n```\n\n**Blank 1.** `ROW_NUMBER()` *&quot;numbers the output of a result set … starting at 1 for the first row in each partition.&quot;* With `ORDER BY LastUpdated DESC`, row 1 is the most recent update for that `CustomerID`.\n- `LAST_VALUE()` returns a *value* from the window frame, not a rank, so there would be nothing to filter on — and by default its frame ends at the current row, a classic trap.\n- `NTILE()` splits a partition into N buckets; it requires an argument and does not identify a single newest row.\n\n**Blank 2.** Window functions cannot be referenced in `WHERE` in the same query level — that is precisely why the `ROW_NUMBER()` is computed in the CTE and filtered in the outer query. `WHERE X = 1` keeps the newest row per customer.\n- `Having Max(LastUpdated) = 1` is nonsense: there is no `GROUP BY`, and comparing a datetime to 1 is a type error.\n- `WHERE LastUpdated = Max(LastUpdated)` puts an aggregate in `WHERE`, which T-SQL rejects.\n\nThe PDF supplies only &quot;Box 1: ROW_NUMBER()&quot; with no key for box 2; the top comments (+39, +17, +5) all give the full query above.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "ROW_NUMBER (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/row-number-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "OVER clause (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-over-clause-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p050_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q11"
    ],
