  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q12",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q12",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou plan to create a Fabric notebook that will use Spark DataFrames to generate Microsoft Power BI visuals.\nYou run the following code.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "The code embeds an existing Power BI report.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "The code creates a Power BI report.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "The code displays a summary of the DataFrame.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No — Yes — Yes.** The code is the `powerbiclient` **quick visualize** path:\n\n```python\nfrom powerbiclient import QuickVisualize, get_dataset_config, Report\n\nPBI_visualize = QuickVisualize(get_dataset_config(df))\nPBI_visualize\n```\n\n1. **Embeds an existing report → No.** Embedding an *existing* report is the `Report` class (`Report(group_id=..., report_id=...)`). It is imported on line 1 but never used. `QuickVisualize` starts from a DataFrame, and there is no report id anywhere in the code.\n2. **Creates a Power BI report → Yes.** `QuickVisualize` *&quot;create[s] a Power BI report from your data&quot;* — Fabric autogenerates a new report over the DataFrame passed through `get_dataset_config(df)`.\n3. **Displays a summary of the DataFrame → Yes.** Evaluating `PBI_visualize` on the last line renders the widget inline in the notebook, and the autogenerated report is a set of summary visuals over the DataFrame&#x27;s columns.\n\n*Note:* statement 3 is contested. The single highest-voted comment (+49) answers **No**, reading &quot;summary&quot; as `df.describe()`-style summary *statistics*. Two other comments (+22, +12) answer **Yes**, and the PDF&#x27;s answer-key image marks **Yes**. The wording is &quot;a summary of the DataFrame&quot;, not &quot;summary statistics&quot;, and the autogenerated report is exactly that — so Yes is the better reading.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Notebook visualization in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/notebook-visualization?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Explore and visualize data in a Fabric notebook",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-science/tutorial-data-science-explore-notebook?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p052_i1.png, p052_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
