  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q13",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q13",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are the administrator of a Fabric workspace that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains the following tables:\nTable1: A Delta table created by using a shortcut\nTable2: An external table created by using Spark\nTable3: A managed table -\nYou plan to connect to Lakehouse1 by using its SQL endpoint.\nWhat will you be able to do after connecting to Lakehouse1?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Read Table3."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Update the data Table3."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Read Table2."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Update the data in Table1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Two facts decide every option here.\n\n**1. The SQL analytics endpoint is read-only.** The doc states it plainly: *&quot;The SQL analytics endpoint … is a read-only experience. It doesn&#x27;t support the full T-SQL surface area of a Fabric warehouse.&quot;* You can `SELECT`, create views and apply security, but you cannot `INSERT`, `UPDATE` or `DELETE`. That kills **B** (update Table3) and **D** (update Table1) regardless of table kind.\n\n**2. The endpoint only surfaces managed Delta tables in the lakehouse `Tables` area.** Spark **external** tables are registered in the metastore with their own `LOCATION`; they appear in Lakehouse explorer but are *not* projected into the SQL analytics endpoint. That kills **C**.\n\n**Table3 is a managed table**, so it is exposed and readable — **A**. (Table1, the shortcut, is also readable, but the option offered for it asks to *update* it, which is not allowed.)</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "SQL analytics endpoint for a lakehouse",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sql-analytics-endpoint?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q13"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q14",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q14",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nYou use a dataflow to load a new dataset from OneLake to the warehouse.\nYou need to add a PowerQuery step to identify the maximum values for the numeric columns.\nWhich function should you include in the step?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Table.MaxN"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Table.Max"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Table.Range"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Table.Profile"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Read the ask precisely: the maximum values for **the numeric columns** — plural, across columns, in **one** Power Query step.\n\n**`Table.Profile`** does exactly that. It returns one row *per column* with the columns `Min`, `Max`, `Average`, `StandardDeviation`, `Count`, `NullCount` and `DistinctCount` — so a single step yields the maximum of every numeric column at once.\n\n- **B `Table.Max`** returns *one row* — the largest row of the table according to a comparison criterion you must supply. It gives you a record, not a per-column maximum, and you would need a separate step per column.\n- **A `Table.MaxN`** is the same idea for the *N* largest rows.\n- **C `Table.Range`** returns a slice of rows by position. Nothing to do with maxima.\n\n*Note:* about a third of the community votes B, arguing that &quot;profiling&quot; is a pane in the UI rather than a step. But `Table.Profile` is a genuine M function that you can add as a step, and it is the only option that answers the question for all numeric columns at once.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Table.Profile — Power Query M",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/powerquery-m/table-profile?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Table.Max — Power Query M",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/powerquery-m/table-max?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q14"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q15",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q15",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a machine learning model registered in a Fabric workspace.\nYou need to use the model to generate predictions by using the PREDICT function in a Fabric notebook.\nWhich two languages can you use to perform model scoring? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "T-SQL"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "DAX"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Spark SQL"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "PySpark"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "C",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The Fabric `PREDICT` function is a **Spark** construct. Microsoft documents three ways to call it, and the two that are offered here are **PySpark** and **Spark SQL**:\n\n- the low-code **PREDICT wizard** in the ML model UI,\n- the **Spark DataFrame API** from a notebook — `MLFlowTransformer(...)` in **PySpark** (and Scala/R),\n- **Spark SQL**, by registering the model as a Spark SQL UDF and calling it in a `SELECT`.\n\n- **A T-SQL** – Fabric notebooks do not run T-SQL, and `PREDICT` in a warehouse is a different, unrelated feature (it is not how you score a model registered in a Fabric workspace from a notebook).\n- **B DAX** – a semantic-model query language. It has no model-scoring function and cannot run in a notebook.\n\nThe notebook language set is PySpark, Scala Spark, Spark SQL and SparkR — of the four options, only C and D are in it.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Machine learning model scoring with PREDICT",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-science/model-scoring-predict?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q15"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q16",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q16",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are analyzing the data in a Fabric notebook.\nYou have a Spark DataFrame assigned to a variable named df.\nYou need to use the Chart view in the notebook to explore the data manually.\nWhich function should you run to make the data available in the Chart view?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "displayHTML"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "show"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "write"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "display"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`display(df)`** is the Fabric notebook function that renders the rich output widget — and that widget is where the **Chart view** toggle lives. The doc: *&quot;You can use the `display` function … on Spark DataFrames … to produce the rich dataframe table view and chart view.&quot;*\n\n- **B `show`** – `df.show()` prints a plain ASCII table to the cell output. No widget, no chart view.\n- **A `displayHTML`** – renders an arbitrary HTML string. It is unrelated to DataFrames and gives you no chart view.\n- **C `write`** – `df.write` persists the DataFrame to storage; it produces no output at all.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Notebook visualization in Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/notebook-visualization?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
