  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q17",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q17",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1. Report1 includes a Python visual.\nData displayed by the visual is grouped automatically and duplicate rows are NOT displayed.\nYou need all rows to appear in the visual.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Reference the columns in the Python code by index."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Modify the Sort Column By property for all columns."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Add a unique field to each row."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Modify the Summarize By property for all columns."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Power BI aggregates the data it hands to a Python (or R) visual: identical rows are collapsed before the script ever runs. The documented workaround is to make every row distinguishable. From the *Tips* section of the Python visuals doc: *&quot;In some cases, you might not want automatic grouping to occur, or you might want all rows to appear, including duplicates. In those cases, you can **add an index field to your dataset that causes all rows to be considered unique** and prevents grouping.&quot;*\n\nAdding a field whose value is different on every row is exactly **C — add a unique field to each row**.\n\n- **A** – *&quot;Reference the columns in the Python code by index&quot;* is about how your script addresses `dataset` columns. It changes nothing about the grouping Power BI has already applied before the script runs.\n- **B** – *Sort Column By* controls display order only.\n- **D** – *Summarize By* governs how a numeric column is aggregated in a visual (Sum, Average, …). Setting it to *Don&#x27;t summarize* stops measures being summed, but the row-level de-duplication that feeds the Python visual still happens.\n\n*Note:* the highest-voted comment (+73) answers **A** — but it quotes the doc sentence above, which describes adding an **index field** (a new column of unique values), not &quot;referencing columns by index&quot;. A later comment (+13) catches the mix-up. The PDF key and 53% of the community are on **C**.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Create Power BI visuals by using Python",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/desktop-python-visuals?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q17"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q18",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q18",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model. The model contains data about retail stores.\nYou need to write a DAX query that will be executed by using the XMLA endpoint. The query must return a table of stores that have opened since\nDecember 1, 2023.\nHow should you complete the DAX expression? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more\nthan once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Line 1 (before  VAR _SalesSince = DATE (2023, 12, 01) )",
        "options": [
          "DEFINE",
          "EVALUATE",
          "FILTER",
          "SUMMARIZE",
          "TABLE"
        ],
        "answer": "DEFINE"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Line 3 (before  FILTER( )",
        "options": [
          "DEFINE",
          "EVALUATE",
          "FILTER",
          "SUMMARIZE",
          "TABLE"
        ],
        "answer": "EVALUATE"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Inside FILTER:  ______ (Store, Store[Name], Store[OpenDate]),",
        "options": [
          "DEFINE",
          "EVALUATE",
          "FILTER",
          "SUMMARIZE",
          "TABLE"
        ],
        "answer": "SUMMARIZE"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**DEFINE — EVALUATE — SUMMARIZE.** A DAX *query* (the kind the XMLA endpoint executes, as opposed to a measure) has a fixed skeleton:\n\n```dax\nDEFINE\n    VAR _SalesSince = DATE ( 2023, 12, 01 )\nEVALUATE\n    FILTER (\n        SUMMARIZE ( Store, Store[Name], Store[OpenDate] ),\n        Store[OpenDate] &gt;= _SalesSince\n    )\n```\n\n- **`DEFINE`** introduces the optional definition block. It is *&quot;used to define entities that exist only for the duration of the query&quot;* — here the `VAR _SalesSince`. It must come first, and a query may have at most one.\n- **`EVALUATE`** is mandatory and introduces the table expression that the query returns: *&quot;the EVALUATE statement … contains a table expression to be evaluated and returned.&quot;*\n- **`SUMMARIZE`** is the missing function inside `FILTER`. `FILTER(&lt;table&gt;, &lt;condition&gt;)` needs a *table* as its first argument, and `SUMMARIZE(Store, Store[Name], Store[OpenDate])` produces the two-column table of stores that the filter then narrows to those opened on or after 1 December 2023.\n\n- **`TABLE`** is not a DAX query keyword (it is a `DEFINE TABLE` sub-clause, which does not fit any of these slots).\n- **`FILTER`** is already printed in the code, so it is a distractor in the Values pane.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "DEFINE statement (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/define-statement-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "EVALUATE statement (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/evaluate-statement-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SUMMARIZE function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/summarize-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p068_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q18"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p068_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p068_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q19",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q19",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a dataflow named Dataflow1. Dataflow1 has a query that returns 2,000 rows.\nYou view the query in Power Query as shown in the following exhibit.\nWhat can you identify about the pickupLongitude column?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "The column has duplicate values."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "All the table rows are profiled."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "The column has missing values."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "There are 935 values that occur only once."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Read the **Column statistics** panel in the exhibit for `pickupLongitude`:\n\n| Statistic | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Count | **1000** |\n| Error count | 0 |\n| Null count | **0** |\n| Distinct count | **935** |\n| Unique count | **871** |\n| NaN count | 0 |\n\n**A — the column has duplicate values.** 1,000 values but only **935 distinct** ones, so 65 of them repeat a value already seen. Duplicates, confirmed.\n\n- **B** – the query returns **2,000** rows but `Count` is **1,000**. Power Query profiles only the **top 1,000 rows** by default (the status bar offers &quot;Column profiling based on top 1000 rows&quot;), so not all rows are profiled.\n- **C** – `Null count` is **0** and `Error count` is **0**. No missing values.\n- **D** – 935 is the **Distinct count** (how many different values exist), not how many occur exactly once. The count of values occurring only once is the **Unique count**, which is **871**.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Data profiling tools in Power Query",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/data-profiling-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p070_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q19"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p070_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q20",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q20",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant named Tenant1 that contains a workspace named WS1. WS1 uses a capacity named C1 and contains a dataset named\nDS1.\nYou need to ensure read-write access to DS1 is available by using XMLA endpoint.\nWhat should be modified first?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "the DS1 settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "the WS1 settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "the C1 settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the Tenant1 settings"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The XMLA endpoint has **two** switches, and the question asks which to modify *first* to get **read-write**:\n\n| Where | Setting | Default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Tenant settings | *Allow XMLA endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises datasets* | **Enabled** |\n| **Capacity settings** | **XMLA Endpoint** — Off / **Read Only** / Read Write | **Read Only** |\n\nThe tenant switch is already on by default, so nothing needs changing there. The capacity switch defaults to **Read Only**, which is precisely what blocks read-write access. The doc&#x27;s instruction is *&quot;In the capacity settings, expand the Power BI workloads, and under XMLA Endpoint select Read Write.&quot;*\n\n- **A / B** – there is no XMLA setting on a dataset or on a workspace.\n- **D** – the tenant setting governs whether XMLA endpoints may be used *at all*; it is on by default and does not control read-write.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint — enable XMLA read-write",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q20"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q21",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q21",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 is assigned to a Fabric capacity.\nYou need to recommend a solution to provide users with the ability to create and publish custom Direct Lake semantic models by using external\ntools. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich three actions in the Fabric Admin portal should you include in the recommendation? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, set Allow XMLA Endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises datasets to Enabled."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, set Allow Azure Active Directory guest users to access Microsoft Fabric to Enabled."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, select Users can edit data model in the Power BI service."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "From the Capacity settings, set XMLA Endpoint to Read Write."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, set Users can create Fabric items to Enabled."
      },
      {
        "id": "F",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, enable Publish to Web."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Three things have to be true before a user can build and publish a custom Direct Lake model from an external tool such as Tabular Editor:\n\n- **A — Tenant settings → *Allow XMLA Endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises datasets* = Enabled.** This is the org-wide master switch for XMLA connectivity. With it off, nothing below matters.\n- **D — Capacity settings → *XMLA Endpoint* = Read Write.** The capacity default is **Read Only**, which permits external tools to connect and query but not to deploy. Publishing a model requires **Read Write**.\n- **E — Tenant settings → *Users can create Fabric items* = Enabled.** A Direct Lake semantic model is a Fabric item, so users need the right to create Fabric items at all.\n\nRejected:\n- **B** *Allow Azure AD guest users…* – about external identities, irrelevant here, and granting it would violate least privilege.\n- **C** *Users can edit data model in the Power BI service* – enables the **web** modelling experience. The requirement is **external tools** over XMLA, which is a different path.\n- **F** *Publish to Web* – publishes reports anonymously to the public internet. Unrelated, and a serious least-privilege violation.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Direct Lake overview",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q21"
    ],
