  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q22",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q22",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are creating a semantic model in Microsoft Power BI Desktop.\nYou plan to make bulk changes to the model by using the Tabular Model Definition Language (TMDL) extension for Microsoft Visual Studio Code.\nYou need to save the semantic model to a file.\nWhich file format should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "PBIP"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "PBIX"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "PBIT"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "PBIDS"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**PBIP — Power BI Project.** Saving as a project writes the report and the semantic model out as **plain-text folders on disk** instead of one binary file, which is what makes the model definition editable in VS Code and diffable in Git. The semantic model folder is where the **TMDL** files live (`definition/tables/*.tmdl`), so the TMDL extension for VS Code can open and bulk-edit them.\n\n- **B PBIX** – the standard Power BI Desktop file. It is a single binary archive; external text tooling cannot edit the model inside it.\n- **C PBIT** – a Power BI *template*: a PBIX without the data. Still binary, still not text-editable.\n- **D PBIDS** – a tiny &quot;data source&quot; connection file used to start a new report against a given source. It contains no model at all.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Power BI Desktop projects (PBIP)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Tabular Model Definition Language (TMDL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/analysis-services/tmdl/tmdl-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q22"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q23",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q23",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains three schemas named schemaA, schemaB, and\nschemaC.\nYou need to ensure that a user named User1 can truncate tables in schemaA only.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "GRANT  ______",
        "options": [
          "ALTER",
          "CONNECT",
          "EXECUTE"
        ],
        "answer": "ALTER"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "ON  ______  TO User1;",
        "options": [
          "DATABASE::schemaA",
          "OBJECT::schemaA",
          "SCHEMA::schemaA"
        ],
        "answer": "SCHEMA::schemaA"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`GRANT ALTER ON SCHEMA::schemaA TO User1;`**\n\n**The permission — `ALTER`.** There is no `TRUNCATE` permission in T-SQL. `TRUNCATE TABLE` requires, at minimum, **`ALTER` permission on the table**: *&quot;The minimum permission required is ALTER on table_name.&quot;* Granting `ALTER` at the schema level covers every table in that schema, present and future.\n- `CONNECT` only lets a principal open a connection to the database.\n- `EXECUTE` applies to programmable objects (procedures, functions), not to truncating tables.\n\n**The securable — `SCHEMA::schemaA`.** The requirement is *schemaA only*. Schema-scoped grants use the `SCHEMA::` class, and this scopes the permission to exactly one schema — schemaB and schemaC are untouched.\n- `DATABASE::schemaA` is the wrong class *and* the wrong scope: it would name the whole database (and `schemaA` is not a database).\n- `OBJECT::schemaA` scopes to a single object such as one table; `schemaA` is a schema, not an object.\n\n*Note:* one commenter points out that Fabric Warehouse did not originally support `TRUNCATE TABLE`. That does not change the answer — the question is testing the GRANT syntax, and `ALTER ON SCHEMA::` is the least-privilege grant for it.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "TRUNCATE TABLE (Transact-SQL) — Permissions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/truncate-table-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "GRANT Schema Permissions (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/grant-schema-permissions-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p081_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q23"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p081_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p081_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q24",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q24",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You plan to deploy Microsoft Power BI items by using Fabric deployment pipelines. You have a deployment pipeline that contains three stages\nnamed Development, Test, and Production. A workspace is assigned to each stage.\nYou need to provide Power BI developers with access to the pipeline. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nEnsure that the developers can deploy items to the workspaces for Development and Test.\nPrevent the developers from deploying items to the workspace for Production.\nFollow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich three levels of access should you assign to the developers? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Build permission to the production semantic models"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Admin access to the deployment pipeline"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Viewer access to the Development and Test workspaces"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Viewer access to the Production workspace"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces"
      },
      {
        "id": "F",
        "text": "Contributor access to the Production workspace"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "B",
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Deployment pipelines need permission in **two** places: on the **pipeline** (to see and operate it) and on each **workspace** (to read from the source and write to the target). Least privilege then decides the level in each workspace.\n\n- **B — Admin access to the deployment pipeline.** Pipeline access is a single role: users granted access to a pipeline get pipeline admin rights. Without it the developers cannot open or run the pipeline at all.\n- **E — Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces.** Deploying *into* a stage requires a workspace role that can write items — **Contributor** is the lowest such role, so it satisfies least privilege while still allowing the required deployments.\n- **D — Viewer access to the Production workspace.** The developers must be *prevented* from deploying to Production but still need the pipeline to function. **Viewer** is read-only: they can see the Production stage and compare it, but any deployment into it is blocked. Giving them nothing at all would be an option in principle, but Viewer is the level offered and it is read-only.\n\nRejected:\n- **A** *Build permission on the production semantic models* – lets someone build new content on a model; it has nothing to do with deploying and adds privilege without need.\n- **C** *Viewer on Development and Test* – read-only, so they could not deploy to the two stages they are supposed to deploy to.\n- **F** *Contributor on Production* – would let them deploy to Production, which is the one thing the requirements forbid.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Deployment pipelines — permissions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Roles in workspaces",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q24"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q25",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q25",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace that contains a DirectQuery semantic model. The model queries a data source that has 500 million rows.\nYou have a Microsoft Power Bi report named Report1 that uses the model. Report1 contains visuals on multiple pages.\nYou need to reduce the query execution time for the visuals on all the pages.\nWhat are two features that you can use? Each correct answer presents a complete solution,\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "user-defined aggregations"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "automatic aggregation"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "query caching"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "OneLake integration"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Both aggregation features answer a DAX query from a small pre-summarised cache instead of sending it to the 500-million-row source, and both work for **every** visual on **every** page — which is what &quot;all the pages&quot; demands.\n\n- **A — user-defined aggregations.** You build an aggregation table yourself and map its columns to the detail table. The doc: *&quot;Aggregations in Power BI can improve query performance over very large DirectQuery semantic models.&quot;* Queries that can be answered from the aggregation hit the cache; only the rest fall through to DirectQuery.\n- **B — automatic aggregation.** The same idea, managed for you: Power BI trains on the query log and maintains the aggregation table itself. It is designed specifically for DirectQuery models.\n\nRejected:\n- **C — query caching.** Not available for DirectQuery. The doc&#x27;s limitation is explicit: query caching applies to models in **Import** mode; it does not apply to DirectQuery or live connections. It also only helps the *initial* page load for returning users, not &quot;all the pages&quot;.\n- **D — OneLake integration.** A semantic-model setting that writes **Import**-mode table data into OneLake as Delta. It does nothing for a DirectQuery model and is not a query-performance feature.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Use aggregations in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-aggregations?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Automatic aggregations",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/aggregations-auto?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Query caching in Power BI Premium",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/power-bi-query-caching?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q25"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q26",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q26",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains 30 CSV files in OneLake. The files are updated daily.\nYou create a Microsoft Power BI semantic model named Model1 that uses the CSV files as a data source. You configure incremental refresh for\nModel1 and publish the model to a Premium capacity in the Fabric tenant.\nWhen you initiate a refresh of Model1, the refresh fails after running out of resources.\nWhat is a possible cause of the failure?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Query folding is occurring."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Only refresh complete days is selected."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "XMLA Endpoint is set to Read Only."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Query folding is NOT occurring."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "The delta type of the column used to partition the data has changed."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Incremental refresh depends on query folding.** The `RangeStart` / `RangeEnd` parameters are supposed to be translated into a filter that the *source* applies, so each partition reads only its own slice. Microsoft states the requirement directly: *&quot;Incremental refresh is designed for data sources that support query folding.&quot;* And the failure mode is documented too — when folding does not happen, *&quot;the Power Query engine … must retrieve all rows and then apply the filter locally&quot;*, once **per partition**.\n\nCSV files are flat files: there is no query engine behind them to push a filter into, so nothing folds. Every partition drags in all 30 files in full and filters them in memory, and the refresh runs out of resources. That is exactly **D**.\n\n- **A** – folding *occurring* is the healthy case; it would make the refresh cheap, not fail.\n- **B** – *Only refresh complete days* trims the newest partial day. It reduces work slightly; it cannot cause resource exhaustion.\n- **C** – the XMLA endpoint governs external-tool connectivity. It has no bearing on a refresh initiated in the service.\n- **E** – changing the data type of the partition column breaks incremental refresh with a *schema/type* error, not an out-of-resources failure.\n\n*Note:* 37% of the community picks E, and one highly-voted comment objects that folding &quot;does not make sense on a CSV source&quot;. That objection is the answer restated: because a CSV cannot fold, the filters land in memory — which is precisely why the refresh dies.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Incremental refresh and real-time data for semantic models",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q26"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q27",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q27",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that uses a Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity.\nYou need to enable scale-out for a semantic model.\nWhat should you do first?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "At the semantic model level, set Large dataset storage format to Off."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "At the tenant level, set Create and use Metrics to Enabled."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "At the semantic model level, set Large dataset storage format to On."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "At the tenant level, set Data Activator to Enabled."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Semantic model **scale-out** creates read-only replicas of a model so that read queries and refreshes do not contend. Its documented prerequisite is the large-model storage format: *&quot;Before you can enable scale-out for a semantic model, the model must be in large semantic model storage format.&quot;* So the **first** step is to set **Large dataset storage format = On** at the semantic model level.\n\n- **A** – setting it *Off* is the opposite of what is required.\n- **B** *Create and use Metrics* – enables Power BI goals/scorecards. Unrelated.\n- **D** *Data Activator* – the real-time alerting item (now Activator). Unrelated.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Configure semantic model scale-out",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-scale-out-configure?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q27"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q28",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q28",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse. The warehouse uses row-level security (RLS).\nYou create a Direct Lake semantic model that uses the Delta tables and RLS of the warehouse.\nWhen users interact with a report built from the model, which mode will be used by the DAX queries?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "DirectQuery"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Dual"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Direct Lake"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Import"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Direct Lake normally reads Delta/Parquet straight from OneLake with no SQL engine in the path. **Row-level security defined on the warehouse is enforced by the SQL engine**, so a model that must honour warehouse RLS cannot use that shortcut — it has to route the query through SQL. The doc says so in one sentence: *&quot;Power BI queries on a warehouse in Direct Lake mode will fall back to DirectQuery mode to abide by row-level security.&quot;*\n\nSo the DAX queries run in **DirectQuery** mode — **A**.\n\n- **C Direct Lake** – what the model is *configured* as, but not what the queries end up *using* once RLS forces the fallback. This is the trap, and 43% of the community falls into it.\n- **B Dual** – a storage mode you set on a table in a composite model. Direct Lake fallback does not produce it.\n- **D Import** – nothing is cached into the model; the data stays in the warehouse.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Row-level security in Fabric Data Warehousing",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Direct Lake overview — DirectQuery fallback",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q28"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q29",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q29",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a complex semantic model. The model is based on a star schema and contains many tables, including a\nfact table named Sales.\nYou need to create a diagram of the model. The diagram must contain only the Sales table and related tables.\nWhat should you use from Microsoft Power BI Desktop?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "data categories"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Data view"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Model view"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DAX query view"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Model view** is the Power BI Desktop view that draws the tables and the relationships between them — the model diagram. It also supports **layouts**: you can add a new layout tab and drag in just the `Sales` table, then use *Add related tables* to pull in exactly its related dimensions. That produces a diagram containing only Sales and its neighbours, which is what the question asks for.\n\n- **A data categories** – tag a column as e.g. a City or a URL so visuals render it correctly. Not a diagram.\n- **B Data view** – shows the rows of one table. No relationships, no diagram.\n- **D DAX query view** – write and run DAX queries against the model. No diagram.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Model view in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-relationship-view?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q29"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q30",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q30",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model. The model uses Direct Lake mode.\nYou suspect that some DAX queries load unnecessary columns into memory.\nYou need to identify the frequently used columns that are loaded into memory.\nWhat are two ways to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Use the Analyze in Excel feature."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Use the Vertipaq Analyzer tool."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Query the $System.DISCOVER_STORAGE_TABLE_COLUMN_SEGMENTS dynamic management view (DMV)."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Query the DISCOVER_MEMORYGRANT dynamic management view (DMV)."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "B",
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>In Direct Lake, columns are **paged into memory on demand** (transcoded from Parquet the first time a query touches them) and stay resident until evicted. To see which columns are actually resident, you need column-segment-level telemetry.\n\n- **C — `$System.DISCOVER_STORAGE_TABLE_COLUMN_SEGMENTS`.** This DMV reports the in-memory segments per column, including the Direct-Lake-specific `TEMPERATURE` and `LAST_ACCESSED` columns. Temperature is literally a frequency-of-use score, so this is the direct answer to &quot;which frequently used columns are loaded into memory&quot;.\n- **B — VertiPaq Analyzer.** The standard tool (built into DAX Studio and Tabular Editor) that reads those same DMVs and presents per-column size and cardinality. Microsoft&#x27;s own Direct Lake analysis guidance points at it.\n\nRejected:\n- **A Analyze in Excel** – connects a PivotTable to the model. It tells you nothing about memory residency.\n- **D `DISCOVER_MEMORYGRANT`** – reports memory *grants for running queries*, not which columns are paged in.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Analyze query processing for Direct Lake semantic models",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-analyze-query-processing?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Dynamic management views for semantic models",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/analysis-services/instances/use-dynamic-management-views-dmvs-to-monitor-analysis-services?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q30"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q31",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q31",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have the source data model shown in the following exhibit.\nThe primary keys of the tables are indicated by a key symbol beside the columns involved in each key.\nYou need to create a dimensional data model that will enable the analysis of order items by date, product, and customer.\nWhat should you include in the solution? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "The relationship between OrderItem and Product must be based on:",
        "options": [
          "The ProductID column",
          "Both the CompanyID and the ProductID columns",
          "A new key that combines the CompanyID and ProductID columns"
        ],
        "answer": "The ProductID column"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "The Company entity must be:",
        "options": [
          "Omitted",
          "Denormalized into the Product entity only",
          "Denormalized into the Customer and Product entities"
        ],
        "answer": "Omitted"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**The ProductID column — Omitted.**\n\nThe source is a normalised OLTP model in which `Company` sits beside `Product`, `Customer` and `OrderItem`, and both `Product` and `Customer` carry a **composite primary key** that includes `CompanyID`. The target is a star schema: fact `OrderItem`, dimensions `Date`, `Product`, `Customer`.\n\n**Blank 2 — Omitted.** The stated goal is *&quot;analysis of order items by date, product, and customer&quot;*. `Company` is not one of those dimensions, and in the exhibit the `Company` table has **exactly one column — `CompanyID`**, its own key. A table with no descriptive attributes carries nothing to analyse by and nothing to denormalise; keeping it would only add a join. Dropping it is the correct dimensional-modelling call.\n\n**Blank 1 — The ProductID column.** This follows from blank 2: once `Company` is out of the model, `CompanyID` is no longer part of the key structure, so `OrderItem` relates to `Product` on `ProductID` alone. That is also the only shape a Power BI relationship can take — **model relationships are single-column**; the doc states you *&quot;can&#x27;t create relationships based on multiple columns&quot;*. That rules out *Both the CompanyID and the ProductID columns* outright, whatever you do with `Company`.\n\n**⚠ This question is genuinely contested — read this before trusting the grade.** Weighted by upvotes the community splits:\n\n| Blank | Reading | Weight |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | The ProductID column | **64** |\n| 1 | Both CompanyID and ProductID | 32 |\n| 1 | A new combined key | 13 |\n| 2 | Denormalized into Customer and Product | **84** |\n| 2 | Omitted | 25 |\n\nSo the community prefers *ProductID* for blank 1 (agreeing with the key) but *Denormalized* for blank 2 (disagreeing with it). Note that the most popular combination, *ProductID + Denormalized*, is **internally inconsistent**: if you denormalise `Company` into `Product` and `Customer`, `CompanyID` survives as an attribute and the product key stays composite — so the relationship could not be on `ProductID` alone. The two self-consistent pairs are **ProductID + Omitted** (the answer key) and **a new combined key + Denormalized**. The key&#x27;s pair is the one the exam rewards, and the attribute-free `Company` table supports it.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Model relationships in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-relationships-understand?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
