  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q32",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q32",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 uses Import mode. Model1 contains a table named Orders.\nOrders has 100 million rows and the following fields.\nYou need to reduce the memory used by Model1 and the time it takes to refresh the model.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Split OrderDateTime into separate date and time columns."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Replace TotalQuantity with a calculated column."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Convert Quantity into the Text data type."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Replace TotalSalesAmount with a measure."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Both goals — less memory **and** faster refresh — point at the same two culprits in the table: a very high-cardinality column and a calculated column.\n\n- **A — split `OrderDateTime` into separate date and time columns.** VertiPaq compresses column by column, and compression collapses as cardinality rises. A `Date/Time` down to the second over 100 million rows is close to unique per row — the worst case for the dictionary. Split it and you get at most a few thousand distinct dates and 86,400 distinct times, both of which compress hard. This is Microsoft&#x27;s own guidance: *&quot;split the column into separate date and time columns&quot;* to reduce model size.\n- **D — replace `TotalSalesAmount` with a measure.** It is currently a **calculated column** (`Quantity * Price`). Calculated columns are **materialised at refresh and stored in the model**, so they cost both refresh time and memory — 100 million decimal values of it. A measure is evaluated at query time and stores nothing. The guidance: *&quot;calculated columns … increase the model size and can prolong data refresh time … consider a measure instead.&quot;*\n\nRejected:\n- **B — replace `TotalQuantity` with a calculated column.** `TotalQuantity` is already a **measure** (see the Description column). This swaps the cheap thing for the expensive thing — backwards on both goals.\n- **C — convert `Quantity` to Text.** Text columns are larger and slower than integers and cannot be aggregated. Strictly worse.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Data reduction techniques for Import modeling",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/import-modeling-data-reduction?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Avoid using calculated columns",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/power-bi-optimization?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#avoid-using-calculated-columns"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p103_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q32"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p103_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q33",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q33",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model.\nYou need to prevent report creators from populating visuals by using implicit measures.\nWhat are two tools that you can use to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Tabular Editor"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DAX Studio"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>An *implicit* measure is what Power BI creates on the fly when a report author drags a raw numeric column into a visual and lets it be summarised. You suppress that behaviour with the model-level property **`DiscourageImplicitMeasures`**, which forces authors to use explicit measures instead.\n\n- **A — Power BI Desktop.** Exposed in **Model Explorer** as the semantic model property *Discourage implicit measures* (it is also set automatically the moment you create a calculation group).\n- **B — Tabular Editor.** Sets `Model.DiscourageImplicitMeasures` directly against the TOM, which was the only way to do it before Desktop surfaced it.\n\nRejected:\n- **C SSMS** – can connect to the XMLA endpoint and script a model, but it is not a model-authoring tool for this property.\n- **D DAX Studio** – a query and performance-analysis tool. It is **read-only** against the model; it cannot change model metadata.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Model explorer in Power BI Desktop — discourage implicit measures",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/model-explorer?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q33"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q34",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q34",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains two lakehouses.\nYou are building a dataflow that will combine data from the lakehouses. The applied steps from one of the queries in the dataflow is shown in the\nfollowing exhibit.\nUse the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic.\n\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "______ of the transformation steps in the query will fold.",
        "options": [
          "All",
          "None",
          "Some"
        ],
        "answer": "Some"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "The Added custom step will be performed in ______.",
        "options": [
          "each lakehouse's query engine",
          "the Microsoft Power Query engine",
          "the source lakehouse query engine"
        ],
        "answer": "the Microsoft Power Query engine"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Some — the Microsoft Power Query engine.**\n\nRead the **step folding indicators** down the Applied steps pane. Power Query marks each step with one of these:\n\n| Indicator | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| *(no icon)* / plain | folds — will be evaluated at the source |\n| **orange/red warning icon** | **not folded** — this step breaks folding |\n| grey | folding could not be determined |\n\n**Blank 1 — Some.** The early steps (`Source`, `Navigation 1`) fold, but **`Capitalized each word`** carries the not-folded marker. Folding stops at the first step that cannot be translated into the source query, so everything before it folds and everything from it onward does not. That is *some*, not *All* and not *None*.\n\n**Blank 2 — the Microsoft Power Query engine.** `Added custom` sits **after** the break, so it can never be pushed down. The doc: once folding is broken, the remaining transformations *&quot;are processed by the Power Query engine&quot;* — data is pulled out of the source and transformed locally. It is also worth noting this query does an **Appended query** across two different lakehouses; a query that spans two sources has no single engine to fold into, which is why the options naming a lakehouse engine are wrong.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Step folding indicators in Power Query",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/step-folding-indicators?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Power Query query folding",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/query-folding-basics?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p107_i1.png, p107_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q34"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p107_i1.png",
      "p107_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p107_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q35",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q35",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse’. Lakehouse1 contains a table named Tablet.\nYou are creating a new data pipeline.\nYou plan to copy external data to Table’. The schema of the external data changes regularly.\nYou need the copy operation to meet the following requirements:\nReplace Table1 with the schema of the external data.\nReplace all the data in Table1 with the rows in the external data.\nYou add a Copy data activity to the pipeline.\nWhat should you do for the Copy data activity?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From the Source tab, add additional columns."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "From the Destination tab, set Table action to Overwrite."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "From the Settings tab, select Enable staging."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "From the Source tab, select Enable partition discovery."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "From the Source tab, select Recursively."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The Copy data activity&#x27;s **Destination** tab has a *Table action* setting with two choices, and their documented meanings decide this:\n\n| Table action | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Append** | adds rows to the existing table, keeping its schema |\n| **Overwrite** | **drops and recreates the table**, so both the schema and all the data are replaced |\n\nThe two requirements — *replace Table1 with the schema of the external data* and *replace all the data* — are exactly what **Overwrite** does, which matters here because *&quot;the schema of the external data changes regularly&quot;*.\n\n- **A** *add additional columns* – appends constant/metadata columns to the source; it does not replace anything.\n- **C** *Enable staging* – routes the copy through interim storage for throughput. No effect on schema or on replacing rows.\n- **D** *Enable partition discovery* – interprets a folder hierarchy as partition columns when reading.\n- **E** *Recursively* – reads sub-folders of the source path.\n\nNote that *truncate*-style behaviour would not be enough even if it were offered: truncating removes rows but leaves the old schema in place.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Copy activity in Data Factory for Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/copy-data-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q35"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q36",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q36",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse.\nYou plan to query sales data files by using the SQL endpoint. The files will be in an Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) storage bucket.\nYou need to recommend which file format to use and where to create a shortcut.\nWhich two actions should you include in the recommendation? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Create a shortcut in the Files section."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Use the Parquet format"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Use the CSV format."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Create a shortcut in the Tables section."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Use the delta format."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Two separate constraints, both driven by &quot;query … by using the SQL endpoint&quot;.\n\n- **D — create the shortcut in the Tables section.** The SQL analytics endpoint only exposes what the lakehouse recognises as a **table**. A shortcut in the **Files** section is unmanaged storage: it is visible to Spark and to `OPENROWSET`-style file access, but it is not surfaced as a table, so the SQL endpoint cannot query it. Table-section shortcuts are auto-discovered and appear in the endpoint.\n- **E — use the delta format.** The Tables area of a lakehouse is Delta Lake territory: *&quot;The Tables section … only supports Delta Lake tables.&quot;* A folder is promoted to a table when it carries a `_delta_log`; a bare Parquet or CSV folder dropped into Tables is flagged as unidentified and never reaches the SQL endpoint.\n\nRejected:\n- **A** – Files section, as above: invisible to the SQL endpoint.\n- **B Parquet** / **C CSV** – neither is a table format on its own. Delta *is* Parquet plus a transaction log, so choosing delta gives you the Parquet storage anyway, plus the log that makes it a table.\n\n*Note:* the vote is close (DE 50% / BD 44%), and the main dissenting comment claims *&quot;Delta format not supported by S3&quot;*. That is incorrect — Delta Lake is an open format that stores `_delta_log` alongside the Parquet files, and it works on S3 exactly as it does on ADLS. Amazon S3 is a supported shortcut target, so nothing blocks delta there.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "OneLake shortcuts",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Create an Amazon S3 shortcut",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/create-s3-shortcut?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Lakehouse and Delta Lake tables",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-and-delta-tables?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q36"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q37",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q37",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a subfolder named Subfolder1 that contains CSV\nfiles.\nYou need to convert the CSV files into the delta format that has V-Order optimization enabled.\nWhat should you do from Lakehouse explorer?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Use the Load to Tables feature."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Create a new shortcut in the Files section."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Create a new shortcut in the Tables section."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Use the Optimize feature."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Load to Tables** is the Lakehouse explorer command that converts files into managed Delta tables, and the doc states the output format outright: you can *&quot;load **CSV** or **Parquet** data and keep output in **Delta format with V-order optimization**.&quot;* Both halves of the requirement — delta format *and* V-Order — are covered by the one action, with no configuration.\n\n- **B / C — create a shortcut.** A shortcut is a *pointer*. It converts nothing; the underlying files stay CSV, which means a Tables-section shortcut would not even be recognised as a table.\n- **D — Optimize.** `OPTIMIZE` compacts small files in an **existing Delta table** (and can apply V-Order while doing so). It cannot turn CSV files into a Delta table in the first place, so there is nothing for it to act on yet.\n\nIn Lakehouse explorer: right-click the folder (here `Subfolder1`) → **Load to Tables** → *New table*.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lakehouse Load to Delta Lake tables",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/load-to-tables?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q37"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q38",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q38",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains an unpartitioned table named Table1.\nYou plan to copy data to Table1 and partition the table based on a date column in the source data.\nYou create a Copy activity to copy the data to Table1.\nYou need to specify the partition column in the Destination settings of the Copy activity.\nWhat should you do first?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From the Destination tab, set Mode to Append."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "From the Destination tab, select the partition column."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "From the Source tab, select Enable partition discovery."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "From the Destination tabs, set Mode to Overwrite."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>This is a UI-sequencing question: the partition column picker **does not exist** until the table action allows the table to be rebuilt. Microsoft&#x27;s partitioning tutorial gives the order of operations verbatim: *&quot;Expand **Advanced**, in **Table action**, select **Overwrite**, and then select **Enable partition**. Under **Partition columns**, select **Add column**, and choose the column you want to use as the partition column.&quot;*\n\nThe reason is structural: **partitioning is part of a table&#x27;s physical layout**, fixed when the table is written. `Table1` already exists and is **unpartitioned**, so you cannot bolt a partition scheme onto it by appending — the table has to be dropped and recreated, which is what **Overwrite** does. Hence **D**, *first*.\n\n- **B — select the partition column** is the *goal*, not the first step; the option is greyed out/absent until Overwrite is set.\n- **A — Append** keeps the existing unpartitioned layout, so the partition option never appears.\n- **C — Enable partition discovery** is a **Source** setting. It reads partitions *out of* a source folder hierarchy; it has nothing to do with how the destination is written.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Tutorial: Load data to a lakehouse using partition columns",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/tutorial-lakehouse-partition?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q38"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q39",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q39",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the\nproblem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also\npossible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYour network contains an on-premises Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) domain named contoso.com that syncs with a Microsoft Entra\ntenant by using Microsoft Entra Connect.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model.\nYou enable dynamic row-level security (RLS) for the model and deploy the model to the Fabric service.\nYou query a measure that includes the USERNAME() function, and the query returns a blank result.\nYou need to ensure that the measure returns the user principal name (UPN) of a user.\nSolution: You add user objects to the list of synced objects in Microsoft Entra Connect.\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No.** This is one of four &quot;same scenario, different solution&quot; items in the set. The problem is *which DAX function the measure calls*, not how identities reach Microsoft Entra ID.\n\nSyncing more user objects through Entra Connect changes nothing about `USERNAME()`. If the user can already sign in to Fabric and run the report, their identity is by definition already present in Entra ID — so the sync is not the missing piece. The documented fix is to call **`USERPRINCIPALNAME()`**, which *&quot;returns the user principal name&quot;* — reliably, in Desktop and in the service alike. `USERNAME()` returns `DOMAIN\\user` in Desktop, and is the wrong tool when you specifically need a UPN.\n\nAcross the set:\n\n| Question | Proposed solution | Meets the goal? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **T1Q39** | add user objects to Entra Connect sync | **No** |\n| T1Q129 | use `USEROBJECTID()` | No — returns the object GUID, not a UPN |\n| **T1Q130** | use `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` | **Yes** |\n| T1Q134 | create a role in the model | No — roles scope RLS; they do not change what the function returns |\n\n*Note:* the vote is a close 58/42. The &quot;Yes&quot; camp argues an unsynced user would produce a blank — but such a user could not have run the report at all, and only one solution in the set can be the intended fix.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "USERPRINCIPALNAME function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/userprincipalname-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "USERNAME function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/username-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q39"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q40",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q40",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have source data in a folder on a local computer.\nYou need to create a solution that will use Fabric to populate a data store. The solution must meet the following requirements:\nSupport the use of dataflows to load and append data to the data store.\nEnsure that Delta tables are V-Order optimized and compacted automatically.\nWhich type of data store should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "an Azure SQL database"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a warehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "a KQL database"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Both requirements point at the lakehouse:\n\n- **Dataflows can load and *append* to it.** Dataflow Gen2 lists Lakehouse as a data destination with Append and Replace update methods.\n- **Its tables *are* Delta tables.** The requirement is phrased as *&quot;ensure that **Delta tables** are V-Order optimized and compacted automatically&quot;* — Delta tables are the thing you manage in a lakehouse. Fabric&#x27;s Spark runtime applies V-Order on write and uses **Optimize Write** to bin-pack small files as they are produced, so the maintenance happens without you scheduling it.\n\nRejected:\n- **B — Azure SQL database** is not a Fabric data store and has no Delta tables or V-Order at all.\n- **D — KQL database** is for real-time/telemetry ingestion. Dataflows do not target it, and V-Order is not a KQL concept.\n- **C — warehouse** is the genuine contender (see the note below), but its storage is managed for you as SQL tables; you do not work with &quot;Delta tables&quot; there the way the requirement describes.\n\n**⚠ Doc drift — read this.** When this question was written, V-Order was **on by default** for Fabric Spark, which made the lakehouse an easy fit. The current doc reverses that default: *&quot;In Fabric, **V-order is disabled by default for all newly created workspaces** to optimize performance for write-heavy data engineering workloads&quot;* (`spark.sql.parquet.vorder.default = false`). Meanwhile the warehouse doc still guarantees it unconditionally: *&quot;Regardless of how you ingest data into warehouses, the data ingestion task optimizes the parquet files it produces by using V-Order write optimization.&quot;*\n\nSo on today&#x27;s documentation a warehouse is arguably the safer read of &quot;automatically&quot;, which is why 33% of the community votes **C**. The exam key and 67% of the community are on **A**, and &quot;Delta tables&quot; is the wording that gets you there — but be aware the ground has shifted under this question.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Optimize Delta Lake tables with V-Order in Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/delta-optimization-and-v-order?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Ingest data into the Warehouse",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/ingest-data?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Dataflow Gen2 data destinations",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/dataflow-gen2-data-destinations-and-managed-settings?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
