  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q42",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q42",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a data flow named Dataflow1 contains a query that returns the data shown in the\nfollowing exhibit.\nYou need to transform the data columns into attribute-value pairs, where columns become rows.\nYou select the VendorID column.\nWhich transformation should you select from the context menu of the VendorID column?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Group by"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Unpivot columns"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Unpivot other columns"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Split column"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Remove other columns"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>You want every column *except* `VendorID` turned into attribute/value rows, keeping `VendorID` as the identifier. You have selected `VendorID`, so the command that acts on **everything else** is **Unpivot other columns**.\n\nThe doc distinguishes the three unpivot commands:\n\n| Command | Acts on |\n| --- | --- |\n| Unpivot columns | the columns you selected |\n| **Unpivot other columns** | **every column you did *not* select** |\n| Unpivot only selected columns | the selected columns, and only those |\n\n*Unpivot other columns* has a second advantage the doc calls out: *&quot;Use this command in a query when not all columns are known. New columns added during a refresh operation are also unpivoted.&quot;*\n\n- **B Unpivot columns** would unpivot `VendorID` itself — the opposite of what is wanted.\n- **A Group by** aggregates rows; it does not reshape columns into rows.\n- **D Split column** divides one column into several by delimiter or position.\n- **E Remove other columns** deletes the other columns instead of unpivoting them.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Unpivot columns (Power Query)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/unpivot-column?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p126_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q42"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p126_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q43",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q43",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a data pipeline.\nYou need to ensure that the pipeline runs every four hours on Mondays and Fridays.\nTo what should you set Repeat for the schedule?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Daily"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "By the minute"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Weekly"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Hourly"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>A Fabric schedule has a single **Repeat** granularity, and only **Weekly** lets you pick *which days of the week* the schedule applies to. Set Repeat = **Weekly**, tick **Monday** and **Friday**, then add the six times (00:00, 04:00, 08:00, 12:00, 16:00, 20:00) to get the every-four-hours behaviour on just those two days.\n\n- **A Daily** – would run on all seven days. There is no day-of-week selector.\n- **D Hourly** – gives an every-N-hours interval, but applies it every day; it cannot be restricted to Monday and Friday.\n- **B By the minute** – same problem, at a finer interval.\n\nThe day-of-week restriction is the binding constraint, and Weekly is the only option that offers it.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Schedule a data pipeline in Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/pipeline-runs?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q43"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q44",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q44",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nSeveral times a day, the performance of all warehouse queries degrades. You suspect that Fabric is throttling the compute used by the warehouse.\nWhat should you use to identify whether throttling is occurring?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "the Capacity settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "the Monitoring hub"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "dynamic management views (DMVs)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The **Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app** is the tool Microsoft points you at for throttling. It reports capacity units consumed, overage, carry-forward and the throttling state over time, so it shows both *whether* throttling is happening and *what* caused it.\n\nThere is also a decisive practical reason the alternatives fail. From the throttling doc: *&quot;Just like most Warehouse operations, dynamic management views (DMVs) are also classified as background and covered by the Background Rejection policy. As a result, **DMVs cannot be queried when capacity is throttled**. Even though DMVs aren&#x27;t available, capacity admins can go to **Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app** to understand the root cause.&quot;*\n\n- **C DMVs** – exactly the thing that stops responding during throttling.\n- **A Capacity settings** – shows the SKU and its configuration, not live consumption.\n- **B Monitoring hub** – lists item runs and their status. It can show queries are slow; it cannot tell you the capacity is throttled.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Burstable capacity, smoothing and throttling in Fabric Data Warehousing",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/compute-capacity-smoothing-throttling?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/enterprise/metrics-app?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q44"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q45",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q45",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace that uses the default Spark starter pool and runtime version 1.2.\nYou plan to read a CSV file named Sales_raw.csv in a lakehouse, select columns, and save the data as a Delta table to the managed area of the\nlakehouse. Sales_raw.csv contains 12 columns.\nYou have the following code.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "The Spark engine will read only the 'SalesOrderNumber', 'OrderDate', 'CustomerName', 'UnitPrice' columns from Sales_raw.csv.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Removing the partition will reduce the execution time of the query.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Adding inferSchema='true' to the options will increase the execution time of the query.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No — No — Yes.** The code reads a 12-column CSV, keeps four columns, derives `Year` and writes a Delta table partitioned by `Year`.\n\n**1. Reads only the four selected columns → No.** Column pruning is a *columnar-format* optimisation: Parquet and Delta store each column separately, so Spark can skip the rest on disk. **CSV is row-based** — every byte of every line has to be read and parsed to find the field delimiters. The `.select(...)` prunes columns *after* the rows are read, so the whole file is still read.\n\n**2. Removing the partition will reduce the execution time of the query → No.** Partitioning by `Year` is what lets a later query skip irrelevant folders entirely (**partition pruning**). Remove it and every query scans the whole table, so query time goes **up**, not down. On the write side the cost is small here — a handful of distinct years means a handful of output folders.\n\n**3. `inferSchema=&quot;true&quot;` increases execution time → Yes.** Schema inference is an **extra pass over the data**: Spark reads the file once to work out each column&#x27;s type, then reads it again to load it. Supplying an explicit schema avoids that second pass, which is why Microsoft recommends it for large files.\n\n*Note:* statement 2 is contested. The highest-voted comment (+44) answers **Yes**, reading &quot;the query&quot; as this write job and arguing that `partitionBy` adds file-creation overhead. The next comment (+23) and the PDF answer-key image both answer **No**. The wording says *&quot;the query&quot;*, and partitioning is a read-side optimisation, so **No** is the better reading — but if you interpret it as the write job, the other side has a case.\n\n*(Two OCR slips in the exhibit: `.red` is `.read`, and `&quot;heade&quot;` is `&quot;header&quot;`.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Delta Lake table optimization and V-Order",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/delta-optimization-and-v-order?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p131_i1.png, p131_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q45"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p131_i1.png",
      "p131_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p131_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q46",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q46",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nA user discovers that a report that usually takes two minutes to render has been running for 45 minutes and has still not rendered.\nYou need to identify what is preventing the report query from completing.\nWhich dynamic management view (DMV) should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "sys.dm_exec_requests"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "sys.dm_exec_sessions"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "sys.dm_exec_connections"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "sys.dm_pdw_exec_requests"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`sys.dm_exec_requests`** lists every request **currently executing**, with the columns you need to diagnose a stuck query: `status`, `command`, `total_elapsed_time`, and — critically — **`blocking_session_id`** and **`wait_type` / `wait_time`**. Those last ones answer &quot;what is *preventing* the query from completing&quot;.\n\nFabric Warehouse supports a small set of DMVs for exactly this task; the monitoring doc&#x27;s worked example joins `sys.dm_exec_sessions`, `sys.dm_exec_requests` and `sys.dm_exec_connections`, and it is `dm_exec_requests` that carries the execution state.\n\n- **B `sys.dm_exec_sessions`** – who is connected (login, host, session state). It does not describe the running request.\n- **C `sys.dm_exec_connections`** – the transport layer: protocol, client address, connect time.\n- **D `sys.dm_pdw_exec_requests`** – the **Azure Synapse dedicated SQL pool (PDW)** equivalent. It is not one of the three DMVs Fabric Warehouse supports.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Monitor connections, sessions, and requests using DMVs",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/monitor-using-dmv?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q46"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q47",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q47",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are creating a data flow in Fabric to ingest data from an Azure SQL database by using a T-SQL statement.\nYou need to ensure that any foldable Power Query transformation steps are processed by the Microsoft SQL Server engine.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once,\nor not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Query =  ______ . …",
        "options": [
          "EnableFolding",
          "NativeQuery",
          "Optimize",
          "Record",
          "StopFolding",
          "Table",
          "Value"
        ],
        "answer": "Value"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Query = Value .  ______  ( Database, \"SELECT …\", null, [ … ] )",
        "options": [
          "EnableFolding",
          "NativeQuery",
          "Optimize",
          "Record",
          "StopFolding",
          "Table",
          "Value"
        ],
        "answer": "NativeQuery"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "[  ______  = true ]",
        "options": [
          "EnableFolding",
          "NativeQuery",
          "Optimize",
          "Record",
          "StopFolding",
          "Table",
          "Value"
        ],
        "answer": "EnableFolding"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Value — NativeQuery — EnableFolding.** The completed step is:\n\n```m\nQuery = Value.NativeQuery(\n    Database,\n    &quot;SELECT * FROM customer WHERE country IN (&#x27;USA&#x27;, &#x27;UK&#x27;)&quot;,\n    null,\n    [EnableFolding = true]\n)\n```\n\n- **`Value.NativeQuery`** is the M function that runs a native (T-SQL) statement against a source. Its signature is `Value.NativeQuery(target as any, query as text, optional parameters as any, optional options as nullable record)`.\n- **`[EnableFolding = true]`** is the fourth argument, the options **record**. By default a native query is a folding *dead end* — Power Query cannot see inside your SQL, so every later step is evaluated locally. Setting this flag tells the mashup engine it may continue folding on top of the native query: *&quot;you can indicate that you want to fold the transformations … by setting `EnableFolding` to true.&quot;* That is precisely the requirement, &quot;any foldable Power Query transformation steps are processed by the Microsoft SQL Server engine&quot;.\n\nDistractors: **`Table`** and **`Record`** are the wrong M namespaces (`Table.NativeQuery` does not exist); **`Optimize`** is not an M function; **`StopFolding`** is not a real option (and would do the opposite of what is asked).</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Native query folding in Power Query",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/native-query-folding?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Value.NativeQuery — Power Query M",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/powerquery-m/value-nativequery?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p137_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q47"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p137_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p137_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q48",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q48",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nReadings from 100 IoT devices are appended to a Delta table in Lakehouse1. Each set of readings is approximately 25 KB. Approximately 10 GB of\ndata is received daily.\nAll the table and SparkSession settings are set to the default.\nYou discover that queries are slow to execute. In addition, the lakehouse storage contains data and log files that are no longer used.\nYou need to remove the files that are no longer used and combine small files into larger files with a target size of 1 GB per file.\nWhat should you do? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not\nat all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Remove the files:",
        "options": [
          "Set the autoCompact table setting.",
          "Set the optimizeWrite table setting.",
          "Run the VACUUM command on a schedule.",
          "Set the autoCompact SparkSession setting.",
          "Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule.",
          "Set the parallelDelete SparkSession setting."
        ],
        "answer": "Run the VACUUM command on a schedule."
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Combine the files:",
        "options": [
          "Set the autoCompact table setting.",
          "Set the optimizeWrite table setting.",
          "Run the VACUUM command on a schedule.",
          "Set the autoCompact SparkSession setting.",
          "Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule.",
          "Set the parallelDelete SparkSession setting."
        ],
        "answer": "Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**VACUUM to remove — OPTIMIZE to combine.** These are the two Delta Lake maintenance commands, and each maps to one requirement:\n\n| Requirement | Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| *&quot;remove the files that are no longer used&quot;* | **`VACUUM`** | *&quot;Reduces storage costs by deleting data files no longer referenced by the table&quot;* — files orphaned by updates/deletes, outside the retention window (7 days by default). |\n| *&quot;combine small files into larger files&quot;* | **`OPTIMIZE`** | *&quot;Improves query performance by optimizing file sizes&quot;* — bin-packs many small Parquet files into fewer large ones. |\n\nThe scenario is the classic small-file problem: 100 devices × ~25 KB appends, ~10 GB/day, which produces a huge number of tiny files and is why &quot;queries are slow to execute&quot;.\n\nWhy the settings are wrong for *this* question — note the stem says *&quot;All the table and SparkSession settings are set to the default&quot;*, and asks what to do about files that **already exist**:\n- **autoCompact** and **optimizeWrite** are *write-time* settings. They improve the shape of **future** writes; they do not go back and compact the backlog, and neither of them deletes anything.\n- **parallelDelete** only tunes how `VACUUM` parallelises its deletions — it is not an action that removes files by itself.\n- Neither write-time setting can hit the stated **1 GB target file size**, which is what `OPTIMIZE` (with `spark.databricks.delta.optimize.maxFileSize`) is for.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Delta Lake table optimization and V-Order",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/delta-optimization-and-v-order?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Table maintenance in the Fabric lakehouse",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-table-maintenance?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
