  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q49",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q49",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You need to create a data loading pattern for a Type 1 slowly changing dimension (SCD).\nWhich two actions should you include in the process? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Update rows when the non-key attributes have changed."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Insert new rows when the natural key exists in the dimension table, and the non-key attribute values have changed."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Update the effective end date of rows when the non-key attribute values have changed."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Insert new records when the natural key is a new value in the table."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>A **Type 1** slowly changing dimension **overwrites** — it keeps no history. That single fact settles all four options.\n\n- **A — update rows when the non-key attributes have changed.** This *is* Type 1: *&quot;A Type 1 SCD always reflects the latest values, and when changes in source data are detected, the dimension table data is overwritten.&quot;*\n- **D — insert new records when the natural key is a new value.** A key you have never seen is a genuinely new member, so it is inserted. Every SCD type does this; together with A it forms the complete upsert (MERGE) pattern.\n\nRejected:\n- **B — insert a new row when the key exists and attributes changed.** That is **Type 2**: a second row per member, versioned. Type 1 updates in place instead.\n- **C — update the effective end date.** Effective start/end dates are the **Type 2** validity window. A Type 1 dimension has no such columns, because there is no history to bound.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Understand star schema — slowly changing dimensions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#slowly-changing-dimensions"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q49"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q50",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q50",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 and an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account named storage1. Workspace1 contains a\nlakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nYou need to create a shortcut to storage1 in Lakehouse1.\nWhich connection and endpoint should you specify? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Connection:",
        "options": [
          "abfs",
          "abfss",
          "https"
        ],
        "answer": "https"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Endpoint:",
        "options": [
          "blob",
          "dfs",
          "file"
        ],
        "answer": "dfs"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**https — dfs.** The ADLS Gen2 shortcut dialog asks for a **connection URL**, and the doc gives its exact shape:\n\n```\nhttps://&lt;account_name&gt;.dfs.core.windows.net\n```\n\n- **Connection = `https`.** The shortcut is created over the storage account&#x27;s **HTTPS REST endpoint**. `abfs` / `abfss` are the **Hadoop driver** schemes you use from Spark code to *read* a path — they are not what you type when creating the shortcut.\n- **Endpoint = `dfs`.** ADLS Gen2 exposes two endpoints on the same account: `blob.core.windows.net` (flat Blob API) and `dfs.core.windows.net` (**Data Lake Storage Gen2 API**, with real hierarchical namespace support). Shortcuts require the **DFS** endpoint; `file` is Azure Files, a different service entirely.\n\nOnce the shortcut exists you address it inside Fabric with an `abfss://` OneLake path — which is what the `abfs`/`abfss` distractors are fishing for. One commenter who sat the exam reports the live item labels the first blank **&quot;protocol&quot;** rather than &quot;connection&quot;; the answer is the same.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Create an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 shortcut",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/create-adls-shortcut?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p143_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q50"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
