  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q51",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q51",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are analyzing customer purchases in a Fabric notebook by using PySpark.\nYou have the following DataFrames:\ntransactions: Contains five columns named transaction_id, customer_id, product_id, amount, and date and has 10 million rows, with each row\nrepresenting a transaction. customers: Contains customer details in 1,000 rows and three columns named customer_id, name, and country.\nYou need to join the DataFrames on the customer_id column. The solution must minimize data shuffling.\nYou write the following code.\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\nresults =\nWhich code should you run to populate the results DataFrame?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "transactions.join(F.broadcast(customers), transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "transactions.join(customers, transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id).distinct()"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "transactions.join(customers, transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "transactions.crossJoin(customers).where(transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>A plain join of a **10,000,000-row** DataFrame to a **1,000-row** one triggers a *shuffle hash / sort-merge* join: Spark hash-partitions **both** sides on `customer_id` and sends every row across the network. That is the shuffle the question wants eliminated.\n\n`F.broadcast(customers)` forces a **broadcast hash join**: the tiny DataFrame is copied once to every executor, and each executor then joins its local slice of `transactions` against the in-memory copy. The large side never moves. Microsoft&#x27;s guidance: *&quot;Broadcast hash join … avoids shuffling the large table, which significantly improves performance&quot;*, and it is the recommended pattern when one side is small.\n\n- **C** – the same join without the hint. Spark *may* broadcast it automatically if the table is under `spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold` (10 MB by default) and statistics are available, but it is not guaranteed — so it does not *minimize* shuffling by construction.\n- **B** – C plus a `.distinct()`, which adds **another** full shuffle. Strictly worse.\n- **D** – a `crossJoin` materialises 10 billion row pairs before filtering. Catastrophic.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Apache Spark performance — join strategies",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/spark-compute?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q51"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q52",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q52",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI report and a semantic model that uses Direct Lake mode.\nFrom Power BI Desktop, you open Performance analyzer as shown in the following exhibit.\nUse the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "The Direct Lake fallback behavior is set to",
        "options": [
          "Automatic",
          "DirectLakeOnly",
          "DirectQueryOnly"
        ],
        "answer": "Automatic"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "The query for the table visual is executed by using",
        "options": [
          "the composite model",
          "Direct Lake",
          "Direct Query"
        ],
        "answer": "Direct Lake"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Automatic — Direct Lake.**\n\nThe exhibit is Performance analyzer over the model from Microsoft&#x27;s *Analyze query processing for Direct Lake semantic models* article, which contains both a **table** and a **view**.\n\n**Blank 1 — Automatic.** `DirectLakeBehavior` has three settings:\n\n| Setting | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Automatic** (default) | use Direct Lake when possible, **fall back to DirectQuery** when not |\n| DirectLakeOnly | Direct Lake only; any query that cannot use it **fails** |\n| DirectQueryOnly | always DirectQuery |\n\nThe trace shows *both* modes in use — one visual served by Direct Lake, another falling back to DirectQuery because it reads a **view** (views always fall back; Direct Lake reads Delta tables). Mixed behaviour in one report is only possible under **Automatic**: `DirectLakeOnly` would have errored, `DirectQueryOnly` would have used DirectQuery throughout.\n\n**Blank 2 — Direct Lake.** The table visual is bound to the Delta **table**, so its columns are paged straight from OneLake with no SQL query — the Performance analyzer row shows a **Direct query** duration of 0 for it, which is the signature of Direct Lake. &quot;The composite model&quot; is not a query mode at all.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Analyze query processing for Direct Lake semantic models",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-analyze-query-processing?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Direct Lake overview — fallback behavior",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p146_i1.png, p146_i2.png"
