  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q55",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q55",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a new semantic model in OneLake.\nYou use a Fabric notebook to read the data into a Spark DataFrame.\nYou need to evaluate the data to calculate the min, max, mean, and standard deviation values for all the string and numeric columns.\nSolution: You use the following PySpark expression:\ndf.show()\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No.** `df.show()` simply **prints rows** — the first 20 by default — as a formatted ASCII table. It is the PySpark equivalent of eyeballing the data. It performs no aggregation, so it produces none of the requested min, max, mean or standard deviation values.\n\nUse **`df.summary()`** (or `df.describe()`) instead; those return the statistics as a DataFrame, which you then display with `.show()` — e.g. `df.summary().show()`. The `.show()` on its own is only the rendering half of that.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Notebook visualization in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/notebook-visualization?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#display-summary-view"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q55"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q56",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q56",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a new semantic model in OneLake.\nYou use a Fabric notebook to read the data into a Spark DataFrame.\nYou need to evaluate the data to calculate the min, max, mean, and standard deviation values for all the string and numeric columns.\nSolution: You use the following PySpark expression:\ndf.summary()\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Yes.** `df.summary()` *&quot;computes specified statistics for numeric and string columns&quot;*, and with no arguments its default set is exactly:\n\n**count, mean, stddev, min, 25%, 50%, 75%, max**\n\nThat contains all four requested values — min, max, mean and standard deviation — and the doc&#x27;s own wording covers the *&quot;all the string and numeric columns&quot;* part of the requirement.\n\n```\n+-------+------------------+------------------+\n|summary|          fruit_id|            amount|\n+-------+------------------+------------------+\n|  count|                 3|                 3|\n|   mean|1.6666666666666667|11.666666666666666|\n| stddev|0.5773502691896257| 2.886751345948129|\n|    min|                 1|                10|\n|    max|                 2|                15|\n+-------+------------------+------------------+\n```\n\n*Note:* the vote is a narrow 54/46. The &quot;No&quot; camp argues that mean and stddev are meaningless for string columns — true, but the function still *runs over* string columns (returning count/min/max for them), which is what the documented description claims and what the question asks. `summary()` is the broader of the two options here; `describe()` returns only count/mean/stddev/min/max.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "DataFrame.Summary(String[]) Method",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/microsoft.spark.sql.dataframe.summary?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
