  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q57",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q57",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a Delta table named Customer.\nWhen you query Customer, you discover that the query is slow to execute. You suspect that maintenance was NOT performed on the table.\nYou need to identify whether maintenance tasks were performed on Customer.\nSolution: You run the following Spark SQL statement:\nDESCRIBE HISTORY customer -\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Yes.** `DESCRIBE HISTORY` returns the Delta table&#x27;s **transaction log as a table** — one row per commit, with `version`, `timestamp`, `userName`, and crucially **`operation`** and `operationParameters`.\n\nMaintenance commands are ordinary Delta commits, so they appear there by name: `OPTIMIZE`, `VACUUM START` / `VACUUM END`, alongside `WRITE`, `MERGE` and the rest. Reading the `operation` column tells you directly whether maintenance has been run and when — exactly the goal.\n\n```sql\nDESCRIBE HISTORY customer\n```\n\nThe default history retention is 30 days, so this answers &quot;was maintenance performed recently&quot;. Compare the other solutions in this set: `REFRESH TABLE` (T1Q58) only reloads cached metadata, and `EXPLAIN TABLE` (T1Q59) is not even valid syntax.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Delta Lake table history",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/databricks/delta/history?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q57"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q58",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q58",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a Delta table named Customer.\nWhen you query Customer, you discover that the query is slow to execute. You suspect that maintenance was NOT performed on the table.\nYou need to identify whether maintenance tasks were performed on Customer.\nSolution: You run the following Spark SQL statement:\nREFRESH TABLE customer -\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No.** `REFRESH TABLE` invalidates and reloads Spark&#x27;s **cached metadata and cached data** for a table, so that subsequent queries see the current files. It is what you run after something outside your session has changed the table.\n\nIt returns **no result set** — nothing about past operations, nothing about `OPTIMIZE` or `VACUUM`. The command that exposes maintenance history is `DESCRIBE HISTORY` (T1Q57).</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Inspect Delta table metadata",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/delta-lake-describe?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#use-describe-history"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
