  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q68",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q68",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou need to configure OneLake security for users shown in the following table.\nThe solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich permission should you assign to each user? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "User1 — \"Read all the Spark data.\"",
        "options": [
          "Read",
          "ReadAll",
          "ReadData"
        ],
        "answer": "ReadAll"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "User2 — \"Read all the SQL endpoint data.\"",
        "options": [
          "Read",
          "ReadAll",
          "ReadData"
        ],
        "answer": "ReadData"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**ReadAll — ReadData.** These are the three lakehouse sharing permissions, and the doc maps each to an access path:\n\n| Permission | Grants |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Read** | open the lakehouse item — metadata only, no data |\n| **ReadAll** | *&quot;read all data using **Apache Spark** and OneLake APIs&quot;* |\n| **ReadData** | *&quot;read data from the **SQL analytics endpoint** through T-SQL&quot;*, subject to any SQL security policy |\n\n- **User1** needs *&quot;Read all the Spark data&quot;* → **ReadAll**.\n- **User2** needs *&quot;Read all the SQL endpoint data&quot;* → **ReadData**.\n\nPlain **Read** is insufficient for either: it lets you see the item but returns no data. And the two are not interchangeable — ReadData does not grant OneLake file access, and ReadAll is broader than the SQL endpoint, so swapping them would break least privilege as well as the requirement.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lakehouse sharing and permission management",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sharing?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p196_i1.png, p196_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q68"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p196_i1.png",
      "p196_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p196_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q69",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q69",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have an Azure Repos repository named Repo1 and a Fabric-enabled Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity. The capacity contains two\nworkspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2. Git integration is enabled at the workspace level.\nYou plan to use Microsoft Power BI Desktop and Workspace1 to make version-controlled changes to a semantic model stored in Repo1. The\nchanges will be built and deployed to Workspace2 by using Azure Pipelines.\nYou need to ensure that report and semantic model definitions are saved as individual text files in a folder hierarchy. The solution must minimize\ndevelopment and maintenance effort.\nIn which file format should you save the changes?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "PBIP"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "PBIDS"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "PBIT"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "PBIX"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**PBIP.** The requirement — *&quot;report and semantic model definitions … saved as individual text files in a folder hierarchy&quot;* — is a verbatim description of the **Power BI Project** format. Saving as `.pbip` produces:\n\n```\nMyProject.pbip\nMyProject.Report/        definition/ …            (report pages, visuals)\nMyProject.SemanticModel/ definition/tables/*.tmdl (model metadata as TMDL text)\n```\n\nEvery artefact is a separate text file, which is what makes meaningful diffs, merges and Azure Pipelines builds possible — and it keeps development effort low because Desktop writes the structure for you.\n\n- **D PBIX** – one binary archive. Git can store it, but every change is an opaque whole-file blob: no diffs, no merges.\n- **C PBIT** – a binary template (PBIX minus data). Same problem.\n- **B PBIDS** – a small connection-definition file that opens a new report against a data source. It contains no report or model at all.\n\n*(Same fact tested from a different angle in T1Q22.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Power BI Desktop projects (PBIP)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q69"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q70",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q70",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are implementing a medallion architecture in a single Fabric workspace.\nYou have a lakehouse that contains the Bronze and Silver layers and a warehouse that contains the Gold layer.\nYou create the items required to populate the layers as shown in the following table.\nYou need to ensure that the layers are populated daily in sequential order such that Silver is populated only after Bronze is complete, and Gold is\npopulated only after Silver is complete. The solution must minimize development effort and complexity.\nWhat should you use to execute each set of items? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct items. Each option may be used once,\nmore than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Orchestration pipeline:",
        "options": [
          "A pipeline Copy activity",
          "A pipeline Dataflow activity",
          "A pipeline Stored procedure activity",
          "A schedule",
          "A Spark job definition",
          "An Invoke pipeline activity"
        ],
        "answer": "A schedule"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Bronze layer:  (items are \"Pipelines with Copy activities\")",
        "options": [
          "A pipeline Copy activity",
          "A pipeline Dataflow activity",
          "A pipeline Stored procedure activity",
          "A schedule",
          "A Spark job definition",
          "An Invoke pipeline activity"
        ],
        "answer": "An Invoke pipeline activity"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Silver layer:  (items are \"Dataflows\")",
        "options": [
          "A pipeline Copy activity",
          "A pipeline Dataflow activity",
          "A pipeline Stored procedure activity",
          "A schedule",
          "A Spark job definition",
          "An Invoke pipeline activity"
        ],
        "answer": "A pipeline Dataflow activity"
      },
      {
        "id": "3",
        "label": "Gold layer:  (items are \"Stored procedures\")",
        "options": [
          "A pipeline Copy activity",
          "A pipeline Dataflow activity",
          "A pipeline Stored procedure activity",
          "A schedule",
          "A Spark job definition",
          "An Invoke pipeline activity"
        ],
        "answer": "A pipeline Stored procedure activity"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**A schedule — An Invoke pipeline activity — A pipeline Dataflow activity — A pipeline Stored procedure activity.**\n\nThe question asks what should **execute each set of items that already exists**, so match each layer to the activity that runs *that kind of item* from the orchestration pipeline:\n\n| Layer | Items you already built | Activity that executes them |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Bronze | **Pipelines** with Copy activities | **Invoke pipeline activity** |\n| Silver | Dataflows | Dataflow activity |\n| Gold | Stored procedures | Stored procedure activity |\n\nChaining the three with `On success` arrows gives the required Bronze → Silver → Gold ordering, and a **schedule** on the orchestration pipeline makes it run daily. (A schedule is the *trigger* for the outer pipeline; it is not an activity you place inside one.)\n\n**The PDF answer-key image is wrong on the Bronze layer** — it marks *A pipeline Copy activity*. The table states that the Bronze items **are already &quot;Pipelines with Copy activities&quot;**. Putting a bare Copy activity in the orchestration pipeline would mean *re-implementing* those copies by hand, leaving the pipelines you built unused — which directly contradicts *&quot;minimize development effort and complexity&quot;*. The **Invoke pipeline activity** exists precisely to call an existing pipeline from another one.\n\nThe community is emphatic: the three top comments (+30, +14, +8) all correct the key the same way.\n\n- **A Spark job definition** is a distractor — no layer here uses Spark.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Invoke pipeline activity",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/invoke-pipeline-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Activity overview — control flow",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/activity-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Implement medallion lakehouse architecture in Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-medallion-lakehouse-architecture?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p200_i1.png, p200_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
