    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**withColumn — col — cast.**\n\n```python\ndf.withColumn(&quot;age&quot;, col(&quot;age&quot;).cast(&quot;int&quot;)).show()\n```\n\n- **`withColumn`** is the method that satisfies *&quot;return a DataFrame that includes **all** the columns&quot;*. It *&quot;returns a new DataFrame by adding a column or **replacing the existing column that has the same name**&quot;* — so passing the existing name `&quot;age&quot;` swaps that one column and leaves the other three untouched.\n- **`col(&quot;age&quot;)`** builds the `Column` reference that the new value is computed from.\n- **`.cast(&quot;int&quot;)`** converts it from string to integer.\n\nDistractors:\n- **`select`** / **`selectExpr`** would return **only** the columns you list, dropping the other three — this is the trap the &quot;all the columns&quot; requirement is guarding.\n- **`transform`** applies a function to the whole DataFrame; wrong shape for this call.\n- **`get`** is not a DataFrame or Column method for this purpose.\n\n*(`df.age.cast(&quot;int&quot;)` and `col(&quot;age&quot;).cast(IntegerType())` are equivalent alternatives, but only `col` is on offer here.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "PySpark basics",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/databricks/pyspark/basics?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#cast-column-types"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p202_i1.png"
