  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q72",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q72",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account named storage1 that contains a Parquet file named sales.parquet.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1.\nUsing a notebook in Workspace1, you need to load the content of the file to the default lakehouse. The solution must ensure that the content will\ndisplay automatically as a table named Sales in Lakehouse explorer.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "df.write.mode(\"overwrite\").format(\"  ______  \")",
        "options": [
          "csv",
          "delta",
          "parquet"
        ],
        "answer": "delta"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": ".saveAsTable(\"  ______  \")",
        "options": [
          "sales",
          "files/sales",
          "tables/sales"
        ],
        "answer": "sales"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**delta — sales.**\n\n```python\ndf = spark.read.parquet(&quot;abfss://fs1@storage1.dfs.core.windows.net/files/sales.parquet&quot;)\ndf.write.mode(&quot;overwrite&quot;).format(&quot;delta&quot;).saveAsTable(&quot;sales&quot;)\n```\n\n**Format — `delta`.** The requirement is that the content *&quot;display automatically as a **table** named Sales in Lakehouse explorer&quot;*. Lakehouse managed tables are Delta tables; writing the source `parquet` through unchanged (or `csv`) would leave files, not a table.\n\n**Name — `sales`.** This is the key distinction from T1Q65: **`saveAsTable()` takes a table *name*, not a path.** The lakehouse resolves it to the `Tables/` area for you. Passing `tables/sales` or `files/sales` would be interpreted as part of the identifier and is wrong — path-style arguments belong to `.save()`.\n\n| Method | Argument |\n| --- | --- |\n| `.saveAsTable(name)` | `&quot;sales&quot;` |\n| `.save(path)` | `&quot;Tables/sales&quot;` |\n\n*Note:* a handful of comments answer *tables/sales*, conflating the two APIs; the highest-voted (+46) and the answer key both give the bare name.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Load data into a lakehouse with a notebook",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-notebook-load-data?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p204_i1.png"
