  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q73",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q73",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nIn Workspace1, you create a data pipeline named Pipeline1.\nYou have CSV files stored in an Azure Storage account.\nYou need to add an activity to Pipeline1 that will copy data from the CSV files to Lakehouse1. The activity must support Power Query M formula\nlanguage expressions.\nWhich type of activity should you add?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Dataflow"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Notebook"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Script"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Copy data"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The deciding constraint is *&quot;must support **Power Query M formula language** expressions&quot;*. Power Query M is the language of **Dataflow Gen2**, and a pipeline can run one through the **Dataflow activity**.\n\n- **D Copy data** – the obvious choice for &quot;copy CSV files to a lakehouse&quot;, and it is the answer to many similar questions. But Copy has no transformation language at all: it maps columns and moves bytes. No M.\n- **B Notebook** – Python/Scala/SQL/R. Not M.\n- **C Script** – runs a SQL script against a database. Not M, and it cannot read CSV files from storage.\n\nSo the M requirement, not the copy requirement, is what the question is testing.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Dataflow Gen2 activity in a pipeline",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/tutorial-dataflows-gen2-pipeline-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q73"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q74",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q74",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a Delta table with eight columns.\nYou receive new data that contains the same eight columns and two additional columns.\nYou create a Spark DataFrame and assign the DataFrame to a variable named df. The DataFrame contains the new data.\nYou need to add the new data to the Delta table to meet the following requirements:\n• Keep all the existing rows.\n• Ensure that all the new data is added to the table.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "df.write.format(\"delta\").mode  ______",
        "options": [
          "(\"append\")",
          "(\"error\")",
          "(\"errorifexists\")",
          "(\"ignore\")",
          "(\"overwrite\")"
        ],
        "answer": "(\"append\")"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": ".option  ______",
        "options": [
          "(\"mergeSchema\",\"false\")",
          "(\"mergeSchema\",\"true\")",
          "(\"overwriteSchema\",\"false\")",
          "(\"overwriteSchema\",\"true\")"
        ],
        "answer": "(\"mergeSchema\",\"true\")"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`(&quot;append&quot;)` — `(&quot;mergeSchema&quot;,&quot;true&quot;)`.** Each requirement maps to one blank.\n\n```python\ndf.write.format(&quot;delta&quot;).mode(&quot;append&quot;) \\\n  .option(&quot;mergeSchema&quot;, &quot;true&quot;) \\\n  .saveAsTable(&quot;table&quot;)\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — *&quot;Keep all the existing rows&quot;* → `append`.** `overwrite` would replace them. `error` / `errorifexists` throw if the table exists; `ignore` silently does nothing — so with those, the new data would never land.\n\n**Blank 2 — *&quot;Ensure that all the new data is added&quot;* → `mergeSchema = true`.** The incoming DataFrame has **two columns the table does not have**. By default Delta enforces the schema and the write fails. Schema evolution fixes it: *&quot;Columns that are present in the DataFrame but missing from the table are automatically added as part of a write transaction when `write` … [has] `.option(&quot;mergeSchema&quot;, &quot;true&quot;)`.&quot;* The two new columns are added and existing rows get `NULL` for them.\n- `mergeSchema = false` is the default and would error.\n- `overwriteSchema` is for *replacing* a schema — changing a column&#x27;s type or name, or dropping a column — and it only applies with `overwrite` mode, which would destroy the existing rows.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Update Delta Lake table schema — automatic schema evolution",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/databricks/delta/update-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p208_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q74"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
