    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q75",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q75",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Sales.Orders. Sales.Orders contains the following columns.\nYou need to write a T-SQL query that will return the following columns.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\n\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "______  (Weight, Quantity, 1) AS ChargedQuantity,",
        "options": [
          "CHOOSE",
          "COALESCE",
          "IIF"
        ],
        "answer": "COALESCE"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "______  (ListPrice, SalePrice) AS OrderPrice",
        "options": [
          "COALESCE",
          "LEAST",
          "MIN"
        ],
        "answer": "LEAST"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**COALESCE — LEAST.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT OrderID, CustomerID, ProductID,\n  COALESCE(Weight, Quantity, 1) AS ChargedQuantity,\n  LEAST(ListPrice, SalePrice)   AS OrderPrice\nFROM Sales.Orders;\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `COALESCE`.** `Weight` and `Quantity` are both nullable (see the schema), and the requirement is a fallback chain: use the weight, else the quantity, else 1. `COALESCE` *&quot;evaluates the arguments in order and returns the current value of the first expression that initially doesn&#x27;t evaluate to NULL&quot;* — and it takes any number of arguments, which is what makes the three-item list work.\n- `IIF` takes a boolean and exactly two results; it cannot chain three fallbacks.\n- `CHOOSE(index, …)` picks by ordinal position, not by nullability.\n\n**Blank 2 — `LEAST`.** You want the smaller of two values **on the same row**. `LEAST` *&quot;returns the minimum value from a list of one or more expressions&quot;*, row by row.\n- `MIN` is an **aggregate**: it collapses rows into groups and would require a `GROUP BY`. This is the trap.\n- `COALESCE` returns the first non-null, which is not the same as the smaller one (`ListPrice` is `NOT NULL`, so it would always win).</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "COALESCE (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/coalesce-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "LEAST (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/logical-functions-least-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p210_i1.png, p210_i2.png, p210_i3.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
