  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q76",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q76",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse.\nYou plan to use a visual query to merge two tables.\nYou need to ensure that the query returns all the rows in both tables.\nWhich type of join should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "inner"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "full outer"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "left outer"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "right anti"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "right outer"
      },
      {
        "id": "F",
        "text": "left anti"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Full outer** is the only join kind that returns *&quot;all the rows in both tables&quot;* — matched rows once, plus unmatched rows from the left with nulls on the right, plus unmatched rows from the right with nulls on the left.\n\nThe visual query merge dialog offers the same set Power Query does:\n\n| Join kind | Returns |\n| --- | --- |\n| Inner | only rows that match in both |\n| Left outer | all from the left, matches from the right |\n| Right outer | all from the right, matches from the left |\n| **Full outer** | **all rows from both** |\n| Left anti | only left rows with no match |\n| Right anti | only right rows with no match |</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Merge queries overview — join kinds",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/merge-queries-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q76"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q77",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q77",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a Delta table that has one million Parquet files.\nYou need to remove files that were NOT referenced by the table during the past 30 days. The solution must ensure that the transaction log remains\nconsistent, and the ACID properties of the table are maintained.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From OneLake file explorer, delete the files."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Run the OPTIMIZE command and specify the Z-order parameter."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Run the OPTIMIZE command and specify the V-order parameter."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Run the VACUUM command."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`VACUUM`** is the Delta command for removing data files that are no longer referenced by the table and are older than the retention threshold. It is transaction-log aware: it reads the log to determine exactly which files are still reachable (including by time travel), so the log stays consistent and ACID guarantees hold.\n\n```sql\nVACUUM customer RETAIN 720 HOURS   -- 30 days\n```\n\n- **A — delete the files from OneLake file explorer.** This is the trap, and it is destructive. Deleting Parquet files behind Delta&#x27;s back leaves the transaction log referencing files that no longer exist, which corrupts the table and breaks ACID — precisely what the question forbids.\n- **B — `OPTIMIZE … ZORDER`.** Compacts small files and co-locates data by column value to improve data skipping. It *creates* new files and leaves the old ones behind; it deletes nothing.\n- **C — `OPTIMIZE … V-ORDER`.** Same story with a different write-time layout optimisation.\n\n`OPTIMIZE` and `VACUUM` are the standard pairing: optimize to compact, then vacuum to clean up what compaction orphaned.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Table maintenance in the Fabric lakehouse",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-table-maintenance?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q77"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q78",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q78",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are implementing two dimension tables named Customers and Products in a Fabric warehouse.\nYou need to use slowly changing dimension (SCD) to manage the versioning of data. The solution must meet the requirements shown in the\nfollowing table.\nWhich type of SCD should you use for each table? To answer, drag the appropriate SCD types to the correct tables. Each SCD type may be used\nonce, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Customers — \"Create a new version of the row.\"",
        "options": [
          "Type 0",
          "Type 1",
          "Type 2",
          "Type 3"
        ],
        "answer": "Type 2"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Products — \"Overwrite the existing value in the latest row.\"",
        "options": [
          "Type 0",
          "Type 1",
          "Type 2",
          "Type 3"
        ],
        "answer": "Type 1"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Customers → Type 2, Products → Type 1.** The change action named in the requirements table *is* the definition of the SCD type:\n\n| Type | Behaviour on change |\n| --- | --- |\n| Type 0 | ignore the change — the attribute is fixed forever |\n| **Type 1** | **overwrite the existing value; no history kept** |\n| **Type 2** | **insert a new row (a new version), and close off the old one** |\n| Type 3 | keep a &quot;previous value&quot; column alongside the current one — one step of history, not a new row |\n\n- **Customers — &quot;Create a new version of the row&quot;** → **Type 2**. New rows, typically with a surrogate key plus effective start/end dates or an *is-current* flag, so the full history is queryable.\n- **Products — &quot;Overwrite the existing value in the latest row&quot;** → **Type 1**. *&quot;A Type 1 SCD always reflects the latest values, and when changes in source data are detected, the dimension table data is overwritten.&quot;*\n\nType 0 and Type 3 are distractors: neither matches a stated action.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Understand star schema — slowly changing dimensions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#slowly-changing-dimensions"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p214_i1.png, p214_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q78"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p214_i1.png",
      "p214_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p214_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q79",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q79",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 and an Azure SQL database.\nYou plan to create a dataflow that will read data from the database, and then transform the data by performing an inner join.\nYou need to ignore spaces in the values when performing the inner join. The solution must minimize development effort.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Append the queries by using fuzzy matching."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Merge the queries by using fuzzy matching."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Append the queries by using a lookup table."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Merge the queries by using a lookup table."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Two independent decisions:\n\n**Merge, not Append.** *&quot;Merge queries … creates a new query from two queries in a **join**&quot;*; *&quot;Append queries … creates a new query that contains all rows from a first query **followed by** all rows from a second query&quot;* — a union. The requirement says **inner join**, so it is Merge.\n\n**Fuzzy matching, not a lookup table.** The join keys differ only by spaces, so exact matching would drop rows. Power Query&#x27;s **fuzzy matching** option on the merge dialog *&quot;relies on … similarity&quot;* rather than exact equality and is *&quot;applied at the time of the merge operation&quot;*. Its **Ignore space** transformation option does exactly what is asked, and it is a checkbox — which is what satisfies *&quot;minimize development effort&quot;*.\n\nA lookup/transformation table is a manual mapping you have to build and maintain for every variation — far more effort. (It *is* an option within the fuzzy merge dialog, but it is not needed when a built-in option covers the case.)</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Fuzzy merge in Power Query",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/merge-queries-fuzzy-match?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Merge queries overview",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/merge-queries-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q79"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q80",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q80",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains two schemas name schema1 and schema2 and a\ntable named schema1.city.\nYou need to make a copy of schema1.city in schema2. The solution must minimize the copying of data.\nWhich T-SQL statement should you run?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "INSERT INTO schema2.city SELECT * FROM schema1.city;"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "SELECT * INTO schema2.city FROM schema1.city;"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "CREATE TABLE schema2.city AS CLONE OF schema1.city;"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "CREATE TABLE schema2.city AS SELECT * FROM schema1.city;"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Zero-copy clone.** `CREATE TABLE … AS CLONE OF` creates a new table that **points at the existing Parquet files** rather than duplicating them. The doc: *&quot;Microsoft Fabric offers the capability to create near-instantaneous **zero-copy clones** with minimal storage costs.&quot;* Only the metadata is written, so &quot;minimize the copying of data&quot; is satisfied absolutely — no data is copied at all.\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE schema2.city AS CLONE OF schema1.city;\n```\n\nCloning across **schemas within the same warehouse** is supported, which is exactly this scenario.\n\n- **D `CREATE TABLE … AS SELECT` (CTAS)** – the runner-up at 33% of the vote. It produces an independent physical copy: every row is read and rewritten. Correct result, maximum data movement.\n- **A `INSERT INTO … SELECT`** – also a full copy, and it requires `schema2.city` to exist first.\n- **B `SELECT * INTO`** – full copy again, and `SELECT … INTO` is not supported in Fabric Warehouse.\n\nThe clone is independent after creation: changes to either table do not affect the other, because Delta files are immutable and each table gets its own log going forward.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Clone table in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/clone-table?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CREATE TABLE AS CLONE OF (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-table-as-clone-of-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
