  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q81",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q81",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nYou need to prevent new tables added to Lakehouse1 from being added automatically to the default semantic model of the lakehouse.\nWhat should you configure?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "the SQL analytics endpoint settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "the semantic model settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "the workspace settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the Lakehouse1 settings"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The auto-add behaviour belongs to the **SQL analytics endpoint**, not to the lakehouse item and not to the semantic model. The setting is *&quot;Sync the default Power BI semantic model&quot;*, and the doc says where it lives: *&quot;To change this setting, open the settings of either the **SQL analytics endpoint** object of a lakehouse or the warehouse object.&quot;* Turning it off stops new tables and views being pushed into the default semantic model.\n\n- **B the semantic model settings** – the default semantic model is generated *for* you; it has no switch governing what the endpoint syncs into it. (40% of the community picks this.)\n- **D the Lakehouse1 settings** – covers the lakehouse item itself, not the endpoint.\n- **C the workspace settings** – no such per-workspace toggle.\n\nIn the UI: open the lakehouse, switch the view selector to **SQL analytics endpoint**, then **Settings → Default Power BI semantic model** and clear the sync option.\n\n*Note:* one commenter observes that Microsoft later changed the **default** so tables are *not* auto-added. That changes the default, not where the setting lives — **A** stands.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Default Power BI semantic models in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/semantic-models?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q81"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q82",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q82",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1 and a warehouse\nnamed Warehouse1.\nYou need to create a new table in Warehouse1 named POSCustomers by querying the customer table in Lakehouse1.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Line 1",
        "options": [
          "CREATE TABLE dbo.POSCustomers",
          "CREATE TABLE dbo.POSCustomers AS CLONE OF",
          "CREATE TABLE dbo.POSCustomers AS SELECT"
        ],
        "answer": "CREATE TABLE dbo.POSCustomers AS SELECT"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Final line",
        "options": [
          "FROM dbo.Customer",
          "FROM dbo.POSCustomers",
          "FROM lakehouse1.dbo.customer"
        ],
        "answer": "FROM lakehouse1.dbo.customer"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`CREATE TABLE dbo.POSCustomers AS SELECT` — `FROM lakehouse1.dbo.customer`.**\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE dbo.POSCustomers AS SELECT\n  DISTINCT customerid,\n           customer,\n           postalcode,\n           category\nFROM lakehouse1.dbo.customer;\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — CTAS.** The body is a `SELECT DISTINCT …` list, so the statement has to be **`CREATE TABLE … AS SELECT`**.\n- Plain `CREATE TABLE dbo.POSCustomers` expects a column definition list in parentheses, not a `SELECT`.\n- **`AS CLONE OF` cannot be used here** for two reasons: cloning takes a table reference, not a `SELECT DISTINCT` projection; and the doc states *&quot;Table clones **across warehouses** in a workspace are not currently supported&quot;* — and a lakehouse is a different item again.\n\n**Blank 2 — three-part name across items.** Within one workspace, a warehouse can read a lakehouse&#x27;s SQL analytics endpoint by **cross-database query** using `item.schema.table`, hence `lakehouse1.dbo.customer`. `dbo.Customer` would look inside Warehouse1, where the table does not exist, and `dbo.POSCustomers` is the table being created.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Query the SQL analytics endpoint / cross-database queries",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/query-warehouse?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Clone table — limitations",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/clone-table?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p223_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q82"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p223_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p223_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q83",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q83",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating an Azure Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Switch"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Copy data"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Append variable"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Lookup"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Two requirements: execute a stored procedure, **and** make its result available downstream. Only **Lookup** does both. The doc: *&quot;Lookup activity reads and returns the content of a configuration file or table. **It also returns the result of executing a query or stored procedure.** The output can be a singleton value or an array of attributes, which can be consumed in a subsequent copy, transformation, or control flow activity.&quot;*\n\nDownstream activities then read it as `@activity(&#x27;Lookup1&#x27;).output.firstRow.&lt;column&gt;`.\n\n- **The Stored procedure activity** (not offered here) would execute the procedure but **discards the result set** — which is why Lookup is the answer when the values must flow onward.\n- **A Switch** – control flow branching on a value; it does not execute procedures.\n- **B Copy data** – can *run* a stored procedure as a source, but it writes the rows to a sink rather than exposing them to later activities.\n- **C Append variable** – adds a value to an existing array variable. It executes nothing.\n\n*Note:* a commenter who sat the exam reports a live variant whose options are Switch / Copy / Script / Stored procedure — a different option set that would need a different answer. With the options as printed here, Lookup is correct.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lookup activity in Data Factory for Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/lookup-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q83"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q84",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q84",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains two workspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named\nLakehouse1. Workspace2 contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse2. Lakehouse1 contains a table named dbo.Sales. Lakehouse2 contains a table\nnamed dbo.Customers.\nYou need to ensure that you can write queries that reference both dbo.Sales and dbo.Customers in the same SQL query without making additional\ncopies of the tables.\nWhat should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a shortcut"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a dataflow"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a view"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "a managed table"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The tables live in **two different workspaces**, and the requirement is a single SQL query over both *&quot;without making additional copies of the tables&quot;*. That is the definition of a **shortcut**: *&quot;Shortcuts are objects in OneLake that point to other storage locations … Shortcuts allow you to reuse data **without copying it**.&quot;*\n\nCreate a shortcut in Lakehouse1 pointing at Lakehouse2&#x27;s `dbo.Customers`; it then appears in Lakehouse1&#x27;s Tables area and its SQL analytics endpoint, so `dbo.Sales` and the shortcut can be joined in one query.\n\n- **C a view** – a view cannot reach across workspaces. Cross-database queries work **within** a workspace; to span workspaces you must create the shortcut *first* (and then a view over it, if you want one).\n- **B a dataflow** – would copy the data into the target. Explicitly excluded.\n- **D a managed table** – also a copy.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "OneLake shortcuts",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q84"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q85",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q85",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nYou are designing a star schema model that will contain a customer dimension. The customer dimension table will be a Type 2 slowly changing\ndimension (SCD).\nYou need to recommend which columns to add to the table. The columns must NOT already exist in the source.\nWhich three types of columns should you recommend? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a foreign key"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a natural key"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "an effective end date and time"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "a surrogate key"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "an effective start date and time"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "C",
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>A **Type 2** dimension keeps history by inserting a **new row** each time a tracked attribute changes. That forces three columns that do not exist in the source system:\n\n- **D — a surrogate key.** Once the same customer has several rows, the natural key is no longer unique, so it can no longer be the primary key. A model-generated surrogate key restores uniqueness and is what the fact table points at, so each fact row is tied to the version of the customer that was current at the time.\n- **E — an effective start date and time** and **C — an effective end date and time.** These bound the validity window of each version, so you can answer &quot;what did this customer look like on date X&quot;.\n\nRejected:\n- **B — a natural key.** It comes *from* the source (the business key, e.g. `CustomerID`). It must be carried into the dimension, but it is not something you *add* — and the question says the columns *&quot;must NOT already exist in the source&quot;*.\n- **A — a foreign key.** Foreign keys live in the **fact** table, pointing at the dimension. A dimension in a star schema does not carry one (that would be snowflaking).</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Understand star schema — slowly changing dimensions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#slowly-changing-dimensions"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q85"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q86",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q86",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou plan to create a data pipeline named Pipeline1. Pipeline1 will include two activities that will execute in sequence.\nYou need to ensure that a failure of the first activity will NOT block the second activity.\nWhich conditional path should you configure between the first activity and the second activity?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Upon Failure"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Upon Completion"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Upon Skip"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Upon Skip"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Pipeline activities are wired together with **conditional paths**, and each one is a different promise about when the next activity runs:\n\n| Path | Runs the next activity when the first… |\n| --- | --- |\n| On success | succeeded |\n| On fail | failed |\n| On skip | was skipped |\n| **On completion** | **finished — success *or* failure** |\n\nThe requirement is that *&quot;a failure of the first activity will NOT block the second activity&quot;* — the second must run either way. That is **Upon Completion**.\n\n- **A Upon Failure** inverts the problem: the second activity would run *only* if the first failed, and would be skipped when it succeeded.\n- **C / D Upon Skip** (listed twice — a typo in the question) fires only when the first activity was itself skipped, which is not the case here.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Activity overview — conditional paths",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/activity-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q86"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q87",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q87",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model.\nYou need to identify any surrogate key columns in the model that have the Summarize By property set to a value other than to None. The solution\nmust minimize effort.\nWhat should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "DAX Formatter in DAX Studio"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Model explorer in Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Model view in Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Best Practice Analyzer in Tabular Editor"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Best Practice Analyzer (BPA) in Tabular Editor** scans the whole model against a rule set and lists every object that violates a rule. Microsoft&#x27;s published BPA rules for Power BI include exactly this one — *&quot;Set `IsAvailableInMdx` to false / set Summarize By to None on non-attribute columns&quot;* — and BPA rules can match on naming patterns such as columns ending in `Key`. One click, a complete list. That is what *&quot;minimize effort&quot;* rewards.\n\n- **C Model view** – you would click each column in turn and read its Properties pane. Correct but manual across every table.\n- **B Model explorer** – a navigation tree over model objects. Still one column at a time, and no rule evaluation.\n- **A DAX Formatter in DAX Studio** – pretty-prints DAX expressions. It does not inspect column properties at all.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Best practice rules to improve your model's performance",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/power-bi-optimization?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q87"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q88",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q88",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1.\nReport1 is slow to render. You suspect that an inefficient DAX query is being executed.\nYou need to identify the slowest DAX query, and then review how long the query spends in the formula engine as compared to the storage engine.\nWhich five actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and\narrange them in the correct order.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Step 1",
        "options": [
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio.",
          "View the Server Timings tab."
        ],
        "answer": "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording."
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Step 2",
        "options": [
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio.",
          "View the Server Timings tab."
        ],
        "answer": "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order."
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Step 3",
        "options": [
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio.",
          "View the Server Timings tab."
        ],
        "answer": "Copy the first query to DAX Studio."
      },
      {
        "id": "3",
        "label": "Step 4",
        "options": [
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio.",
          "View the Server Timings tab."
        ],
        "answer": "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query."
      },
      {
        "id": "4",
        "label": "Step 5",
        "options": [
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio.",
          "View the Server Timings tab."
        ],
        "answer": "View the Server Timings tab."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The workflow crosses two tools: Performance analyzer **finds** the slow query, DAX Studio **explains** it.\n\n1. **From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.** *Optimize ribbon → Performance analyzer → Start recording*, then refresh the visuals. Each visual gets a row broken into DAX query / Visual display / Other.\n2. **Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order.** Puts the slowest work at the top.\n3. **Copy the first query to DAX Studio.** Performance analyzer offers *Copy query* on the DAX query line; paste it into DAX Studio.\n4. **Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.** These two toggles must be on **before** you run, because the timings are collected from the trace during execution.\n5. **View the Server Timings tab.** This is the only place that reports the **FE** (formula engine) versus **SE** (storage engine) split the question asks for, along with the SE query count and scanned rows.\n\n**Why not &quot;View the Query Timings tab&quot;?** Query Timings shows the total and the phases of the run, but the FE/SE breakdown lives in **Server Timings**.\n\n*Note:* this variant offers two sort options — the plain one and *&quot;…by DAX query time&quot;*. The answer-key image marks the plain one; a commenter who sat the live exam reports it offered only the *by DAX query time* wording. Both orderings are accepted here.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Performance analyzer in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-performance-analyzer?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Troubleshoot report performance in Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/report-performance-troubleshoot?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p234_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q88"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p234_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p234_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q89",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q89",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model. The model contains 15 tables.\nYou need to programmatically change each column that ends in the word Key to meet the following requirements:\n• Hide the column.\n• Set Nullable to False\n• Set Summarize By to None.\n• Set Available in MDX to False.\n• Mark the column as a key column.\nWhat should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "ALM Toolkit"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Tabular Editor"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DAX Studio"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The word doing the work is **programmatically**, across **15 tables**, driven by a naming convention. **Tabular Editor** has a **C# scripting** engine over the Tabular Object Model, so the whole job is a short loop:\n\n```csharp\nforeach (var c in Model.AllColumns.Where(c =&gt; c.Name.EndsWith(&quot;Key&quot;))) {\n    c.IsHidden        = true;\n    c.IsNullable     = false;\n    c.SummarizeBy     = AggregateFunction.None;\n    c.IsAvailableInMDX = false;\n    c.IsKey           = true;\n}\n```\n\nThose five lines are the five bullet points in the question, one for one.\n\n- **A Power BI Desktop** – every property is there in the Properties pane, but you would set them by hand, column by column, across 15 tables. Not programmatic.\n- **B ALM Toolkit** – *compares and deploys* differences between two models. It does not author property changes.\n- **D DAX Studio** – queries and profiles a model; it is read-only against model metadata.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "External tools in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-external-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q89"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q90",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q90",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model.\nYou plan to implement calculation groups.\nYou need to create a calculation item that will change the context from the selected date to month-to-date (MTD).\nHow should you complete the DAX expression? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "______  (",
        "options": [
          "CALCULATE",
          "GENERATE",
          "MEASURE"
        ],
        "answer": "CALCULATE"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "______  (),   DATESMTD ( 'Date'[Date] )",
        "options": [
          "COMBINEVALUES",
          "SELECTEDMEASURE",
          "SELECTEDVALUE"
        ],
        "answer": "SELECTEDMEASURE"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**CALCULATE — SELECTEDMEASURE.** The finished calculation item is the standard MTD pattern:\n\n```dax\nCALCULATE (\n    SELECTEDMEASURE (),\n    DATESMTD ( &#x27;Date&#x27;[Date] )\n)\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `CALCULATE`.** Changing the filter context is what `CALCULATE` does: it evaluates its first argument under the filters given in the remaining arguments. Here that filter is `DATESMTD`, which returns the month-to-date set of dates.\n- `GENERATE` produces a cross-join style table; `MEASURE` is a DAX-query keyword for defining a measure, not an expression.\n\n**Blank 2 — `SELECTEDMEASURE()`.** A calculation item must work with *whatever measure the user puts in the visual*. `SELECTEDMEASURE` is the placeholder for it: it *&quot;returns a reference to the measure that is currently in context when a calculation item is being evaluated&quot;*, and the engine substitutes the real measure at query time. Without it the item could only ever transform one hard-coded measure.\n- `SELECTEDVALUE` returns a scalar from a *column* when exactly one value is filtered — unrelated.\n- `COMBINEVALUES` concatenates values for relationship keys.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "SELECTEDMEASURE function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/selectedmeasure-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Calculation groups",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/analysis-services/tabular-models/calculation-groups?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "DATESMTD function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/datesmtd-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
