  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q94",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q94",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains measures. The measures use multiple CALCULATE functions and a FILTER function.\nYou are evaluating the performance of the measures.\nIn which use case will replacing the FILTER function with the KEEPFILTERS function reduce execution time?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "when the FILTER function uses a nested calculate function"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "when the FILTER function references a measure"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "when the FILTER function references columns from multiple tables"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "when the FILTER function references a column from a single table that uses Import mode"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Microsoft&#x27;s DAX best-practice article *Avoid using FILTER as a filter argument* explains the optimisation. A filter argument to `CALCULATE` can be written either as a **table expression** (`FILTER(...)`) or as a **Boolean expression** — and the Boolean form is faster, because the engine can push it down as a simple predicate instead of materialising and iterating a table. `KEEPFILTERS` is how you keep the intersect-with-existing-context semantics while using the Boolean form.\n\nBut a Boolean filter expression carries documented **restrictions**. It:\n- cannot reference columns from **multiple tables**,\n- cannot reference a **measure**,\n- cannot use a nested `CALCULATE`.\n\nOptions **A**, **B** and **C** each name one of those restrictions, so in each of those cases you *cannot* make the swap — `FILTER` is required. **D** describes the one case where the swap is both legal and beneficial: a single-table column reference. The article also notes it *&quot;is especially relevant for model calculations that apply filters to **Import** tables&quot;*, which is the rest of D.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "DAX: Avoid using FILTER as a filter argument",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/best-practices/dax-avoid-avoid-filter-as-filter-argument?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q94"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q95",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q95",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains five tables that all use Import mode. Model1 contains a dynamic row-level security\n(RLS) role named HR. The HR role filters employee data so that HR managers only see the data of the department to which they are assigned.\nYou publish Model1 to a Fabric tenant and configure RLS role membership. You share the model and related reports to users.\nAn HR manager reports that the data they see in a report is incomplete.\nWhat should you do to validate the data seen by the HR Manager?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Select Test as role to view the data as the HR role."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Filter the data in the report to match the intended logic of the filter for the HR department."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Select Test as role to view the report as the HR manager."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Ask the HR manager to open the report in Microsoft Power BI Desktop."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The word that decides this is **dynamic**. A dynamic RLS role filters on the *current user&#x27;s identity* — typically `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` matched against an employee table. So the filter produces a **different result for every user**, and testing &quot;the HR role&quot; in the abstract tells you nothing about what this particular manager sees.\n\nIn the Fabric/Power BI service, **Security → Test as role** lets you select the role **and** enter a specific user to impersonate. Viewing the report **as the HR manager** reproduces their exact filter context, which is the only way to confirm whether the data is genuinely incomplete or the RLS logic is misfiring for them. That is **C**.\n\n- **A — Test as role to view the data as the HR role.** This is the right *tool* with the wrong *scope*. With **static** RLS it would be enough; with dynamic RLS there is no single &quot;HR role view&quot; to inspect, because the filter depends on who is asking. 41% of the community picks this, reasoning that the role is named &quot;HR&quot;, not &quot;HR manager&quot; — but the role name is not the issue, the identity is.\n- **B — filter the report manually.** Reproduces what you *think* the logic should be, not what RLS actually does. It cannot validate anything.\n- **D — have the manager open it in Desktop.** Desktop&#x27;s *View as* does not apply the service&#x27;s role membership, and the manager may not even have the model.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-admin-rls?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Row-level security in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/security/service-admin-row-level-security?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q95"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q96",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q96",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Fabric tenant that contains a dataflow.\nYou are exploring a new semantic model.\nFrom Power Query, you need to view column information as shown in the following exhibit.\nWhich three Data view options should you select? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Show column value distribution"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Enable details pane"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Enable column profile"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Show column quality details"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Show column profile in details pane"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "C",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The exhibit shows, for each column: a **Valid / Error / Empty** bar with percentages, a **bar chart of the value distribution**, and a *&quot;n distinct, n unique&quot;* footer. Three separate Data-view checkboxes produce those three things:\n\n| Option | What it adds to the exhibit |\n| --- | --- |\n| **D — Show column quality details** | the **Valid / Error / Empty** percentages |\n| **A — Show column value distribution** | the **bar chart** plus the *distinct / unique* counts |\n| **C — Enable column profile** | the underlying profiling that feeds the *Column profile* pane |\n\n- **B — Enable details pane** is not one of the Data-view profiling options; nothing in the exhibit corresponds to it.\n\nAll three live on the **View** ribbon in Power Query, under *Data preview*. Note the counts are based on the **top 1,000 rows** unless you switch profiling to the entire data set (the same default that makes T1Q19 answerable).</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Data profiling tools in Power Query",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/data-profiling-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
