  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q97",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q97",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Sales.Products. Sales.Products contains the following columns.\nYou need to write a T-SQL query that will return the following columns.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "______ (ListPrice, WholesalePrice, AgentPrice) AS HighestSellingPrice,",
        "options": [
          "COALESCE",
          "GREATEST",
          "IIF",
          "MAX"
        ],
        "answer": "GREATEST"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "______ (ListPrice, WholesalePrice, AgentPrice) AS TradePrice",
        "options": [
          "CHOOSE",
          "COALESCE",
          "IIF",
          "MAX"
        ],
        "answer": "COALESCE"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**GREATEST — COALESCE.** Two requirements that sound similar but are not:\n\n| Output column | Requirement | Function |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `HighestSellingPrice` | *&quot;the **highest value** from ListPrice, WholesalePrice and AgentPrice&quot;* | **`GREATEST`** |\n| `TradePrice` | *&quot;AgentPrice **if present**, otherwise WholesalePrice if present, otherwise ListPrice&quot;* | **`COALESCE`** |\n\n**`GREATEST`** *&quot;returns the maximum value from a list of one or more expressions&quot;* — a **row-wise** comparison across columns.\n- **`MAX` is the trap.** It is an **aggregate**: it finds the maximum down a column across rows, and it needs a `GROUP BY`. It cannot compare three columns on one row.\n- `IIF` handles one condition and two outcomes; comparing three values would need nesting.\n\n**`COALESCE`** *&quot;evaluates the arguments in order and returns the current value of the first expression that initially doesn&#x27;t evaluate to NULL&quot;* — the &quot;if present, otherwise…&quot; chain, exactly. It works here because `WholesalePrice` and `AgentPrice` are nullable while `ListPrice` is `NOT NULL`, so the chain always terminates.\n- `CHOOSE(index, …)` selects by ordinal position, not by nullability.\n\n*(Note the printed argument order for `TradePrice` is `(ListPrice, WholesalePrice, AgentPrice)`, which is the reverse of the stated precedence — an error in the question. The arguments are pre-filled and not part of the blank; `COALESCE` is still the only function that expresses the requirement.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "GREATEST (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/logical-functions-greatest-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "COALESCE (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/coalesce-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p253_i1.png, p253_i2.png, p253_i3.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q97"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
