    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q99",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q99",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Sales.Orders. Sales.Orders contains the following columns.\nYou need to write a T-SQL query that will return the following columns.\nHow should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "______ (month, OrderDate) AS PeriodDate,",
        "options": [
          "DATE_BUCKET",
          "DATEFROMPARTS",
          "DATEPART",
          "DATETRUNC"
        ],
        "answer": "DATETRUNC"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "DATENAME(  ______ , OrderDate) AS DayName",
        "options": [
          "day",
          "dayofyear",
          "weekday"
        ],
        "answer": "weekday"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**DATETRUNC — weekday.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT OrderID, CustomerID,\n  DATETRUNC(month, OrderDate)   AS PeriodDate,\n  DATENAME(weekday, OrderDate)  AS DayName\nFROM Sales.Orders;\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `DATETRUNC`.** The spec is *&quot;a date representing the **first day of the month** for OrderDate&quot;*. `DATETRUNC` *&quot;returns an input date truncated to a specified datepart&quot;* — `DATETRUNC(month, &#x27;2024-03-17&#x27;)` gives `2024-03-01`, a **date**.\n- **`DATEPART`** returns an **integer** (`3`), not a date. This is the trap, and the distinction is what the question tests.\n- `DATEFROMPARTS(y, m, d)` builds a date from three separate integers; it does not take a date as input.\n- `DATE_BUCKET` snaps a date to fixed-width buckets from an origin — more machinery than needed, and not a month-start truncation.\n\n**Blank 2 — `weekday`.** The spec is *&quot;the **name of the day**, such as Wednesday&quot;*. `DATENAME` returns a character string for the requested part, and the part that yields the weekday name is **`weekday`** (`dw`).\n- `day` returns the day **of the month** — `DATENAME(day, …)` gives `&quot;17&quot;`.\n- `dayofyear` gives the ordinal day in the year — `&quot;77&quot;`.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "DATETRUNC (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/datetrunc-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "DATENAME (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/datename-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
