  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q103",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q103",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a dataflow named Dataflow1. Dataflow1 returns 500 rows of data.\nYou need to identify the min and max values for each column in the query results.\nWhich three Data view options should you select? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Show column value distribution"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Enable column profile"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Show column profile in details pane"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Show column quality details"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Enable details pane"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "B",
      "C",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Min and max** are **column statistics**, and those appear in the **details pane**, not on the column headers. Three checkboxes have to be on, and they stack:\n\n- **B — Enable column profile.** Turns on profiling. Without it there are no statistics to show.\n- **E — Enable details pane.** Opens the pane at the bottom of the Power Query window.\n- **C — Show column profile in details pane.** Routes the profile (Column statistics + Value distribution) *into* that pane, which is where **Min** and **Max** are listed.\n\nRejected:\n- **A Show column value distribution** – the little bar chart under the header. It shows the *shape* of the data, not min/max values.\n- **D Show column quality details** – Valid / Error / Empty percentages. Nothing about magnitude.\n\nNote the query returns **500 rows**, under the 1,000-row profiling default, so the statistics cover the whole result without switching to whole-dataset profiling.\n\n*(Contrast T1Q96, where the exhibit shows header-level quality bars and distribution charts, so the answer there is A, C and D.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Data profiling tools in Power Query",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/data-profiling-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q103"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
