  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q106",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q106",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a new semantic model in OneLake.\nYou use a Fabric notebook to read the data into a Spark DataFrame.\nYou need to evaluate the data to calculate the min, max, mean, and standard deviation values for all the string and numeric columns.\nSolution: You use the following PySpark expression:\ndf.explain().show()\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No** — twice over.\n\n1. **`explain()` computes no statistics.** It prints the query execution plan. Even on its own it answers none of min / max / mean / stddev.\n2. **`df.explain().show()` does not even run.** `explain()` **prints to stdout and returns `None`**, so chaining `.show()` onto it raises `AttributeError: &#x27;NoneType&#x27; object has no attribute &#x27;show&#x27;`.\n\nThe working expression is `df.summary().show()` — `summary()` returns a DataFrame of statistics, and `.show()` renders it. *(T1Q54 is the same solution without the `.show()`; both are No.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Notebook visualization",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/notebook-visualization?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#display-summary-view"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q106"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q107",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q107",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1.\nYou discover that the following query performs slowly against Model1.\nYou need to reduce the execution time of the query.\nSolution: You replace line 4 by using the following code:\nISEMPTY ( RELATEDTABLE ( &#x27;Order Item&#x27; ) )\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No — the logic is inverted.** The original query is\n\n```dax\nEVALUATE\n  FILTER (\n    VALUES ( Customer[Customer Name] ),\n    CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( &#x27;Order Item&#x27; ) ) &gt; 0   -- line 4\n  )\nORDER BY Customer[Customer Name]\n```\n\nLine 4 keeps customers that **have** at least one order. The proposed replacement is\n\n```dax\nISEMPTY ( RELATEDTABLE ( &#x27;Order Item&#x27; ) )\n```\n\nwhich is `TRUE` when the customer has **no** orders. It returns the exact complement of the intended set — customers *without* orders. Wrong result, so it cannot &quot;meet the goal&quot; no matter how fast it is.\n\nThe missing piece is the negation: **`NOT ISEMPTY ( ... )`** — which is what T1Q108 proposes, and why that one is **Yes**.\n\n*(`ISEMPTY` is genuinely the faster idiom: it stops at the first row instead of counting them all. The optimisation is right; the sign is wrong.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "ISEMPTY function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/isempty-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p272_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q107"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p272_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q108",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q108",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1.\nYou discover that the following query performs slowly against Model1.\nYou need to reduce the execution time of the query.\nSolution: You replace line 4 by using the following code:\nNOT ISEMPTY ( CALCULATETABLE ( &#x27;Order Item &#x27; ) )\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Yes.** Replacing line 4 with\n\n```dax\nNOT ISEMPTY ( CALCULATETABLE ( &#x27;Order Item&#x27; ) )\n```\n\nis both **correct** and **faster** than `CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( &#x27;Order Item&#x27; ) ) &gt; 0`.\n\n- **Correct.** `CALCULATETABLE(&#x27;Order Item&#x27;)` returns the order items in the current filter context (the customer being tested); `ISEMPTY` is `TRUE` when there are none, and `NOT` flips it — so the filter keeps customers that *have* orders. Same set as the original.\n- **Faster.** `COUNTROWS` must materialise and count **every** matching row before comparing to zero. `ISEMPTY` only has to establish whether **one** row exists, so the storage engine can stop at the first hit. For customers with many orders that is a large saving, and it avoids the counting work entirely for the rest.\n\n*Note:* one commenter votes No on the grounds that `NOT` needs parentheses. It does not — `NOT` is a DAX **unary operator**, so `NOT ISEMPTY(...)` is valid syntax (`NOT(...)` is also accepted). 86% of the community votes Yes.\n\n*(Compare T1Q107, the same idea without the `NOT` — which inverts the result and is therefore No.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "ISEMPTY function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/isempty-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CALCULATETABLE function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/calculatetable-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p274_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q108"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p274_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q109",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q109",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1.\nYou discover that the following query performs slowly against Model1.\nYou need to reduce the execution time of the query.\nSolution: You replace line 4 by using the following code:\nCALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( &#x27;Order Item&#x27; ) ) &gt;= 0\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No.** The proposed line 4 is\n\n```dax\nCALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( &#x27;Order Item&#x27; ) ) &gt;= 0\n```\n\nThe only change from the original is `&gt; 0` → **`&gt;= 0`**, and it breaks the query on both counts:\n\n- **Wrong result.** A row count can never be negative, so `&gt;= 0` is **always true** for any customer that has orders — and the filter now keeps customers it should have excluded. The query stops filtering anything meaningful.\n- **No faster.** `COUNTROWS` still runs for every customer, counting every row. The expensive part is untouched; only the comparison operator changed.\n\nBoth halves of &quot;meet the goal&quot; fail. The solution in this set that works is `NOT ISEMPTY ( CALCULATETABLE ( &#x27;Order Item&#x27; ) )` (T1Q108).</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "COUNTROWS function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/countrows-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
