    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Same scenario as T1Q4 and T1Q119, but the option set is different — and that changes which one survives.\n\nThe case study creates the shortcut **`ResearchProduct` in `Lakehouse1`**, and requires that the data be *&quot;presented as managed tables in Lakehouse explorer&quot;*. A shortcut in the **Tables** area is registered as a table, so Spark SQL can address it by name:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.sql(&quot;SELECT * FROM Lakehouse1.ResearchProduct&quot;)\n```\n\nThis is the exact form Microsoft shows for querying a shortcut: `spark.sql(&quot;SELECT * FROM MyLakehouse.MyShortcut&quot;)`.\n\n- **A** – `load(&quot;Files/ResearchProduct&quot;)` points at the **Files** area. The shortcut is under **Tables**, so the path is wrong. (In T1Q119, where the offered path is `Tables/ResearchProduct`, that option *is* the answer.)\n- **C** – `Lakehouse1.Tables.ResearchProduct` invents a three-part name. `Tables` is a physical area, not a schema you reference in SQL.\n- **D** – `external_table(...)` is not a Fabric notebook construct.\n\n*Note:* the vote is only 50% B, with 39% on A. The A camp argues the shortcut is &quot;just a folder&quot; and so must be read by path — but the case study explicitly requires managed-table presentation, which puts it under `Tables/`, and then A&#x27;s `Files/` path is wrong anyway.</pre>",
    "images": [],
