  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q112",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q112",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace that uses the default Spark starter pool and runtime version 1.2.\nYou plan to read a CSV file named Sales_raw.csv in a lakehouse, select columns, and save the data as a Delta table to the managed area of the\nlakehouse. Sales_raw.csv contains 12 columns.\nYou have the following code.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "The Spark engine will read only the 'SalesOrderNumber', 'OrderDate', 'CustomerName', 'UnitPrice' columns from Sales_raw.csv.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "The Year column replaces the OrderDate column in the table.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Adding inferSchema='true' to the options will increase the execution time of the query.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No — No — Yes.**\n\n**1. Reads only the four selected columns → No.** *(The PDF answer-key image marks **Yes** here; it is wrong.)* Column pruning at the storage layer is a property of **columnar** formats — Parquet and Delta keep each column in its own chunk, so unread columns are never fetched. **CSV is row-based**: every line must be read and scanned for delimiters before any field can be located. The `.select(...)` runs on the rows Spark has already read, so the full file is read either way.\n\nThis also contradicts the answer key of **T1Q45**, which poses the *identical* statement over the *identical* code and marks it **No** — the reading that matches documented behaviour. The community agrees: the two top comments here (+41, +9) both answer No; a third (+12) answers Yes.\n\n**2. The Year column replaces the OrderDate column → No.** `withColumn(&quot;Year&quot;, year(&quot;OrderDate&quot;))` *adds* a column. The doc: it *&quot;returns a new DataFrame by adding a column or replacing the existing column **that has the same name**&quot;* — the name here is `Year`, not `OrderDate`, so `OrderDate` survives untouched. (The table also ends up partitioned by `Year`, which does not remove `OrderDate` either.)\n\n**3. `inferSchema=&quot;true&quot;` increases execution time → Yes.** Inference costs an **extra pass over the file**: Spark reads it once to deduce column types, then again to load. Supplying an explicit schema skips that pass.\n\n*(Two OCR slips in the exhibit: `.red` is `.read`, `&quot;heade&quot;` is `&quot;header&quot;`.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Work with data in a Spark dataframe",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/training/modules/use-apache-spark-work-files-lakehouse/4-dataframe?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
