  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q113",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q113",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that\nmight meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a new semantic model in OneLake.\nYou use a Fabric notebook to read the data into a Spark DataFrame.\nYou need to evaluate the data to calculate the min, max, mean, and standard deviation values for all the string and numeric columns.\nSolution: You use the following PySpark expression:\ndf.describe().show()\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Yes.** `df.describe()` *&quot;computes basic statistics for numeric and string columns, including **count, mean, stddev, min, and max**. If no columns are given, this function computes statistics for all numerical or string columns.&quot;*\n\nThe requirement asks for **min, max, mean and standard deviation** across **string and numeric** columns — every one of those is in the list, and the no-argument form covers all columns. Wrapping it in `.show()` renders the resulting DataFrame, so the values are actually displayed.\n\n*Note:* 39% vote No, on the grounds that `summary()` is the broader function. That is true — `summary()` adds the quartiles and lets you pick statistics — but &quot;broader&quot; does not make `describe()` insufficient. Everything the question asks for is in `describe()`&#x27;s output, so the solution meets the goal. Both `df.summary()` (T1Q56) and `df.describe().show()` are Yes in this series.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "describe (PySpark DataFrame.describe) - Azure Databricks",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/databricks/pyspark/reference/classes/dataframe/describe?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q113"
    ],
