  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q114",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q114",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating a Fabric Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Switch"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "KQL"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Append variable"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Lookup"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Lookup.** The requirement has two halves — *execute the stored procedure* **and** *make the returned values available to downstream activities* — and Lookup is the activity documented to do both: *&quot;Lookup activity reads and returns the content of a configuration file or table. **It also returns the result of executing a query or stored procedure.** The output can be a singleton value or an array of attributes, which can be consumed in a subsequent copy, transformation, or control flow activity.&quot;*\n\nDownstream you read it as `@activity(&#x27;Lookup1&#x27;).output.firstRow.ActiveCustomers`.\n\n- **A Switch** – branches on a value; executes nothing.\n- **B KQL** – runs a Kusto query against a KQL database, not a stored procedure in a warehouse.\n- **C Append variable** – appends to an array variable; it has nothing to execute.\n\n*(Identical to T1Q83; only one distractor differs.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lookup activity in Data Factory for Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/lookup-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q114"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q115",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q115",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named model1. The two largest columns in model1 are shown in the following table.\nYou need to optimize model1. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Reduce the model size.\n• Increase refresh performance when using Import mode.\n• Ensure that the datetime value for each sales transaction is available in the model.\nWhat should you do on each column? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "TransactionKey:  (cardinality 2,500,350,000 — 160 GB — incrementing surrogate key for fact table SalesTransaction)",
        "options": [
          "Change the data type of the column.",
          "Remove the column.",
          "Split the column.",
          "Truncate the data."
        ],
        "answer": "Remove the column."
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "SaleDateTime:  (cardinality 6,850,273 — 120 GB — datetime to the nearest second of when a sale occurred)",
        "options": [
          "Change the data type of the column.",
          "Remove the column.",
          "Split the column.",
          "Truncate the data."
        ],
        "answer": "Split the column."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Remove the column — Split the column.** Both columns are enormous because VertiPaq compression collapses as **cardinality** rises, and each needs a different fix.\n\n**`TransactionKey` → Remove.** 2.5 billion distinct values, essentially one per row, costing **160 GB**. It is an *incrementing surrogate key* on a **fact** table — and a fact-table surrogate key has no analytical use: nobody slices, filters or aggregates by it, and no relationship needs it (relationships point *outward* to dimensions). Microsoft&#x27;s data-reduction guidance says to remove unnecessary columns, and *&quot;consider removing… a primary key column of a fact table&quot;*. Dropping it is the single biggest win and speeds up refresh because there is that much less to encode.\n\n**`SaleDateTime` → Split.** The third requirement — *&quot;ensure that the **datetime value** for each sales transaction is available in the model&quot;* — forbids removing or truncating it. Splitting into a **date** column and a **time** column preserves the full value while collapsing cardinality: 6,850,273 distinct datetimes become a few thousand dates × 86,400 possible times, both of which compress well. This is Microsoft&#x27;s documented technique: *&quot;split the column into separate date and time columns&quot;*.\n\nRejected:\n- **Truncate the data** on `SaleDateTime` would drop the time component, breaking the &quot;to the nearest second&quot; requirement.\n- **Change the data type** on either column is marginal — the cost is the *number of distinct values*, which a type change does not reduce.\n- **Remove** `SaleDateTime` violates the third requirement outright.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Data reduction techniques for Import modeling",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/import-modeling-data-reduction?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p293_i1.png, p293_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q115"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
