  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q116",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q116",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a data warehouse named DW1. DW1 contains a table named DimCustomer. DimCustomer contains the\nfields shown in the following table.\nYou need to identify duplicate email addresses in DimCustomer. The solution must return a maximum of 1,000 records.\nWhich four T-SQL statements should you run in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate statements from the list of statements to the answer\narea and arrange them in the correct order.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "1",
        "options": [
          "SELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey",
          "FROM DimCustomer",
          "SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "LIMIT 1000",
          "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1",
          "WHERE COUNT(*) > 1"
        ],
        "answer": "SELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "2",
        "options": [
          "SELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey",
          "FROM DimCustomer",
          "SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "LIMIT 1000",
          "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1",
          "WHERE COUNT(*) > 1"
        ],
        "answer": "FROM DimCustomer"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "3",
        "options": [
          "SELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey",
          "FROM DimCustomer",
          "SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "LIMIT 1000",
          "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1",
          "WHERE COUNT(*) > 1"
        ],
        "answer": "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey"
      },
      {
        "id": "3",
        "label": "4",
        "options": [
          "SELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey",
          "FROM DimCustomer",
          "SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "LIMIT 1000",
          "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1",
          "WHERE COUNT(*) > 1"
        ],
        "answer": "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The finished query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)\nFROM DimCustomer\nGROUP BY CustomerAltKey\nHAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;\n```\n\n`CustomerAltKey` is the email column (see the sample data). Group by it, then keep only the groups that occur more than once — those are the duplicates.\n\nThree distractors, each testing a specific rule:\n- **`LIMIT 1000`** – `LIMIT` is MySQL/PostgreSQL/Spark syntax. T-SQL uses **`TOP (n)`**, which is why the row cap is baked into the `SELECT` line rather than appended at the end.\n- **`SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)`** – correct projection but no row cap, so it fails *&quot;a maximum of 1,000 records&quot;*.\n- **`WHERE COUNT(*) &gt; 1`** – invalid. `WHERE` filters rows **before** aggregation and cannot contain an aggregate function; filtering groups is what **`HAVING`** is for.\n\nThe clause order is fixed by T-SQL syntax: `SELECT … FROM … GROUP BY … HAVING …`.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "SELECT — HAVING (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-having-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "TOP (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/top-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
