  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q117",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q117",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named WH1.\nYou run the following T-SQL query against WH1.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Dimension.GetDirectReports is a scalar T-SQL function.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "The Dimension.GetDirectReports function will run only once when the query runs.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "The output rows will include at least one row for each row in the Dimension.Employee table.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No — No — Yes.** The query is\n\n```sql\nSELECT e.[WWI Employee ID], e.Employee, e.[Preferred Name],\n       gdr.[WWI Employee ID] AS [Direct Report ID],\n       gdr.Employee            AS [Direct Report]\nFROM Dimension.Employee AS e\nOUTER APPLY Dimension.GetDirectReports(e.[Employee Key]) AS gdr;\n```\n\n**1. It is a scalar function → No.** *(The PDF answer-key image marks **Yes**; it is wrong.)* Two things in the query prove it is a **table-valued function**: it is given a **table alias** (`AS gdr`), and **individual columns are selected from that alias** (`gdr.[WWI Employee ID]`, `gdr.Employee`). A scalar function returns *one value* — it has no columns to alias and cannot appear as the right operand of `APPLY`. The community is clear on this too (+22, +10, +6 all answer No).\n\n**2. It will run only once → No.** `APPLY` is a **correlated** operator: the doc describes it as invoking the right-hand table expression *&quot;for each row of the left table expression&quot;*. Its argument here is `e.[Employee Key]`, which differs per row — so the function runs once per employee.\n\n**3. At least one output row per Employee row → Yes.** This is the difference between the two forms of APPLY:\n\n| Form | Left rows with no function output |\n| --- | --- |\n| `CROSS APPLY` | **dropped** (inner-join semantics) |\n| **`OUTER APPLY`** | **kept, with NULLs** (left-outer-join semantics) |\n\nBecause it is `OUTER APPLY`, an employee with no direct reports still produces one row, with NULL in the Direct Report columns. So every Employee row yields at least one output row.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "FROM clause plus JOIN, APPLY, PIVOT (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/from-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CREATE FUNCTION (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-function-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p297_i1.png, p297_i2.png"
    ],
