  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q120",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q120",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI Premium Per User (PPU) workspace that contains a semantic model.\nYou have an Azure App Service app named App1 that modifies row-level security (RLS) for the model by using the XMLA endpoint.\nApp1 requires users to sign in by using their Microsoft Entra credentials to access the XMLA endpoint.\nYou need to configure App1 to use a service account to access the model.\nWhat should you do first?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Add a managed identity to the workspace."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Modify the XMLA Endpoint setting."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Upgrade the workspace to Premium capacity."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Add a managed identity to App1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Upgrade the workspace to Premium capacity — the PDF answer (D) is wrong.**\n\nThe workspace is **Premium Per User (PPU)**, and PPU flatly forbids the pattern App1 needs. From the XMLA endpoint doc&#x27;s *Terms of use*, for a **multi-user application** (which App1 is — it serves users and modifies their RLS):\n\n&gt; **Premium Per User (PPU) workspaces** — The application must require each user to sign in to Power BI. For each user, the application uses an access token to access the semantic models. **The application can&#x27;t use a service account or other app identity to perform tasks on behalf of individual users.** Each user must have their own Power BI account…\n&gt;\n&gt; **For Premium workspaces**, the application can use either a service account or app identity on behalf of end users without requiring each user to sign in to Power BI.\n\nSo on PPU no amount of identity configuration will work: the blocker is the **licensing mode of the workspace**. Moving it to a Premium (or Fabric F) capacity is what makes a service account legal — and the question asks what to do **first**.\n\n- **D — add a managed identity to App1.** You will need an app identity eventually, and this is the natural next step *after* the upgrade. Done first, against a PPU workspace, it simply will not be allowed to connect on users&#x27; behalf.\n- **A — add a managed identity to the workspace.** Managed identities belong to Azure resources (such as App Services). A Power BI workspace does not take one.\n- **B — modify the XMLA Endpoint setting.** Read-write is needed to modify RLS, but that is a *later* step and does not change who may authenticate.\n\n*Note:* the vote is an exact 50/50 split, and the commenter arguing C quotes the same limitation. Worth knowing separately: the doc also states *&quot;Service principals do not work with RLS and OLS, and cannot be added as model role members&quot;* — they can **define** roles through the endpoint, which is what App1 does, but not be members of them.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint — Terms of use",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q120"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q121",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q121",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named WH1.\nYou have source data in a CSV file that has the following fields:\n• SalesTransactionID\n• SaleDate\n• CustomerCode\n• CustomerName\n• CustomerAddress\n• ProductCode\n• ProductName\n• Quantity\n• UnitPrice\nYou plan to implement a star schema for the tables in WH1. The dimension tables in WH1 will implement Type 2 slowly changing dimension (SCD)\nlogic.\nYou need to design the tables that will be used for sales transaction analysis and load the source data.\nWhich type of target table should you specify for the CustomerName, CustomerCode, and SaleDate fields? To answer, select the appropriate\noptions in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "CustomerCode:",
        "options": [
          "Dimension",
          "Fact",
          "Factless fact",
          "Junk dimension"
        ],
        "answer": "Dimension"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "CustomerName:",
        "options": [
          "Dimension",
          "Fact",
          "Factless fact",
          "Junk dimension"
        ],
        "answer": "Dimension"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "SaleDate:",
        "options": [
          "Dimension",
          "Fact",
          "Factless fact",
          "Junk dimension"
        ],
        "answer": "Fact"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Dimension — Dimension — Fact.**\n\nThe star schema over this CSV is:\n\n- **FactSales** — one row per sales transaction: `SalesTransactionID` (a degenerate dimension), the date of the sale, the foreign keys to the customer and product dimensions, and the measures `Quantity` and `UnitPrice`.\n- **DimCustomer** (Type 2) — `CustomerCode` as the natural/business key, plus `CustomerName` and `CustomerAddress` as the attributes whose changes are versioned.\n- **DimProduct** (Type 2) — `ProductCode`, `ProductName`.\n\n**`CustomerCode` and `CustomerName` → Dimension.** Descriptive attributes of a customer, versioned by the Type 2 logic. They are not a *junk dimension* either — a junk dimension collects unrelated low-cardinality flags and indicators, not a business key and a name.\n\n**`SaleDate` → Fact.** *(The PDF answer-key image marks **Dimension**; it is wrong.)* `SaleDate` is **when the transaction happened** — an attribute of the *event*, so it belongs on the fact row (as the date itself, or as a `DateKey` pointing at a conformed date dimension). Two things rule out loading it into a dimension:\n1. **A date dimension is generated, not loaded from transactions.** It has to be contiguous — every day in the range, including days with no sales — so seeding it from the `SaleDate` values in a sales file would leave gaps. (T1Q62 makes exactly this point: *&quot;There is no existing data source for the date dimension&quot;*, so it must be generated.)\n2. **The question states the dimension tables implement Type 2 SCD logic.** A date dimension is never Type 2 — 17 March 2024 does not change and get a new version. Putting `SaleDate` in a Type 2 dimension is incoherent.\n\n**Factless fact** is the remaining distractor: that is a fact table with no measures (attendance, coverage). This one has `Quantity` and `UnitPrice`, so it is an ordinary fact table.\n\nThe community is unanimous against the key on this blank — the three comments that answer it (+20, +11, +4) all say **Dim, Dim, Fact**.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Star schema — degenerate and junk dimensions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#dimension-tables"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p306_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q121"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p306_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p307_i1.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q122",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q122",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating a Fabric Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Get metadata"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Copy data"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Lookup"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Append variable"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Lookup** — the activity that both *runs* a stored procedure and *returns* its result set to the rest of the pipeline: *&quot;Lookup activity reads and returns the content of a configuration file or table. It also returns the result of executing a query or stored procedure. The output can be a singleton value or an array of attributes, which can be consumed in a subsequent copy, transformation, or control flow activity.&quot;*\n\n- **A Get metadata** – returns *metadata about a file or folder* (name, size, last modified, child items). It cannot execute a stored procedure.\n- **B Copy data** – can call a stored procedure as a source, but it writes the rows to a sink instead of exposing them downstream.\n- **D Append variable** – appends a value to an array variable; it executes nothing.\n\n*(This question appears four times in the bank with different distractor sets — T1Q83, T1Q114, T1Q122, T1Q128. The answer is Lookup every time.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lookup activity in Data Factory for Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/lookup-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q122"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q123",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q123",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains a fact table named FactSales that has one billion\nrows.\nYou run the following T-SQL statement.\nCREATE TABLE test.FactSales AS CLONE OF dbo.FactSales;\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "A replica of dbo.FactSales is created in the test schema by copying the metadata only.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Additional schema changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Additional data changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Yes — No — No.** Two documented properties of a Fabric table clone settle all three.\n\n**1. Metadata only → Yes.** The `CREATE TABLE AS CLONE OF` reference is explicit: *&quot;Creates a new table as a **zero-copy clone** of another table in Warehouse in Microsoft Fabric. **Only the metadata of the table is copied. The underlying data of the table, stored as parquet files, is not copied.**&quot;* That is why a clone of a one-billion-row fact table is near-instantaneous.\n\n**2 &amp; 3. Later changes propagate → No, and No.** A clone is a point-in-time snapshot, not a link: *&quot;Upon creation, a table clone is an **independent and separate** entity… Any changes made to the source table after the clone is created do not reflect in the clone, and vice versa.&quot;* This works because Parquet files are immutable — the clone simply keeps referencing the set of files that existed at clone time, while new writes to `dbo.FactSales` create new files that only its own log knows about. Schema changes (2) and data changes (3) are both invisible to the clone.\n\n*Note:* one commenter argues all three are No, on the grounds that a clone &quot;includes the data&quot;. It does make the data *visible*, but it does not **copy** it — which is precisely the wording of statement 1. The two other comments (+24, +6) and the answer key all give Yes/No/No.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Clone table in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/clone-table?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CREATE TABLE AS CLONE OF (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-table-as-clone-of-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p309_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q123"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p309_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p309_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q124",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q124",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have an Amazon Web Services (AWS) subscription that contains an Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) bucket named bucket1.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named LH1.\nIn LH1, you plan to create a OneLake shortcut to bucket1.\nYou need to configure authentication for the connection.\nWhich two values should you provide? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "the shared access signature (SAS) token"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "the secret access key"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "the access ID"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the access key ID"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "the certificate thumbprint"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "B",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>An Amazon S3 shortcut authenticates with an **IAM user&#x27;s access key pair**. The doc lists exactly two values for the connection: the **Access Key ID** and the **Secret Access Key**.\n\n| Field | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| URL | `https://&lt;bucket-name&gt;.s3.&lt;region-code&gt;.amazonaws.com` |\n| Authentication kind | Key |\n| **Access Key ID** | **D** |\n| **Secret Access Key** | **B** |\n\nThe key pair must belong to a user with `s3:GetObject`, `s3:GetBucketLocation` and `s3:ListBucket` on the bucket.\n\nRejected:\n- **A a SAS token** – an **Azure** Storage concept. S3 has presigned URLs, not SAS.\n- **C the access ID** – not an AWS term; the field is *Access **Key** ID*. A near-miss distractor.\n- **E a certificate thumbprint** – used for Microsoft Entra service principal / certificate authentication, not for S3.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Create an Amazon S3 shortcut",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/create-s3-shortcut?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q124"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q125",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q125",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a data warehouse.\nYou need to load rows into a large Type 2 slowly changing dimension (SCD). The solution must minimize resource usage.\nWhich T-SQL statement should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "UPDATE AND INSERT"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "MERGE"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "TRUNCATE TABLE and INSERT"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "CREATE TABLE AS SELECT"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**MERGE.** A Type 2 SCD load is an upsert with two shapes of change in one pass: **update** the rows whose tracked attributes changed (close off the old version) and **insert** the new versions plus genuinely new members. `MERGE` expresses that as a **single statement over a single scan** of the source and target — which is what &quot;minimize resource usage&quot; rewards on a large dimension.\n\n```sql\nMERGE INTO DimCustomer AS tgt\nUSING StagedCustomer AS src ON tgt.CustomerCode = src.CustomerCode AND tgt.IsCurrent = 1\nWHEN MATCHED AND (tgt.CustomerName &lt;&gt; src.CustomerName OR …)\n  THEN UPDATE SET tgt.EffectiveEnd = SYSDATETIME(), tgt.IsCurrent = 0\nWHEN NOT MATCHED BY TARGET\n  THEN INSERT (…) VALUES (…);\n```\n\n- **A UPDATE and INSERT** – produces the same result, but as **two statements**, each scanning and joining the tables independently. More work, more logging, and a window in which the two are inconsistent.\n- **C TRUNCATE and INSERT** – destroys the existing rows, which **is the history**. It turns a Type 2 dimension into a full reload and breaks every fact row pointing at an older surrogate key.\n- **D CTAS** – creates a *new* table. No history, and no way to incrementally version an existing one.\n\n**⚠ Doc drift worth knowing.** Several highly-voted comments argue for **A**, because when they were written `MERGE` was **not supported** in Fabric Warehouse. That has changed: the T-SQL surface-area doc now states *&quot;`MERGE` syntax is supported and is a **generally available** feature.&quot;* So B is correct both as the exam intends it and on current Fabric — but if you sat the exam in 2024 the community&#x27;s objection was fair.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "T-SQL surface area in Fabric Data Warehouse",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "MERGE (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/merge-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q125"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q126",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q126",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Which syntax should you use in a notebook to access the Research division data for Productline1?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>HOTSPOT\n-\nCase study\n-\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case. However,\nthere may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You must manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions\nincluded on this exam in the time provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case study. Case studies might\ncontain exhibits and other resources that provide more information about the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is\nindependent of the other questions in this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers and to make changes before you move to\nthe next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you cannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\n-\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to explore the content of the case study\nbefore you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays information such as business requirements, existing environment, and problem\nstatements. If the case study has an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the question.\nOverview\n-\nContoso, Ltd. is a US-based health supplements company. Contoso has two divisions named Sales and Research. The Sales division contains two\ndepartments named Online Sales and Retail Sales. The Research division assigns internally developed product lines to individual teams of\nresearchers and analysts.\nExisting Environment\n-\nIdentity Environment\n-\nContoso has a Microsoft Entra tenant named contoso.com. The tenant contains two groups named ResearchReviewersGroup1 and\nResearchReviewersGroup2.\nData Environment\n-\nContoso has the following data environment:\n• The Sales division uses a Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity.\n• The semantic model of the Online Sales department includes a fact table named Orders that uses Import made. In the system of origin, the\nOrderID value represents the sequence in which orders are created.\n• The Research department uses an on-premises, third-party data warehousing product.\n• Fabric is enabled for contoso.com.\n• An Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 storage account named storage1 contains Research division data for a product line named Productline1. The\ndata is in the delta format.\n• A Data Lake Storage Gen2 storage account named storage2 contains Research division data for a product line named Productline2. The data is\nin the CSV format.\nRequirements\n-\nPlanned Changes\n-\nContoso plans to make the following changes:\n• Enable support for Fabric in the Power BI Premium capacity used by the Sales division.\n• Make all the data for the Sales division and the Research division available in Fabric.\n• For the Research division, create two Fabric workspaces named Productline1ws and Productline2ws.\n• In Productline1ws, create a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\n• In Lakehouse1, create a shortcut to storage1 named ResearchProduct.\nData Analytics Requirements\n-\nContoso identifies the following data analytics requirements:\n• All the workspaces for the Sales division and the Research division must support all Fabric experiences.\n• The Research division workspaces must use a dedicated, on-demand capacity that has per-minute billing.\n• The Research division workspaces must be grouped together logically to support OneLake data hub filtering based on the department name.\n• For the Research division workspaces, the members of ResearchReviewersGroup1 must be able to read lakehouse and warehouse data and\nshortcuts by using SQL endpoints.\n• For the Research division workspaces, the members of ResearchReviewersGroup2 must be able to read lakehouse data by using Lakehouse\nexplorer.\n• All the semantic models and reports for the Research division must use version control that supports branching.\nData Preparation Requirements\n-\nContoso identifies the following data preparation requirements:\n• The Research division data for Productline1 must be retrieved from Lakehouse1 by using Fabric notebooks.\n• All the Research division data in the lakehouses must be presented as managed tables in Lakehouse explorer.\nSemantic Model Requirements\n-\nContoso identifies the following requirements for implementing and managing semantic models:\n• The number of rows added to the Orders table during refreshes must be minimized.\n• The semantic models in the Research division workspaces must use Direct Lake mode.\nGeneral Requirements\n-\nContoso identifies the following high-level requirements that must be considered for all solutions:\n• Follow the principle of least privilege when applicable.\n• Minimize implementation and maintenance effort when possible.</pre>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "spark.sql(\"SELECT * FROM Lakehouse1.Tables.ResearchProduct\")"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "spark.read.format(\"delta\").load(\"Tables/productline1/ResearchProduct\")"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "external_table(ResearchProduct)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "spark.read.format(\"delta\").load(\"Tables/ResearchProduct\")"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Fourth variant of the Contoso shortcut-access question. Here, as in T1Q119, the **correct path** is on offer:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.read.format(&quot;delta&quot;).load(&quot;Tables/ResearchProduct&quot;)\n```\n\nThe case study creates `ResearchProduct` as a shortcut in `Lakehouse1`, the source data in `storage1` is already **delta**, and the data preparation requirement is that it be *&quot;presented as managed tables in Lakehouse explorer&quot;* — so it lives under **`Tables/`**.\n\n- **B** – `Tables/productline1/ResearchProduct` inserts the *workspace* name into the path. A lakehouse-relative table path does not include it (and a folder level under `Tables/` would be read as a schema).\n- **A** – `Lakehouse1.Tables.ResearchProduct` invents a three-part name; `Tables` is a storage area, not a SQL schema.\n- **C** – `external_table(...)` is not a Fabric notebook construct.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "OneLake shortcuts — access from Apache Spark",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q126"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
