  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q127",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q127",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named LH1.\nYou create new tables in LH1.\nYou need to ensure that the tables are added automatically to the default semantic model.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Disable Query Caching for the default semantic model."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "From the settings pane of LH1, enable Sync the default Power BI semantic model."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Enable Refresh for the default semantic model."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "From the Endorsement and discovery settings of LH1, select Make discoverable."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The doc gives two ways to get objects into the default semantic model, and the first is this setting: *&quot;Manually enable the **Sync the default Power BI semantic model** setting that will automatically add objects to the semantic model&quot;* (the alternative being to add tables manually).\n\n- **A Disable Query Caching** – affects whether query results are cached for report viewers. It adds nothing to the model.\n- **C Enable Refresh** – governs when *data* is reloaded, not which *objects* are in the model.\n- **D Make discoverable** – an endorsement/discovery setting that lets other users find the item in the OneLake data hub. Unrelated.\n\n*(This is the mirror image of T1Q81, which asks how to **stop** the auto-add. Same setting, opposite direction. Note that the setting is reached through the **SQL analytics endpoint** view of the lakehouse — the wording &quot;from the settings pane of LH1&quot; in this option is loose, but it is the only option that names the right control.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Default Power BI semantic models in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/semantic-models?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q127"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q128",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q128",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating a Fabric Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Append variable"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Lookup"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Copy data"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "KQL"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Lookup** — it executes the stored procedure **and** returns the result for downstream activities: *&quot;It also returns the result of executing a query or stored procedure. The output can be a singleton value or an array of attributes, which can be consumed in a subsequent copy, transformation, or control flow activity.&quot;*\n\n- **A Append variable** – appends to an array variable; executes nothing.\n- **C Copy data** – can run a procedure as a source, but pipes the rows into a sink rather than exposing them to later activities.\n- **D KQL** – queries a KQL database, not a warehouse.\n\n*(Fourth appearance of this question — see T1Q83, T1Q114, T1Q122.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lookup activity in Data Factory for Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/lookup-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q128"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q129",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q129",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the\nproblem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also\npossible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYour network contains an on-premises Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) domain named contoso.com that syncs with a Microsoft Entra\ntenant by using Microsoft Entra Connect.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model.\nYou enable dynamic row-level security (RLS) for the model and deploy the model to the Fabric service.\nYou query a measure that includes the USERNAME() function, and the query returns a blank result.\nYou need to ensure that the measure returns the user principal name (UPN) of a user.\nSolution: You update the measure to use the USEROBJECTID() function.\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No.** The goal is for the measure to return the **user principal name** — something like `jane@contoso.com`. **`USEROBJECTID()`** returns the *object ID*: the user&#x27;s **Microsoft Entra GUID** (`oid` claim), e.g. `aaaaaaaa-bbbb-cccc-dddd-eeeeeeeeeeee`. Correct identity, wrong representation.\n\n`USEROBJECTID` is genuinely useful for dynamic RLS — a GUID is immutable, whereas a UPN changes when someone is renamed — but it does not answer the question as asked.\n\nThe solution in this set that meets the goal is **`USERPRINCIPALNAME()`** (T1Q130).</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "USEROBJECTID function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/userobjectid-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q129"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q130",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q130",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the\nproblem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also\npossible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYour network contains an on-premises Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) domain named contoso.com that syncs with a Microsoft Entra\ntenant by using Microsoft Entra Connect.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model.\nYou enable dynamic row-level security (RLS) for the model and deploy the model to the Fabric service.\nYou query a measure that includes the USERNAME() function, and the query returns a blank result.\nYou need to ensure that the measure returns the user principal name (UPN) of a user.\nSolution: You update the measure to use the USERPRINCIPALNAME() function.\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Yes.** This is the one solution in the set that meets the goal. `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` *&quot;returns the user principal name&quot;* — that is exactly what the requirement asks for, and it behaves consistently in Power BI Desktop and in the service.\n\n`USERNAME()`, the function in the failing measure, is the problem: in **Desktop** it returns `DOMAIN\\user`, and it is the wrong tool when a UPN is specifically required. Swapping it for `USERPRINCIPALNAME()` fixes the measure without touching identity sync, roles or anything else.\n\n| Question | Solution | Meets the goal? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| T1Q39 | add user objects to Entra Connect sync | No |\n| T1Q129 | `USEROBJECTID()` | No — returns the Entra object GUID |\n| **T1Q130** | **`USERPRINCIPALNAME()`** | **Yes** |\n| T1Q134 | create a role in the model | No |\n\n*Note:* 25% vote No. There is no good argument for it — every other solution in the set changes something unrelated to what the function returns.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "USERPRINCIPALNAME function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/userprincipalname-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-admin-rls?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
