  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q133",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q133",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains the semantic model shown in the following exhibit.\n\nUse the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Querying SQL views from the warehouse of the model will cause a fallback to ______ mode.",
        "options": [
          "Automatic",
          "Direct Lake",
          "DirectQuery"
        ],
        "answer": "DirectQuery"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Row and column security is ______.",
        "options": [
          "configured to use object-level security (OLS)",
          "configured to use row-level security (RLS)",
          "undefined"
        ],
        "answer": "undefined"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**DirectQuery — undefined.**\n\n**Blank 1 — DirectQuery.** Direct Lake reads **Delta tables** straight from OneLake. A **view** is not a Delta table — it is a SQL object that must be evaluated by the SQL engine — so a Direct Lake model can never serve it from memory. The documented behaviour is that such queries *fall back to DirectQuery mode*, and DirectQuery is in any case the **only** mode Direct Lake can fall back to. *Automatic* is a setting name, not a query mode, and &quot;falling back to Direct Lake&quot; is self-contradictory.\n\n**Blank 2 — undefined.** Read it off the exhibit: the Model tree shows **`Roles (0)`**. RLS and OLS are both defined *inside roles* — RLS as a DAX table filter on a role, OLS as a per-role table/column permission. With zero roles there is nothing configured either way, so row and column security is **undefined**.\n\n**⚠ Read this before trusting blank 1.** The exhibit also shows **Direct Lake Behavior = `Direct Lake Only`**, and that setting *disables fallback*: *&quot;DirectLakeOnly: All queries use Direct Lake storage mode only. Fall back to DirectQuery mode is disabled. If data can&#x27;t be loaded into memory, **an error is returned**.&quot;* Taken literally, then, querying a view on this model would **error rather than fall back** — and no option says that. Two commenters (+12, +6) quote exactly this and lean toward *Direct Lake*; one (+4) answers *DirectQuery*, matching the answer key. The statement presupposes that a fallback happens and only asks which mode, and the answer to that is always DirectQuery — but be aware the exhibit and the statement are in tension.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Manage Direct Lake semantic models — Direct Lake behavior",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-manage?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Direct Lake overview — DirectQuery fallback",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p325_i1.png, p325_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q133"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p325_i1.png",
      "p325_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p326_i1.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q134",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q134",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Note: This section contains one or more sets of questions with the same scenario and problem. Each question presents a unique solution to the\nproblem. You must determine whether the solution meets the stated goals. More than one solution in the set might solve the problem. It is also\npossible that none of the solutions in the set solve the problem.\nAfter you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return. As a result, these questions do not appear on the Review Screen.\nYour network contains an on-premises Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) domain named contoso.com that syncs with a Microsoft Entra\ntenant by using Microsoft Entra Connect.\nYou have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model.\nYou enable dynamic row-level security (RLS) for the model and deploy the model to the Fabric service.\nYou query a measure that includes the USERNAME() function, and the query returns a blank result.\nYou need to ensure that the measure returns the user principal name (UPN) of a user.\nSolution: You create a role in the model.\nDoes this meet the goal?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No.** Creating a **role** changes *who is filtered*, not *what `USERNAME()` returns*. Roles are the container for RLS table filters and role membership; the measure would still call `USERNAME()` and still return the same thing it does now.\n\nThe fix is to change the function: **`USERPRINCIPALNAME()`** *&quot;returns the user principal name&quot;* (T1Q130). Note also that the scenario already says *&quot;You **enable dynamic row-level security** for the model&quot;*, so roles exist — adding another one changes nothing.\n\nLast of the four solutions in this set; the only Yes is T1Q130.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "USERPRINCIPALNAME function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/userprincipalname-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Row-level security (RLS) with Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-admin-rls?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q134"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q135",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q135",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains two workspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2 and a user named User1.\nYou need to ensure that User1 can perform the following tasks:\n• Create a new domain.\n• Create two subdomains named subdomain1 and subdomain2.\n• Assign Workspace1 to subdomain1.\n• Assign Workspace2 to subdomain2.\nThe solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich role should you assign to User1?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "domain admin"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "domain contributor"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Fabric admin"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "workspace Admin"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Least privilege usually pushes you down the role list — but here the **first** task sets a floor. The domains doc states it plainly: *&quot;Only **Fabric admins** can create domains.&quot;* Nothing below that can do it, so the answer cannot be lower than **Fabric admin**.\n\nChecked against all four tasks:\n\n| Task | Minimum role |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Create a new domain** | **Fabric admin** |\n| Create subdomains | Fabric admin *or* domain admin |\n| Assign workspaces to a (sub)domain | Fabric admin, domain admin, or domain contributor |\n\n- **A domain admin** – can create **subdomains** of a domain they already own and manage its workspaces, but cannot create the top-level domain in the first place. This is the near-miss, and 20% of the community picks it.\n- **B domain contributor** – the weakest domain role: it can only assign workspaces it already administers to a domain.\n- **D workspace Admin** – scoped to a single workspace; no domain rights at all.\n\n*(Note that a Fabric admin would normally then **delegate** by assigning a domain admin — but the question asks which single role lets User1 do all four things.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Domains in Microsoft Fabric — roles",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/governance/domains?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q135"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q136",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q136",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains three users named User1, User2, and User3. The tenant contains a security group named Group1. User1\nand User3 are members of Group1.\nThe tenant contains the workspaces shown in the following table.\nThe tenant contains the domains shown in the following table.\nUser1 creates a new workspace named Workspace3.\nYou assign Domain1 as the default domain of Group1.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "User2 is assigned the Contributor role for Workspace3.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "User3 is assigned the Viewer role for Workspace3.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "User3 is assigned the Contributor role for Workspace1.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No — No — No.** The whole question turns on one fact: **domains do not grant workspace roles.**\n\nWhat the setup actually does. Assigning **Domain1 as the *default domain* of Group1** means that when a member of Group1 creates a workspace, that workspace is **automatically associated with Domain1**. The doc describes it as a convenience for organising content: a default domain *&quot;will automatically be assigned to workspaces created by members of that group&quot;*. It is a **content-organisation** feature — it affects OneLake data hub filtering and domain-level governance, not who can open or edit anything.\n\nWorkspace access comes from exactly one place: an explicit role assignment (Admin / Member / Contributor / Viewer) on that workspace. So:\n\n1. **User2 → Contributor on Workspace3? No.** User2 is not even in Group1, and nobody assigned them anything on Workspace3. (User2 is the admin of Workspace2 and of Domain2 — neither reaches Workspace3.)\n2. **User3 → Viewer on Workspace3? No.** User3 *is* in Group1, so Workspace3 lands in Domain1 — but sharing a domain with a workspace confers no role in it. Only User1, who created Workspace3, is its admin.\n3. **User3 → Contributor on Workspace1? No.** Workspace1&#x27;s admin is User1. No role was granted to User3 or to Group1.\n\n*Note:* one comment (+6) answers Yes to statement 2, reasoning that User3 &quot;likely inherits&quot; Viewer through the domain. There is no such inheritance; two other comments and the answer key all give No/No/No.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Domains in Microsoft Fabric — default domains",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/governance/domains?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p330_i1.png, p330_i2.png, p330_i3.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q136"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p330_i1.png",
      "p330_i2.png",
      "p330_i3.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p330_i4.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q137",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q137",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse named Warehouse1 that contains a table named Table1. Table1 contains customer data.\nYou need to implement row-level security (RLS) for Table1. The solution must ensure that users can see only their respective data.\nWhich two objects should you create? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "DATABASE ROLE"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "STORED PROCEDURE"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "CONSTRAINT"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "FUNCTION"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "SECURITY POLICY"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Row-level security in a Fabric warehouse is the standard SQL Server RLS mechanism, and it is built from exactly two objects:\n\n1. **D — a FUNCTION.** An **inline table-valued function** that encodes the filter logic and acts as the *security predicate*. For &quot;users see only their own data&quot; it typically compares a column to the caller:\n\n```sql\nCREATE FUNCTION Security.tvf_securitypredicate(@SalesRep AS nvarchar(50))\n    RETURNS TABLE WITH SCHEMABINDING\nAS\n    RETURN SELECT 1 AS Result WHERE @SalesRep = USER_NAME();\n```\n\n2. **E — a SECURITY POLICY.** The object that **binds** the predicate to the table. Without it the function is just a function and filters nothing:\n\n```sql\nCREATE SECURITY POLICY SalesFilter\nADD FILTER PREDICATE Security.tvf_securitypredicate(SalesRep) ON dbo.Table1\nWITH (STATE = ON);\n```\n\nRejected:\n- **A DATABASE ROLE** – the 27% runner-up. Roles are a perfectly normal way to *organise* who the predicate should apply to, and RLS is often combined with them — but they are **optional**. The requirement here is per-*user* filtering, which `USER_NAME()` handles directly. The two objects RLS cannot exist without are the function and the policy.\n- **B STORED PROCEDURE** – a security predicate must be an inline TVF; a procedure cannot be used.\n- **C CONSTRAINT** – constrains what can be *written*, not what can be *read*.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Row-level security in Fabric Data Warehousing",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CREATE SECURITY POLICY (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-security-policy-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q137"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q138",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q138",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a table named Table1.\nTable1 contains the following data.\nYou need to perform the following actions:\n• Load the data from Table1 into a star schema.\n• Create a product dimension table named DimProduct and a fact table named FactSales.\nWhich three columns should you include in DimProduct?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "ProductColor, ProductID, and ProductName."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "ProductName, SalesAmount, and TransactionlD."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Date, ProductID, and TransactionlD."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "ProductID, ProductName, and SalesAmount"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Split the columns by what each one *describes*:\n\n| Column | Describes | Goes to |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `TransactionID` | the transaction | FactSales (degenerate dimension) |\n| `Date` | when the transaction happened | FactSales |\n| **`ProductID`** | **a unique ID for each product** | **DimProduct** (key) |\n| **`ProductName`** | **a unique name for each product** | **DimProduct** (attribute) |\n| **`ProductColor`** | **a descriptive attribute of the product** | **DimProduct** (attribute) |\n| `SalesAmount` | the money for the transaction | FactSales (measure) |\n\nSo DimProduct = **ProductColor, ProductID, ProductName** — **A**. A dimension holds the *who / what* context; the fact table holds the numeric measures and the event keys.\n\n- **B** and **D** include `SalesAmount`, a **measure**. Measures belong in the fact table; putting one in a dimension would fix the amount per product rather than per transaction.\n- **C** includes `Date` and `TransactionID`, both attributes of the *transaction*, not of the product.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p334_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q138"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p334_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q139",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q139",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace that contains a Dataflow Gen2 query. The query returns the following data.\nYou need to filter the results to ensure that only the latest version of each customer’s record is retained. The solution must ensure that no new\ncolumns are loaded to the semantic model.\nWhich four actions should you perform in sequence in Power Query Editor? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the\nanswer area and arrange them in the correct order.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "1",
        "options": [
          "Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value.",
          "Remove the max version date column.",
          "Expand the All Rows detail column.",
          "Group by CustomerID and calculate the max version date per customer ID.",
          "Remove duplicates based on CustomerID.",
          "Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID."
        ],
        "answer": "Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID."
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "2",
        "options": [
          "Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value.",
          "Remove the max version date column.",
          "Expand the All Rows detail column.",
          "Group by CustomerID and calculate the max version date per customer ID.",
          "Remove duplicates based on CustomerID.",
          "Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID."
        ],
        "answer": "Expand the All Rows detail column."
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "3",
        "options": [
          "Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value.",
          "Remove the max version date column.",
          "Expand the All Rows detail column.",
          "Group by CustomerID and calculate the max version date per customer ID.",
          "Remove duplicates based on CustomerID.",
          "Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID."
        ],
        "answer": "Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value."
      },
      {
        "id": "3",
        "label": "4",
        "options": [
          "Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value.",
          "Remove the max version date column.",
          "Expand the All Rows detail column.",
          "Group by CustomerID and calculate the max version date per customer ID.",
          "Remove duplicates based on CustomerID.",
          "Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID."
        ],
        "answer": "Remove the max version date column."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The classic Power Query &quot;keep the latest row per key&quot; pattern, in four steps:\n\n1. **Group by CustomerID, use the All Rows operation, and calculate the max version date per customer ID.** Two aggregations in one *Group By*: a `Max` over the version date, **and** an **All Rows** aggregation that stashes the original rows of each group in a nested table column. The All Rows part is what makes this work — a plain Group By would collapse every other column (customer name and the rest) out of existence.\n2. **Expand the All Rows detail column.** Unpacks the nested table, restoring the original columns — now with the group&#x27;s max version date sitting alongside every row.\n3. **Filter the query where the version date value equals the max version date value.** Each row&#x27;s own version date is compared to its customer&#x27;s maximum; only the latest survives.\n4. **Remove the max version date column.** Satisfies *&quot;no new columns are loaded to the semantic model&quot;* — the helper column is dropped once it has done its job.\n\nRejected actions:\n- **Group by CustomerID and calculate the max version date per customer ID** (without All Rows) — returns two columns only. Everything else is lost, and there is nothing left to expand.\n- **Remove duplicates based on CustomerID** — keeps an arbitrary row per customer (whichever comes first in the current sort order), not the latest one. It is the tempting shortcut and it is wrong.\n\n*Note:* the PDF&#x27;s answer image marks the four slots with connector lines that are unreadable in the scan. The sequence above is the unanimous reading of the community (+10, +6, +2, no dissent) and is the only ordering that satisfies both requirements.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Group or summarize rows (Power Query)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/group-by?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Working with duplicates (Power Query)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/working-with-duplicates?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p335_i1.png, p335_i2.png"
