  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q141",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q141",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1.\nYou plan to deploy a semantic model named Model1 by using the XMLA endpoint.\nYou need to optimize the deployment of Model1. The solution must minimize how long it takes to deploy Model1.\nWhat should you do in Workspace1?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Select Small semantic model storage format."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Select Users can edit data models in the Power BI service."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Set Enable Cache for Shortcuts to On."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Select Large semantic model storage format."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Large semantic model storage format.** Microsoft recommends it specifically for XMLA deployments, regardless of model size: *&quot;If you&#x27;re planning to use XMLA endpoint-based tools for semantic model **write** operations, be sure to enable the setting, **even for semantic models that you wouldn&#x27;t necessarily characterize as a large semantic model**. When enabled, the large semantic model storage format can **improve XMLA write operations performance**.&quot;*\n\nThe reason is how the model is persisted: the large format stores the model in **premium files** with incremental, per-object writes, instead of serialising the whole model on every write. Deployments touch far less data and finish faster.\n\n- **A Small semantic model storage format** – the opposite, and the default. Slower XMLA writes.\n- **B Users can edit data models in the Power BI service** – enables web modelling. Nothing to do with XMLA deployment speed.\n- **C Enable Cache for Shortcuts** – a OneLake shortcut caching setting for external data reads. Unrelated to semantic models.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Large semantic models in Power BI Premium",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-large-models?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q141"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q142",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q142",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI project that contains a semantic model.\nYou plan to use Azure DevOps for version control.\nYou need to modify the .gitignore file to prevent the data values from the data sources from being pushed to the repository.\nWhich file should you reference?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "unappliedChanges.json"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "cache.abf"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "localSettings.json"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "model.bim"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`cache.abf`.** In a Power BI project&#x27;s `.SemanticModel` folder, `cache.abf` is the **Analysis Services backup file** holding the model&#x27;s **cached data** — the actual imported rows from your data sources. It is binary, it can be very large, and it is the one file that would push real data values into the repository. `.gitignore` it.\n\nWhat the others are:\n- **D `model.bim`** – the model **definition** in TMSL/JSON: tables, columns, measures, relationships. Metadata only, and it is exactly what you *want* under version control.\n- **A `unappliedChanges.json`** – pending edits not yet applied to the model. Metadata.\n- **C `localSettings.json`** – per-user, per-machine settings. It *should* also be excluded — and the doc notes *&quot;By default, Git ignores this file&quot;* — but it holds preferences, not data values, so it does not answer the question as asked.\n\nThe default `.gitignore` that Power BI Desktop generates for a project already excludes `cache.abf` and `.pbi/localSettings.json` for these reasons.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Power BI Desktop project semantic model folder",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-dataset?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Power BI Desktop projects overview",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q142"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q143",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q143",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 uses Pro license mode and contains a semantic model\nnamed Model1.\nYou need to ensure that Model1 supports XMLA connections.\nWhich setting should modify?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Users can edit data models in the Power BI service"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Enforce strict access control for all data connection types"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Enable Cache for Shortcuts"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "License mode"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**License mode.** The XMLA endpoint is not available on **Pro** workspaces at all. Microsoft scopes it explicitly: *&quot;Power BI Premium, Premium Per User, and Power BI Embedded workspaces use an XMLA endpoint…&quot;* — Pro is absent from that list. So the blocker is the workspace&#x27;s licensing tier, and the fix is to change **Workspace settings → License mode** to Premium per user, Premium capacity, Embedded, or a Fabric capacity.\n\n- **A Users can edit data models in the Power BI service** – enables browser modelling; it does not expose an XMLA endpoint.\n- **B Enforce strict access control for all data connection types** – a data-connection governance setting.\n- **C Enable Cache for Shortcuts** – OneLake shortcut caching. Unrelated.\n\nOnly once the workspace is on a supported licence do the other XMLA switches (the tenant setting, and the capacity&#x27;s Read Write endpoint setting) become relevant.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q143"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q144",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q144",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a complex semantic model. The model is based on a star schema and contains many tables, including a\nfact table named Sales.\nYou need to visualize a diagram of the model. The diagram must contain only the Sales table and related tables.\nWhat should you use from Microsoft Power BI Desktop?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "data categories"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Data view"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Model view"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DAX query view"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Model view.** It draws the tables and relationships, and — the part that matters here — it supports **separate diagram layouts**: *&quot;With Model view, you can create diagrams of your model that contain only a subset of the tables in your model.&quot;*\n\nAdd a new layout tab, drag in `Sales`, then right-click it and choose **Add related tables**. You get a diagram of the fact table and its dimensions only, without the rest of a complex model cluttering the picture.\n\n- **A data categories** – tag a column&#x27;s semantic type (City, URL, …) so visuals render it correctly.\n- **B Data view** – the rows of a single table.\n- **D DAX query view** – author and run DAX queries.\n\n*(Duplicate of T1Q29.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Model view in Power BI Desktop — create separate diagrams",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-modeling-view?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q144"
    ],
