    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q145",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q145",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named LH1.\nYou need to deploy a new semantic model. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Support complex calculated columns that include aggregate functions, calculated tables, and Multidimensional Expressions (MDX) user\nhierarchies.\n• Minimize page rendering times.\nHow should you configure the model? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Mode:",
        "options": [
          "Direct Lake",
          "DirectQuery",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "Import"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Query Caching:",
        "options": [
          "Capacity default",
          "Off",
          "On"
        ],
        "answer": "On"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Import — On.**\n\n**Mode — Import.** The first requirement lists three things that **Direct Lake does not support**. From the storage-mode comparison table in the Direct Lake docs:\n\n| Capability | Direct Lake | Import | DirectQuery |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Calculated columns with aggregate functions | **No** | **Yes** | No |\n| Calculated tables | **No** | **Yes** | No |\n| MDX user hierarchies | **No** | **Yes** | Yes |\n\nOnly **Import** answers Yes to all three. (Direct Lake carves out a narrow exception for calculation groups, what-if parameters and field parameters, which implicitly create calculated tables — but not for the general case the requirement describes.) DirectQuery fails on calculated columns and calculated tables, and is the slowest of the three anyway.\n\n**Query Caching — On.** The second requirement is *&quot;minimize page rendering times&quot;*. Query caching stores the results of a report&#x27;s queries so that subsequent loads are served from the cache instead of re-querying the model — it is the setting whose stated purpose is faster report loads. *Capacity default* leaves it to chance; *Off* forgoes the benefit. It is available on Premium/Embedded and applies to **Import** models, which fits the mode chosen above.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Direct Lake overview — comparison to other storage modes",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Query caching in Power BI Premium",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/power-bi-query-caching?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p345_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q145"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p345_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p345_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q146",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q146",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have the following KQL query.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "The query excludes sales that have a Status of Cancelled.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "The query calculates the total sales of each product category for the last 30 days.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "The query includes product categories that have had zero sales during the last 30 days.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Yes — Yes — No.** Read the KQL one operator at a time:\n\n```kusto\nSales\n| where Status != &quot;Cancelled&quot;                              // 1\n| where OrderDate &gt;= ago(30d)                              // 2\n| summarize TotalSales = sum(SalesAmount) by ProductCategory   // 2\n| where TotalSales &gt; 0                                     // 3\n```\n\n**1. Excludes cancelled sales → Yes.** `!=` is &quot;not equal to&quot;, so `where Status != &quot;Cancelled&quot;` keeps every row whose status is anything other than Cancelled.\n\n**2. Total sales per product category for the last 30 days → Yes.** `ago(30d)` returns the timestamp 30 days before now, so `OrderDate &gt;= ago(30d)` keeps the last 30 days. `summarize sum(SalesAmount) by ProductCategory` then totals within each category. Both halves of the statement hold.\n\n**3. Includes categories with zero sales → No.** Two independent reasons:\n- `summarize … by ProductCategory` only produces groups for categories that **have rows** in the filtered input. A category with no sales in the window contributes no rows, so no group.\n- The final `| where TotalSales &gt; 0` explicitly discards anything that totalled zero (or less, e.g. a refund-only category).\n\n*(One commenter reads statement 2 as No; the last line filters *after* the summarize, so it does not change what is being calculated — it only trims the output. The answer key and the other comments give Yes/Yes/No.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "summarize operator (KQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/summarize-operator?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "ago() function (KQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/ago-function?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p347_i1.png, p347_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q146"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p347_i1.png",
      "p347_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p347_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q147",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q147",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named SalesOrderDetail, SalesOrderDetail contains three columns named OrderQty, ProductID\nand SalesOrderlD. SalesOrderDetail contains one row per combination of SalesOrderlD and ProductID.\nYou need to calculate the proportion of the total quantity of each sales order represented by each product within the sales order.\nWhich T-SQL statement should you run?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "SUM(OrderQty) OVER (PARTITION BY ProductID)"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "SUM(OrderQty) OVER (ORDER BY SalesOrderID)"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "SUM(OrderQty) OVER (ORDER BY ProductID)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "SUM(OrderQty) OVER (PARTITION BY SalesOrderID)"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>The requirement is *&quot;the proportion of the total quantity of **each sales order** represented by each product **within the sales order**&quot;*. The denominator is therefore the total for the **sales order**, which means the window must be partitioned by `SalesOrderID`:\n\n```sql\nSELECT SalesOrderID, ProductID, OrderQty\n  , CAST(1. * OrderQty / SUM(OrderQty) OVER (PARTITION BY SalesOrderID) * 100 AS DECIMAL(5,2)) AS PercentbyProductID\nFROM Sales.SalesOrderDetail;\n```\n\nThat is **D**. Two details are doing quiet work: `PARTITION BY` with **no** `ORDER BY` gives a window over the *entire* partition (the whole order), which is what a denominator needs; and `1. *` forces decimal arithmetic so the division does not truncate to an integer.\n\n- **A — `PARTITION BY ProductID`.** Divides by the total quantity of that **product across all orders**. Right function, wrong grouping key — the classic trap.\n- **B — `ORDER BY SalesOrderID`** and **C — `ORDER BY ProductID`.** `OVER` with an `ORDER BY` and no frame clause defaults to `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`, making `SUM` a **running total** rather than a partition total. The denominator would grow row by row, which is not a proportion of anything.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "OVER clause (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-over-clause-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SUM (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/sum-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q147"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q148",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q148",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Which syntax should you use in a notebook to access the Research division data for Productline1?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>HOTSPOT\n-\nCase study\n-\nThis is a case study. Case studies are not timed separately. You can use as much exam time as you would like to complete each case. However,\nthere may be additional case studies and sections on this exam. You must manage your time to ensure that you are able to complete all questions\nincluded on this exam in the time provided.\nTo answer the questions included in a case study, you will need to reference information that is provided in the case study. Case studies might\ncontain exhibits and other resources that provide more information about the scenario that is described in the case study. Each question is\nindependent of the other questions in this case study.\nAt the end of this case study, a review screen will appear. This screen allows you to review your answers and to make changes before you move to\nthe next section of the exam. After you begin a new section, you cannot return to this section.\nTo start the case study\n-\nTo display the first question in this case study, click the Next button. Use the buttons in the left pane to explore the content of the case study\nbefore you answer the questions. Clicking these buttons displays information such as business requirements, existing environment, and problem\nstatements. If the case study has an All Information tab, note that the information displayed is identical to the information displayed on the\nsubsequent tabs. When you are ready to answer a question, click the Question button to return to the question.\nOverview\n-\nContoso, Ltd. is a US-based health supplements company. Contoso has two divisions named Sales and Research. The Sales division contains two\ndepartments named Online Sales and Retail Sales. The Research division assigns internally developed product lines to individual teams of\nresearchers and analysts.\nExisting Environment\n-\nIdentity Environment\n-\nContoso has a Microsoft Entra tenant named contoso.com. The tenant contains two groups named ResearchReviewersGroup1 and\nResearchReviewersGroup2.\nData Environment\n-\nContoso has the following data environment:\n• The Sales division uses a Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity.\n• The semantic model of the Online Sales department includes a fact table named Orders that uses Import made. In the system of origin, the\nOrderID value represents the sequence in which orders are created.\n• The Research department uses an on-premises, third-party data warehousing product.\n• Fabric is enabled for contoso.com.\n• An Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 storage account named storage1 contains Research division data for a product line named Productline1. The\ndata is in the delta format.\n• A Data Lake Storage Gen2 storage account named storage2 contains Research division data for a product line named Productline2. The data is\nin the CSV format.\nRequirements\n-\nPlanned Changes\n-\nContoso plans to make the following changes:\n• Enable support for Fabric in the Power BI Premium capacity used by the Sales division.\n• Make all the data for the Sales division and the Research division available in Fabric.\n• For the Research division, create two Fabric workspaces named Productline1ws and Productline2ws.\n• In Productline1ws, create a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\n• In Lakehouse1, create a shortcut to storage1 named ResearchProduct.\nData Analytics Requirements\n-\nContoso identifies the following data analytics requirements:\n• All the workspaces for the Sales division and the Research division must support all Fabric experiences.\n• The Research division workspaces must use a dedicated, on-demand capacity that has per-minute billing.\n• The Research division workspaces must be grouped together logically to support OneLake data hub filtering based on the department name.\n• For the Research division workspaces, the members of ResearchReviewersGroup1 must be able to read lakehouse and warehouse data and\nshortcuts by using SQL endpoints.\n• For the Research division workspaces, the members of ResearchReviewersGroup2 must be able to read lakehouse data by using Lakehouse\nexplorer.\n• All the semantic models and reports for the Research division must use version control that supports branching.\nData Preparation Requirements\n-\nContoso identifies the following data preparation requirements:\n• The Research division data for Productline1 must be retrieved from Lakehouse1 by using Fabric notebooks.\n• All the Research division data in the lakehouses must be presented as managed tables in Lakehouse explorer.\nSemantic Model Requirements\n-\nContoso identifies the following requirements for implementing and managing semantic models:\n• The number of rows added to the Orders table during refreshes must be minimized.\n• The semantic models in the Research division workspaces must use Direct Lake mode.\nGeneral Requirements\n-\nContoso identifies the following high-level requirements that must be considered for all solutions:\n• Follow the principle of least privilege when applicable.\n• Minimize implementation and maintenance effort when possible.</pre>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "spark.read.format(“delta”).load(“Tables/ResearchProduct”)"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "spark.read.format(“delta”).load(“Files/ResearchProduct”)"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "spark.sql(“SELECT * FROM Lakehouse1.productline1.ResearchProduct”)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "spark.read.format(“delta”).load(“Tables/productline1/ResearchProduct”)"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Fifth and last variant of the Contoso shortcut-access question. The correct path is on offer, so it wins:\n\n```python\ndf = spark.read.format(&quot;delta&quot;).load(&quot;Tables/ResearchProduct&quot;)\n```\n\nThis is Microsoft&#x27;s documented form for reading a shortcut from Spark, and it matches the case study: the data in `storage1` is already **delta**, and the data preparation requirement says everything must be *&quot;presented as managed tables in Lakehouse explorer&quot;* — so the shortcut is under **`Tables/`**.\n\n- **B** – `Files/ResearchProduct` is the unmanaged area. A commenter argues shortcuts always land in `/Files`; that is true only if you create them there, and doing so would break the managed-table requirement.\n- **D** – `Tables/productline1/ResearchProduct` inserts the workspace name; a lakehouse-relative path does not include it, and a folder level under `Tables/` is interpreted as a schema.\n- **C** – `Lakehouse1.productline1.ResearchProduct` is a three-part name whose middle part is a workspace, not a schema.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "OneLake shortcuts — access from Apache Spark",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
