  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q149",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q149",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric lakehouse named Lakehouse1 that contains the following data.\nYou need build a T-SQL statement that will return the total sales amount by OrderDate only for the days that are holidays in Australia. The total\nsales amount must sum the quantity multiplied by the price on each row in the dbo.sales table.\nHow should you complete the statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "SELECT s.OrderDate,  ______  AS TotalSalesAmt",
        "options": [
          "s.Quantity * S.UnitPrice",
          "Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)",
          "Sum(s.quantity) * s.UnitPrice",
          "Sum(s.Quantity) * Sum(s.UnitPrice)"
        ],
        "answer": "Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "______  JOIN [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[publicholidays] AS ph ON s.OrderDate = ph.date",
        "options": [
          "Cross",
          "Full outer",
          "Inner",
          "Right outer"
        ],
        "answer": "Inner"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)` — `Inner`.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT s.OrderDate, Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice) AS TotalSalesAmt\nFROM [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[sales] AS s\nINNER JOIN [Lakehouse1].[dbo].[publicholidays] AS ph ON s.OrderDate = ph.date\nWHERE ph.countryOrRegion = &#x27;Australia&#x27;\nGROUP BY s.OrderDate\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `Sum(s.Quantity * s.UnitPrice)`.** The requirement is to *&quot;sum the quantity multiplied by the price **on each row**&quot;* — so multiply first, per row, then aggregate.\n- `s.Quantity * S.UnitPrice` has no aggregate at all, which breaks the `GROUP BY s.OrderDate` (a non-aggregated, non-grouped column).\n- `Sum(s.Quantity) * Sum(s.UnitPrice)` sums each column separately and multiplies the totals — mathematically a different (and wrong) number.\n- `Sum(s.quantity) * s.UnitPrice` is wrong for the same reason, **and** it would fail in any case: `s.UnitPrice` is neither aggregated nor in the `GROUP BY`.\n\n**Blank 2 — `Inner`.** The requirement is *&quot;only for the days that are holidays in Australia&quot;*. An **inner join** keeps only the sales rows whose `OrderDate` matches a row in `publicholidays` — non-holiday days are dropped, which is exactly what is wanted.\n- **Full outer** would keep all sales *and* all holidays, including non-holiday sales days (with NULLs) — and the `WHERE ph.countryOrRegion = &#x27;Australia&#x27;` would then quietly convert it back into an inner join anyway.\n- **Right outer** would keep every holiday even with no sales.\n- **Cross** produces the Cartesian product; the `ON` clause is not even valid with `CROSS JOIN`.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "FROM clause plus JOIN (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/from-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SUM (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/sum-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p354_i1.png, p354_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q149"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p354_i1.png",
      "p354_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p354_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q150",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q150",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1. DW1 contains two\ntables named Employees and Sales. All users have read access to Dw1.\nYou need to implement access controls to meet the following requirements:\n• For the Sales table, ensure that the users can see only the sales data from their respective region.\n• For the Employees table, restrict access to all Personally Identifiable Information (PII).\n• Maintain access to unrestricted data for all the users.\nWhat should you use for each table? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Employees:  (restrict access to all PII, keep the rest visible)",
        "options": [
          "Column-level security",
          "Item permissions",
          "Row-level security (RLS)",
          "Workspace permissions"
        ],
        "answer": "Column-level security"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Sales:  (users see only their own region’s sales)",
        "options": [
          "Column-level security",
          "Item permissions",
          "Row-level security (RLS)",
          "Workspace permissions"
        ],
        "answer": "Row-level security (RLS)"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Column-level security — Row-level security.** Two requirements that differ along different axes of the table:\n\n| Requirement | Restricts | Feature |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Employees: *&quot;restrict access to all **Personally Identifiable Information**&quot;* | specific **columns** | **Column-level security (CLS)** |\n| Sales: *&quot;see only the sales data from their respective **region**&quot;* | specific **rows** | **Row-level security (RLS)** |\n\n- **CLS** — *&quot;allows you to restrict column access to protect sensitive data&quot;*. PII lives in named columns (SSN, date of birth, home address), so you deny those columns and the rest of the Employees table stays readable. This also satisfies *&quot;maintain access to unrestricted data for all the users&quot;*.\n- **RLS** — *&quot;control access to rows in a database table … you can ensure that workers access only those data rows that are pertinent to their department&quot;*. Region is a row attribute, so a filter predicate on it is the right tool.\n\nRejected:\n- **Item permissions** and **Workspace permissions** are all-or-nothing on the whole item or workspace. Revoking either would remove access to the unrestricted data too, breaking the third requirement — and the stem already says *&quot;all users have read access to DW1&quot;*.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Column-level security in Fabric data warehousing",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/column-level-security?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Row-level security in Fabric data warehousing",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/row-level-security?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p356_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q150"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p356_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p356_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q151",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q151",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You need to create a Microsoft Power BI file that will be used to create multiple reports. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• The file must include predefined data source connections.\n• The file must include the report structure and formatting.\n• The file must NOT contain any data.\nWhich file format should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "PBIT"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "PBIDS"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "PBIX"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "PBIP"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**PBIT — a Power BI template.** Match the three requirements against what each format holds:\n\n| Format | Data source connections | Report structure &amp; formatting | Contains data |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **PBIT** (template) | **yes** | **yes** | **no** |\n| PBIX (report) | yes | yes | **yes** |\n| PBIDS (data source) | yes | no | no |\n| PBIP (project) | yes | yes | yes — `cache.abf` holds the imported data |\n\nA `.pbit` is a `.pbix` with the data cache stripped out: queries, model, measures, visuals and theme all survive, and opening one prompts for parameter values and then refreshes. That is precisely a reusable starting point for *&quot;multiple reports&quot;* with no data in it.\n\n- **C PBIX** fails the &quot;no data&quot; requirement.\n- **B PBIDS** is only a connection definition — no report structure at all.\n- **D PBIP** is the folder-based project format for source control; it includes `cache.abf`, which is the data (see T1Q142).</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Create and use report templates in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-templates?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q151"
    ],
