  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q152",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q152",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a single semantic model that has two Microsoft\nPower BI reports.\nYou have a Microsoft 365 subscription that contains a data loss prevention (DLP) policy named DLP1.\nYou need to apply DLP1 to the items in Workspace1.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Create a workspace identity."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Apply a certified endorsement to the semantic model."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Apply sensitivity labels to the semantic model and reports."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Apply a master data endorsement to the semantic model."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Apply sensitivity labels.** DLP policies for Fabric do not act on items directly — they act on **what an item is labelled or what sensitive information it contains**. From the Purview doc: *&quot;You define a DLP policy in the data loss prevention section of the Microsoft Purview portal. In the policy, you specify the conditions, such as **which sensitivity labels** and/or sensitive info types you want to detect.&quot;*\n\nSo to bring the semantic model and its two reports under DLP1, you label them; the policy then matches on the label and applies its actions (policy tips, alerts, blocking).\n\n- **A a workspace identity** – a managed identity that lets the *workspace* authenticate to Azure resources. Nothing to do with DLP.\n- **B / D endorsements** (Certified, Master data) – **discovery and trust** signals that help users find quality content. They carry no security or compliance enforcement and are not DLP conditions.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Get started with DLP policies for Fabric and Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/purview/dlp-powerbi-get-started?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Configure data loss prevention policies for Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/governance/data-loss-prevention-configure?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q152"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q153",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q153",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. User1 is assigned the Contributor role for\nWorkspace1.\nYou plan to configure Workspace1 to use an Azure DevOps repository for version control.\nYou need to ensure that User1 can commit items to the repository.\nWhich two settings should you enable for User1? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Users can sync workspace items with GitHub repositories"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Users can create and use Data workflows"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Users can create Fabric items"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Users can synchronize workspace items with their Git repositories"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "C",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Two tenant settings gate Git commits from a workspace:\n\n- **D — *Users can synchronize workspace items with their Git repositories*.** The master switch for Fabric Git integration. Without it there is no Connect/Commit/Update at all.\n- **C — *Users can create Fabric items*.** Committing writes item definitions, and syncing back creates and updates items in the workspace. Without the right to create Fabric items the user cannot complete the operation.\n\nRejected:\n- **A — *Users can sync workspace items with GitHub repositories*.** The deliberate trap. The scenario says **Azure DevOps**, which is covered by the generic *Git repositories* setting (D). GitHub has its own **separate** tenant switch, and turning it on does nothing for an Azure Repos connection. &quot;Azure DevOps uses Git&quot; ≠ &quot;Azure DevOps is GitHub&quot;.\n- **B — *Users can create and use Data workflows*.** Apache Airflow jobs. Unrelated.\n\nNote that User1 already has the **Contributor** workspace role, which is the minimum role that can commit — so the remaining gaps are the two tenant settings.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction to Git integration",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/git-integration/intro-to-git-integration?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Microsoft Fabric tenant settings",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/admin/tenant-settings-index?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q153"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q154",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q154",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a data pipeline named Pipeline1 and a lakehouse\nnamed Lakehouse1.\nYou perform the following actions:\n• Create a workspace named Workspace2.\n• Create a deployment pipeline named DeployPipeline1 that will deploy items from Workspace1 to Workspace2.\n• Add a folder named Folder1 to Workspace1.\n• Move Lakehouse1 to Folder1.\n• Run DeployPipeline1.\nWhich structure will Workspace2 have when DeployPipeline1 is complete?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "\\Folder1\\Pipeline1 \\Folder1\\Lakehouse1"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "\\Pipeline1 \\Lakehouse1"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "\\Pipeline1 \\Folder1\\Lakehouse1"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "\\Folder1\\Lakehouse1"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`\\Pipeline1` and `\\Folder1\\Lakehouse1`.** Two rules combine.\n\n**1. Folders travel with their items.** The doc: *&quot;In Deployment pipelines, **folders are considered part of an item&#x27;s name** (an item name includes its full path). When an item is deployed, after its path was changed (moved from folder A to folder B, for example), then Deployment pipelines applies this change to its paired item during deployment… **If folder B doesn&#x27;t exist in the stage we&#x27;re deploying to, it&#x27;s created in its workspace first.**&quot;*\n\nLakehouse1 was moved into `Folder1` **before** the deployment ran, so its full name is now `Folder1\\Lakehouse1`. Workspace2 gets `Folder1` created for it, with Lakehouse1 inside.\n\n**2. Pipeline1 never moved.** It is still at the root of Workspace1, so it deploys to the root of Workspace2 — and it *does* deploy; Data pipelines are a supported item type.\n\nRejected:\n- **D `\\Folder1\\Lakehouse1`** only – drops Pipeline1 entirely. Nothing excluded it from the deployment.\n- **B `\\Pipeline1` `\\Lakehouse1`** – assumes folders are ignored by deployment. The doc says the opposite.\n- **A** – puts Pipeline1 in Folder1 too, but it was never moved there.\n\n*Note:* the vote is a three-way split (C 41 / D 39 / B 20), which is a good sign that the folder rule is worth memorising.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "The Fabric deployment pipelines process — folders",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q154"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q155",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q155",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>Your company has a finance department.\nYou have a Fabric tenant, an Azure Storage account named storage1, and a Microsoft Entra group named Group1. Group1 contains the users in the\nfinance department.\nYou need to create a new workspace named Workspace1 in the tenant. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Ensure that the finance department users can create and edit items in Workspace1.\n• Ensure that Workspace1 can securely access storage1 to read and write data.\n• Ensure that you are the only admin of Workspace1.\n• Minimize administrative effort.\nYou create Workspace1.\nWhich two actions should you perform next? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Assign the Contributor role to Group1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Create a workspace identity."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Assign the Admin role to yourself."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Assign the Contributor role to each finance department user."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Four requirements, and two actions cover them:\n\n- **A — assign the Contributor role to Group1.** *&quot;Finance department users can create and edit items&quot;* → **Contributor** is the lowest workspace role that can create and edit. Assigning it to **Group1** rather than to individuals satisfies *&quot;minimize administrative effort&quot;*: new starters inherit access through group membership with no extra work.\n- **B — create a workspace identity.** *&quot;Workspace1 can **securely** access storage1 to read and write data&quot;* → a **workspace identity** is the Microsoft Entra service principal automatically created for the workspace; you grant it a role on `storage1` and Fabric items authenticate with it. No keys, no SAS tokens, no stored credentials — that is the &quot;securely&quot; part.\n\nRejected:\n- **C — assign the Admin role to yourself.** Unnecessary: *&quot;The deploying/creating user automatically becomes … the only admin of the new workspace.&quot;* You created Workspace1, so you already are its admin — and the requirement is that you remain the **only** one.\n- **D — assign Contributor to each finance user individually.** Same end state as A but the maximum administrative effort, and it drifts out of date as the department changes.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Workspace identity in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/security/workspace-identity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
