    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p373_i1.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q159",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q159",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a warehouse named\nDW1. LH1 contains a table named signindata that is in the dbo schema.\nYou need to create a stored procedure in DW1 that deduplicates the data in the signindata table.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "CREATE PROCEDURE dbo.usp_GetPerson  AS  ______",
        "options": [
          "BEGIN",
          "BEGIN DISTRIBUTED TRANSACTION",
          "SET"
        ],
        "answer": "BEGIN"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "SELECT  ______  PersonID, FirstName, LastName",
        "options": [
          "DISTINCT",
          "GROUP BY",
          "TOP (100) PERCENT WITH TIES"
        ],
        "answer": "DISTINCT"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**BEGIN — DISTINCT.**\n\n```sql\nCREATE PROCEDURE dbo.usp_GetPerson\nAS\nBEGIN\n    SELECT DISTINCT PersonID, FirstName, LastName\n    FROM LH1.dbo.signindata\nEND\n```\n\n**Blank 1 — `BEGIN`.** A stored procedure body is a `BEGIN … END` block, and the `END` on the last line of the exhibit is its partner.\n- `BEGIN DISTRIBUTED TRANSACTION` starts an MS DTC transaction across servers. This procedure reads one table in one workspace; there is nothing distributed about it, and the closing `END` would have no matching `BEGIN`.\n- `SET` changes a session option (`SET NOCOUNT ON`, …). It is a statement, not a block opener, so `END` would be orphaned.\n\n**Blank 2 — `DISTINCT`.** The requirement is to **deduplicate**, and `SELECT DISTINCT` *&quot;specifies that only unique rows can appear in the result set&quot;* — one statement, no extra clauses.\n- `GROUP BY` is not valid there (it follows `FROM`, not `SELECT`). Even placed correctly it would need all three columns restated to achieve the same thing.\n- `TOP (100) PERCENT WITH TIES` returns every row — no deduplication at all (and `WITH TIES` requires an `ORDER BY`).\n\nNote the cross-item read `FROM LH1.dbo.signindata`: a warehouse can query a lakehouse in the same workspace by three-part name.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "SELECT clause — DISTINCT (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-clause-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CREATE PROCEDURE (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/create-procedure-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p374_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q159"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p374_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p374_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q160",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q160",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating a Fabric Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Get metadata"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Switch"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Lookup"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Append variable"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Lookup** — the only activity that executes a stored procedure **and** hands its result to later activities: *&quot;It also returns the result of executing a query or stored procedure. The output can be a singleton value or an array of attributes, which can be consumed in a subsequent copy, transformation, or control flow activity.&quot;*\n\n- **A Get metadata** – returns metadata about a file or folder (name, size, child items). It executes nothing.\n- **B Switch** – control-flow branching on a value.\n- **D Append variable** – appends to an array variable.\n\n*(Fifth appearance of this item — see T1Q83, T1Q114, T1Q122, T1Q128. Only the distractors change.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lookup activity in Data Factory for Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/lookup-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q160"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q161",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q161",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have the following T-SQL statement.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "The statement returns Region values when a Sales item has a RefundStatus of Refunded.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "The statement only returns TransactionDate values that occurred during the current year.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "The TotalRevenue calculation aggregates SalesAmount values that have a RefundStatus of Refunded.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Yes — Yes — No.** The query:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  ProductCategory,\n  Region,\n  SUM(CASE WHEN RefundStatus &lt;&gt; &#x27;Refunded&#x27; THEN SalesAmount ELSE 0 END) AS TotalRevenue\nFROM Sales\nWHERE YEAR(TransactionDate) = YEAR(GETDATE())\nGROUP BY ProductCategory, Region;\n```\n\nThe key insight is **where** the refund logic lives: it is inside the `CASE`, **not** in the `WHERE` clause.\n\n**1. Returns Region values for refunded items → Yes.** Nothing filters refunded rows out of the result set. A refunded sale still belongs to its `ProductCategory`/`Region` group, so that group is still returned — the refund merely contributes **0** to its `TotalRevenue`. (Had the author written `WHERE RefundStatus &lt;&gt; &#x27;Refunded&#x27;`, a region whose every sale was refunded would vanish. They did not.)\n\n**2. Only current-year transactions → Yes.** `WHERE YEAR(TransactionDate) = YEAR(GETDATE())` restricts the input rows to the current calendar year, so nothing outside it is considered.\n\n**3. TotalRevenue aggregates refunded SalesAmount values → No.** This is what the `CASE` prevents: for a refunded row the expression evaluates to the literal `0`, so the row&#x27;s actual `SalesAmount` never enters the sum. Refunded rows are *counted in the grouping* but their amounts are *excluded from the total* — which is exactly the distinction statements 1 and 3 are testing from opposite sides.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "CASE (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/case-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SELECT — GROUP BY (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-group-by-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p377_i1.png, p377_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q161"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p377_i1.png",
      "p377_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p377_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q162",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q162",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains the following data.\nThe data has the following characteristics:\n• Each customer is assigned a unique CustomerID value.\n• Each customer is associated to a single SalesRegion value.\n• Each customer is associated to a single CustomerAddress value.\n• The Customer table contains 5 million rows.\n• All foreign key values are non-null.\nYou need to create a view to denormalize the data into a customer dimension that contains one row per distinct CustomerID value. The solution\nmust minimize query processing time and resources.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\n\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "______  SalesLT.SalesRegion SR on C.SalesRegionID = SR.SalesRegionID",
        "options": [
          "full outer join",
          "inner join",
          "left outer join"
        ],
        "answer": "inner join"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "inner join [SalesLT].[Address] A on  ______",
        "options": [
          "A.AddressID = CA.AddressID",
          "C.AddressID = CA.AddressID",
          "C.CustomerID = A.CustomerID"
        ],
        "answer": "A.AddressID = CA.AddressID"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**inner join — `A.AddressID = CA.AddressID`.**\n\n**Blank 2 — `A.AddressID = CA.AddressID`.** `CustomerAddress` is the bridge table between `Customer` and `Address`; it has already been joined to `Customer` on the line above (`CA.customerid = C.customerid`), so the remaining join is `Address` to that bridge, on **AddressID**.\n- `C.AddressID = CA.AddressID` — `Customer` has no `AddressID`; that is the whole reason the bridge table exists.\n- `C.CustomerID = A.CustomerID` — `Address` has no `CustomerID`, again because the relationship is many-to-many through the bridge.\n\n**Blank 1 — `inner join`, and here the PDF answer key is wrong** (it marks *left outer join*).\n\nThe stem hands you the two facts that decide it:\n- *&quot;Each customer is associated to a **single** SalesRegion value.&quot;*\n- *&quot;**All foreign key values are non-null**.&quot;*\n\nTogether these guarantee that **every** `Customer` row has exactly one matching `SalesRegion` row. When a match is certain, an inner join and a left outer join return **identical rows** — but they are not equally cheap. A left outer join obliges the optimizer to preserve every left row, which constrains join order and join algorithms and blocks some simplifications; an inner join leaves the optimizer free. With a **5-million-row** Customer table and an explicit requirement to *&quot;minimize query processing time and resources&quot;*, the inner join is the answer.\n\nIt is also the consistent choice: the other two joins in the same view are already `inner join`, for the same reason.\n\n*Community:* the three comments that address this (+16, +6, +4) all say inner join, each citing the non-null foreign keys. None defends the key.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "FROM clause plus JOIN (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/from-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Understand star schema — denormalizing dimensions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p379_i1.png, p380_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q162"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p379_i1.png",
      "p380_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p380_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q163",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q163",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a query in Microsoft Power BI Desktop that contains two columns named Order_Date and Shipping_Date.\nYou need to create a column that will calculate the number of days between Order_Date and Shipping_Date for each row.\nWhich Power Query function should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "DateTime.LocalNow"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Duration.Days"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Duration.From"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Date.AddDays"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Subtracting two dates in Power Query yields a **duration** value, and `Duration.Days` extracts the whole number of days from it:\n\n```m\nDuration.Days([Shipping_Date] - [Order_Date])\n```\n\n- **C `Duration.From`** – *converts* a value to a duration. It does not give you a day count, and the subtraction has already produced a duration anyway.\n- **D `Date.AddDays`** – shifts a date forward or back by N days. That is the inverse operation.\n- **A `DateTime.LocalNow`** – returns the current local date and time. Nothing to do with the gap between two columns.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Duration.Days — Power Query M",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/powerquery-m/duration-days?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Duration functions — Power Query M",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/powerquery-m/duration-functions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q163"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q164",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q164",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model.\nYou need to modify object-level security (OLS) for the model.\nWhat should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "the Fabric service"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "ALM Toolkit"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Tabular Editor"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Tabular Editor.** Object-level security is defined **inside model roles**, and Microsoft states the tooling constraint directly: *&quot;Like row-level security (RLS), OLS is also defined within model roles. **Currently, you can&#x27;t create OLS definitions natively in Power BI Desktop.** To create roles on Power BI Desktop semantic models, use external tools such as **Tabular Editor**.&quot;*\n\n- **B Power BI Desktop** – can create roles and RLS DAX filters, but not OLS.\n- **A the Fabric service** – manages role *membership*, not role definitions.\n- **C ALM Toolkit** – compares and deploys differences between models; it does not author security metadata.\n\n*(T1Q93 is the step-by-step version of the same fact.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Object-level security in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/security/service-admin-object-level-security?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q164"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q165",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q165",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains a measure named TotalSalesAmount. TotalSalesAmount returns a sales revenue\namount that is translated into a selected currency.\nYou need to ensure that the value returned by TotalSalesAmount is formatted to use the correct currency symbol.\nWhat should you include in the solution?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a field parameter"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a linguistic schema"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a dynamic format string"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the WINDOW DAX function"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>A **dynamic format string** lets a measure decide its own formatting at evaluation time: *&quot;With dynamic format strings for measures, you can determine how measures appear in visuals by **conditionally applying a format string with a separate DAX formula**.&quot;*\n\nThat is exactly the requirement — the measure already returns a value translated into the selected currency, so the format string must follow the same selection:\n\n```dax\nSWITCH (\n    SELECTEDVALUE ( Currency[Code] ),\n    &quot;USD&quot;, &quot;$#,##0.00&quot;,\n    &quot;EUR&quot;, &quot;€#,##0.00&quot;,\n    &quot;GBP&quot;, &quot;£#,##0.00&quot;,\n    &quot;#,##0.00&quot;\n)\n```\n\nA static format string on the measure could only ever show one symbol.\n\n- **A a field parameter** – lets report users swap which *fields* a visual shows. It does not control formatting.\n- **B a linguistic schema** – vocabulary for Q&amp;A natural-language queries.\n- **D the WINDOW DAX function** – returns a subset of rows from a table for window calculations. Unrelated to formatting.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Dynamic format strings for measures",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-dynamic-format-strings?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q165"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q166",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q166",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You plan to use Fabric to store data.\nYou need to create a data store that supports the following:\n• Writing data by using T-SQL\n• Multi-table transactions\n• Dynamic data masking\nWhich type of data store should you create?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "KQL database"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "warehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "semantic model"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Only the **warehouse** has all three capabilities:\n\n| Requirement | Warehouse | Lakehouse | KQL database |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Write data by using T-SQL** | ✔ | ✘ — its SQL analytics endpoint is **read-only**; you write with Spark | ✘ — KQL, not T-SQL |\n| **Multi-table transactions** | ✔ — full ACID `BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK` across tables | ✘ — Delta transactions are per-table | ✘ |\n| **Dynamic data masking** | ✔ | ✘ | ✘ |\n\nMulti-table transaction support is the clearest discriminator: it is a documented warehouse feature, and Delta Lake (and therefore the lakehouse) commits one table at a time. Dynamic data masking is documented as *&quot;Applies to: SQL analytics endpoint and **Warehouse** in Microsoft Fabric&quot;*.\n\n- **D a semantic model** is not a data store at all — it is an analytics layer over one.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Transactions in Warehouse tables in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/transactions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Dynamic data masking in Fabric data warehousing",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dynamic-data-masking?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Fabric decision guide: choose a data store",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-data-store?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q166"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q167",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q167",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 uses the Pro license mode and contains a semantic model\nnamed Model1.\nYou have an Azure DevOps organization.\nYou need to enable version control for Workspace1. The solution must ensure that Model1 is added to the repository.\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and\narrange them in the correct order.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "1",
        "options": [
          "Connect Workspace1 to a Git provider.",
          "Sync Workspace1 with the repository.",
          "Configure branch policies in Azure DevOps.",
          "Create a deployment pipeline.",
          "Assign Workspace1 to a Fabric capacity."
        ],
        "answer": "Assign Workspace1 to a Fabric capacity."
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "2",
        "options": [
          "Connect Workspace1 to a Git provider.",
          "Sync Workspace1 with the repository.",
          "Configure branch policies in Azure DevOps.",
          "Create a deployment pipeline.",
          "Assign Workspace1 to a Fabric capacity."
        ],
        "answer": "Connect Workspace1 to a Git provider."
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "3",
        "options": [
          "Connect Workspace1 to a Git provider.",
          "Sync Workspace1 with the repository.",
          "Configure branch policies in Azure DevOps.",
          "Create a deployment pipeline.",
          "Assign Workspace1 to a Fabric capacity."
        ],
        "answer": "Sync Workspace1 with the repository."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Assign to a Fabric capacity → Connect to a Git provider → Sync with the repository.**\n\n1. **Assign Workspace1 to a Fabric capacity.** The workspace is on the **Pro** license mode, and Git integration has a hard prerequisite: *&quot;To access the Git integration feature, **you need a Fabric capacity**.&quot;* Nothing else can happen first.\n2. **Connect Workspace1 to a Git provider.** *Workspace settings → Git integration* → choose Azure DevOps, then the organization, project, repository and branch.\n3. **Sync Workspace1 with the repository.** Connecting establishes the link; the initial **commit/sync** is what actually writes the workspace items — including **Model1** — into the repo. Without it the requirement *&quot;ensure that Model1 is added to the repository&quot;* is unmet.\n\nRejected:\n- **Create a deployment pipeline** – deployment pipelines move content **between stages** (CI/CD release). They are not version control, and nothing lands in Azure Repos.\n- **Configure branch policies in Azure DevOps** – a governance nicety (required reviewers, build validation). It does not enable version control or add Model1 to the repo.\n\n*(One commenter claims Pro supports Git integration; the prerequisites page is explicit that a Fabric capacity is required, and the top reply corrects them.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Get started with Git integration — prerequisites",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/git-integration/git-get-started?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p386_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q167"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p386_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p386_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q168",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q168",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Enterprise. Enterprise contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains a\ndate parameter named Date1 that was created in Power Query.\nYou build a deployment pipeline named Enterprise Data that includes two stages named Development and Test. You assign the Enterprise\nworkspace to the Development stage.\nYou need to perform the following actions:\n• Create a workspace named Enterprise [Test] and assign the workspace to the Test stage.\n• Configure a rule that will modify the value of Date1 when changes are deployed to the Test stage.\nWhich two settings should you use? To answer, select the appropriate settings in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "To create the Enterprise [Test] workspace and assign it to the Test stage, use:",
        "options": [
          "The green \"Deploy to this stage or Assign a workspace\" control in the Test stage",
          "The Deploy button in the Development stage",
          "The Settings button on the pipeline toolbar",
          "The Manage Access button on the pipeline toolbar"
        ],
        "answer": "The green \"Deploy to this stage or Assign a workspace\" control in the Test stage"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "To configure a rule that modifies the value of Date1 when changes are deployed to Test, use:",
        "options": [
          "The deployment rules icon on the Test stage header",
          "The pencil (rename stage) icon on the Test stage header",
          "The Deployment history button on the pipeline toolbar",
          "The Publish app link in the Development stage"
        ],
        "answer": "The deployment rules icon on the Test stage header"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Both actions are performed on the **Test** stage, in two different places.\n\n**Creating and assigning the workspace** — the big green control in the empty Test stage reading **&quot;Deploy to this stage or Assign a workspace&quot;**. Picking a workspace from its dropdown and selecting *Assign a workspace* attaches `Enterprise [Test]` to the stage. (The *Deploy* button in the Development stage does the other thing — it copies content once a target workspace already exists.)\n\n**Configuring the parameter rule** — the **deployment rules** icon in the Test stage header (the rightmost of the small icons, next to the pencil). Deployment rules are always set on the **target** stage, and `Date1` is a Power Query parameter, so this is a **parameter rule**: *&quot;Parameter rules … change the value of a parameter&quot;* on deployment. The doc confirms both parameters and data sources support rules, and that the rule is defined on the stage you deploy *into*.\n\n- The **pencil** icon renames the stage — a commenter guesses this one, but renaming has nothing to do with rules.\n- **Deployment history** and **Manage Access** are pipeline-wide and read-only with respect to rules.\n\n*(This item is an image hotspot on the live exam — you click the two spots on the screenshot. It is transcribed here as two dropdowns.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Create deployment rules for Fabric deployment pipelines",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/create-rules?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p388_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q168"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
