  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q169",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q169",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have two Microsoft Power BI queries named Employee and Retired Roles.\nYou need to merge the Employee query with the Retired Roles query. The solution must ensure that duplicate rows in each query are removed.\nWhich column and Join Kind should you use in Power Query Editor? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Column (in the Employee query):",
        "options": [
          "ID",
          "Division",
          "Title Detail",
          "Role",
          "Department",
          "Department Number",
          "Division Number"
        ],
        "answer": "Division"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Join Kind:",
        "options": [
          "Left Outer (all from first, matching from second)",
          "Right Outer (all from second, matching from first)",
          "Full Outer (all rows from both)",
          "Inner (only matching rows)",
          "Left Anti (rows only in first)",
          "Right Anti (rows only in second)"
        ],
        "answer": "Inner (only matching rows)"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Division — Inner (only matching rows).**\n\n**Column — `Division`.** The **Retired Roles** query has only two columns, and the dialog already shows them both selected as match keys: `Role` is marked **1** and `Division` is marked **2**. The Employee side therefore needs the same two columns in the same order, and the one still to be picked is **Division**. The other Employee columns have no counterpart in Retired Roles — note especially **Division Number**, a numeric surrogate that would never match the text values (`Services`) in Retired Roles.\n\n**Join Kind — `Inner`.** Of the six kinds, only **Inner** keeps *nothing but* the rows that match on both sides:\n\n| Join kind | Keeps |\n| --- | --- |\n| Left Outer | all Employee rows, matched or not |\n| Right Outer | all Retired Roles rows |\n| Full Outer | everything from both |\n| **Inner** | **only rows present in both** |\n| Left Anti | only Employee rows with **no** match |\n| Right Anti | only Retired Roles rows with no match |\n\nEvery outer kind would drag through unmatched rows padded with nulls; the anti joins would return the complement of what is wanted.\n\n*Note on the wording:* the stem says the merge must *&quot;ensure that duplicate rows in each query are removed&quot;*, and one commenter (+6) reasonably objects that **no** join kind deduplicates. That is true — the phrasing is loose. What it is reaching for is &quot;drop the rows that do not correspond on both sides&quot;, and Inner is the only option that does that.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Merge queries overview — join kinds",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/merge-queries-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Inner join (Power Query)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/merge-queries-inner?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p390_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q169"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p390_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p391_i1.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q170",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q170",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1.\nYou share DW1 with User1 and assign User1 the default permissions for DW1.\nWhat can User1 do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Build reports by using the default dataset."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Read data from the tables in DW1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Connect to DW1 via the Azure SQL Analytics endpoint."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Read the underlying Parquet files from OneLake."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>Sharing a warehouse without ticking any of the extra boxes grants only the base **Read** permission, and the doc says precisely what that buys: *&quot;If no additional permissions are selected — the shared recipient by default receives **Read** permission, which **only allows the recipient to connect to the SQL analytics endpoint**.&quot;*\n\nConnecting is all it allows. Querying data requires more:\n\n| Capability | Permission needed | Granted by default? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Connect to the SQL endpoint** | Read | **yes** |\n| Read data from the tables | **ReadData** | no |\n| Build reports on the default semantic model | **Build** | no |\n| Read the underlying Parquet in OneLake | **ReadAll** (OneLake/Spark) | no |\n\nSo **C**. Options **B**, **A** and **D** each map to one of the *additional* permissions that were not selected — which is exactly the distinction the question is testing.\n\n*Note:* one third of the community picks **B**, reading &quot;Read permission&quot; as &quot;can read the data&quot;. In Fabric&#x27;s warehouse sharing model those are deliberately different grants.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Share your warehouse and manage permissions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/share-warehouse-manage-permissions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q170"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q171",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q171",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named DBUser. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named\nLakehouse1. DBUser does NOT have access to the tenant.\nYou grant DBUser access to Lakehouse1 as shown in the following exhibit.\n\nUse the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "DBUser can read the data in Lakehouse1 by using the ______.",
        "options": [
          "default semantic model",
          "OneLake endpoint",
          "Tabular Data Stream (TDS) endpoint"
        ],
        "answer": "OneLake endpoint"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "DBUser can query the data in Lakehouse1 by using ______.",
        "options": [
          "a KQL queryset",
          "OneLake file explorer",
          "Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS)"
        ],
        "answer": "OneLake file explorer"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**OneLake endpoint — OneLake file explorer.**\n\nRead the exhibit carefully. Of the three *Additional permissions*, only **Read all Apache Spark** is ticked:\n\n| Additional permission | Ticked? | What it grants |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Read all SQL endpoint data | ☐ | read through the SQL analytics endpoint (T-SQL) |\n| **Read all Apache Spark** | **☑** | *&quot;read all data using Apache Spark and **OneLake APIs**&quot;* |\n| Build reports on the default semantic model | ☐ | build over the default model |\n\n**Blank 1 — the OneLake endpoint.** That is the access path *Read all Apache Spark* opens. The **Tabular Data Stream (TDS)** endpoint is the wire protocol of the **SQL** analytics endpoint — which needs the unticked *Read all SQL endpoint data* — and the **default semantic model** needs the unticked Build permission.\n\n**Blank 2 — OneLake file explorer.** It is the only listed client that reaches the lakehouse over OneLake APIs, so it is the only one DBUser&#x27;s permission actually covers. \n- **SSMS** connects over TDS to the SQL analytics endpoint — blocked, as above.\n- **a KQL queryset** queries a **KQL database / eventhouse**. It cannot target a lakehouse at all, whatever the permissions.\n\n*Note:* the top comment (+5) answers *KQL queryset* for blank 2. A KQL queryset has no connection to a lakehouse, so that cannot be right; the answer key gives OneLake file explorer. (The wording &quot;query&quot; is generous — File Explorer browses and reads files rather than running queries — but it is the only reachable option.)</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lakehouse sharing and permission management",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-sharing?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "OneLake file explorer",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-file-explorer?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p396_i1.png, p396_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q171"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p396_i1.png",
      "p396_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p397_i1.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q172",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q172",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1.\nYou have three groups named Group1, Group2, and Group3.\nYou need to assign a workspace role to each group. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege and meet the following requirements:\n• Group1 must be able to write data to Workspace1, but be unable to add members to Workspace1.\n• Group2 must be able to configure and maintain the settings of Workspace1.\n• Group3 must be able to write data and add members to Workspace1, but be unable to delete Workspace1.\nWhich workspace role should you assign to each group? To answer, drag the appropriate roles to the correct groups. Each role may be used once,\nmore than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Group1",
        "options": [
          "Admin",
          "Contributor",
          "Member",
          "Viewer"
        ],
        "answer": "Contributor"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Group2",
        "options": [
          "Admin",
          "Contributor",
          "Member",
          "Viewer"
        ],
        "answer": "Admin"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Group3",
        "options": [
          "Admin",
          "Contributor",
          "Member",
          "Viewer"
        ],
        "answer": "Member"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Group1 → Contributor, Group2 → Admin, Group3 → Member.** Fabric has exactly four workspace roles, strictly nested: **Admin ⊃ Member ⊃ Contributor ⊃ Viewer**. Work the requirements from the bottom up and each group lands on the *smallest* role that still satisfies it.\n\n| Capability | Viewer | Contributor | Member | Admin |\n|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n| Read items / view reports | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| **Create and write items** (write data) | ✘ | **✔** | ✔ | ✔ |\n| **Add members** (grant Member/Contributor/Viewer) | ✘ | ✘ | **✔** | ✔ |\n| Add **Admins** | ✘ | ✘ | ✘ | ✔ |\n| **Update/​delete the workspace**, manage its settings | ✘ | ✘ | ✘ | **✔** |\n\n**Group1 — writes data, cannot add members → Contributor.** Contributor is the first role that can write, and it is the last one that still cannot grant access. Member would satisfy the write half but violates the &quot;unable to add members&quot; half.\n\n**Group2 — configures and maintains the workspace settings → Admin.** Updating and deleting the workspace, and managing its settings and access, are Admin-only. Nothing smaller works, so least privilege does not constrain this one.\n\n**Group3 — writes data and adds members, but cannot delete the workspace → Member.** This is the diagnostic row of the table: Member is defined precisely as &quot;can add other people with lower permissions&quot; *without* the workspace-lifecycle rights. Admin would also let them delete Workspace1, which the requirement forbids.\n\n**Viewer** is unused — every group has to write or administer something, and Viewer can do neither.\n\n*Watch the trap in Group1 vs Group3:* both &quot;write data&quot;, and the only thing separating them is whether they may add members. That single clause is what forces Contributor for one and Member for the other.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Give users access to workspaces (Power BI)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/collaborate-share/service-give-access-new-workspaces?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
