  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q173",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q173",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant named Tenant1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nYou need to add data to Lakehouse1 from a CSV file in an Azure Storage account outside of Fabric. The solution must minimize development\neffort.\nWhat should you use to add the data?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "copy job"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "shortcut"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "pipeline"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Dataflow Gen2"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**A — copy job.** The verb in the stem is *&quot;add data **to** Lakehouse1&quot;*, and only a copy actually lands bytes in the lakehouse.\n\n- **B shortcut** ✘ — a shortcut is a **reference**, not a copy: *&quot;Shortcuts are objects in OneLake that point to other storage locations… the data is not copied.&quot;* Lakehouse1 would *show* the CSV, but the data still lives in (and is still served from) the external storage account. This is the 48% answer, and it is a genuinely reasonable reading if you take &quot;add data&quot; to mean &quot;make the data available&quot; — but the exam consistently treats shortcut as *virtualize* and copy as *add*.\n- **C pipeline** ✘ — a pipeline *can* do it via a Copy activity, but that is more development effort than a copy job (you build and orchestrate the pipeline yourself). The stem&#x27;s tiebreaker is *&quot;minimize development effort&quot;*.\n- **D Dataflow Gen2** ✘ — same objection, plus it is a transformation tool. You would author Power Query steps to move a file that needs no transformation.\n\n**Copy job** is the purpose-built, wizard-driven item for exactly this: pick a source, pick a destination, choose full or incremental, done — *&quot;a simplified experience… without needing to create a pipeline&quot;*. Among the three options that genuinely copy, it is the one with the least development effort.\n\n*Rule of thumb for this family of questions:* **&quot;add / ingest / load / copy&quot; ⇒ Copy job or Copy activity. &quot;access / reference / without duplicating / keep in place&quot; ⇒ shortcut.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "What is Copy job in Data Factory",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/what-is-copy-job?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "OneLake shortcuts",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-shortcuts?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Decision guide: copy activity, copy job, dataflow, or Spark",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-pipeline-dataflow-spark?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
