  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q174",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q174",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Table1. Table1 contains the following data.\nYou need to create a T-SQL statement that meets the following requirements:\n• Outputs the item name of each item and returns a null value if the item name is longer than 20 characters\n• Outputs the PurchaseDate value in the format of МММ dd, yy\nHow should you complete the statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "SELECT item_id as ItemId, [answer choice] as ItemName",
        "options": [
          ",convert(varchar(20), item_name)",
          ",convert(varchar(max), item_name)",
          ",try_cast(item_name as varchar(20))"
        ],
        "answer": ",try_cast(item_name as varchar(20))"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "[answer choice] as PurchaseDate",
        "options": [
          ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 7)",
          ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 109)",
          ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 112)"
        ],
        "answer": ",convert(varchar, purchase_date, 7)"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`,try_cast(item_name as varchar(20))`** and **`,convert(varchar, purchase_date, 7)`**.\n\n### Blank 1 — &quot;returns a **null** value if the item name is longer than 20 characters&quot;\n\nThe word *null* decides it. The `TRY_` family is the only thing in T-SQL that converts **or returns NULL instead of erroring**:\n\n| Expression | Name longer than 20 chars |\n|---|---|\n| `CONVERT(varchar(20), item_name)` | **Silently truncates** to 20 characters — no error, no NULL, and you have quietly corrupted the data |\n| `CONVERT(varchar(max), item_name)` | Returns the full name — no truncation *and* no NULL |\n| **`TRY_CAST(item_name AS varchar(20))`** | **NULL** ✔ |\n\nNote the subtlety that makes this question worth its point: `CAST`/`CONVERT` to a *shorter* character type does **not** raise an error the way an overflowing numeric conversion does — it truncates. `TRY_CAST`, however, treats the truncation as a failed conversion and yields `NULL`, which is precisely the requested behaviour.\n\n### Blank 2 — output format `MMM dd, yy`\n\nThis is the `CONVERT` style-number table. Only style **7** produces `Apr 20, 22`:\n\n| Style | Output | Matches `MMM dd, yy`? |\n|:-:|---|:-:|\n| **7** | `Mon dd, yy` → `Apr 20, 22` | **✔** |\n| 109 | `mon dd yyyy hh:mi:ss:mmmAM` → `Apr 20 2022 10:00:00:000AM` | ✘ four-digit year, plus a time |\n| 112 | `yyyymmdd` → `20220420` | ✘ ISO, no month name |\n\nUseful shortcuts for the styles that keep reappearing: **1** = `mm/dd/yy`, **101** = `mm/dd/yyyy`, **103** = `dd/mm/yyyy` (British/French), **112** = `yyyymmdd` (ISO, sort-safe), **120** = `yyyy-mm-dd hh:mi:ss` (ODBC canonical). The general pattern: **styles under 100 use a two-digit year; add 100 for the four-digit-year variant.** Style 7 and style 107 are the same layout, differing only in `yy` vs `yyyy` — which is exactly why the requirement spells out `yy`.\n\n*(The top-voted comment&#x27;s complaint is fair — nobody memorises these. But `MMM dd, yy` narrows it to a two-digit-year style with a month abbreviation, and 109/112 are visibly neither.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "TRY_CAST (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/try-cast-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CAST and CONVERT — date and time styles",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/cast-and-convert-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#date-and-time-styles"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p403_i1.png, p403_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q174"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p403_i1.png",
      "p403_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p403_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q175",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q175",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse named Warehouse1 that contains a table named dbo.Product.\ndbo.Product contains the following columns.\nYou need to use a T-SQL query to add a column named PriceRange to dbo.Product. The column must categorize each product based on UnitPrice.\nThe solution must meet the following requirements:\n• If UnitPrice is 0, PriceRange is &quot;Not for resale&quot;.\n• If UnitPrice is less than 50, PriceRange is &quot;Under $50&quot;.\n• If UnitPrice is between 50 and 250, PriceRange is &quot;Under $250“.\n• In all other instances, PriceRange is &quot;$250+&quot;.\nHow should you complete the query? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once,\nor not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "PriceRange = [1]",
        "options": [
          "BEGIN",
          "CASE",
          "ELSE",
          "END",
          "IF",
          "WHILE"
        ],
        "answer": "CASE"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "[2] '$250+'",
        "options": [
          "BEGIN",
          "CASE",
          "ELSE",
          "END",
          "IF",
          "WHILE"
        ],
        "answer": "ELSE"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "[3]  (closes the expression, before FROM)",
        "options": [
          "BEGIN",
          "CASE",
          "ELSE",
          "END",
          "IF",
          "WHILE"
        ],
        "answer": "END"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**CASE … ELSE … END.** The finished query is:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    Item,\n    UnitPrice,\n    PriceRange = CASE\n        WHEN UnitPrice = 0 THEN &#x27;Not for resale&#x27;\n        WHEN UnitPrice &lt; 50 THEN &#x27;Under $50&#x27;\n        WHEN UnitPrice &gt;= 50 AND UnitPrice &lt; 250 THEN &#x27;Under $250&#x27;\n        ELSE &#x27;$250+&#x27;\n    END\nFROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[Product]\n```\n\nThree things are being tested:\n\n**1. `CASE`, not `IF`.** This is the one that catches people. `IF` in T-SQL is a **statement** — it controls which statements run; it cannot produce a value inside a `SELECT` list. `CASE` is an **expression** — it evaluates to a single scalar per row, which is exactly what a computed column needs. (`IIF()` would also work as an expression, but it is binary and is not on the list.)\n\n**2. `ELSE` for &quot;in all other instances&quot;.** The requirement&#x27;s wording is a direct translation of the `ELSE` branch. Leave `ELSE` out and unmatched rows get `NULL` rather than `&#x27;$250+&#x27;`.\n\n**3. `END` closes the expression.** Every `CASE` must be terminated by `END` before the query continues to `FROM`. Note the asymmetry worth remembering: `CASE` pairs with `END`, but **not** with `BEGIN` — `BEGIN…END` delimits a *statement block*, a completely different construct. `BEGIN`, `IF`, and `WHILE` are all procedural statement keywords and none of them belongs inside a `SELECT` list.\n\n*Also note the `WHEN` ordering, even though it is pre-filled:* the branches are evaluated **top to bottom, first match wins**. `WHEN UnitPrice = 0` has to come before `WHEN UnitPrice &lt; 50`, or a price of 0 would be caught by the second branch and labelled `&#x27;Under $50&#x27;`.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "CASE (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/case-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "IF...ELSE (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/if-else-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "BEGIN...END (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/language-elements/begin-end-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p405_i1.png, p405_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q175"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p405_i1.png",
      "p405_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p405_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q176",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q176",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou are creating a Fabric Data Factory pipeline.\nYou have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month.\nYou need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream\nactivities of the pipeline.\nWhich type of activity should you add?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Append variable"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Script"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Stored procedure"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Get metadata"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**B — Script.** The trap is that option C is named after the thing you want to do, and is still wrong.\n\nThe requirement that decides it is *&quot;the returned values must be available to the downstream activities&quot;*. Microsoft states the limitation plainly: **&quot;When the stored procedure has Output parameters, instead of using stored procedure activity, use lookup activity and Script activity. Stored procedure activity does not support calling SPs with Output parameter yet.&quot;**\n\n- **C Stored procedure** ✘ — it *executes* the procedure perfectly well, but it is fire-and-forget. It surfaces no result set and no output parameters to the pipeline, so nothing downstream can read the customer count or the average sales.\n- **B Script** ✔ — runs SQL (including `EXEC dbo.MyProc`) against the warehouse **and** exposes the results in its activity output as `resultSets`, referenceable downstream as `@activity(&#x27;Script1&#x27;).output.resultSets[0].rows[0].ColumnName`.\n- **A Append variable** ✘ — appends a value to an array variable. Executes nothing.\n- **D Get metadata** ✘ — returns metadata about files/folders/tables (name, size, structure). Executes nothing.\n\n**Lookup** is the other activity that would satisfy both halves, and it is the usual answer when it appears (see T1Q83, T1Q114, T1Q122, T1Q128, T1Q160). Here it is deliberately **not** offered, which forces Script — so read the option list before pattern-matching to Lookup.\n\n| Activity | Executes the proc? | Returns values downstream? |\n|---|:-:|:-:|\n| Stored procedure | ✔ | **✘** |\n| **Script** | ✔ | **✔** |\n| Lookup *(not offered here)* | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Append variable / Get metadata | ✘ | — |</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Script activity in Data Factory for Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/script-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Stored procedure activity in Data Factory for Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/stored-procedure-activity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Transform data by using a stored procedure activity",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/data-factory/transform-data-using-stored-procedure?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q176"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q177",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q177",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains the following data.\nYou need to create a T-SQL statement that will denormalize the tables and include the ContractType and StartDate attributes in the results. The\nsolution must meet the following requirements:\n• Include attributes from matching rows in the Contract table.\n• Ensure that all the rows from the Employee table are preserved.\n• Return the total number of employees per contract type for all the contract types that have more than two employees.\nHow should you complete the statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "FROM Employee AS e [answer choice] Contract AS c on c.EmployeeID = e.EmployeeID)",
        "options": [
          "CROSS JOIN",
          "INNER JOIN",
          "LEFT OUTER JOIN",
          "RIGHT OUTER JOIN"
        ],
        "answer": "LEFT OUTER JOIN"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "GROUP BY ContractType [answer choice] COUNT(DISTINCT EmployeeID) > 2",
        "options": [
          "CONTAINS",
          "HAVING",
          "LIMIT",
          "WHERE"
        ],
        "answer": "HAVING"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**LEFT OUTER JOIN** and **HAVING**.\n\n### Blank 1 — the join\n\nTwo requirements pull in opposite directions and the join type has to satisfy both:\n\n- *&quot;Include attributes from matching rows in the Contract table&quot;* — so contract columns come along where a match exists.\n- *&quot;Ensure that **all** the rows from the Employee table are preserved&quot;* — so an employee with no contract must still appear.\n\nThe second requirement is the decisive one, and `Employee` is on the **left** of the join (`FROM Employee AS e … Contract AS c`):\n\n| Join | Employees with no contract | Verdict |\n|---|---|:-:|\n| INNER JOIN | **dropped** | ✘ violates &quot;all rows preserved&quot; |\n| RIGHT OUTER JOIN | **dropped** (preserves *Contract* instead) | ✘ right table is the wrong one |\n| CROSS JOIN | Cartesian product — every employee × every contract; the `ON` clause is not even legal | ✘ |\n| **LEFT OUTER JOIN** | kept, with `NULL` contract columns | **✔** |\n\n*Read the table order, not the option names.* `RIGHT OUTER JOIN` would be correct had the query been written `FROM Contract AS c … Employee AS e` — swapping the operands is exactly how this distractor is built.\n\n### Blank 2 — the filter\n\n*&quot;…for all the contract types that have **more than two employees**&quot;* filters on an **aggregate** (`COUNT(DISTINCT EmployeeID) &gt; 2`), and the placement in the query is immediately after `GROUP BY`:\n\n- **HAVING** ✔ — filters **groups, after aggregation**. The only clause that may reference `COUNT(...)`.\n- **WHERE** ✘ — filters **rows, before aggregation**, and cannot contain an aggregate function; `WHERE COUNT(...) &gt; 2` is a syntax error.\n- **LIMIT** ✘ — MySQL/PostgreSQL syntax. T-SQL uses `TOP` or `OFFSET…FETCH`, and it caps row counts rather than filtering on a condition.\n- **CONTAINS** ✘ — full-text search predicate for matching words inside character columns.\n\n**WHERE before GROUP BY, HAVING after** is the one-line rule; the appearance of an aggregate inside the condition settles it every time.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "FROM clause plus JOIN, APPLY, PIVOT (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/from-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SELECT — HAVING (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-having-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "T-SQL surface area in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/tsql-surface-area?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p409_i1.png, p409_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q177"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p409_i1.png",
      "p409_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p410_i1.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q178",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q178",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a KQL database that contains a table named Readings.\nYou need to query Readings and return the results shown in the following table.\nHow should you complete the query? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "[answer choice] PrevMeterReading = prev(MeterReading), PrevTimestamp = prev(Datetime)",
        "options": [
          "| evaluate",
          "| extend",
          "| lookup",
          "| project",
          "| summarize"
        ],
        "answer": "| extend"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "[answer choice] City, Area, MeterReading, Datetime, PrevMeterReading, PrevDatetime",
        "options": [
          "| evaluate",
          "| extend",
          "| lookup",
          "| project",
          "| summarize"
        ],
        "answer": "| project"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`| extend` then `| project`.** The finished KQL query:\n\n```kusto\nReadings\n| filter City == &quot;Copenhagen&quot;\n| sort by Datetime\n| extend PrevMeterReading = prev(MeterReading), PrevTimestamp = prev(Datetime)\n| project City, Area, MeterReading, Datetime, PrevMeterReading, PrevDatetime\n```\n\nMatch each operator to what the target table shows:\n\n**Blank 1 — `extend`: adds calculated columns, keeps everything else.** The expected output still has the original `City`, `Area`, `MeterReading` and `Datetime` columns *plus* the two new ones, so the operator must be additive. That is `extend` by definition: *&quot;Create calculated columns and append them to the result set.&quot;* Using `project` here would work syntactically but is the wrong shape of operator for the step — and `summarize` would collapse the rows, destroying the row-by-row `prev()` relationship.\n\nThe `prev()` function is why `| sort by Datetime` precedes it: `prev()` reads the value from the **previous row in serialized order**, so the rows have to be ordered first. Notice the first output row has `None` for both Prev columns — there is no previous row, so `prev()` returns null.\n\n**Blank 2 — `project`: selects and orders the final column list.** A bare list of existing column names with no assignments is a projection — KQL&#x27;s equivalent of SQL&#x27;s `SELECT` list. It both restricts the output to those columns and fixes their order.\n\nThe rejected operators:\n\n| Operator | What it actually does |\n|---|---|\n| `summarize` | **Aggregates** rows into groups (`count()`, `avg()`, `by …`). Would not preserve one row per reading. |\n| `lookup` | **Joins** in columns from a second (dimension) table. There is no second table here. |\n| `evaluate` | Invokes a **plug-in** (`bag_unpack`, `pivot`, `narrow`, …). Unrelated. |\n\n**The SQL translation that makes this stick: `extend` ≈ adding a computed column, `project` ≈ `SELECT` list, `summarize` ≈ `GROUP BY`, `lookup` ≈ `LEFT JOIN`, `filter`/`where` ≈ `WHERE`.**\n\n*(Cosmetic bug in the exam item: the `extend` line names the column `PrevTimestamp` while the `project` line asks for `PrevDatetime`. Run as written this would error — it does not affect which operators are correct.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "extend operator (KQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/extend-operator?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "project operator (KQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/project-operator?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "prev() (KQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/prev-function?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SQL to Kusto Query Language cheat sheet",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/sql-cheat-sheet?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
