  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q180",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q180",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1.\nWorkspace1 contains multiple semantic models, including a model named Model1. Model1 is updated by using an XMLA endpoint.\nYou need to increase the speed of the write operations of the XMLA endpoint.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Delete any unused semantic models from Workspace1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Select Large semantic model storage format for Workspace1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Configure Model 1 to use the Direct Lake storage format."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Delete any unused columns from Model1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**B — Select Large semantic model storage format for Workspace1.** Microsoft documents this under the heading *&quot;Optimize semantic models for write operations by enabling large models&quot;*: with the large-model format enabled, **write operations through the XMLA endpoint can be significantly faster**, because the model is stored in premium files rather than as a single in-memory blob that must be rewritten wholesale.\n\nEverything else changes the wrong variable:\n\n- **A Delete unused semantic models** ✘ — frees capacity memory. It has no effect on the throughput of a write to *this* model.\n- **C Direct Lake storage format** ✘ — Direct Lake is about **read** performance (query straight off Delta tables in OneLake, no import refresh). It is also the wrong direction here: the stem says Model1 *is updated by using an XMLA endpoint*, which is an import-model workflow.\n- **D Delete unused columns from Model1** ✘ — genuinely good modelling hygiene and it does shrink the model, but it is a one-off data reduction, not a change to how writes are processed. The exam wants the setting that targets XMLA write speed directly.\n\nSetting it: **Workspace settings → Semantic models → Large semantic model storage format**, or tenant-wide via the admin portal. Note it is a **workspace-level** (or per-model) setting, which is why the correct option names Workspace1 rather than Model1.\n\n*(Same underlying fact as T1Q141 — recognise &quot;XMLA write performance ⇒ large semantic model storage format&quot;.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint — optimize for write operations",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#optimize-semantic-models-for-write-operations-by-enabling-large-models"
      },
      {
        "title": "Large semantic models in Power BI Premium",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-large-models?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
