  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q181",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q181",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1.\nYou need to create a semantic model named Model1 and publish Model1 to Workspace1. The solution must meet the following requirements:\n• Can revert to previous versions of Model1 as required.\n• Identifies differences between saved versions of Model1.\n• Uses Microsoft Power Bl=I Desktop to publish to Workspace1.\n• Can edit item definition files by using Microsoft Visual Studio Code.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Enable Git integration for Workspace1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Save Model1 in Power BI Desktop as a PBIT file."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Enable users to edit data models in the Power BI service."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Save Model1 in Power BI Desktop as a PBIP file."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**A + D — enable Git integration for Workspace1, and save Model1 as a PBIP file.** The four requirements split cleanly into two pairs, one per answer.\n\n**A covers the versioning pair.**\n- *&quot;Can revert to previous versions&quot;* → Git history; `git revert` / restore a prior commit, then sync the workspace.\n- *&quot;Identifies differences between saved versions&quot;* → **diffs**. This is the requirement that rules out everything except source control: Fabric Git integration shows changed items and, because the definitions are plain text, line-level diffs in Git.\n\n**D covers the file-format pair.**\n- *&quot;Can edit item definition files by using Visual Studio Code&quot;* → **PBIP** (Power BI Project) saves the report and semantic model as a **folder of plain-text files** — `definition/` with TMDL or `model.bim`, `.pbir`, etc. — which VS Code can edit and Git can diff. A `.pbix` is an opaque binary; a diff of it is meaningless.\n- *&quot;Uses Power BI Desktop to publish to Workspace1&quot;* → PBIP is saved and published from Desktop as normal.\n\nThe two work together: PBIP produces the text files, Git integration versions them. Neither alone satisfies all four bullets — which is why this is a two-answer question.\n\n**Why not B or C:**\n- **B Save as a PBIT file** ✘ — the 14% answer. A `.pbit` **template** is still a single binary file; it merely strips the data. It gives you no text definitions for VS Code and no meaningful diffs. PBIT is for redistributing a model shell to other authors, not for source control.\n- **C Enable users to edit data models in the Power BI service** ✘ — browser-based model editing. It is the *opposite* of the stated workflow (which is explicitly Desktop + VS Code) and contributes nothing to versioning or diffing.\n\n*Recognition rule:* **&quot;edit definition files in VS Code / text-based / diffable&quot; ⇒ PBIP. &quot;version history, revert, compare versions&quot; ⇒ Git integration.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Power BI Desktop projects (PBIP)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Get started with Git integration (Fabric)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/git-integration/git-get-started?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Power BI Desktop project semantic model folder",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/developer/projects/projects-dataset?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q181"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q182",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q182",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that is assigned to a newly created Fabric capacity named Capacity1.\nYou create a semantic model named Model1 and deploy Model1 to Workspace1.\nYou need to publish changes to Model1 directly from Tabular Editor.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "For Workspace1, enable Git integration."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "For Model1, enable external sharing."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "For Workspace1, create a managed private endpoint."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "For Capacity1, set XMLA Endpoint to Read Write."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**D — For Capacity1, set XMLA Endpoint to Read Write.** External tools talk to a semantic model over the **XMLA endpoint**, and Microsoft spells out the requirement for this exact tool: *&quot;**Tabular Editor** — … Requires XMLA **read-only** for query operations. Requires **read-write** for metadata operations.&quot;* Publishing changes *is* a metadata write, so read-only is not enough.\n\nThe detail that makes the question worth asking: on a **newly created capacity the XMLA endpoint defaults to `Read Only`**, and the setting lives at the **capacity** level (Admin portal → Capacity settings → Power BI workloads → Semantic models → XMLA Endpoint), **not** on the workspace or the model. The stem plants both clues — &quot;newly created Fabric capacity&quot; and &quot;directly from Tabular Editor&quot;.\n\n- **A Enable Git integration** ✘ — a deployment/source-control path (commit the change, sync the workspace). It is a perfectly good way to get changes into a workspace, but it is not &quot;**directly** from Tabular Editor&quot;, and Tabular Editor still cannot connect without the endpoint. This is the 11% answer.\n- **B Enable external sharing for Model1** ✘ — governs sharing content with users **outside your organisation** (B2B). Nothing to do with tool connectivity.\n- **C Create a managed private endpoint** ✘ — lets Fabric reach **into** a network-isolated *data source*. It is inbound-to-the-source networking, the opposite direction from a client tool connecting to the model.\n\n*Same fact family as T1Q180:* XMLA is the single door used by Tabular Editor, SSMS, DAX Studio, ALM Toolkit, SQL Server Profiler and Analysis Services deployment. **Read anything ⇒ Read Only suffices. Write anything ⇒ Read Write.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint — client applications and tools",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#client-applications-and-tools"
      },
      {
        "title": "Enable XMLA read-write",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#enable-xmla-read-write"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q182"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q183",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q183",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are creating a report and a semantic model in Microsoft Power BI Desktop.\nThe Value measure has the expression shown in the following exhibit.\n\nUse the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "A [answer choice] was added to the Value measure.",
        "options": [
          "calculation group",
          "data category",
          "dynamic format string",
          "synonym"
        ],
        "answer": "dynamic format string"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "The Value measure can return values formatted as [answer choice].",
        "options": [
          "percentages only",
          "percentages or decimals",
          "whole numbers only",
          "percentages or whole numbers"
        ],
        "answer": "percentages or whole numbers"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**A dynamic format string was added**, and the measure can return **percentages or whole numbers**.\n\n### Blank 1 — read the ribbon, not the DAX\n\nThe exhibit shows the measure ribbon with **Format** set to **`Dynamic`**, and the formula bar switched to the **Format** dropdown (left of the expression) rather than the measure expression. That combination is the definition of a **dynamic format string**: a second DAX expression attached to the measure that returns the *format string* to apply, evaluated in the current filter context.\n\nThe expression being edited is the format string itself:\n\n```dax\nSWITCH(SELECTEDVALUE(Metric[Metric]),\n    &quot;# of Customers&quot;, &quot;#&quot;,\n    &quot;Gross Margin %&quot;, &quot;0.00%&quot;,\n    &quot;#,###&quot;)\n```\n\n- **calculation group** ✘ — the plausible distractor, and the choice the highly-voted comment argues for. Dynamic format strings *can* be defined on a calculation item, but the exhibit shows the **Format → Dynamic** property of a single measure named `Value` on home table `Fact`, with no calculation group in sight. The question also asks what was added **to the Value measure** — a calculation group is a separate model object, not something added to a measure. Since Power BI Desktop shipped dynamic format strings **for measures** (May 2023 preview, GA later), this is exactly what that UI is.\n- **data category** ✘ — tags a column as Address / Web URL / Image URL etc. for maps and visuals. Note the exhibit shows Data category = **Uncategorized**, so this one is visibly ruled out on screen.\n- **synonym** ✘ — an alternate name for Q&amp;A natural-language queries. No formatting role at all.\n\n### Blank 2 — enumerate what the SWITCH can return\n\nTake each branch of the format string literally:\n\n| Selected metric | Format string returned | Renders as |\n|---|---|---|\n| `# of Customers` | `&quot;#&quot;` | **whole number**, no thousands separator → `85` |\n| `Gross Margin %` | `&quot;0.00%&quot;` | **percentage** → `12.34%` |\n| anything else (Gross Margin, Total COGS, Total Revenue) | `&quot;#,###&quot;` | **whole number** with thousands separator → `1,234` |\n\nSo across all branches the measure yields **percentages or whole numbers** — and never a decimal, because no branch&#x27;s format string contains a decimal place except the `%` one. That kills **&quot;percentages or decimals&quot;**: `0.00%` displays two decimals *of a percentage*, which is still a percentage, not a decimal number. **&quot;Percentages only&quot;** and **&quot;whole numbers only&quot;** each ignore two of the three branches.\n\n*The technique for this whole question type:* read the `SWITCH` branch by branch and classify each returned **format string**, not each underlying value. `#` and `#,###` are integer masks; `0.00%` is a percentage mask; `0.00` would have been a decimal mask and is absent.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Dynamic format strings for measures (Power BI)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-dynamic-format-strings?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Custom format strings in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-custom-format-strings?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SWITCH function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/switch-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SELECTEDVALUE function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/selectedvalue-function?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p419_i1.png, p419_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q183"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p419_i1.png",
