  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q184",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q184",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nYou have the data sources shown in the following table.\nYou need to ingest data from both data sources into Lakehouse1. The solution must minimize maintenance and development time.\nWhat should you use to ingest the data into each data source? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct data sources. Each option\nmay be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Datasource1 — Azure SQL managed instance, CDC on all tables, daily load of 500,000+ rows / 20 GB",
        "options": [
          "A Dataflow Gen1",
          "A Dataflow Gen2",
          "A notebook",
          "A pipeline with a Copy activity",
          "An eventstream"
        ],
        "answer": "A pipeline with a Copy activity"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Datasource2 — manufacturing machine data streamed to an Azure event hub",
        "options": [
          "A Dataflow Gen1",
          "A Dataflow Gen2",
          "A notebook",
          "A pipeline with a Copy activity",
          "An eventstream"
        ],
        "answer": "An eventstream"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Datasource1 → A pipeline with a Copy activity. Datasource2 → An eventstream.**\n\n&gt; ⚠️ **Flagged: this overrides the PDF answer key.** The key marks Datasource1 as *A Dataflow Gen2*. Every commenter in the discussion says *pipeline with a Copy activity* (+11 top-voted, plus two more, **no dissent**), and Microsoft&#x27;s own decision guide backs them. Reasoning below — decide for yourself before exam day.\n\n### Datasource2 — An eventstream (uncontested)\n\n*&quot;Data that is **streamed to an Azure event hub**&quot;* is the textbook Eventstream trigger phrase. Eventstream connects natively to Azure Event Hubs, needs no schedule, and lands the data in a lakehouse with no code. Every alternative is a **batch, scheduled** tool that would need you to poll the hub.\n\n### Datasource1 — why a pipeline with a Copy activity, not a Dataflow Gen2\n\nThe stem plants a volume figure for a reason: **&gt;500,000 rows and 20 GB per day**. Microsoft&#x27;s decision guide draws exactly this line:\n\n- Copy activity — *&quot;pipelines provide **high scale data ingestion that can move petabyte-scale data**. Copy activity is the best low-code and no-code choice to move petabytes of data to lakehouses and warehouses from varieties of sources.&quot;*\n- Dataflow Gen2 — chosen in the guide&#x27;s scenario where *&quot;the data volume is in the **low to medium range** to achieve desired performance.&quot;* Its value is the 300+ Power Query transformations — and this scenario asks for **no transformation at all**, just ingestion.\n\n| | Dataflow Gen2 | Pipeline + Copy activity |\n|---|---|---|\n| Positioned for | low–medium volume, **transformation** | **high-volume raw movement**, lightweight transform |\n| 20 GB/day | Power Query engine, mashup overhead | designed for it |\n| Development effort | no-code Power Query | no-code wizard/canvas — **equally low** |\n| Transformations needed here | none | none |\n\nBoth are no-code, so *&quot;minimize maintenance and development time&quot;* does **not** favour the dataflow; it only rules out **a notebook** (hand-written Spark) and **a Dataflow Gen1** (legacy, lakehouse destination not supported the same way, superseded by Gen2).\n\n**The case for the key&#x27;s answer,** so you can judge it: if you read *&quot;minimize maintenance and development time&quot;* as the *only* tiebreaker and treat the volume figure as scenery, Dataflow Gen2 is the most hands-off option in the list, and its **incremental refresh** feature is declarative. That is a defensible reading — it just requires ignoring the sentence that exists purely to describe scale.\n\n**Aside — what Microsoft would actually recommend.** Scenario 5 of the decision guide is *this exact case*: *&quot;ingesting incremental data from an on-premises SQL Server… CDC enabled on key tables… select multiple native CDC enabled source tables, perform an initial full load, automatically switch to incremental data loads based on CDC&quot;* → **Copy job**. Copy job is not offered here, and a pipeline with a Copy activity is its nearest relative (same connectors, same engine, slightly more orchestration to author).\n\n*Recognition rules for this whole family:* **event hub / Kafka / real-time ⇒ Eventstream. Large batch, little or no transformation ⇒ Copy activity or Copy job. Lots of Power Query transformation at modest volume ⇒ Dataflow Gen2. Custom logic or ML ⇒ notebook.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Fabric decision guide: copy activity, copy job, dataflow, Eventstream, or Spark",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-pipeline-dataflow-spark?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "What is Copy job in Data Factory",
