  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q187",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q187",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains an eventstream named Eventstream1.\nEventstream1 reads data from an Azure event hub named Eventhub1.\nEventhub1 contains the following columns.\nYou need to add a continuous percentile calculation to the Payload column. The solution must minimize development effort.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Add a KQL queryset to Workspace1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Add a Group by transformation to Eventstream1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Add a Manage fields transformation to Eventstream1."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Add an Aggregate transformation to Eventstream1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**D — Add an Aggregate transformation to Eventstream1.**\n\nTwo constraints have to be satisfied at once: the calculation must be **continuous** (it runs on the stream as events arrive) and the solution must **minimize development effort** (no code). The Eventstream **Aggregate** transformation is the no-code operator that applies an aggregate function — `SUM`, `AVG`, `MIN`, `MAX`, `COUNT`, and the **percentile** functions — to a column over a time window, right on the event processor canvas.\n\nThe distractors, each failing a different constraint:\n\n- **A Add a KQL queryset to Workspace1** ✘ — the 20% answer. KQL absolutely can compute percentiles (`percentile()`, `percentiles_array()`, `percentilew()`), and it is the right tool for *analysing data already landed in an eventhouse*. But a queryset is **query-time, on-demand** — it is not a continuous calculation applied to the stream — and it means writing KQL rather than clicking a transformation. It fails both the &quot;continuous&quot; and the &quot;minimize development effort&quot; tests.\n- **B Add a Group by transformation** ✘ — this is the near-miss. Group by does aggregate over a window, but it is oriented around partitioning events by a key; the Aggregate operator is the one Microsoft documents for applying an aggregate function to a column. Where the exam offers both, &quot;compute a statistical function on one column&quot; ⇒ **Aggregate**.\n- **C Add a Manage fields transformation** ✘ — renames, removes, retypes and adds fields. Pure schema shaping, zero computation.\n\n*Note the `Payload` column is typed **Dynamic**.* That is deliberate flavour text — the aggregate still needs a numeric value inside the payload — but it does not change which operator you reach for.\n\n**Rule: &quot;transform the stream itself ⇒ an Eventstream transformation. Analyse what already landed ⇒ a KQL queryset.&quot;**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Add transformations to eventstreams",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/real-time-intelligence/event-streams/route-events-based-on-content?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Eventstream transformation operators",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/real-time-intelligence/event-streams/process-events-using-event-processor-editor?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "percentiles() (KQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/percentiles-aggregation-function?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p426_i1.png"
    ],
