  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q188",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q188",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Table1. Table1 contains three columns named SalesAmount, ProductCategory, and\nTransactionDate.\nYou need to create a Microsoft Power BI query that will calculate the total sales amount of each product category for transactions that occurred\nduring the last quarter.\nWhich two actions should you perform in the visual query editor? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "For the TransactionDate column, select Remove duplicates."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "For the TransactionDate column, select Keep top rows and set Number of rows to 90."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "For the TransactionDate column, select Filter rows and set the value to last quarter."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "For the ProductCategory column, select Group by and sum the SalesAmount column."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "For the SalesAmount column, select Transformation number column - Standard, select Add, and then set the value to 90."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "C",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**C + D.** The requirement decomposes into exactly two Power Query steps, and each answer supplies one:\n\n- *&quot;for transactions that occurred during the **last quarter**&quot;* → **C. TransactionDate → Filter rows → last quarter.** Power Query&#x27;s date filters include relative-period options (**Date → Quarter → Last quarter**), so this is a single built-in click rather than a hand-written date range — and it stays correct as time passes.\n- *&quot;calculate the **total sales amount of each product category**&quot;* → **D. ProductCategory → Group by → Sum of SalesAmount.** Group by is Power Query&#x27;s aggregation step: group on the category, add an aggregation of `Sum` over `SalesAmount`.\n\nOrder matters in practice — filter *then* group, so you aggregate only last quarter&#x27;s rows (and push the filter down to the warehouse) — but the question only asks which two actions.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A Remove duplicates on TransactionDate** ✘ — actively destructive. It would keep **one transaction per distinct date** and throw away every other sale, wrecking the totals.\n- **B Keep top rows = 90** ✘ — the 12% answer, and the trap is the number: 90 ≈ &quot;a quarter of days&quot;. But *Keep top rows* keeps the first **90 rows**, not 90 days, and only in whatever order the rows happen to arrive. With multiple transactions per day it is neither a quarter nor the *last* quarter. As one commenter puts it, &quot;B does not achieve the goal.&quot;\n- **E Transform number column → Standard → Add → 90** ✘ — arithmetic: it would add 90 to every `SalesAmount`. Nothing to do with dates or grouping.\n\n**Filter rows = WHERE. Group by = GROUP BY.** Map the sentence onto SQL first and the two steps fall out.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Filter by values (Power Query)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/filter-values?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Group or summarize rows (Power Query)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/group-by?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Query using the visual query editor (Fabric)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/visual-query-editor?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q188"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
