  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q189",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q189",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model.\nYou need to identify any surrogate key columns in the model that have the Summarize By property set to a value other than to None. The solution\nmust minimize effort.\nWhat should you use?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Performance Analyzer in Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Model explorer in Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Model view in Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DAX Formatter in DAX Studio"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**C — Model view in Power BI Desktop.** `Summarize By` is a **column property**, and the Properties pane of **Model view** is where column properties are inspected and edited. Select a column on the diagram (or several at once with Ctrl-click) and the pane on the right shows **Advanced → Summarize by**, so you can both spot the offenders and set them to `None` without leaving the view. That multi-select is what satisfies *&quot;minimize effort&quot;*.\n\n**Why not B — Model explorer** (the 33% answer, and the closest call): Model explorer is a **navigation tree** of semantic model objects (tables, measures, roles, calculation groups, relationships) that lives *inside* Model view. It helps you *find* objects in a large model; it does not itself display the `Summarize by` setting. If you use it, you are still reading the answer out of Model view&#x27;s Properties pane — so Model view is both the necessary and the sufficient answer.\n\n- **A Performance Analyzer** ✘ — records visual render and query durations to diagnose slow reports. It reports no metadata.\n- **D DAX Formatter in DAX Studio** ✘ — pretty-prints DAX expressions. It does not read column properties, and `Summarize By` is not DAX.\n\n### Why this matters (the modelling point behind the question)\n\nA **surrogate key** is a meaningless integer whose only job is to join tables. Leaving `Summarize By` at its default (`Sum`, which Power BI applies automatically to numeric columns) means the column carries the **Σ** icon and a user can drag `CustomerKey` into a visual and get a nonsensical total of ID numbers. Setting `Summarize by = None` on every key column is standard model hygiene — alongside hiding the key columns entirely.\n\n*Real-world footnote:* the genuinely lowest-effort way to audit this across a whole model is **Tabular Editor&#x27;s Best Practice Analyzer**, which ships with a rule for exactly this. It is not among the options, and a commenter raises it for the same reason.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Work with Model view in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-relationship-view?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Work with Model explorer in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/model-explorer?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Star schema guidance — surrogate keys",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#surrogate-keys"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q189"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
