  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q190",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q190",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse.\nYou use a dataflow to load a new dataset from OneLake to the warehouse.\nYou need to add a Power Query step to identify the maximum values for the numeric columns.\nWhich function should you include in the step?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Table.Range"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Table.MaxN"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Table.Profile"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**C — `Table.Profile`.** It *&quot;returns a profile for the columns in a table&quot;* — one output row per column, with the statistics that data profiling needs:\n\n| Profile column | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `Min`, **`Max`** | smallest / **largest** value |\n| `Average`, `StandardDeviation` | for numeric columns |\n| `Count`, `NullCount`, `DistinctCount` | row and cardinality counts |\n\nSo a single `Table.Profile(Source)` step surfaces the maximum for **every** numeric column at once — exactly the request.\n\n- **A `Table.Range`** ✘ — `Table.Range(table, offset, count)` returns a **slice of rows** starting at an offset, the Power Query equivalent of a skip/take. &quot;Range&quot; here means a range of *rows*, not a min–max range — that is the whole trick of this distractor.\n- **B `Table.MaxN`** ✘ — tempting because of the name, but it returns the **N rows** with the largest values according to a comparison criterion (`Table.MaxN(table, &quot;Sales&quot;, 5)` → the top five rows). It gives you rows, for one specified column, not the maximum value of each numeric column. (`List.Max` would give a single column&#x27;s maximum — also not offered.)\n\nIn the Power Query UI the same feature is the **Column profile / Column statistics** pane under the **View** ribbon; `Table.Profile` is what it calls under the covers.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Table.Profile",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/powerquery-m/table-profile?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Table.MaxN",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/powerquery-m/table-maxn?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Table.Range",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/powerquery-m/table-range?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Data profiling tools (Power Query)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/data-profiling-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
