  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q191",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q191",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric warehouse named Warehouse1 that contains a table named TaxiTrips.\nTaxiTrips contains the following columns.\nYou need to create a query that shows the top three taxi companies based on the total miles traveled.\nHow should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "SELECT [answer choice] company AS TaxiCompany, …",
        "options": [
          "FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY",
          "Limit (3)",
          "Top (3)"
        ],
        "answer": "Top (3)"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "FROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[TaxiTrips] [answer choice]",
        "options": [
          "GROUP BY company",
          "GROUP BY TaxiCompany",
          "ORDER BY tripDistance"
        ],
        "answer": "GROUP BY company"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "[answer choice] DESC;",
        "options": [
          "GROUP BY company",
          "ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)",
          "ORDER BY tripDistance"
        ],
        "answer": "ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`TOP (3)`**, **`GROUP BY company`**, **`ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)`**. The finished statement:\n\n```sql\nSELECT TOP (3)\n    company AS TaxiCompany,\n    SUM(passengerCount) AS TotalPassengers,\n    SUM(tripDistance) AS TotalDistance\nFROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[TaxiTrips]\nGROUP BY company\nORDER BY SUM(tripDistance) DESC;\n```\n\n### Blank 1 — `TOP (3)`\n\nPosition decides it: the blank sits **immediately after `SELECT`**, and `TOP (n)` is the only one of the three that belongs there.\n\n- `FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY` ✘ — valid T-SQL, but it goes at the **end** of the statement and requires a preceding `OFFSET … ROWS` clause (`ORDER BY … OFFSET 0 ROWS FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY`). It cannot appear after `SELECT`.\n- `LIMIT (3)` ✘ — MySQL/PostgreSQL/SQLite syntax. **T-SQL has no `LIMIT`.** (Same distractor as T1Q177 blank 2.)\n\n### Blank 2 — `GROUP BY company`\n\nTwo aggregates in the `SELECT` list (`SUM(passengerCount)`, `SUM(tripDistance)`) alongside a non-aggregated column force a `GROUP BY` on that column.\n\n- `GROUP BY TaxiCompany` ✘ — **the alias trap.** `TaxiCompany` is a column alias defined in the `SELECT` list, and `GROUP BY` is evaluated **before** `SELECT`, so the alias does not exist yet. You must group by the underlying column, `company`. (`ORDER BY` is the one clause that *can* see `SELECT` aliases, because it runs last.)\n- `ORDER BY tripDistance` ✘ — wrong clause entirely for this slot, and the query needs a `GROUP BY`.\n\n### Blank 3 — `ORDER BY SUM(tripDistance)` DESC\n\n*&quot;Top three taxi companies based on **the total miles travelled**&quot;* — the ranking is on the **total**, so the sort key must be the aggregate.\n\n- `ORDER BY tripDistance` ✘ — the tempting near-miss. After a `GROUP BY company`, the bare column `tripDistance` is not available (it is neither grouped nor aggregated), so this is an error — and even conceptually it would rank on a single trip rather than the company total.\n- `GROUP BY company` ✘ — already used, and a statement has one `GROUP BY`.\n\n**`TOP` is only meaningful with `ORDER BY`.** Together they are the T-SQL idiom for &quot;top N by X&quot;: `SELECT TOP (N) … GROUP BY … ORDER BY AGG(X) DESC`. Without the `ORDER BY`, `TOP (3)` returns an arbitrary three rows.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "TOP (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/top-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SELECT — ORDER BY clause (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-order-by-clause-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SELECT — GROUP BY (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-group-by-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p432_i1.png, p432_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q191"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p432_i1.png",
      "p432_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p433_i1.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q192",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q192",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains the workspaces shown in the following table.\nYou have a deployment pipeline named Pipeline1 that deploys items from Workspace_DEV to Workspace_TEST. In Pipeline1, all items that have\nmatching names are paired.\nYou deploy the contents of Workspace_DEV to Workspace_TEST by using Pipeline1.\nWhat will the contents of Workspace_TEST be once the deployment is complete?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Lakehouse2 - Notebook2 - SemanticModel1 -"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Lakehouse1 - Notebook1 - Pipeline1 - SemanticModel1 -"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Lakehouse1 - Lakehouse2 - Notebook1 - Notebook2 - Pipeline1 - SemanticModel1 -"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Lakehouse2 - Notebook2 - Pipeline1 - SemanticModel1"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**C — Lakehouse1, Lakehouse2, Notebook1, Notebook2, Pipeline1, SemanticModel1.**\n\n| | Workspace_DEV (before) | Workspace_TEST (before) | Workspace_TEST (after) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| | Lakehouse1 | Lakehouse2 | Lakehouse1 *(new)* |\n| | Notebook1 | Notebook2 | Lakehouse2 *(untouched)* |\n| | Pipeline1 | SemanticModel1 | Notebook1 *(new)* |\n| | SemanticModel1 | | Notebook2 *(untouched)* |\n| | | | Pipeline1 *(new)* |\n| | | | SemanticModel1 *(paired → overwritten)* |\n\nTwo rules produce this, and the distractors each break one of them:\n\n**1. Deployment is additive, not a mirror.** Deploying copies the source stage&#x27;s items forward; it **does not delete** items that exist only in the target. Lakehouse2 and Notebook2 have no counterpart in DEV, so they simply stay. This kills **A** and **D**, which both drop Lakehouse1 and Notebook1 — and it is the single most-tested fact about deployment pipelines.\n\n**2. Matching names are paired and overwritten, not duplicated.** SemanticModel1 exists in both stages and is paired, so the DEV copy overwrites the TEST one — you end up with **one** SemanticModel1, not two. (Pairing is by item **name and type**; an unpaired item is created fresh in the target.)\n\n**B** fails rule 1 in the other direction — it lists only what came from DEV and silently deletes Lakehouse2 and Notebook2.\n\n*Read `Pipeline1` carefully:* there are two different things called Pipeline1 here — the **deployment pipeline** doing the work, and a **data pipeline item** sitting in Workspace_DEV&#x27;s contents list. It is the latter that gets deployed. Deployment pipelines are not workspace items and never deploy themselves.\n\n*(Duplicate of T1Q156 — identical scenario and answer.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Introduction to deployment pipelines",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/intro-to-deployment-pipelines?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Understand the deployment process",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Deployment pipelines — item pairing",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#item-pairing"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p434_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q192"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
