  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q193",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q193",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1.\nYou share DW1 with User1 and assign User1 the default permissions for DW1.\nWhat can User1 do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Read data from the tables in DW1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Build reports by using the default dataset."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Read the underlying Parquet files from OneLake."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Connect to DW1 via the TDS (Tabular Data Stream) endpoint."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**D — Connect to DW1 via the TDS (Tabular Data Stream) endpoint.**\n\n&gt; ⚠️ **Flagged: this overrides the PDF answer key,** which marks **A** (*read data from the tables*). Microsoft&#x27;s documentation states the opposite in as many words. The discussion is split (A 60% / D 30%), but the 30% are quoting the docs.\n\nThe decisive sentence, from *Share your warehouse and manage permissions*:\n\n&gt; **&quot;If no additional permissions are selected** — The shared recipient by default receives **&quot;Read&quot;** permission, which **only allows the recipient to *connect* to the SQL analytics endpoint**, the equivalent of `CONNECT` permissions in SQL Server. **The shared recipient won&#x27;t be able to query any table or view** or execute any function or stored procedure unless they&#x27;re provided access to objects within the Warehouse using T-SQL `GRANT`.&quot;\n\nSo the default grant is *connect and nothing more* — and the Fabric warehouse&#x27;s SQL connection string **is** the TDS endpoint (TDS is the wire protocol every SQL client, from SSMS to Power BI, speaks to it). Option D is a restatement of the default permission; option A describes what the docs explicitly say you **cannot** do.\n\n### The sharing dialog, in full\n\n| What you tick | Permission | What the recipient gains |\n|---|---|---|\n| *(nothing — always granted)* | **Read** | **connect to the SQL/TDS endpoint** — `CONNECT` only |\n| &quot;Read all data using SQL&quot; | **ReadData** | `SELECT` on all tables and views — ≈ `db_datareader` |\n| &quot;Read all data using Apache Spark&quot; | **ReadAll** | read the underlying Parquet/OneLake files via ABFS |\n| &quot;Build reports on the default semantic model&quot; | **Build** | build reports on the default semantic model |\n\nMap the options straight onto that table:\n\n- **A Read data from the tables** = **ReadData** — an *optional* checkbox, not the default. ✘\n- **B Build reports using the default dataset** = **Build** — also optional. ✘\n- **C Read the underlying Parquet files from OneLake** = **ReadAll** — also optional. ✘\n- **D Connect via the TDS endpoint** = **Read** — the default. ✔\n\nNote the doc&#x27;s own tip that **ReadData** and **ReadAll** *&quot;are separate permissions that do not overlap&quot;* — Spark access does not imply SQL access, or vice versa.\n\n**Why the question is written this way:** the &quot;share with no extra permissions, then `GRANT` object by object&quot; pattern is Microsoft&#x27;s documented recipe for granular access. The default Read permission exists precisely so that an admin can open the door to the endpoint and then hand out table-level rights individually.\n\n*(If your exam marks A, the examiner is treating &quot;default permissions&quot; loosely as &quot;the Viewer-style read access&quot;. Know the distinction anyway — Read vs ReadData is a recurring DP-600 security theme.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Share your warehouse and manage permissions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/share-warehouse-manage-permissions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Connectivity to data warehousing in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/connectivity?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SQL granular permissions in Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/sql-granular-permissions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q193"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q194",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q194",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains two workspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2.\nWorkspace1 is used as the development environment. Workspace2 is used as the production environment. Each environment uses a different\nstorage account.\nWorkspace1 contains a Dataflow Gen2 named Dataflow1. The data source of Dataflow1 is a CSV file in blob storage.\nYou plan to implement a deployment pipeline to deploy items from Workspace1 to Workspace2.\nYou need to ensure that the data source references the correct location in the production environment.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Create a data source rule only."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Create a parameter rule only."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Create a data source rule and a parameter rule."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "After implementing the deployment pipeline, manually change the data source"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**C — create a data source rule *and* a parameter rule.** Deployment pipelines offer exactly three rule types, and the two relevant ones solve two different halves of a dev→prod data source switch:\n\n| Rule type | What it overrides in the target stage |\n|---|---|\n| **Data source rule** | the **connection** — swap the dev server/account for the prod one. *&quot;You can only change to a data source from the same type.&quot;* |\n| **Parameter rule** | the **value of a Power Query parameter** — e.g. the container, folder path, or file name inside that account |\n| Default lakehouse rule | which lakehouse a **notebook** defaults to (not relevant here) |\n\nThe scenario gives you both halves: *&quot;each environment uses a different **storage account**&quot;* (the connection) and the dataflow points at a *&quot;**CSV file** in blob storage&quot;* (a path within it). The data source rule redirects the account; the parameter rule redirects the path. Together they make the deployed Dataflow1 read the production file without anyone touching it.\n\n- **A data source rule only** ✘ (the 33% answer) — swaps the account but leaves the path pinned to whatever dev used. Defensible only if you assume the container and file name are identical in both environments, which the stem does not say.\n- **B parameter rule only** ✘ *by the exam&#x27;s logic* — see the caveat below, where this is stronger than it looks.\n- **D manually change the data source after deployment** ✘ — defeats the purpose of a pipeline. Deployment rules exist so this is automatic and repeatable; a manual step has to be redone after **every** deployment and is the classic wrong answer in ALM questions.\n\n&gt; ⚠️ **Doc drift worth knowing.** Today&#x27;s documentation makes this item shakier than the key admits, and if you meet it in the wild the reasoning has moved on:\n&gt; - The rules matrix lists data source and parameter rules for **Dataflow *Gen1***, semantic models, paginated reports and mirrored databases. **Dataflow Gen2 is not in the table at all.**\n&gt; - Data source rules are supported only for a fixed list of source types — AAS, Azure Synapse, SSAS, Azure SQL, SQL Server, OData, Oracle, SAP HANA, SharePoint, Teradata. **Azure Blob Storage is not on it**, and Microsoft says: *&quot;For other data sources, we recommend using parameters to configure your data source.&quot;*\n&gt; - A listed limitation: *&quot;Adding data source rules… on data sources which are **parametrized**&quot;* is **not supported** — so a data source rule and a parameter cannot both target the same source.\n&gt;\n&gt; Taken literally, that guidance points at **parameters** (option B) for a blob-storage-backed Dataflow Gen2. The exam item predates Dataflow Gen2 CI/CD support and tests the conceptual pairing instead. **Answer C on the exam; use parameters in production.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Create deployment rules for Fabric ALM",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/create-rules?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CI/CD best practices — use parameters for configurations that change between stages",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/best-practices-cicd?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Dataflow Gen2 with CI/CD and Git integration",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/dataflow-gen2-cicd-and-git-integration?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q194"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q195",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q195",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a semantic model named Model1 that contains data that relates to customers and their bank account balances.\nModel1 has the following tables and columns.\nA customer can have one or more accounts. Each account can be associated to multiple customers.\nYou need to ensure that users can query Model1 to identify the total transaction amounts by customer.\nWhat should you add to Model1?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a many-to-many relationship between FactTransaction and Dim Customer"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a bridge table with relationships to DimCustomer and DimAccount"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a bridge table with relationships to FactTransaction and DimCustomer"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the CustomerKey column in FactTransaction and a relationship to DimCustomer"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**B — a bridge table with relationships to DimCustomer and DimAccount.**\n\nRead the grain first:\n\n- `FactTransaction` has `AccountKey`, `Date`, `Amount` — it is at **account** grain and has **no `CustomerKey`**.\n- *&quot;A customer can have one or more accounts. Each account can be associated to multiple customers.&quot;* — that is a **many-to-many between two dimensions**, `DimCustomer` ↔ `DimAccount` (think joint accounts).\n\nThe relationship that is missing is therefore **between the two dimensions**, not between the fact and a dimension. The standard resolution is a **bridge table** (also called a factless fact or an account-customer mapping table) holding one row per valid `CustomerKey` / `AccountKey` pair, with a one-to-many relationship from each dimension into it:\n\n```\nDimCustomer (1) ──&lt; BridgeCustomerAccount &gt;── (1) DimAccount (1) ──&lt; FactTransaction\n```\n\nFilter now propagates `DimCustomer → Bridge → DimAccount → FactTransaction`, so `SUM(FactTransaction[Amount])` sliced by customer gives the total transaction amount per customer. (Cross-filtering on the `DimAccount`↔`Bridge` relationship must be set to **Both** for the filter to travel up and back down.)\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A many-to-many relationship between FactTransaction and DimCustomer** ✘ — `FactTransaction` has no customer column to relate on, so there is nothing to build the relationship from. Power BI&#x27;s native many-to-many (many-to-many cardinality) is also discouraged between a fact and a dimension: it produces ambiguous filter paths and unexpected blank-row behaviour.\n- **C a bridge table with relationships to FactTransaction and DimCustomer** ✘ — the plausible-looking one. It bridges the **wrong pair**. The many-to-many is customer↔**account**; the fact already joins cleanly to `DimAccount` on `AccountKey` and needs no bridge of its own.\n- **D add CustomerKey to FactTransaction and relate it to DimCustomer** ✘ — this *appears* to be the clean star-schema fix, and it is the one to think hardest about. It fails because a transaction on a joint account belongs to **several** customers: you would have to either pick one arbitrarily (losing the others) or duplicate the transaction row per customer (**double-counting the amount**). Neither is acceptable. Denormalising a key only works when the relationship is genuinely many-to-**one**.\n\n**The pattern to recognise: two dimensions with a many-to-many between them ⇒ bridge table between *those two dimensions*.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Many-to-many relationship guidance — relate many-to-many dimensions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/relationships-many-to-many?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#relate-many-to-many-dimensions"
      },
      {
        "title": "Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Bi-directional relationship guidance",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/relationships-bidirectional-filtering?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p439_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q195"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p439_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q196",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q196",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains 30 CSV files in OneLake. The files are updated daily.\nYou create a Microsoft Power BI semantic model named Model1 that uses the CSV files as a data source. You configure incremental refresh for\nModel1 and publish the model to an F64 capacity in the Fabric tenant.\nWhen you initiate a refresh of Model1, the refresh fails after running out of resources.\nWhat is a possible cause of the failure?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "XMLA Endpoint is set to Read Only."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Query folding is occurring."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "The data type of the column used to partition the data has changed."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Only refresh complete days is selected."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Query folding is NOT occurring."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**E — Query folding is NOT occurring.**\n\nIncremental refresh is built entirely on **query folding**. You define `RangeStart` / `RangeEnd` datetime parameters and filter the table on them; Power Query is supposed to translate that filter into the source&#x27;s own query language so that **only the rows in the partition window ever leave the source**. When folding works, refreshing one day&#x27;s partition transfers one day of data.\n\nWhen folding **fails**, the mashup engine has no choice but to pull the **entire dataset** into memory and apply the `RangeStart`/`RangeEnd` filter locally — for *every partition*. Thirty daily-updated files, refreshed partition by partition, each reading everything: the model exhausts the capacity&#x27;s memory and the refresh dies with a resource error. That is precisely the symptom described.\n\n**The detail that makes this the intended answer:** the source is **CSV files**. Flat files have no query engine to fold into, so folding is impossible by construction. Microsoft is explicit that incremental refresh should target sources that fold — relational databases, SQL endpoints — and warns against file-based sources for exactly this reason. The F64 capacity is a red herring: no SKU saves you from loading 30 full files per partition.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **B Query folding IS occurring** ✘ — the exact opposite. Folding is the thing that *prevents* this failure.\n- **C The data type of the partition column has changed** ✘ — the 40% answer, and it is a real failure mode, but the wrong one. Changing the type of the `RangeStart`/`RangeEnd` column breaks the **policy**: you get a validation or partition error (&quot;the filter must be on a date/datetime column&quot;), not *&quot;ran out of resources&quot;*. Match the symptom to the cause — this question hands you a memory-exhaustion symptom.\n- **A XMLA Endpoint is Read Only** ✘ — blocks third-party tools writing metadata. A scheduled refresh does not go through XMLA write, and it would fail with a permission error, not resource exhaustion.\n- **D Only refresh complete days is selected** ✘ — a legitimate policy option that *shrinks* the work (it skips today&#x27;s partial day). If anything it reduces resource use.\n\n**Always verify folding before shipping an incremental refresh policy:** right-click the last applied step → **View Native Query**. If it is greyed out, the query is not folding and incremental refresh will quietly degrade into a full load.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Incremental refresh and real-time data for semantic models",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Configure incremental refresh — query folding requirements",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-configure?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Power Query query folding",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/query-folding-basics?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
