  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q197",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q197",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are implementing two dimension tables named Customers and Products in a Fabric warehouse.\nYou need to create two slowly changing dimensions that meet the requirements shown in the following table.\nWhich type of SCD should you use for each table? To answer, drag the appropriate SCD types to the correct tables. Each SCD type may be used\nonce, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Customers — \"Create a new version of the customer record while keeping the previous versions.\"",
        "options": [
          "Type 0",
          "Type 1",
          "Type 2",
          "Type 3",
          "Type 6"
        ],
        "answer": "Type 2"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Products — \"Overwrite the existing value in the current record.\"",
        "options": [
          "Type 0",
          "Type 1",
          "Type 2",
          "Type 3",
          "Type 6"
        ],
        "answer": "Type 1"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Customers → Type 2. Products → Type 1.** Each requirement is the textbook one-line definition of its SCD type:\n\n| Type | Behaviour on a change | History |\n|:-:|---|---|\n| 0 | **Ignore** the change — the attribute is fixed for all time (e.g. original credit score) | n/a |\n| **1** | **Overwrite the existing value in the current record** ← *Products* | **none** — the old value is gone |\n| **2** | **Add a new row** (new surrogate key) and expire the old one ← *Customers* | **full** — every version preserved |\n| 3 | Add a **column** (`PreviousValue`) alongside the current one | limited — one prior version only |\n| 6 | 1 + 2 + 3 combined (&quot;hybrid&quot;) | full, plus current-value columns on every row |\n\n**Customers → Type 2:** *&quot;create a new version… while keeping the previous versions&quot;* (plural) means a full history of rows. A Type 2 dimension carries the bookkeeping columns that make that work — a **surrogate key** (so each version is uniquely addressable), `StartDate`/`EndDate` (or `ValidFrom`/`ValidTo`), and usually an `IsCurrent` flag. Facts join to the surrogate key that was current when the event happened, so a sale made under a customer&#x27;s old address stays attributed to that old address.\n\n**Products → Type 1:** *&quot;overwrite the existing value in the current record&quot;* — the simplest behaviour, a plain `UPDATE`. There is exactly one row per product and no history, so a correction (a typo in a product name, a re-categorisation) restates all past reporting as though the new value had always applied. That is usually what you want for corrections and rarely what you want for genuine business change.\n\n*Type 3 is the near-miss for Customers* — it also &quot;keeps the previous value&quot;, but only **one**, in a side column. The plural *&quot;previous versions&quot;* rules it out.\n\n*(Duplicate of T1Q78.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Implement slowly changing dimensions in a Fabric warehouse",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#slowly-changing-dimensions"
      },
      {
        "title": "Dimensional modeling in Fabric Warehouse — dimension tables",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-dimension-tables?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p441_i1.png, p441_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q197"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p441_i1.png",
