    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q198",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q198",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric eventhouse that contains a KQL database. The database contains a table named TaxiData that stores the following data.\nYou need to create a column named FirstPickupDateTime that will contain the first value of each hour from tpep_pickup_datetime partitioned by\npayment_type.\nHow should you complete the query? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "| extend FirstPickupDateTime = [answer choice] (tpep_pickup_datetime, 1h, 0m, payment_type … prev(payment_type))",
        "options": [
          "row_cumsum",
          "row_rank_dense",
          "row_rank_min",
          "row_window_session"
        ],
        "answer": "row_window_session"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "… payment_type [answer choice] prev(payment_type))",
        "options": [
          "!=",
          "<=",
          "==",
          ">="
        ],
        "answer": "!="
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`row_window_session`** and **`!=`**. The finished query:\n\n```kusto\nTaxiData\n| sort by tpep_pickup_datetime asc, payment_type asc\n| extend FirstPickupDateTime = row_window_session(tpep_pickup_datetime, 1h, 0m, payment_type != prev(payment_type))\n```\n\n### Blank 1 — row_window_session\n\nThe requirement is *&quot;the **first value of each hour** from `tpep_pickup_datetime`, partitioned by `payment_type`&quot;* — i.e. group rows into one-hour sessions and stamp every row with the timestamp the session **started**. That is the literal definition of the function: *&quot;Calculates **session start values** of a column in a serialized row set… If a new session is created, the function returns the current value of Expr. Otherwise, it returns the previous value.&quot;*\n\nThe argument shape in the exhibit confirms it before you even read the names — `(datetime_column, 1h, 0m, boolean)` matches `row_window_session(Expr, MaxDistanceFromFirst, MaxDistanceBetweenNeighbors [, Restart])` and nothing else:\n\n| Argument | Value here | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Expr` | `tpep_pickup_datetime` | the datetime being sessionized |\n| `MaxDistanceFromFirst` | `1h` | **a session can&#x27;t span more than an hour** — this is the &quot;each hour&quot; |\n| `MaxDistanceBetweenNeighbors` | `0m` | no gap-based split; neighbours never break the session on their own |\n| `Restart` | `payment_type != prev(payment_type)` | force a new session when the partition changes |\n\n- **`row_rank_min`** ✘ — the highly-voted comment&#x27;s answer, and wrong. It returns a **rank** (a number: 1, 2, 3…), not a datetime, so it cannot produce a column called `FirstPickupDateTime`. It also takes one argument, not four.\n- **`row_rank_dense`** ✘ — dense ranking. Same objections.\n- **`row_cumsum`** ✘ — a running total over a numeric column.\n\n### Blank 2 — `!=`, not `==`\n\nThis is the half most people get backwards. Read the parameter definition literally: ***Restart*** — *&quot;If specified, every value that evaluates to **`true`** immediately **restarts** the session.&quot;*\n\nYou want a fresh session whenever the `payment_type` **changes**, so the expression must be **true on change** → `payment_type != prev(payment_type)`. Writing `==` inverts it: the session would restart on every row where the payment type *stayed the same* and continue across the boundary where it changed — exactly wrong.\n\nMicrosoft&#x27;s own documented example is this query with the names swapped:\n\n```kusto\n| sort by ID asc, Timestamp asc\n| extend SessionStarted = row_window_session(Timestamp, 1h, 5m, ID != prev(ID))\n```\n\n*Note the `| sort by … asc` that precedes it (already filled in).* Like `prev()`, `row_window_session()` is a **serialized row-set** function: it reads rows in their current order, so the data must be sorted by partition key **and** timestamp first or the sessions are meaningless.\n\n**Mnemonic for `Restart`: it is &quot;start a new one when TRUE&quot;, so the condition describes the *boundary*, not the *continuation*.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "row_window_session() (KQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/row-window-session-function?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Window functions overview — serialized row set (KQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/window-functions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "prev() (KQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/prev-function?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p442_i1.png, p442_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q198"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p442_i1.png",
      "p442_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p442_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q199",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q199",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have Fabric tenant that contains four workspaces named Development, Test, QA, and Production. All the workspaces are in Premium Per User\n(PPU) license mode.\nYou plan to use a release pipeline to support the development lifecycle from Development to Production.\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and\narrange them in the correct order.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Step 1",
        "options": [
          "Move each workspace to Pro license mode.",
          "Create a deployment pipeline.",
          "Create a deployment rule.",
          "Assign workspaces.",
          "Create the QA stage."
        ],
        "answer": "Create a deployment pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Step 2",
        "options": [
          "Move each workspace to Pro license mode.",
          "Create a deployment pipeline.",
          "Create a deployment rule.",
          "Assign workspaces.",
          "Create the QA stage."
        ],
        "answer": "Create the QA stage."
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Step 3",
        "options": [
          "Move each workspace to Pro license mode.",
          "Create a deployment pipeline.",
          "Create a deployment rule.",
          "Assign workspaces.",
          "Create the QA stage."
        ],
        "answer": "Assign workspaces."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Create a deployment pipeline → Create the QA stage → Assign workspaces.**\n\n&gt; ⚠️ **Flagged: this overrides the PDF answer key,** which gives *Create a deployment pipeline → Assign workspaces → Create a deployment rule*. Microsoft&#x27;s setup documentation makes that sequence impossible to execute. The (thin) discussion also rejects the key, though the two commenters disagree with each other.\n\n### Why the QA stage must come second\n\nThe scenario has **four** workspaces — Development, Test, **QA**, Production — and a new pipeline ships with **three** stages. The doc is blunt about when you can fix that:\n\n&gt; *&quot;When you create a pipeline, you define how many stages it should have and what they should be called. **The number of stages are permanent and can&#x27;t be changed after the pipeline is created.**&quot;*\n&gt; *&quot;By default, the pipeline has three stages named Development, Test, and Production. You can accept these default stages or change the number of stages and their names. You can have anywhere between **2-10 stages**.&quot;*\n\nSo the QA stage has to be defined while the pipeline is being set up — and in any case **before** Step 3, because you cannot assign a workspace to a stage that does not exist. The order is forced.\n\n### Why *Create a deployment rule* is not one of the three\n\nIn Microsoft&#x27;s own numbered walkthrough, creating deployment rules is **Step 7 — and it is marked *(optional)***. Rules exist to rewrite data sources and parameters per stage; this scenario mentions no data sources, no parameters, and no per-stage configuration at all. Nothing in the stem requires one. (Rules also cannot be created in the development stage, and cannot be created before the items they target have been deployed — so placing one third, immediately after assigning workspaces, does not even work.)\n\n### Why *Move each workspace to Pro license mode* is wrong\n\nIt is the reverse of the actual requirement. Deployment pipelines need capacity-backed workspaces, and **PPU qualifies** — the docs&#x27; own warning is that you get a prompt only *&quot;if the workspace isn&#x27;t assigned to your organization&#x27;s capacity, **or to your PPU capacity**&quot;*. The four workspaces are already PPU, so nothing needs moving; downgrading them to Pro would remove the capacity backing that deployment pipelines depend on. (The one real PPU caveat: if any stage is PPU, **all** of them must be, which is already true here.)\n\n**The canonical setup sequence: create the pipeline → define its stages → assign a workspace to each stage → deploy → (optionally) add deployment rules.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Get started with deployment pipelines",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/get-started-with-deployment-pipelines?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Assign a workspace to a pipeline stage",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/assign-pipeline?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Deployment pipelines FAQ — what type of capacity do I need",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/faq?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p444_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q199"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p444_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p444_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q200",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q200",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1 and a user named User1.\nYou need to ensure that User1 can read all the data in Lakehouse1. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Assign the Contributor role to User1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Select Read all Apache Spark."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Assign the Viewer role to User1."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Share Lakehouse1 by using item permissions."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Assign the Member role to User1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "B",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**B + D — share Lakehouse1 by using item permissions, and select &quot;Read all Apache Spark&quot;.**\n\nTwo decisions, one per answer.\n\n**D — *how* to grant: item permissions, not a workspace role.** This is what *&quot;principle of least privilege&quot;* is testing. A workspace role applies to **every item in Workspace1**; item-level sharing applies to **Lakehouse1 alone**. Since the requirement names exactly one lakehouse, sharing the item is the narrowest grant that does the job.\n\n**B — *what* to grant: ReadAll (&quot;Read all Apache Spark&quot;).** The requirement is *&quot;read **all the data**&quot;*, and in a lakehouse the data is the OneLake files and Delta tables. **ReadAll** is the permission that opens them:\n\n| Sharing checkbox | Permission | Grants |\n|---|---|---|\n| *(none — always granted)* | Read | connect to the SQL analytics endpoint only |\n| &quot;Read all SQL endpoint data&quot; | ReadData | `SELECT` on tables/views through the SQL endpoint |\n| **&quot;Read all Apache Spark&quot;** | **ReadAll** | **read all files and Delta tables in OneLake** (Spark, notebooks, shortcuts) |\n| &quot;Build reports on the default semantic model&quot; | Build | build reports on the default semantic model |\n\nSharing with no boxes ticked would give User1 only `CONNECT` — enough to reach the endpoint, not enough to read anything (see T1Q193).\n\nWhy the role options fail:\n\n- **C Viewer** ✘ — the tempting &quot;read-only&quot; choice. Viewer *is* the least-privileged **role**, but a role is workspace-wide: User1 would gain read access to every current and future item in Workspace1, not just Lakehouse1. Broader than required.\n- **A Contributor** ✘ and **E Member** ✘ — both grant **write** access to the whole workspace, and Member can also add other users. Wildly beyond &quot;read&quot;.\n\n**Rule: when a question names one item and says &quot;least privilege&quot;, reach for item permissions (Share), not a workspace role.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lakehouse sharing and permissions (OneLake)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/onelake/onelake-security?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Share your warehouse and manage permissions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/share-warehouse-manage-permissions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q200"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q201",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q201",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains a single table named Orders. Orders contains the following columns:\n• Product Name\n• Order Date\n• Quantity\nYou have a Power BI report that contains a page named Inventory. Inventory contains a slicer for the Product Name column and a clustered\ncolumn chart that shows Quantity by Order Date.\nYou need to ensure that users can select whether to display Quantity by Product Name or Quantity by Order Date in the chart. The solution must\nminimize development effort.\nWhat should you create?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a measure"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a dynamic format sting"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a field parameter"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "a calculation group"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**C — a field parameter.** Microsoft describes them in exactly the terms of the requirement: *&quot;Field parameters let report readers **dynamically change the measures or dimensions** they&#x27;re analyzing in a report.&quot;*\n\nYou create one in **Modeling → New parameter → Fields**, add `Product Name` and `Order Date` to it, and drop the resulting parameter table onto the page as a slicer. The chart&#x27;s axis then switches between the two columns as the user clicks — **no DAX, no bookmarks, no duplicate visuals**, which is what wins on *&quot;minimize development effort&quot;*.\n\n- **A a measure** ✘ — a measure can switch which *value* is displayed (via `SWITCH`/`SELECTEDVALUE`), but the requirement is to switch the chart&#x27;s **axis** — a column, not an aggregation. A measure cannot be placed on an axis.\n- **B a dynamic format string** ✘ — changes how a measure&#x27;s result is *formatted* (see T1Q183). Formatting, not field selection.\n- **D a calculation group** ✘ — the real contender, and the reason to know the distinction. Calculation groups apply reusable **calculation logic** to measures (YTD, prior year, % of total) via calculation items. They operate on measures, not on which dimension sits on the axis. They are also markedly more work: you author them in Tabular Editor with DAX per item.\n\n**The discriminator: &quot;let users pick which *field / column / dimension* to see&quot; ⇒ field parameter. &quot;Let users pick which *calculation* to apply to a measure&quot; ⇒ calculation group.**\n\n*(The existing slicer on `Product Name` is scenery — it filters the data. The new field parameter slicer chooses the axis.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Create field parameters in Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/power-bi-field-parameters?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Calculation groups in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/calculation-groups?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q201"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q202",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q202",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have three Fabric workspaces named Workspace1, Workspace2, and Workspace3. Workspace1 contains a report named Report1 and a\nsemantic model named Model1 in Import mode.\nYou perform the following actions:\n• Create a deployment pipeline named Pipeline1 that has three stages named development, test, and production.\n• Assign Workspace1 to the development stage of Pipeline1.\n• Assign Workspace2 to the test stage of Pipeline1.\n• Assign Workspace3 to the production stage of Pipeline1.\n• Modify Model1.\nYou need to ensure that changes to Model1 are available in the production stage as quickly as possible. The solution must NOT affect the\navailability of Report1.\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and\narrange them in the correct order.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Step 1",
        "options": [
          "Refresh Model1 in the test stage.",
          "Run Pipeline1 from the development stage to the test stage.",
          "Refresh Model1 in the development stage.",
          "Run Pipeline1 from the test stage to the production stage.",
          "Refresh Model1 in the production stage."
        ],
        "answer": "Run Pipeline1 from the development stage to the test stage."
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Step 2",
        "options": [
          "Refresh Model1 in the test stage.",
          "Run Pipeline1 from the development stage to the test stage.",
          "Refresh Model1 in the development stage.",
          "Run Pipeline1 from the test stage to the production stage.",
          "Refresh Model1 in the production stage."
        ],
        "answer": "Run Pipeline1 from the test stage to the production stage."
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Step 3",
        "options": [
          "Refresh Model1 in the test stage.",
          "Run Pipeline1 from the development stage to the test stage.",
          "Refresh Model1 in the development stage.",
          "Run Pipeline1 from the test stage to the production stage.",
          "Refresh Model1 in the production stage."
        ],
        "answer": "Refresh Model1 in the production stage."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Deploy dev → test, deploy test → production, then refresh Model1 in production.**\n\n### Deploy first, refresh last\n\nThe fact that drives the whole answer: **deployment pipelines copy metadata, not data.** When a semantic model is deployed, the item definition (tables, relationships, measures, Power Query) moves forward — the imported data does **not**, and neither does the refresh schedule. A model landing in a new stage arrives empty or stale until something refreshes it.\n\nSo refreshing in development or test would be wasted work: the refreshed *data* cannot travel with the next deployment. Only the **final** refresh, in the stage where the data is actually consumed, matters. That is why two of the five tiles — *&quot;Refresh Model1 in the development stage&quot;* and *&quot;Refresh Model1 in the test stage&quot;* — are distractors, and why the refresh is step 3 rather than step 1.\n\n### You cannot skip the test stage\n\n*&quot;As quickly as possible&quot;* might tempt you to deploy straight from development to production, but a deployment pipeline moves content **one stage at a time**, between adjacent stages. With Workspace1 → development, Workspace2 → test and Workspace3 → production, reaching production requires both hops, in order.\n\n### &quot;Must NOT affect the availability of Report1&quot;\n\nReport1 lives in Workspace1 (development) and is the reason nothing is done to the development stage. Deploying **out of** a stage is a read operation on the source — it does not lock, alter or interrupt anything there, so Report1 keeps serving throughout. A refresh of Model1 in development, by contrast, would put the model Report1 depends on under load for no benefit.\n\n**Remember the pair of facts this question tests: (1) pipelines deploy definitions, never data or refresh schedules; (2) deployment is stage-by-stage. Everything else follows.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Understand the deployment process",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/understand-the-deployment-process?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Deploy content to an existing workspace",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/deploy-content?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Semantic models in deployment pipelines",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/deployment-pipelines/intro-to-deployment-pipelines?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#supported-items"
      }
    ],
