  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q204",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q204",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1.\nYou have a GitHub repository named Repo1.\nYou need to connect Workspace1 to the main branch of Repo1.\nWhich information should you provide?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a shared access signature (SAS) token and the branch name of Repo1"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a personal access token (PAT) and the URL of Repo1"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "an access key and the branch name of Repo1"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "an access key and the URL of Repo1"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**B — a personal access token (PAT) and the URL of Repo1.** Connecting a workspace to GitHub asks for exactly three things: a **display name**, a **personal access token**, and the **repository URL**. Only option B names a pair from that list.\n\nBreak the options into their two halves and every distractor falls apart:\n\n**The credential.** GitHub authenticates Fabric with a **fine-grained PAT** carrying `Contents` read/write permission on the repo. The alternatives belong to Azure Storage, not to Git:\n\n- **SAS token** — a time-limited, scoped URL signature for Azure Blob/ADLS.\n- **Access key** — one of the two shared keys on an Azure Storage account.\n\nNeither means anything to GitHub. (For **Azure DevOps** the connection is different again — it uses your Entra ID sign-in, no token at all.)\n\n**The locator.** You supply the **repository URL** (`https://github.com/&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;`). The branch is not typed in as free text — after the connection is authorized, Fabric reads the repo and you **pick the branch and folder from dropdowns**. That is what eliminates A and C even if you were unsure about the credential.\n\n| | Credential | Locator | |\n|---|---|---|:-:|\n| A | SAS token — Azure Storage | branch name — chosen from a list | ✘✘ |\n| **B** | **PAT** | **repository URL** | **✔** |\n| C | access key — Azure Storage | branch name | ✘✘ |\n| D | access key — Azure Storage | repository URL | ✘ |\n\n*Workspace → Workspace settings → Git integration → Git provider: GitHub → Display name / Personal access token / Repository URL → Connect → then choose branch and folder.*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Get started with Git integration — connect to GitHub",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/git-integration/git-get-started?tabs=github&wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Git integration overview (Fabric)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/cicd/git-integration/intro-to-git-integration?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q204"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q205",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q205",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains the following tables.\nYou are designing a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1 that will deliver near-real-time (NRT) shipping data. Report1 will contain data about\nmany shipped packages.\nYou need to ensure that each table is in the appropriate storage mode to support Report1. The solution must support near-real-time data delivery\nwhile maintaining high query performance.\nWhich storage mode should you select for each table? To answer, drag the appropriate storage modes to the correct tables. Each mode may be\nused once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "FactPackageEvents",
        "options": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Hybrid",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "Hybrid"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "DimPackage",
        "options": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Hybrid",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "DirectQuery"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "DimCarrier",
        "options": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Hybrid",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "Dual"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**FactPackageEvents → Hybrid. DimPackage → DirectQuery. DimCarrier → Dual.** Two requirements are in tension — *near-real-time* (argues for DirectQuery) and *high query performance* (argues for Import) — and the answer resolves them **per table**.\n\n### FactPackageEvents → Hybrid\n\nA **hybrid table** is an incremental-refresh table with one extra partition: historical data sits in **imported** partitions (fast, compressed, in-memory) while the newest slice is a **DirectQuery** partition queried live. You get history at Import speed and the latest events with no refresh latency. That is the definition of near-real-time on a large fact table, and it is uncontested here.\n\n### DimPackage → DirectQuery\n\nThe decisive clue is *&quot;Report1 will contain data about **many shipped packages**&quot;*. `DimPackage` has one row **per package**, so it grows continuously and in lockstep with the fact — a newly shipped package creates a new *dimension* row, not just a new fact row.\n\nIf `DimPackage` were Import or Dual, its imported copy would only contain packages as of the last refresh. The hybrid fact&#x27;s DirectQuery partition would return live events for packages that **do not exist in the imported dimension**, and the report would show blanks or drop rows. Near-real-time is only real if the dimension is live too. It is also the largest table of the three, so importing it is the most expensive and least useful thing you could do.\n\n### DimCarrier → Dual\n\n`DimCarrier` is the opposite: small, and carriers are onboarded rarely. **Dual** means the table is *both* — it keeps an imported copy and can also be queried in DirectQuery mode, and the engine picks per query:\n\n- joined only to **imported** partitions → served from memory, fast;\n- joined to the **DirectQuery** partition (or to DirectQuery `DimPackage`) → folded into the same source query, avoiding a slow **limited relationship** across storage modes.\n\nDual on the small dimensions is Microsoft&#x27;s standard companion to a DirectQuery or hybrid fact, and it is why leaving `DimCarrier` as plain Import would hurt: every live query would have to be stitched together in the mashup engine instead of folded.\n\n| Mode | Data location | Latency | Speed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Import | in-memory copy | as of last refresh | fastest |\n| DirectQuery | source only | **live** | slowest |\n| **Dual** | both, chosen per query | live when needed | fast when it can be |\n| **Hybrid** | imported history + live newest partition | **live at the edge** | fast for history |\n\n&gt; 💬 **Contested.** The one discussion comment (+3) marks `DimPackage` as **Dual**, which is the common rule of thumb (&quot;dimensions alongside a DirectQuery fact should be Dual&quot;). It is a reasonable default and would be right for a *static* dimension — but Dual imports the table, so it cannot surface packages created since the last refresh. The exam key&#x27;s DirectQuery is the answer that actually honours &quot;near-real-time&quot;, and the phrase *&quot;many shipped packages&quot;* is there to tell you this dimension is large and fast-moving. Worth knowing both readings.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Configure incremental refresh and real-time data (hybrid tables)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-configure?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Manage storage mode in Power BI Desktop (Import, DirectQuery, Dual)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-storage-mode?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Composite models in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-composite-models?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p450_i1.png, p450_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q205"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p450_i1.png",
      "p450_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p450_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q206",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q206",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains a fact table with millions of rows of shipment data.\nYou need to enable partitioning to improve the query performance and manageability of Model1.\nWhich two tools can you use to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Tabular Editor"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "DAX Studio"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the Microsoft Power BI service"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS)"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**A + E — Tabular Editor and SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS).** Table partitions are **model metadata**, so creating and managing them requires a tool that can *write* metadata to the semantic model over the **XMLA endpoint** (set to Read Write). Exactly two options qualify, and the question says each is a complete solution on its own.\n\n- **A Tabular Editor** ✔ — exposes the `Partitions` collection of every table in its object tree. Add, rename, delete, edit each partition&#x27;s M or native query, and set processing options. Writes via TOM over XMLA.\n- **E SSMS** ✔ — connects to the workspace&#x27;s XMLA endpoint as though it were an Analysis Services server. Right-click a table → **Partitions** for the GUI, or run **TMSL** (`createOrReplace`, `refresh` with `&quot;type&quot;: &quot;full&quot;` on a named partition) in an XMLA query window.\n\nWhy the others cannot:\n\n- **B DAX Studio** ✘ — a *query and tracing* tool. It runs DAX, captures server timings and reads VertiPaq Analyzer statistics (it will happily **show** you existing partitions and their row counts), but it has no metadata-write path. Read-only by design.\n- **C Power BI Desktop** ✘ — the important one to understand. Desktop has **no partition UI at all**. The only partitions it can produce are the ones an **incremental refresh policy** generates automatically once the model is published — you define the policy, the service creates the partitions. You cannot author a custom partition scheme there.\n- **D the Power BI service** ✘ — same story from the other end: the service *applies* an incremental refresh policy and shows refresh history, but its UI exposes no partition management.\n\n**The dividing line: XMLA read-write metadata tools (Tabular Editor, SSMS, ALM Toolkit, TOM/TMSL scripts) can manage partitions; authoring and consumption surfaces (Desktop, the service, DAX Studio) cannot.** See T1Q182 for the XMLA Read Write prerequisite.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Partitions in tabular models",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/analysis-services/tabular-models/partitions-ssas-tabular?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Advanced incremental refresh with the XMLA endpoint",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-xmla?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q206"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q207",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q207",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric semantic model named Model1 that contains a table named Sales.\nYou need to enable incremental refresh for the Sales table.\nWhat should you do first?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Move Model1 to Premium Per User (PPU) workspace."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Create parameters in Power Query."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "From Settings, set Large semantic model storage format to On."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Automate the data preparation by using Dataflow Gen2."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**B — create parameters in Power Query.** The word that matters is ***first***. Before the incremental refresh policy dialog will even open, the table must be filtered by two reserved parameters:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **`RangeStart`** | `Date/Time` | lower bound of the partition window |\n| **`RangeEnd`** | `Date/Time` | upper bound of the partition window |\n\nThe names are **case-sensitive and reserved**, and the type must be `Date/Time` (not Date, not Text). You then filter the `Sales` date column with them, conventionally `&gt;= RangeStart` and `&lt; RangeEnd` so no row is counted in two partitions. Only once that filter exists can you right-click the table → **Incremental refresh** and define the archive/refresh ranges — the service substitutes its own values for the parameters to generate each partition.\n\nWhy the others are not first (or not required at all):\n\n- **A Move Model1 to a PPU workspace** ✘ — incremental refresh is available on **Pro** as well; PPU/Premium/Fabric only add capabilities like real-time hybrid partitions and XMLA partition management. Not a prerequisite.\n- **C Set Large semantic model storage format to On** ✘ — raises the model size ceiling and speeds XMLA writes (see T1Q180). Useful alongside a big incremental model, but unrelated to enabling the policy.\n- **D Automate the data preparation by using Dataflow Gen2** ✘ — a different ingestion approach entirely, and adding a dataflow in the middle can actually *break* incremental refresh by preventing query folding (see T1Q196).\n\n**The sequence to remember: `RangeStart`/`RangeEnd` parameters → filter the table on them → define the incremental refresh policy → publish → first refresh builds the partitions.** And confirm the query still folds, or the policy is worse than useless.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Configure incremental refresh and real-time data",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-configure?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Incremental refresh and real-time data for semantic models",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/incremental-refresh-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q207"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q208",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q208",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have an Azure SQL database named DB1 and a Fabric workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a\nDataflow Gen2 named Dataflow1. Dataflow1 includes a query named Query1 that loads data from DB1, applies transformations to the data, and\nthen filters the data.\nYou discover that Query1 loads all the data before applying the transformations.\nYou need to ensure that Query1 uses query folding.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Remove unused columns from Query1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Enable incremental refresh for Query1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Replace DB1 with a Microsoft SQL Server database."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Move the filter steps before the transformation steps in Query1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**D — move the filter steps before the transformation steps in Query1.**\n\nQuery folding is **all-or-nothing from the point it breaks onward**. The mashup engine walks the applied steps in order and translates each one into native SQL for as long as it can. The moment it hits a step with no SQL equivalent, folding stops — and **every step after that runs locally in Power Query**, which means the engine must first pull the full table out of DB1.\n\nThat is exactly the reported symptom: *&quot;Query1 loads all the data before applying the transformations.&quot;* A non-foldable transformation is sitting in front of the filter, so the filter can never reach the database.\n\n```\n❌ Source → [non-foldable transform] → Filter      →  full table pulled, filtered in memory\n✅ Source → Filter → [non-foldable transform]          →  WHERE clause runs in DB1, only matching rows travel\n```\n\nFilters (`Table.SelectRows`) are among the most reliably foldable operations there are — they become a `WHERE` clause. Putting them immediately after the source is both the fix here and a general best practice: **fold the row- and column-reducing steps first, then do whatever local work you must.**\n\n- **A Remove unused columns** ✘ — good hygiene, and column removal *is* foldable (it becomes a narrower `SELECT` list), but it does nothing about a folding chain that is already broken earlier in the query. It reduces width, not the &quot;loads all the data&quot; problem.\n- **B Enable incremental refresh** ✘ — backwards. Incremental refresh **depends on** query folding; it does not create it. Enabling it on a non-folding query makes things worse, because each partition then pulls the whole table (see T1Q196).\n- **C Replace DB1 with a SQL Server database** ✘ — Azure SQL Database already folds perfectly. The source is not the problem; the step order is.\n\n**How to verify:** right-click each applied step → **View Native Query**. The last step where it is still enabled is where folding stops. Anything you can move above that line gets pushed to the source for free.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Power Query query folding",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/query-folding-basics?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Query folding guidance in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/power-query-folding?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Query folding indicators in Power Query",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-query/step-folding-indicators?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q208"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q209",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q209",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Entra tenant named contoso.com and an external user named user1@fabrikam.com .\nYou have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a semantic model named Model1 and a report named Report1.\nUser1@fabrikam.com has access to Report1.\nYou enable read-write on the XML for Analysis (XMLA) endpoint to provide advanced semantic modeling by using DAX Studio.\nYou need to provide user1@fabrikam.com with a URL that will enable connectivity by using DAX Studio.\nHow should you complete the URL? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "powerbi://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/[answer choice]",
        "options": [
          "common",
          "contoso.com",
          "fabrikam.com",
          "myorg"
        ],
        "answer": "contoso.com"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "/[answer choice]",
        "options": [
          "Model1",
          "Report1",
          "Workspace1"
        ],
        "answer": "Workspace1"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`powerbi://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/contoso.com/Workspace1`**\n\n### Blank 1 — `contoso.com`, not `myorg`\n\nThe XMLA endpoint URL is `powerbi://api.powerbi.com/v1.0/&lt;tenant&gt;/&lt;workspace name&gt;`, and the `&lt;tenant&gt;` segment is the whole point of this question:\n\n| Segment | When to use it |\n|---|---|\n| `myorg` | the **default alias for your own home tenant** — works for internal users signing in to their own organisation |\n| **`contoso.com`** | the **explicit tenant name of the organisation that owns the workspace** — required for **guest / B2B** users |\n\n`user1@fabrikam.com` is an **external user** in contoso.com&#x27;s Entra tenant. If they used `myorg`, the service would resolve it to *fabrikam.com* — their own home tenant — and the workspace would not be found. Guest users must name the **host** tenant explicitly, so the URL has to say `contoso.com`.\n\n- **`fabrikam.com`** ✘ — the user&#x27;s own tenant. Workspace1 does not live there. This is the trap the question is built around: the domain in the URL is the **owner&#x27;s**, never the guest&#x27;s.\n- **`common`** ✘ — a Microsoft Entra *authentication* endpoint alias (`login.microsoftonline.com/common`), used for multi-tenant sign-in. It is not a Power BI workspace path segment.\n\n### Blank 2 — `Workspace1`\n\nThe XMLA endpoint addresses a **workspace**, which DAX Studio and SSMS treat as an Analysis Services *server*. Once connected, the semantic models inside it appear as *databases* — so you select `Model1` **after** connecting, from the database dropdown.\n\n- **`Model1`** ✘ — a database within the server, not part of the server URL.\n- **`Report1`** ✘ — reports are not addressable over XMLA at all; the endpoint exposes semantic models only.\n\n**Remember: XMLA connects to a *workspace*; the workspace is the server, the semantic models are its databases. And `myorg` only means &quot;my org&quot; for members of that org — guests must spell the tenant out.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Semantic model connectivity with the XMLA endpoint — connect to a workspace",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/enterprise/service-premium-connect-tools?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#connect-to-a-premium-workspace"
      },
      {
        "title": "Distribute Power BI content to external guest users (Microsoft Entra B2B)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/whitepaper-azure-b2b-power-bi?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p454_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q209"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p454_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p454_i2.png"
    ]
