  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q210",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q210",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 and a warehouse named Warehouse1. Workspace1 contains a user named User1. User1 is\nassigned the Viewer role for Workspace1.\nYou need to ensure that User1 can share Warehouse1 with other users. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich role should you assign to User1?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Member for Workspace1"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "db_owner for Warehouse1"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Contributor for Workspace1"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Admin for Workspace1"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**A — Member for Workspace1.** The capability being granted is **Reshare**, and the permissions table is unambiguous about who has it:\n\n| Permission | Given by default to |\n|---|---|\n| Read / ReadData | Admin, Member, Contributor, Viewer |\n| ReadAll / Write | Admin, Member, Contributor |\n| **Reshare** | **Admin, Member** |\n| Restore / Audit | Admin |\n\nMicrosoft states the same thing as a prerequisite on the sharing article itself: ***&quot;You must be an admin or member in your workspace to share an item in Microsoft Fabric.&quot;*** With only two qualifying roles, *&quot;principle of least privilege&quot;* picks the lower one — **Member**.\n\n- **C Contributor** ✘ — the near-miss that trips people up. Contributor can *create and write* items, which feels like plenty of power, but it is explicitly **not** granted Reshare. Write access and the right to hand out access are deliberately separate in Fabric.\n- **D Admin** ✘ — would work, but it also allows deleting the workspace, managing all access, and adding other admins. Strictly more privilege than the task needs, so it loses on the tiebreaker.\n- **B db_owner for Warehouse1** ✘ — a **SQL Server** database role, not a Fabric concept. Fabric warehouses use workspace roles plus item permissions plus T-SQL `GRANT`; there is no `db_owner` to assign. (Note the role assignment would also be at the wrong scope: the requirement is to share the *item*, which is a Fabric permission operation, not a database one.)\n\nUser1&#x27;s current **Viewer** role is the starting point the question gives you — read-only, no resharing — and assigning Member replaces it.\n\n**Escalation ladder for these questions: Viewer (read) → Contributor (write) → Member (write + share) → Admin (everything, including delete). &quot;Can share with others&quot; is the bright line between Contributor and Member.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Share your warehouse and manage permissions",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/share-warehouse-manage-permissions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q210"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q211",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q211",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a semantic model that contains the following tables:\n• Sales\n• Product\n• Location\nYou are creating a DAX measure named Measure1 that will calculate the percentage of all the sales of a selected product. Measure1 will use the\nfollowing DAX formula.\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Measure1 will return an error if there are no sales for all products.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Measure1 will return a decimal value that represents the ratio of the current product's total sales to the total sales across all products.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "The denominator of Measure1 will be calculated by using the modified filter context created by the REMOVEFILTERS('Product') DAX function.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**No — Yes — Yes.** The measure:\n\n```dax\n[Percentage of All Product Sales] =\nDIVIDE(\n    [Total Sales Amount],\n    CALCULATE(\n        [Total Sales Amount],\n        REMOVEFILTERS( &#x27;Product&#x27; )\n    )\n)\n```\n\nThis is the canonical &quot;percent of total&quot; pattern: the numerator is evaluated in the **current** filter context (the selected product), and the denominator re-evaluates the same measure with the `Product` filters stripped away, giving the all-products total.\n\n**1. Returns an error if there are no sales for all products → No.** This statement is testing whether you know `DIVIDE` from the `/` operator. If no product has any sales the denominator is blank/zero — and `DIVIDE(numerator, denominator [, alternateResult])` *&quot;performs division and returns an alternate result or BLANK() on division by 0&quot;*. No error is raised; you get `BLANK()` (or the alternate result, had one been supplied). Writing `[Total Sales Amount] / CALCULATE(...)` instead would risk an actual divide-by-zero. **That safety is the entire reason `DIVIDE` exists**, and the statement is false.\n\n**2. Returns a decimal ratio of the current product to all products → Yes.** Exactly what the expression computes. Note it returns a **ratio** (0.25), not a percentage (25%) — turning it into &quot;25.00%&quot; is a *formatting* concern, handled by the format string (see T1Q183 / T1Q218). The statement says &quot;decimal value… ratio&quot;, which is correct.\n\n**3. The denominator uses the modified filter context from `REMOVEFILTERS(&#x27;Product&#x27;)` → Yes.** `CALCULATE` evaluates its first argument in a filter context modified by the filter arguments that follow it. `REMOVEFILTERS(&#x27;Product&#x27;)` clears every filter on the entire `Product` table, so the inner `[Total Sales Amount]` is computed over all products regardless of what the visual has selected. That is the definition of the denominator here.\n\n*Two refinements worth carrying into the exam:*\n- `REMOVEFILTERS` is the modern, explicit name for `ALL` used as a `CALCULATE` modifier — it can **only** be used as a filter-removal modifier, whereas `ALL` doubles as a table function. Prefer `REMOVEFILTERS` for readability.\n- It clears filters on `Product` **only**. Filters from `Location` or a date slicer still apply to the denominator, so this measure means &quot;share of all products *within the rest of the current context*&quot;, which is usually what you want.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "DIVIDE function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/divide-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "REMOVEFILTERS function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/removefilters-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CALCULATE function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/calculate-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "DIVIDE function vs divide operator (/) in DAX",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/dax-divide-function-operator?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p456_i1.png, p456_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q211"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p456_i1.png",
      "p456_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p456_i3.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q212",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q212",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace that contains a large warehouse.\nYou plan to create a lakehouse named Lakehouse1 for a sales dataset. Lakehouse1 will contain the following tables:\n• Sales: Contains sales transactions\n• Stores: Contains a unique list of store names and locations\n• Loyalty: Contains a list of customers and their preferred stores\n• Customers: Contains a unique list of customer names and addresses\n• Products: Contains a unique list of available products and their descriptions\nYou need to configure a star schema for Lakehouse1.\nWhich table should you define as the fact table, and which type of relationship should you configure from the Sales table to the Customers table?\nTo answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Fact table:",
        "options": [
          "Customers",
          "Products",
          "Sales",
          "Stores"
        ],
        "answer": "Sales"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "Relationship (Sales → Customers):",
        "options": [
          "Many-to-one",
          "One-to-one",
          "One-to-many"
        ],
        "answer": "Many-to-one"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Fact table → Sales. Relationship → Many-to-one.**\n\n### The fact table\n\nA fact table records **events or transactions** and holds the numeric measures you aggregate; dimensions hold the descriptive attributes you slice by. Read the five descriptions and only one of them describes events:\n\n| Table | Description | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Sales** | *contains sales **transactions*** | **fact** |\n| Stores | *a **unique list** of store names and locations* | dimension |\n| Customers | *a **unique list** of customer names and addresses* | dimension |\n| Products | *a **unique list** of available products and descriptions* | dimension |\n| Loyalty | *a list of customers and their preferred stores* | **bridge / factless fact** — two foreign keys, no measures |\n\nThe giveaway is the repeated phrase *&quot;a unique list of…&quot;* — one row per entity means a dimension. `Sales` is the only table whose rows are occurrences rather than entities.\n\n*(`Loyalty` is the interesting one: it relates customers to stores and carries no measures, so it is a bridge, not a second fact table. Compare T1Q195 and T1Q203.)*\n\n### The cardinality — read it in the stated direction\n\nThe question asks for the relationship **from `Sales` to `Customers`**, and direction is the whole trick:\n\n- **Many** sales rows per customer — a customer buys repeatedly.\n- **One** customer row per sale — each transaction belongs to exactly one customer.\n\nReading `Sales → Customers` gives **many-to-one**. The *same relationship* read the other way, `Customers → Sales`, is **one-to-many** — which is why that option is sitting there. Always anchor on which table the question names first.\n\n- **One-to-one** ✘ — would mean each customer made exactly one purchase and each purchase had its own customer record. Contradicts a &quot;unique list of customer names&quot;.\n\nIn a star schema every fact-to-dimension relationship is many-to-one with the filter flowing **from** the dimension **to** the fact — dimensions filter facts, never the reverse.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Understand star schema and the importance for Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/star-schema?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Model relationships in Power BI Desktop — cardinality",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/transform-model/desktop-relationships-understand?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Dimensional modeling in Microsoft Fabric Warehouse",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/dimensional-modeling-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p457_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q212"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p457_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p457_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q213",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q213",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1 and a lakehouse named Lakehouse1.\nWarehouse1 contains a table named Table1. Lakehouse1 contains a delta table named Table2.\nYou need to persist data from Table1 to Table2. The solution must use a low-code interface.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Use a notebook to read the data from Table2 and insert the data into Table1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Use a notebook to read the data from Table1 and insert the data into Table2."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Use a Dataflow Gen2 to read the data from Table1 and insert the data into Table2."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Use stored procedure to read the data from Table2 and insert the data into Table1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**C — use a Dataflow Gen2 to read the data from Table1 and insert it into Table2.** Two filters narrow the four options down to one.\n\n**Filter 1 — direction.** *&quot;Persist data **from Table1 to Table2**&quot;*: read from **Warehouse1.Table1**, write to **Lakehouse1.Table2**. Options **A** and **D** both run the other way (read Table2, insert into Table1), so they are eliminated on direction alone — before you consider the tooling at all. Always check the direction in this style of question; it is the cheapest elimination available.\n\n**Filter 2 — *&quot;must use a low-code interface&quot;*.** That leaves B and C:\n\n- **B a notebook** ✘ — correct direction, wrong tooling. A notebook means hand-written PySpark or Spark SQL. That is **code**, which the requirement rules out. (Technically it would work fine, which is what makes it the intended distractor.)\n- **C a Dataflow Gen2** ✔ — authored in **Power Query**, a graphical, no-code/low-code experience, and it supports a **lakehouse as a data destination**. Point it at the warehouse&#x27;s SQL analytics endpoint as the source, set Lakehouse1 as the destination, and it writes Table2 as a Delta table.\n\nNote that &quot;persist&quot; matters too: the data must actually be **written** into Lakehouse1, not merely referenced. A shortcut would surface Table1 inside Lakehouse1 without copying it — low-code, but it does not persist anything (compare T1Q173). A Dataflow Gen2 materialises the rows.\n\n**Rule: &quot;low-code / no-code&quot; ⇒ Dataflow Gen2, a pipeline Copy activity, or a copy job. &quot;notebook / Spark job definition&quot; ⇒ code, and is excluded by that phrase.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Dataflow Gen2 data destinations and managed settings",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-factory/dataflow-gen2-data-destinations-and-managed-settings?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Fabric decision guide: copy activity, copy job, dataflow, Eventstream, or Spark",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/decision-guide-pipeline-dataflow-spark?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q213"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q214",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q214",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains a table named Date. Date contains the following data.\nYou need to ensure that the visuals that use the Date table appear in chronological order. The solutions must minimize administrative effort.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Sort the Month Number column by Column."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Sort the visuals by using the Sort ascending option on Fiscal Month."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Sort the Fiscal Month column by Column."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Sort the Date table by Column."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**C — sort the `Fiscal Month` column by another column** (namely `Month Number`).\n\nThe problem: `Fiscal Month` holds text — `Jan`, `Feb`, `Mar`… — and Power BI sorts text **alphabetically** by default, producing *Apr, Aug, Dec, Feb, Jan…* on every axis and in every slicer. The `Month Number` column (1–12) already encodes the right order; you just have to tell the model to use it.\n\n**Column tools → Sort by column → Month Number**, applied once to `Fiscal Month`. Every visual in every report using that column then renders chronologically, forever. That is what wins on *&quot;minimize administrative effort&quot;* — it is a **model-level** fix, done once.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A Sort the `Month Number` column by Column** ✘ — the 20% answer, and it has the two columns the wrong way round. `Month Number` is already numeric and already sorts correctly (1, 2, 3…); sorting it *by* `Fiscal Month` would actively break it into alphabetical order. The rule is: **sort the column you display, by the column that holds the order.**\n- **B Sort the visuals by using Sort ascending on Fiscal Month** ✘ — the maximum-effort answer. It is a **per-visual** setting that has to be reapplied to every chart, every slicer and every new report — and it sorts alphabetically anyway, which is the original problem.\n- **D Sort the `Date` table by Column** ✘ — Sort by Column is a **column** property; there is no table-level equivalent.\n\n*Two gotchas worth knowing:* the sorting column must have a **one-to-one (or many-to-one) correspondence** with the displayed column — each `Fiscal Month` value must map to exactly one `Month Number`, or Power BI rejects the setting. And remember to **hide** `Month Number` afterwards so report authors are not tempted to use it directly.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Sort one column by another column in Power BI",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-sort-by-column?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Create date tables in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/model-date-tables?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p460_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q214"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p460_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q215",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q215",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains a fact table named FactSales. FactSales has a\nrelationship to a dimension table named DimDate on a column named OrderDate.\nTo FactSales, you add a second relationship between FactSales and DimDate based on a column named ShippedDate.\nYou need to filter by using ShippedDate in a new report. The solution must NOT affect the function of existing reports.\nWhich two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution.\nNOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Enable the ShippedDate relationship."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Use a RELATEDTABLE DAX function."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Use a CROSSFILTER DAX function."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Use a USERELATIONSHIP DAX function."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Disable the ShippedDate relationship between the tables."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**D + E — disable the `ShippedDate` relationship, and use `USERELATIONSHIP` in the new measures.**\n\nA tabular model allows **only one active relationship between any two tables**. `FactSales` already has an active relationship to `DimDate` on `OrderDate`, so the new `ShippedDate` relationship must be **inactive** — the model will not even permit two active ones, and Power BI creates the second as inactive (dashed line) automatically.\n\nThe pair of answers maps onto the two requirements:\n\n- **E Disable the ShippedDate relationship** → *&quot;must NOT affect the function of existing reports.&quot;* Every existing measure resolves through the active `OrderDate` path. Leaving `ShippedDate` inactive guarantees nothing about that changes.\n- **D Use `USERELATIONSHIP`** → *&quot;filter by using ShippedDate in a new report.&quot;* Inside `CALCULATE`, it activates an inactive relationship **for that evaluation only**:\n\n```dax\nSales by Ship Date =\nCALCULATE(\n    [Total Sales],\n    USERELATIONSHIP( FactSales[ShippedDate], DimDate[Date] )\n)\n```\n\nThe new measure follows the ship-date path; every other measure in the model is untouched. This is the **role-playing dimension** pattern — one `DimDate` serving several date roles.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **A Enable the ShippedDate relationship** ✘ — the direct contradiction of the requirement. If it could even be done, it would deactivate the `OrderDate` relationship and silently change the meaning of **every existing report**.\n- **B `RELATEDTABLE`** ✘ — returns the related rows of another table (the table-valued counterpart of `RELATED`). It follows whichever relationship is **active**; it cannot switch to an inactive one.\n- **C `CROSSFILTER`** ✘ — the tempting one, because it *is* a `CALCULATE` relationship modifier. But it changes a relationship&#x27;s **cross-filter direction** (`OneWay`, `Both`, `None`) — it cannot activate an inactive relationship. `USERELATIONSHIP` changes *which* relationship is used; `CROSSFILTER` changes *how* filters travel along one.\n\n*Alternative worth knowing (not offered here):* duplicate `DimDate` into `DimOrderDate` and `DimShipDate` and make both relationships active. That avoids `USERELATIONSHIP` in every measure at the cost of a second date table, and is often preferred when report authors — not modellers — will be building the visuals.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "USERELATIONSHIP function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/userelationship-function-dax?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Active vs inactive relationship guidance",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/guidance/relationships-active-inactive?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "CROSSFILTER function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/crossfilter-function?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
