  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q216",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q216",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant.\nYou need to create a semantic model that delivers fast report rendering, minimizes query latency, and maximizes flexibility for DAX calculations.\nWhich storage mode should you select?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Import"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Direct Lake"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Composite Mode"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DirectQuery"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**A — Import.** Three requirements are listed, and the **third** is the one that decides it.\n\n| Requirement | Import | Direct Lake | DirectQuery | Composite |\n|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n| Fast report rendering | ✔ | ✔ | ✘ | mixed |\n| Minimal query latency | ✔ | ✔ | ✘ | mixed |\n| **Maximum DAX flexibility** | **✔** | **✘** | ✘ | mixed |\n\n**Import wins on DAX flexibility.** The full model is loaded into the VertiPaq in-memory engine, so **everything** is available: calculated columns, calculated tables, every DAX function, any relationship configuration, no formula restrictions.\n\n**Direct Lake is the 33% answer and the one to think about.** It genuinely delivers the first two requirements — near-Import query speed by loading Delta/Parquet columns straight into memory on demand, with no refresh. But it is **restricted on DAX**: Direct Lake tables do not support **calculated columns or calculated tables**, and certain modelling constructs are unavailable. A model that hits an unsupported construct also **falls back to DirectQuery**, at which point requirements 1 and 2 collapse too. When a question explicitly asks for *maximum flexibility for DAX calculations*, that clause exists to exclude Direct Lake.\n\n- **D DirectQuery** ✘ — fails every requirement. Each visual issues a live query to the source, so rendering is slow, latency is high, and DAX is the most restricted of all (no calculated tables, many functions blocked, and measures must be foldable).\n- **C Composite** ✘ — mixes storage modes per table. Useful for real engineering trade-offs (see T1Q205), but it *adds* complexity and its DirectQuery portions inherit DirectQuery&#x27;s limits. It maximises nothing.\n\n*The trade-off Direct Lake buys you, which this question deliberately does not ask for, is **data freshness** — no refresh cycle, data is current as of the last Delta commit. If the stem had said &quot;always reflects the latest data in OneLake&quot;, the answer would flip to B.*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Direct Lake overview — comparison to other storage modes",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Direct Lake — considerations and limitations",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/direct-lake-overview?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#considerations-and-limitations"
      },
      {
        "title": "Semantic model modes in the Power BI service",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/connect-data/service-dataset-modes-understand?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q216"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q217",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q217",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1.\nYou need to ensure that User1 can perform the following tasks in Workspace1:\n• Create dashboards.\n• Publish semantic models.\n• Create and publish reports.\nThe solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhich workspace role should you assign to User1?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Contributor"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Member"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Viewer"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Admin"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**A — Contributor.** Walk the three tasks down the role ladder and find the lowest rung that clears all of them:\n\n| Task | Viewer | **Contributor** | Member | Admin |\n|---|:-:|:-:|:-:|:-:|\n| Create dashboards | ✘ | **✔** | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Publish semantic models | ✘ | **✔** | ✔ | ✔ |\n| Create and publish reports | ✘ | **✔** | ✔ | ✔ |\n| *(not required)* Add members / share items | ✘ | ✘ | ✔ | ✔ |\n| *(not required)* Update or delete the workspace | ✘ | ✘ | ✘ | ✔ |\n\nAll three requirements are **create-and-publish content** operations, which is exactly what Contributor is defined to allow: create, edit and delete items in the workspace, publish reports and semantic models, schedule refreshes.\n\n- **C Viewer** ✘ — read-only. Can open reports and dashboards; cannot create or publish anything.\n- **B Member** ✘ — clears all three, but also adds the right to **share items and add other users**. Nothing in the requirements asks for that, so it fails least privilege.\n- **D Admin** ✘ — clears all three, and can also delete the workspace and manage all access. Furthest from least privilege.\n\n**The decision rule for every Fabric role question: list what the user must *do*, find the smallest role that covers all of it, and stop.** Contributor is the answer whenever the tasks are all &quot;make and publish content&quot;; Member becomes necessary the moment &quot;share with others&quot; or &quot;add users&quot; appears (see T1Q210, T1Q172).</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Roles in workspaces in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/fundamentals/roles-workspaces?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Give users access to workspaces (Power BI)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/collaborate-share/service-give-access-new-workspaces?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q217"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q218",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q218",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You are designing a Fabric semantic model named Model1 for a financial analytics workspace. Model1 includes a DAX measure named Measure1\nthat returns total sales.\nYou have a table named Currency that contains a column named Format. The Format column defines currency formatting strings for each record\nas follows: $#, ##0.00, €#, ##0.00, OR ¥#,##0.00.\nYou have a report that uses the Currency column to identify which symbol and format string to display. In the report, Measure1 must appear with\ntwo decimal places and the appropriate currency symbol based on the selected value in the Currency column.\nYou create the following DAX formula.\nFORMAT = SELECTEDVALUE( ‘Currency’ [Format])\nYou need to ensure that Measure appears correctly in visuals. The solution must minimize logic duplication.\nWhat should you do?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Create a dynamic format string for Measure1 that references the FORMAT measure."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "For Model1, set the data category of Measure1 to Country/Region."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "For Model1, set the format type of Measure1 to Currency."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Copy the DAX expression from the Format column and paste the expression into the format string expression of Measure1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**A — create a dynamic format string for Measure1 that references the `FORMAT` measure.**\n\nA **dynamic format string** attaches a *second* DAX expression to a measure whose result is the **format string** to apply, re-evaluated in the current filter context. Here that expression is a one-liner that simply reuses the measure you already wrote:\n\n```dax\n-- existing helper measure\nFORMAT = SELECTEDVALUE( &#x27;Currency&#x27;[Format] )\n\n-- Measure1 → Format → Dynamic → format string expression:\n[FORMAT]\n```\n\nWhatever currency the user selects, `SELECTEDVALUE` returns that row&#x27;s format string (`$#,##0.00`, `€#,##0.00`, `¥#,##0.00`), the measure renders with the right symbol, and the `0.00` in each string guarantees two decimal places. The formatting logic lives **once**, in the `Currency` table — which is what *&quot;minimize logic duplication&quot;* is asking for.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **D Copy the DAX expression from the Format column and paste it into Measure1&#x27;s format string expression** ✘ — the answer the phrase *&quot;minimize logic duplication&quot;* exists to eliminate. It produces exactly the duplication you were told to avoid: two copies of the same logic that must now be kept in sync, and a new currency means editing the measure instead of adding a row to a table. Referencing `[FORMAT]` is the whole point.\n- **C Set the format type of Measure1 to Currency** ✘ — a **static** format. It pins one symbol (whatever the model culture gives you) to the measure permanently. It cannot respond to the user&#x27;s selection, which is the entire requirement.\n- **B Set the data category of Measure1 to Country/Region** ✘ — data categories tag columns for **visual** semantics (map geocoding, web URLs, image URLs). They have nothing to do with number formatting, and `Country/Region` would only affect how a map plots a field.\n\n*Where to set it:* select Measure1 → **Measure tools → Format → Dynamic** → then switch the formula bar dropdown from the measure expression to **Format** and enter `[FORMAT]`. (Same mechanism as T1Q183 — recognise the **Format: Dynamic** dropdown in an exhibit.)\n\n**Rule: &quot;the format must change based on a user selection or slicer&quot; ⇒ dynamic format string. &quot;One fixed format&quot; ⇒ the static Format property.**</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Dynamic format strings for measures (Power BI)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-dynamic-format-strings?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SELECTEDVALUE function (DAX)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/dax/selectedvalue-function?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Custom format strings in Power BI Desktop",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/power-bi/create-reports/desktop-custom-format-strings?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q218"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [],
    "missingAnswerImages": []
