  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q219",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q219",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a data warehouse that contains the following tables:\n• DimCustomer\n• DimEmployee\n• DimGeography\n• FactInternetSales\nEach table has a primary key and the following relationships:\n• CustomerKey = FactInternetSales.CustomerKey\n• EmployeeKey = FactInternetSales.EmployeeKey\n• Geography = DimCustomer.GeographyKey\nYou have a T-SQL query named Query1 that contains the following statements.\n\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "Query1 will return multiple rows for a single customer if the customer has placed more than one online sales order.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "If a customer record in DimCustomer has a GeographyKey value that does NOT exist in DimGeography, the customer's sales data will still appear in the Query1 results.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "2",
        "label": "Adding the clause GROUP BY g.EnglishCountryRegionName to Query1 will calculate the total sales amount by country.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**Yes — No — No.** The query is a plain fact-to-dimension join with **no aggregation and no grouping**:\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.CustomerKey, c.FirstName, c.LastName,\n       g.CountryRegionCode, g.EnglishCountryRegionName AS Country,\n       e.EmployeeKey, e.FirstName AS SalesPersonFirstName, e.LastName AS SalesPersonLastName,\n       f.SalesOrderNumber, f.OrderDate, f.SalesAmount\nFROM FactInternetSales AS f\nINNER JOIN DimCustomer AS c ON f.CustomerKey = c.CustomerKey\nINNER JOIN DimEmployee AS e ON f.EmployeeKey = e.EmployeeKey\nINNER JOIN DimGeography AS g ON c.GeographyKey = g.GeographyKey;\n```\n\n**1. Multiple rows for one customer with several orders → Yes.** The query is at **fact grain**: one row per `FactInternetSales` row. Joining a fact to a dimension on a many-to-one key does not collapse anything — the customer&#x27;s attributes are simply repeated on each of their order rows. Three orders, three rows. (`SELECT` has no `DISTINCT` and no `GROUP BY` to change that.)\n\n**2. Customer with an orphan GeographyKey still appears → No.** This is the statement the question is really built on. The join to `DimGeography` is an **`INNER JOIN`**, which keeps only rows that match on **both** sides. A `GeographyKey` with no counterpart in `DimGeography` fails the predicate, so that customer&#x27;s rows are **silently dropped from the result set entirely** — their sales disappear, even though `FactInternetSales` and `DimCustomer` matched perfectly. This is the classic referential-integrity trap: one bad dimension key quietly deletes revenue from the report. A `LEFT OUTER JOIN` to `DimGeography` would preserve the rows with `NULL` country (compare T1Q177).\n\n**3. Adding `GROUP BY g.EnglishCountryRegionName` totals sales by country → No.** Two independent reasons, either one fatal:\n- **It would not even run.** Every column in the `SELECT` list must be either in the `GROUP BY` or wrapped in an aggregate. Grouping by country alone leaves `CustomerKey`, `FirstName`, `SalesOrderNumber`, `OrderDate` and nine others unaccounted for → *&quot;Column … is invalid in the select list because it is not contained in either an aggregate function or the GROUP BY clause.&quot;*\n- **There is no aggregate to total.** `f.SalesAmount` is selected raw. `GROUP BY` does not sum anything by itself — you would have to change it to `SUM(f.SalesAmount)` **and** reduce the `SELECT` list to the grouped columns.\n\n**`GROUP BY` alone never aggregates — it only defines the groups. The aggregation has to be written explicitly in the `SELECT` list.**\n\n*(The exhibit contains two typos carried over from the exam — `DimCustomr` and `f.Employee`. Neither affects the three statements.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "FROM clause plus JOIN, APPLY, PIVOT (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/from-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "SELECT — GROUP BY (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/queries/select-group-by-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "Aggregate functions (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/functions/aggregate-functions-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p465_i1.png, p465_i2.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q219"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p465_i1.png",
      "p465_i2.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p466_i1.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q220",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q220",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a notebook.\nYou have a Parquet file named invoice1 that contains a column named InvoiceDateKey of the timestamp data type.\nYou need to create a PySpark script that will import invoice1 and create a table named fact_sale in LH1. The solution must meet the following\nrequirements:\n• A new column named Year must be added to fact_sale.\n• The values in Year must use the InvoiceDateKey column.\nHow should you complete the script? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.\nNOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "0",
        "label": "df = df.[answer choice]('Year', year( … ('InvoiceDateKey')))",
        "options": [
          "transform",
          "withColumn",
          "withColumnRenamed",
          "withMetadata"
        ],
        "answer": "withColumn"
      },
      {
        "id": "1",
        "label": "df = df.withColumn('Year', year( [answer choice] ('InvoiceDateKey')))",
        "options": [
          "col",
          "extract",
          "lit"
        ],
        "answer": "col"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**`withColumn`** and **`col`**. The finished script:\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import *\ntable_name = &#x27;Table/fact_sale&#x27;\ndf = spark.read.format(&quot;parquet&quot;).load(&#x27;Files/parquet/full/invoice1&#x27;)\ndf = df.withColumn(&#x27;Year&#x27;, year(col(&quot;InvoiceDateKey&quot;)))\ndf.write.mode(&quot;overwrite&quot;).format(&quot;delta&quot;).partitionBy(&quot;Year&quot;).save(table_name)\n```\n\n### Blank 1 — `withColumn`\n\nThe requirement is *&quot;a **new column** named `Year` must be **added**&quot;*, and `DataFrame.withColumn(colName, col)` is the PySpark method for exactly that: it returns a new DataFrame with a column added (or replaced, if the name already exists). Its signature — a **name** followed by a **Column expression** — matches the two arguments visible in the exhibit.\n\n| Method | What it does | |\n|---|---|:-:|\n| **`withColumn(name, expr)`** | **add or replace a column** | **✔** |\n| `withColumnRenamed(old, new)` | **rename** an existing column — takes two *strings*, adds nothing | ✘ |\n| `withMetadata(name, metadata)` | attach metadata to an existing column; no data change | ✘ |\n| `transform(func)` | apply a whole function to the DataFrame for chaining; takes a callable, not `(name, expr)` | ✘ |\n\n### Blank 2 — `col`\n\n`year()` needs a **Column object**, and `col(&quot;InvoiceDateKey&quot;)` is how you reference an existing column by name. `year()` then extracts the year from the timestamp — which is the second requirement, *&quot;the values in `Year` must use the `InvoiceDateKey` column&quot;*.\n\n- **`lit`** ✘ — the instructive wrong answer. `lit(&quot;InvoiceDateKey&quot;)` creates a **literal constant**: it would hand `year()` the *string* `&quot;InvoiceDateKey&quot;` rather than the column&#x27;s values, so every row would get the same (null) result. `lit` is for constants, `col` is for columns.\n- **`extract`** ✘ — not a function for this. Spark SQL has an `extract(field FROM source)` **expression** (`extract(YEAR FROM ts)`), but it is not the way to reference a column inside `year()`, and wrapping it here is syntactically wrong.\n\n*(`df[&quot;InvoiceDateKey&quot;]` and `F.col(...)` are equivalent ways to write the same reference; the wildcard import at the top is what makes the bare `year` and `col` available.)*\n\nThe final line writes the result as a **Delta** table partitioned by the new `Year` column — the standard lakehouse landing pattern.</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Lakehouse and Delta tables in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-engineering/lakehouse-and-delta-tables?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [
      "Original question exhibit files not supplied: p467_i1.png"
    ],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q220"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "missingExhibits": [
      "p467_i1.png"
    ],
    "missingAnswerImages": [
      "p467_i2.png"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "new-json-t1q221",
    "sourceId": "json:T1Q221",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<pre>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains the following items:\n• A warehouse named Warehouse1\n• A semantic model named Model1\n• An interactive report named Report1\nYou need to allow a user named User1 to access a single table in Warehouse1. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege.\nWhat should you do first?</pre>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Assign the Viewer role to User1 for Workspace1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Share Warehouse1 with User1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Assign object-level permissions to User1 for Warehouse1."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Assign the db_datareader role to User1 for Warehouse1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<pre>**B — share Warehouse1 with User1** (with no additional permissions selected).\n\n&gt; ⚠️ **Flagged: this overrides the PDF answer key,** which marks **C** (assign object-level permissions). The discussion leans C (75%), but the lone dissenter is quoting Microsoft&#x27;s documented procedure, and the question asks what to do ***first***.\n\nMicrosoft spells out the two-step recipe for exactly this requirement:\n\n&gt; *&quot;If you want to provide **granular access to specific objects** within the Warehouse, **share the Warehouse with no additional permissions**, then provide granular access to specific objects using T-SQL `GRANT` statement.&quot;*\n\nThe order is not arbitrary. Sharing grants the **Read** permission, which is *&quot;the equivalent of `CONNECT` permissions in SQL Server&quot;* — without it User1 cannot even open a connection to the warehouse, and a `GRANT SELECT ON dbo.Table1` would have no one to apply to. You must open the door before you can unlock one room inside it.\n\n| Step | Action | Effect |\n|:-:|---|---|\n| **1** | **Share Warehouse1, no boxes ticked** | **Read** → User1 can *connect*, sees no data |\n| 2 | `GRANT SELECT ON dbo.Table1 TO [User1]` | User1 can query **that one table** |\n\nThe result is the narrowest possible access: one table, in one warehouse, and nothing else in Workspace1 — which is what *&quot;principle of least privilege&quot;* demands.\n\nWhy the others fail:\n\n- **C Assign object-level permissions** ✘ *as the **first** step* — this is step **2**, and it is the right *second* action. Attempting it first leaves User1 unable to connect. (If your exam marks C, the examiner is reading &quot;first&quot; loosely; know the dependency either way.)\n- **A Assign the Viewer role for Workspace1** ✘ — workspace-wide. Viewer carries **ReadData**, so User1 would be able to read **every table in Warehouse1**, plus see Model1 and Report1. Far broader than one table.\n- **D Assign the db_datareader role** ✘ — grants `SELECT` on **all** tables and views. It is the definition of the opposite of granular, and Fabric warehouses use item permissions plus T-SQL `GRANT` rather than SQL Server database roles.\n\n*(Same Read-vs-ReadData distinction as T1Q193, applied one step further.)*</pre>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [
      {
        "title": "Share your warehouse and manage permissions — limitations",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/share-warehouse-manage-permissions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938#limitations"
      },
      {
        "title": "SQL granular permissions in Microsoft Fabric",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/fabric/data-warehouse/sql-granular-permissions?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      },
      {
        "title": "GRANT object permissions (Transact-SQL)",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/sql/t-sql/statements/grant-object-permissions-transact-sql?wt.mc_id=studentamb_657938"
      }
    ],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "json:T1Q221"
    ],
