  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-6-r6",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:6",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You need to create a DAX measure to calculate the average overall satisfaction score. How should you complete the DAX code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<pre><code>Rolling 12 Overall Satisfaction =\nVAR NumberOfMonths = 12\nVAR LastCurrentDate = MAX ( &#x27;Date&#x27; [Date] )\nVAR Period = DATESINPERIOD ( &#x27;Date&#x27; [Date], LastCurrentDate, - NumberOfMonths, MONTH )\nVAR Result =\n    CALCULATE (\n        [dropdown 1],\n        [dropdown 2]\n        &#x27;Survey Question&#x27;[Question Title] = &quot;Overall Satisfaction&quot;\n    )\nRETURN\n    Result</code></pre>\n<ul><li>Dropdown 1: AVERAGE(&#x27;Survey&#x27;[Response Value]), / AVERAGEA(&#x27;Question&#x27;[Question Text]), / AVERAGEX(VALUES(&#x27;Survey&#x27;[Customer Key]),</li><li>Dropdown 2: LastCurrentDate, / NumberOfMonths, / Period,</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>Rolling 12 Overall Satisfaction =\nVAR NumberOfMonths = 12\nVAR LastCurrentDate = MAX ( &#x27;Date&#x27; [Date] )\nVAR Period = DATESINPERIOD ( &#x27;Date&#x27; [Date], LastCurrentDate, - NumberOfMonths, MONTH )\nVAR Result =\n    CALCULATE (\n        [dropdown 1],\n        [dropdown 2]\n        &#x27;Survey Question&#x27;[Question Title] = &quot;Overall Satisfaction&quot;\n    )\nRETURN\n    Result</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "AVERAGE('Survey'[Response Value]),",
          "AVERAGEA('Question'[Question Text]),",
          "AVERAGEX(VALUES('Survey'[Customer Key]),"
        ],
        "answer": "AVERAGE('Survey'[Response Value]),"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "LastCurrentDate,",
          "NumberOfMonths,",
          "Period,"
        ],
        "answer": "Period,"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. Average(Survey[Response Value]) as this one is the only value that can be aggregated.</p>\n<p>Customer Key Does not make any sense , as it just a key to identify a customer, this can never provide us the score.</p>\n<p>2. Period : Variable is defined as to select 1 year of date range. Can directly be passed in the Filter context of Calculate formula.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:6:row6"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Litware"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-7-r7",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:7",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You need to resolve the issue with the pricing group classification. How should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<pre><code>CREATE [dropdown 1] [dbo].[ProductsWithPricingGroup]\nAS\nSELECT ProductId,\n    ProductName,\n    ProductCategory,\n    ListPrice,\n    [dropdown 2]\n        WHEN ListPrice &lt;= 50 THEN &#x27;low&#x27;\n        [dropdown 3]\n        WHEN ListPrice &gt; 1000 THEN &#x27;high&#x27;\n    END AS PricingGroup\nFROM dbo.Products</code></pre>\n<ul><li>Dropdown 1: FUNCTION / PROCEDURE / TABLE / VIEW</li><li>Dropdown 2: CASE / COALESCE / IIF / SET</li><li>Dropdown 3: WHEN(ListPrice &gt; 50 AND ListPrice &lt; 1000) THEN &#x27;medium&#x27; / WHEN(ListPrice &gt;= 50 AND ListPrice &lt; 1000) THEN &#x27;medium&#x27; / WHEN ListPrice BETWEEN 50 AND 1000) THEN &#x27;medium&#x27;</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>CREATE [dropdown 1] [dbo].[ProductsWithPricingGroup]\nAS\nSELECT ProductId,\n    ProductName,\n    ProductCategory,\n    ListPrice,\n    [dropdown 2]\n        WHEN ListPrice &lt;= 50 THEN &#x27;low&#x27;\n        [dropdown 3]\n        WHEN ListPrice &gt; 1000 THEN &#x27;high&#x27;\n    END AS PricingGroup\nFROM dbo.Products</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "FUNCTION",
          "PROCEDURE",
          "TABLE",
          "VIEW"
        ],
        "answer": "VIEW"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "CASE",
          "COALESCE",
          "IIF",
          "SET"
        ],
        "answer": "CASE"
      },
      {
        "id": "d3",
        "label": "d3",
        "options": [
          "WHEN(ListPrice > 50 AND ListPrice < 1000) THEN 'medium'",
          "WHEN(ListPrice >= 50 AND ListPrice < 1000) THEN 'medium'",
          "WHEN ListPrice BETWEEN 50 AND 1000) THEN 'medium'"
        ],
        "answer": "WHEN ListPrice BETWEEN 50 AND 1000) THEN 'medium'"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>VIEW: from an existing table.</p>\n<p>* CASE: correct syntax before the WHENs.</p>\n<p>* WHEN ListPrice BETWEEN 50 AND 1000 THEN &#x27;medium&#x27;: the other two options miss value 1000; on the other hand, the BETWEEN includes both 50 and 1000.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:7:row7"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Litware"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-8-r8",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:8",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>What should you recommend using to ingest the customer data into the data store in the AnalyticsPOC workspace?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a stored procedure"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a pipeline that contains a KQL activity"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a Spark notebook"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "a dataflow"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>D. a dataflow.</p><p>A dataflow ingests the customer data into the data store and applies any needed transformations with Power Query before loading it. A stored procedure executes SQL logic inside a database, a pipeline with a KQL activity targets KQL databases, and a Spark notebook is a code-first compute tool rather than the ingestion service for this scenario.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:8:row8"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Litware"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-9-r9",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:9",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Which type of data store should you recommend in the AnalyticsPOC workspace?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a data lake"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a warehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "an external Hive metastore"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A lakehouse The data store must handle semi-structured and unstructured data, therefore a Lakehouse should be the optimal solution supporting read access with T-SQL and Python.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:9:row9"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Litware"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-10-r10",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:10",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named Staging.Sales. Staging.Sales contains the following columns.</p>\n<p>You need to write a T-SQL query that will return data for the year 2023 that displays ProductID and ProductName and has a summarized Amount that is higher than 10,000. Which query should you use?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Staging.Sales columns</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Data type</th><th>Nullable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ProductID</td><td>Integer</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>ProductName</td><td>Varchar(30)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>SalesDate</td><td>Datetime2(6)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>WholesalePrice</td><td>Decimal(18,2)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Amount</td><td>Decimal(18,2)</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "SELECT ProductID, ProductName, SUM(Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Staging.Sales\nWHERE DATEPART(YEAR,SaleDate) = '2023'\nGROUP BY ProductID, ProductName\nHAVING SUM(Amount) > 10000"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "SELECT ProductID, ProductName, SUM(Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Staging.Sales\nGROUP BY ProductID, ProductName\nHAVING DATEPART(YEAR,SaleDate) = '2023' AND SUM(Amount) > 10000"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "SELECT ProductID, ProductName, SUM(Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Staging.Sales\nWHERE DATEPART(YEAR,SaleDate) = '2023' AND SUM(Amount) > 10000\nGROUP BY ProductID, ProductName"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "SELECT ProductID, ProductName, SUM(Amount) AS TotalAmount\nFROM Staging.Sales\nWHERE DATEPART(YEAR,SaleDate) = '2023'\nGROUP BY ProductID, ProductName\nHAVING TotalAmount > 10000"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>SELECT ProductID, ProductName, SUM(Amount) AS TotalAmount</p>\n<p>FROM Staging.Sales</p>\n<p>WHERE DATEPART(YEAR, SaleDate) = &#x27;2023&#x27;</p>\n<p>GROUP BY ProductID, ProductName</p>\n<p>HAVING SUM(Amount) &gt; 10000</p>\n<p>Answer A is the only answer with valid syntax.</p>\n<p>Answer B is excluded because the filter on year should be included in the WHERE-clause. It is the result of a function, but it is not an aggregate.</p>\n<p>Answer C is excluded because the filter on SUM(Amount) should be included in the HAVING-clause, since this is an aggregate.</p>\n<p>Answer D is excluded because the HAVING-clause does not accept aliases.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:10:row10"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-11-r11",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:11",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a data warehouse that contains a table named Stage.Customers. Stage.Customers contains all the customer record updates from a customer relationship management (CRM) system. There can be multiple updates per customer. You need to write a T-SQL query that will return the customer ID, name. postal code, and the last updated time of the most recent row for each customer ID. How should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<pre><code>WITH CUSTOMERBASE AS (\n    SELECT [CustomerID], [CustomerName], [PostalCode], [LastUpdated],\n    x = [dropdown 1] OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY LastUpdated DESC)\n    FROM [LakehousePOC].[dbo].[CustomerChanges]\n)\nSELECT CustomerID, CustomerName, PostalCode, LastUpdated\nFROM CUSTOMERBASE\n[dropdown 2]</code></pre>\n<ul><li>Dropdown 1: LAST_Value() / NTILE() / ROW_NUMBER()</li><li>Dropdown 2: Having Max(LastUpdated) = 1 / WHERE LastUpdated = Max(LastUpdated) / WHERE X = 1</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>WITH CUSTOMERBASE AS (\n    SELECT [CustomerID], [CustomerName], [PostalCode], [LastUpdated],\n    x = [dropdown 1] OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerID ORDER BY LastUpdated DESC)\n    FROM [LakehousePOC].[dbo].[CustomerChanges]\n)\nSELECT CustomerID, CustomerName, PostalCode, LastUpdated\nFROM CUSTOMERBASE\n[dropdown 2]</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "LAST_Value()",
          "NTILE()",
          "ROW_NUMBER()"
        ],
        "answer": "ROW_NUMBER()"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "Having Max(LastUpdated) = 1",
          "WHERE LastUpdated = Max(LastUpdated)",
          "WHERE X = 1"
        ],
        "answer": "WHERE X = 1"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Box 1: ROW_NUMBER()</p>\n<p>ROW_NUMBER() is a window function that assigns a unique sequential number to each row within a partition, based on the ORDER BY clause. In this case, it is used to assign a row number to each CustomerID, ordering by LastUpdated DESC.</p>\n<p>This ensures that the most recent record for each CustomerID gets ROW_NUMBER() = 1.</p>\n<p>Box 2: WHERE X = 1</p>\n<p>The alias X stores the row number assigned by ROW_NUMBER().</p>\n<p>Since we only want the most recent row per CustomerID, we filter for X = 1.</p>\n<p>This approach ensures we get only the latest entry for each customer, based on LastUpdated.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:11:row11"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-12-r12",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:12",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant. You plan to create a Fabric notebook that will use Spark DataFrames to generate Microsoft Power BI visuals. You run the following code.</p>\n<p>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<pre><code>from powerbiclient import QuickVisualize, get_dataset_config, Report\n\nPBI_visualize = QuickVisualize(get_dataset_config(df))\nPBI_visualize</code></pre>\n<ul><li>The code embeds an existing Power BI report. — Yes / No</li><li>The code creates a Power BI report. — Yes / No</li><li>The code displays a summary of the DataFrame. — Yes / No</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "label": "The code embeds an existing Power BI report.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "s2",
        "label": "The code creates a Power BI report.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "s3",
        "label": "The code displays a summary of the DataFrame.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>&quot;The code embeds an existing Power BI report.&quot;</p>\n<p>Answer: No</p>\n<p>Embedding a Power BI report typically involves using the Power BI Embedded API, which is not indicated here.</p>\n<p>&quot;The code creates a Power BI report.&quot;</p>\n<p>Answer: Yes</p>\n<p>If the code generates a new Power BI report (e.g., using Python, Power BI REST API, or Power BI Desktop automation), then this is correct.</p>\n<p>&quot;The code displays a summary of the DataFrame.&quot;</p>\n<p>Answer: No</p>\n<p>Displaying a summary of a DataFrame would involve functions like df.describe(), df.info(), or df.head() in Pandas. Since this is about Power BI, this function is not relevant.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:12:row12"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-13-r13",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:13",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are the administrator of a Fabric workspace that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains the following tables: Table1: A Delta table created by using a shortcut Table2: An external table created by using Spark Table3: A managed table - You plan to connect to Lakehouse1 by using its SQL endpoint. What will you be able to do after connecting to Lakehouse1?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Read Table3."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Update the data Table3."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Read Table2."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Update the data in Table1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Read Table3.</p>\n<p>When you connect to a Lakehouse SQL endpoint in Microsoft Fabric, you are connecting to:</p>\n<p>The SQL analytics endpoint (read-only T-SQL surface over lakehouse tables)</p>\n<p>The SQL endpoint has important limitations.</p>Key follows from the explanation.",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:13:row13"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-14-r14",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:14",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse. You use a dataflow to load a new dataset from OneLake to the warehouse. You need to add a PowerQuery step to identify the maximum values for the numeric columns. Which function should you include in the step?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Table.MaxN"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Table.Max"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Table.Range"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Table.Profile"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The Table.Profile function in PowerQuery is specifically designed to provide statistical information about a table&#x27;s columns, including the maximum values for numeric columns. When you apply Table.Profile to your dataset, it generates a summary of each column, which includes the minimum, maximum, average, standard deviation, count of distinct values, and other useful statistics.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:14:row14"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-15-r15",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:15",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a machine learning model registered in a Fabric workspace. You need to use the model to generate predictions by using the PREDICT function in a Fabric notebook. Which two languages can you use to perform model scoring? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "T-SQL"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "DAX"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Spark SQL"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "PySpark"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "C",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Spark SQL: This language leverages the Spark framework, which is commonly used for big data processing and machine learning. Spark SQL allows you to run SQL queries over data and includes support for machine learning model scoring with the PREDICT function.</p>\n<p>PySpark: This is the Python API for Spark. PySpark allows you to interact with Spark using Python, which is one of the most popular programming languages for data analysis and machine learning. PySpark includes capabilities for integrating with machine learning models and running predictions using the PREDICT function.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:15:row15"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-16-r16",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:16",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are analyzing the data in a Fabric notebook. You have a Spark DataFrame assigned to a variable named df. You need to use the Chart view in the notebook to explore the data manually. Which function should you run to make the data available in the Chart view?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "displayHTML"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "show"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "write"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "display"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The Fabric built-in visualization function allows you to turn Apache Spark DataFrames, Pandas DataFrames and SQL query results into rich format data visualizations.</p><p>You can use the display function on dataframes created in PySpark and Scala, on Spark DataFrames or Resilient Distributed Datasets (RDD), to produce the rich dataframe table view and chart view.</p><p>The output of a SQL statement appears in the rendered table view by default.</p>Key follows from the explanation.",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:16:row16"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-17-r17",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:17",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1. Report1 includes a Python visual. Data displayed by the visual is grouped automatically and duplicate rows are NOT displayed. You need all rows to appear in the visual. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Reference the columns in the Python code by index."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Modify the Sort Column By property for all columns."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Add a unique field to each row."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Modify the Summarize By property for all columns."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>C. Add a unique field to each row.</p>\n<p>Add a unique field to each row: When you add a unique identifier (such as an ID column or a combination of columns that uniquely identifies each row), Power BI recognizes that each row is unique and will display all rows in the visual without grouping them.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:17:row17"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-18-r18",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:18",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model. The model contains data about retail stores. You need to write a DAX query that will be executed by using the XMLA endpoint. The query must return a table of stores that have opened since December 1, 2023. How should you complete the DAX expression? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<p>Values: DEFINE / EVALUATE / FILTER / SUMMARIZE / TABLE</p>\n<pre><code>[drop target 1]\nVAR _SalesSince =\n    DATE (2023, 12, 01)\n[drop target 2]\nFILTER (\n    [drop target 3] (Store, Store[Name], Store[OpenDate]),\n    Store[OpenDate] &gt;= _SalesSince\n)</code></pre>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>[drop target 1]\nVAR _SalesSince =\n    DATE (2023, 12, 01)\n[drop target 2]\nFILTER (\n    [drop target 3] (Store, Store[Name], Store[OpenDate]),\n    Store[OpenDate] &gt;= _SalesSince\n)</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Target 1",
        "options": [
          "DEFINE",
          "EVALUATE",
          "FILTER",
          "SUMMARIZE",
          "TABLE"
        ],
        "answer": "DEFINE"
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Target 2",
        "options": [
          "DEFINE",
          "EVALUATE",
          "FILTER",
          "SUMMARIZE",
          "TABLE"
        ],
        "answer": "EVALUATE"
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Target 3",
        "options": [
          "DEFINE",
          "EVALUATE",
          "FILTER",
          "SUMMARIZE",
          "TABLE"
        ],
        "answer": "SUMMARIZE"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>DEFINE This section defines a variable (VAR _SalesSince) that stores a specific date (2023-12-01).</p>\n<p>EVALUATE</p>\n<p>This keyword is required for executing and returning a table result in DAX queries.</p>\n<p>SUMMARIZE</p>\n<p>Used to create a summary table with specific columns.</p>\n<p>Here, it summarizes data from the Store table, including Store[Name] and Store[OpenDate].</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:18:row18"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-19-r19",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:19",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a dataflow named Dataflow1. Dataflow1 has a query that returns 2,000 rows. You view the query in Power Query as shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>Power Query view of the query (Name: NYC_Taxi_Green_2015_01). Columns: vendorID, lpepPickupDatetime, passengerCount, tripDistance, pickupLongitude, dropoffLatitude, storeAndFwdFlag. Filter applied: Table.SelectRows(#&quot;Filtered rows&quot;, each [lpepPickupDatetime] &gt;= #date(2015, 1, 1) and [lpepPickupDatetime] &lt;= #date ...). Applied steps include Source, Navigation 1, Navigation 2, Navigation 3, Changed column type, Choose columns, Filtered rows.</p>\n<p>Column profile for pickupLongitude: Count 1000, Error count 0, Null count 0, Distinct count 935, Unique count 871, NaN count 0.</p>\n<p>What can you identify about the pickupLongitude column?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power Query (NYC_Taxi_Green_2015_01)</div><ul><li><b>Formula bar:</b> <code>Table.SelectRows(#&quot;Filtered rows&quot;, each [lpepPickupDatetime] &gt;= #date(2015, 1, 1) and [lpepPickupDatetime] &lt;= #date…</code> (cut off)</li><li><b>Query settings:</b> Name NYC_Taxi_Green_2015_01 · Entity type Custom · Data destination: none</li><li><b>Applied steps:</b> Source → Navigation 1 → Navigation 2 → Navigation 3 → Changed column type → Choose columns → Filtered rows → <b>Filtered rows</b> (selected, last step)</li><li><b>Columns shown:</b> vendorID, lpepPickupDatetime, passengerCount, tripDistance, <b>pickupLongitude</b> (selected), dropoffLatitude, storeAndFwdFlag, …</li></ul><p><b>First rows of data</b> (1,000 rows loaded):</p><div><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>vendorID</th><th>lpepPickupDatetime</th><th>passengerCount</th><th>tripDistance</th><th>pickupLongitude</th><th>dropoffLatitude</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1/16/2015</td><td>3</td><td>24.3</td><td>-73.95768738</td><td>40.8983345</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>1/7/2015</td><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>-73.94503021</td><td>40.87893677</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>1</td><td>1/17/2015</td><td>5</td><td>1.8</td><td>-73.95258331</td><td>40.78994751</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>1/15/2015</td><td>1</td><td>12.4</td><td>-73.88159943</td><td>40.7924118</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>1</td><td>1/28/2015</td><td>1</td><td>6.1</td><td>-73.9666748</td><td>40.87965393</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>1</td><td>1/22/2015</td><td>1</td><td>8.1</td><td>-74.00089264</td><td>40.75233459</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>1</td><td>1/21/2015</td><td>1</td><td>3.1</td><td>-73.95635986</td><td>40.76185989</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>1</td><td>1/21/2015</td><td>1</td><td>2.4</td><td>-73.95615387</td><td>40.76541519</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>1</td><td>1/9/2015</td><td>1</td><td>0.1</td><td>-73.83602905</td><td>40.83687592</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>1</td><td>1/8/2015</td><td>1</td><td>12.8</td><td>-73.94503021</td><td>40.68849182</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>1</td><td>1/30/2015</td><td>1</td><td>10.4</td><td>-73.94632721</td><td>40.78804779</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>1</td><td>1/16/2015</td><td>1</td><td>14.1</td><td>-74.11373901</td><td>40.6802063</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Table column profile details</b> for <code>pickupLongitude</code>:</p><div><table><thead><tr><th>Statistic</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Count</td><td>1000</td></tr><tr><td>Error count</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Null count</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Distinct count</td><td>935</td></tr><tr><td>Unique count</td><td>871</td></tr><tr><td>NaN count</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Value distribution: a bar chart of the individual pickupLongitude values (first bar clearly the tallest).</p></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "The column has duplicate values."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "All the table rows are profiled."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "The column has missing values."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "There are 935 values that occur only once."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Answer A</p>\n<p>Distinct Value: This refers to all different values present in a dataset. When you retrieve distinct values from a column, you eliminate duplicate values so that each value is shown once. For example, if a column contains the values , the distinct values would be .</p>\n<p>Unique Value: This usually refers to values that appear only once in the dataset. Unlike distinct values, a unique value will only be considered if it has no duplicates at all. For example, if a column contains the values , the unique values would be , since only 1 appears without repetition.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:19:row19"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-20-r20",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:20",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant named Tenant1 that contains a workspace named WS1. WS1 uses a capacity named C1 and contains a dataset named DS1. You need to ensure read-write access to DS1 is available by using XMLA endpoint. What should be modified first?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "the DS1 settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "the WS1 settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "the C1 settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the Tenant1 settings"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>C. the C1 settings.</p>\n<p>To enable read-write access via the XMLA endpoint, the XMLA endpoint must be set to Read/Write at the capacity level.</p>\n<p>In Power BI / Fabric:</p>\n<p>XMLA endpoint configuration is controlled at the Premium capacity level</p>\n<p>Workspaces inherit this setting from the capacity.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:20:row20"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-21-r21",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:21",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 is assigned to a Fabric capacity. You need to recommend a solution to provide users with the ability to create and publish custom Direct Lake semantic models by using external tools. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege. Which three actions in the Fabric Admin portal should you include in the recommendation? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, set Allow XMLA Endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises datasets to Enabled."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, set Allow Azure Active Directory guest users to access Microsoft Fabric to Enabled."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, select Users can edit data model in the Power BI service."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "From the Capacity settings, set XMLA Endpoint to Read Write."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, set Users can create Fabric items to Enabled."
      },
      {
        "id": "F",
        "text": "From the Tenant settings, enable Publish to Web."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A Allow XMLA Endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises datasets.</p>\n<p>From the Tenant settings, set Allow XMLA Endpoints and Analyze in Excel with on-premises datasets to Enabled. This allows users to interact with the dataset via XMLA endpoints and analyze data using Excel.</p>\n<p>D. Set XMLA Endpoint to Read Write.</p>\n<p>In the Capacity settings, configure the XMLA Endpoint to Read Write. This ensures that users have the necessary permissions to create and modify semantic models through external tools.</p>\n<p>E Enable Users to Create Fabric Items.</p>\n<p>From the Tenant settings, set Users can create Fabric items to Enabled. This grants users the ability to create custom semantic models within the Fabric workspace.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:21:row21"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-22-r22",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:22",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are creating a semantic model in Microsoft Power BI Desktop. You plan to make bulk changes to the model by using the Tabular Model Definition Language (TMDL) extension for Microsoft Visual Studio Code. You need to save the semantic model to a file. Which file format should you use?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "PBIP"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "PBIX"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "PBIT"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "PBIDS"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. PBIP.</p>\n<p>PBIP (Power BI Project) is a file format that supports the open-source TMDL format and is designed for integrating Power BI with external development environments like Visual Studio Code.</p>\n<p>PBIX is the common Power BI report file format but is not intended for bulk edits through TMDL. PBIT is a Power BI template file, used for creating new reports based on an existing structure but not for bulk editing in Visual Studio Code.</p>\n<p>PBIDS is for creating Power BI dataset connections and is unrelated to TMDL editing.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:22:row22"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-23-r23",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:23",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains three schemas named schemaA, schemaB, and schemaC. You need to ensure that a user named User1 can truncate tables in schemaA only. How should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<pre><code>GRANT [dropdown 1] ON [dropdown 2] TO User1;</code></pre>\n<ul><li>Dropdown 1: ALTER / CONNECT / EXECUTE</li><li>Dropdown 2: DATABASE::schemaA / OBJECT::schemaA / SCHEMA::schemaA</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>GRANT [dropdown 1] ON [dropdown 2] TO User1;</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "ALTER",
          "CONNECT",
          "EXECUTE"
        ],
        "answer": "ALTER"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "DATABASE::schemaA",
          "OBJECT::schemaA",
          "SCHEMA::schemaA"
        ],
        "answer": "SCHEMA::schemaA"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>ALTER: Allows modification of schema objects .</p>\n<p>SCHEMA::schemaA: Grants permissions on an entire schema instead of an individual database or object .</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:23:row23"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-24-r24",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:24",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You plan to deploy Microsoft Power BI items by using Fabric deployment pipelines. You have a deployment pipeline that contains three stages named Development, Test, and Production. A workspace is assigned to each stage. You need to provide Power BI developers with access to the pipeline. The solution must meet the following requirements: Ensure that the developers can deploy items to the workspaces for Development and Test. Prevent the developers from deploying items to the workspace for Production. Follow the principle of least privilege. Which three levels of access should you assign to the developers? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Build permission to the production semantic models"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Admin access to the deployment pipeline"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Viewer access to the Development and Test workspaces"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Viewer access to the Production workspace"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces"
      },
      {
        "id": "F",
        "text": "Contributor access to the Production workspace"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "B",
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Admin access to the deployment pipeline : Admin access to the deployment pipeline is necessary to manage the pipeline stages and deployment processes. This allows developers to configure deployments between stages but does not grant direct access to the workspaces themselves.</p>\n<p>Viewer access to the Production workspace : Providing Viewer access to the Production workspace ensures that developers can see the items in the Production stage but cannot make changes or deploy items to the Production workspace. This restriction aligns with the requirement to prevent developers from deploying to the Production workspace.</p>\n<p>Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces : Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces allows developers to deploy and modify items within these stages. This access is necessary to enable developers to work on and test their items before they move to production.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:24:row24"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-25-r25",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:25",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace that contains a DirectQuery semantic model. The model queries a data source that has 500 million rows. You have a Microsoft Power Bi report named Report1 that uses the model. Report1 contains visuals on multiple pages. You need to reduce the query execution time for the visuals on all the pages. What are two features that you can use? Each correct answer presents a complete solution, NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "user-defined aggregations"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "automatic aggregation"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "query caching"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "OneLake integration"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. User-defined aggregations.</p>\n<p>Aggregations help improve query performance by pre-aggregating data at different levels (e.g., summarizing sales by month instead of querying raw transactions).</p>\n<p>Users define aggregations based on expected queries, reducing the need to scan massive datasets.</p>\n<p>B. Automatic aggregations.</p>\n<p>Automatic aggregations optimize query performance by learning from query patterns and storing frequently used aggregate results in memory.</p>\n<p>This reduces the load on the underlying data source, speeding up DirectQuery performance.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:25:row25"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-26-r26",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:26",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains 30 CSV files in OneLake. The files are updated daily. You create a Microsoft Power BI semantic model named Model1 that uses the CSV files as a data source. You configure incremental refresh for Model1 and publish the model to a Premium capacity in the Fabric tenant. When you initiate a refresh of Model1, the refresh fails after running out of resources. What is a possible cause of the failure?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Query folding is occurring."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Only refresh complete days is selected."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "XMLA Endpoint is set to Read Only."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Query folding is NOT occurring."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "The delta type of the column used to partition the data has changed."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>D. Query folding is NOT occurring.</p>\n<p>Incremental refresh depends on query folding to work efficiently.</p>\n<p>When query folding works:</p>\n<p>Power BI pushes the RangeStart and RangeEnd filters down to the data source.</p>\n<p>Only the required partitions are processed.</p>\n<p>Memory usage is controlled.</p>\n<p>However, with CSV files in OneLake:</p>\n<p>Query folding typically does NOT occur.</p>\n<p>The entire dataset may be loaded into memory first. Filtering happens after loading.</p>\n<p>This can cause excessive memory consumption.</p>\n<p>Result: Refresh fails due to running out of resources, even on Premium capacity.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:26:row26"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-27-r27",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:27",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that uses a Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity. You need to enable scale-out for a semantic model. What should you do first?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "At the semantic model level, set Large dataset storage format to Off."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "At the tenant level, set Create and use Metrics to Enabled."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "At the semantic model level, set Large dataset storage format to On."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "At the tenant level, set Data Activator to Enabled."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>C. At the semantic model level, set Large dataset storage format to On.</p>\n<p>To enable scale-out for a semantic model in Power BI Premium capacity, the model must first:</p>\n<p>Use Large dataset storage format</p>\n<p>Scale-out allows:</p>\n<p>Multiple read-only replicas of the semantic model</p>\n<p>Improved query performance</p>\n<p>Better concurrency handling.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:27:row27"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-28-r28",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:28",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse. The warehouse uses row-level security (RLS). You create a Direct Lake semantic model that uses the Delta tables and RLS of the warehouse. When users interact with a report built from the model, which mode will be used by the DAX queries?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "DirectQuery"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Dual"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Direct Lake"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Import"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Direct Query &quot;Row-level security only applies to queries on a Warehouse or SQL analytics endpoint in Fabric. Power BI queries on a warehouse in Direct Lake mode will fall back to Direct Query mode to abide by row-level security.&quot;</p>\n<p>When users interact with a report built from a Direct Lake semantic model, which leverages row-level security (RLS) and Delta tables from a warehouse, the DAX queries will operate in DirectQuery mode.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:28:row28"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-29-r29",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:29",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a complex semantic model. The model is based on a star schema and contains many tables, including a fact table named Sales. You need to create a diagram of the model. The diagram must contain only the Sales table and related tables. What should you use from Microsoft Power BI Desktop?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "data categories"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Data view"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Model view"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DAX query view"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>C. Model view.</p>\n<p>Model view in Microsoft Power BI Desktop allows you to visualize and manage relationships between tables in a semantic model.</p>\n<p>Since the requirement is to create a diagram that includes only the Sales table and its related tables, Model view is the best choice.</p>\n<p>In Model view, you can filter and arrange tables to focus only on relevant parts of the data model, making it easier to analyze relationships.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:29:row29"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-30-r30",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:30",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model. The model uses Direct Lake mode. You suspect that some DAX queries load unnecessary columns into memory. You need to identify the frequently used columns that are loaded into memory. What are two ways to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Use the Analyze in Excel feature."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Use the Vertipaq Analyzer tool."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Query the $System.DISCOVER_STORAGE_TABLE_COLUMN_SEGMENTS dynamic management view (DMV)."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Query the DISCOVER_MEMORYGRANT dynamic management view (DMV)."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "B",
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>B. Use the Vertipaq Analyzer tool.</p>\n<p>Vertipaq Analyzer: This tool helps analyze the internal structure of your Power BI model. It provides detailed information about the storage and memory usage of your model, including which columns are frequently accessed and loaded into memory. This can help you identify unnecessary columns that are consuming resources.</p>\n<p>Steps:</p>\n<p>Export your Power BI model to a .pbix file.</p>\n<p>Open the .pbix file in Power BI Desktop.</p>\n<p>Use the Vertipaq Analyzer tool to analyze the model and review the column usage statistics.</p>\n<p>C. Query the $System.DISCOVER_STORAGE_TABLE_COLUMN_SEGMENTS dynamic management view (DMV).</p>\n<p>DMVs: Dynamic Management Views (DMVs) provide detailed information about the operations of your Power BI models. Specifically, the $System.DISCOVER_STORAGE_TABLE_COLUMN_SEGMENTS DMV can give you insights into the storage and usage patterns of individual columns within your model.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:30:row30"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-31-r31",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:31",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have the source data model shown in the following exhibit. The primary keys of the tables are indicated by a key symbol beside the columns involved in each key. You need to create a dimensional data model that will enable the analysis of order items by date, product, and customer. What should you include in the solution? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Source data model — tables and key columns (key symbol beside a column):</p>\n<ul><li>Date: Date (key)</li><li>Product: CompanyID (key), ProductID (key)</li><li>OrderItem: OrderDate, CompanyID, ProductID, OrderItemID (key), CustomerID</li><li>Company: CompanyID (key)</li><li>Customer: CompanyID (key), CustomerID (key)</li></ul>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>The relationship between OrderItem and Product must be based on: The ProductID column / Both the CompanyID and the ProductID columns / A new key that combines the CompanyID and ProductID columns</li><li>The Company entity must be: Omitted / Denormalized into the Product entity only / Denormalized into the Customer and Product entities</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Source data model (ER diagram)</div><p>Primary keys are marked with a key symbol beside the columns of each key.</p><div><table><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Columns (key columns marked 🔑)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Date</td><td>🔑 Date</td></tr><tr><td>Product</td><td>🔑 CompanyID, 🔑 ProductID (composite key)</td></tr><tr><td>Company</td><td>🔑 CompanyID</td></tr><tr><td>Customer</td><td>🔑 CompanyID, 🔑 CustomerID (composite key)</td></tr><tr><td>OrderItem</td><td>OrderDate, CompanyID, ProductID, 🔑 OrderItemID, CustomerID</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Relationships</b> (one-to-many, “1” side first): Date → OrderItem · Product → OrderItem · Company → OrderItem · Customer → OrderItem · Company → Product · Company → Customer.</p></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "The relationship between OrderItem and Product must be based on:",
        "options": [
          "The ProductID column",
          "Both the CompanyID and the ProductID columns",
          "A new key that combines the CompanyID and ProductID columns"
        ],
        "answer": "The ProductID column"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "The Company entity must be:",
        "options": [
          "Omitted",
          "Denormalized into the Product entity only",
          "Denormalized into the Customer and Product entities"
        ],
        "answer": "Denormalized into the Customer and Product entities"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The Product ID column.</p>\n<p>In a proper star schema:</p>\n<p>OrderItem is a fact table.</p>\n<p>Product is a dimension table.</p>\n<p>The relationship should use the primary key of the dimension.</p>\n<p>Denormalized into the Customer and Product entities.</p>\n<p>In star schema modeling.</p>\n<p>Snowflake schemas (separate Company table linked to Product and Customer) are not ideal in Power BI.</p>\n<p>Power BI performs best with denormalized dimensions.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:31:row31"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-32-r32",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:32",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 uses Import mode. Model1 contains a table named Orders. Orders has 100 million rows and the following fields.</p>\n<p>You need to reduce the memory used by Model1 and the time it takes to refresh the model. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Semantic model columns</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Data type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OrderID</td><td>Integer</td><td>Column imported from the source</td></tr><tr><td>OrderDateTime</td><td>Date/Time</td><td>Column imported from the source</td></tr><tr><td>Quantity</td><td>Integer</td><td>Column imported from the source</td></tr><tr><td>Price</td><td>Decimal</td><td>Column imported from the source</td></tr><tr><td>TotalSalesAmount</td><td>Decimal</td><td>Calculated column that multiplies Quantity and Price</td></tr><tr><td>TotalQuantity</td><td>Integer</td><td>Measure</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Split OrderDateTime into separate date and time columns."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Replace TotalQuantity with a calculated column."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Convert Quantity into the Text data type."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Replace TotalSalesAmount with a measure."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Split OrderDateTime into separate date and time columns .</p>\n<p>In Import mode, Power BI compresses data more efficiently when Date and Time are stored separately instead of a single DateTime column.</p>\n<p>DateTime values have higher cardinality (more unique values), which increases memory usage.</p>\n<p>Splitting into Date and Time reduces the number of unique values, improving compression and reducing memory usage.</p>\n<p>D. Replace TotalSalesAmount with a measure .</p>\n<p>Measures in Power BI are calculated at query time and do not consume additional memory, unlike calculated columns, which take up storage.</p>\n<p>TotalSalesAmount is likely derived from Quantity * UnitPrice, so instead of storing it as a column, it should be computed dynamically using a DAX measure.</p>\n<p>This reduces model size and improves performance.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:32:row32"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-33-r33",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:33",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model. You need to prevent report creators from populating visuals by using implicit measures. What are two tools that you can use to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Tabular Editor"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DAX Studio"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Microsoft Power BI Desktop: Power BI Desktop allows you to control and manage how measures are used within your reports. By carefully defining and using explicit measures within your data model, you can ensure that report creators use only these predefined measures instead of creating implicit measures automatically.</p>\n<p>B. Tabular Editor: Tabular Editor is a powerful tool for managing and editing Power BI and Analysis Services tabular models. It allows you to enforce best practices, such as disabling implicit measures, by modifying the model&#x27;s properties and ensuring that only explicit measures are available for use in reports.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:33:row33"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-34-r34",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:34",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains two lakehouses. You are building a dataflow that will combine data from the lakehouses. The applied steps from one of the queries in the dataflow is shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>Use the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Query settings — Name: Customers1, Entity type: Custom. Applied steps: Source, Navigation 1, Capitalized each word, Appended query, Changed column type, Added custom, Filtered rows.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>[Answer choice] of the transformation steps in the query will fold. — All / None / Some</li><li>The Added custom step will be performed in [answer choice]. — each lakehouse&#x27;s query engine / the Microsoft Power Query engine / the source lakehouse query engine</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power Query “Query settings” pane</div><ul><li><b>Name:</b> Customers1  ·  <b>Entity type:</b> Custom</li><li><b>Applied steps</b> (in order): Source → Navigation 1 → Capitalized each word → Appended query → Changed column type → Added custom → <b>Filtered rows</b> (selected step)</li><li>Right-click menu on “Filtered rows”: Edit settings · Rename · Delete · Delete until end · Insert step after · Move before · Move after (greyed out) · Extract previous… · View data source query (greyed out) · View query plan (greyed out) · Properties</li></ul></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "All",
          "None",
          "Some"
        ],
        "answer": "Some"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "The Added custom step will be performed in ",
        "options": [
          "each lakehouse's query engine",
          "the Microsoft Power Query engine",
          "the source lakehouse query engine"
        ],
        "answer": "the Microsoft Power Query engine"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1.Some of the transformation steps in the query will fold.&quot;</p>\n<p>Query folding refers to the ability of Power Query to push transformations back to the data source rather than processing them in Power Query itself.</p>\n<p>The answer &quot;Some&quot;.</p>\n<p>Some transformations are foldable (executed at the source).</p>\n<p>Some transformations are not foldable and will be executed locally in Power Query.</p>\n<p>This is common because basic transformations (filtering, joins, aggregations, etc.) often fold, but more complex operations (custom columns, advanced functions, etc.) may not.</p>\n<p>2. The Added custom step will be performed in the Microsoft Power Query engine.</p>\n<p>When a transformation cannot be folded, it is processed in the Power Query engine instead of being pushed to the source.</p>\n<p>Since the question mentions an added custom step, it likely includes:</p>\n<p>Custom calculations</p>\n<p>Complex transformations (like M code or certain joins)</p>\n<p>Operations that cannot be executed at the data source</p>\n<p>Such transformations are executed locally within Power Query rather than at the source (lakehouse query engine).</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:34:row34"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-35-r35",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:35",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse’. Lakehouse1 contains a table named Tablet. You are creating a new data pipeline. You plan to copy external data to Table’. The schema of the external data changes regularly. You need the copy operation to meet the following requirements: Replace Table1 with the schema of the external data. Replace all the data in Table1 with the rows in the external data. You add a Copy data activity to the pipeline. What should you do for the Copy data activity?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From the Source tab, add additional columns."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "From the Destination tab, set Table action to Overwrite."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "From the Settings tab, select Enable staging."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "From the Source tab, select Enable partition discovery."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "From the Source tab, select Recursively."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>B. From the Destination tab, set Table action to Overwrite.</p>\n<p>You need the copy operation to:</p>\n<p>Replace Table1 with the schema of the external data</p>\n<p>Replace all data in Table1 with the external data</p>\n<p>The best way to achieve this is by setting Table action to &quot;Overwrite&quot; in the Destination tab of the Copy Data activity.</p>\n<p>&quot;Overwrite&quot; ensures that the table is completely replaced, including the schema and data, which meets both requirements.</p>\n<p>This means that every time the pipeline runs, Table1 will have the latest schema and data from the external source.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:35:row35"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-36-r36",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:36",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse. You plan to query sales data files by using the SQL endpoint. The files will be in an Amazon Simple Storage Service (Amazon S3) storage bucket. You need to recommend which file format to use and where to create a shortcut. Which two actions should you include in the recommendation? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Create a shortcut in the Files section."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Use the Parquet format"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Use the CSV format."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Create a shortcut in the Tables section."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Use the delta format."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>D. Create Shortcut in Tables Section.</p>\n<p>The SQL endpoint:</p>\n<p>Can only query objects inside the Tables area of a lakehouse</p>\n<p>Cannot directly query items in the Files section</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<p>The S3 shortcut must point to Delta files</p>\n<p>The shortcut must be created under Tables</p>\n<p>This registers the data as a table accessible via SQL.</p>\n<p>E.Delta Format .</p>\n<p>The SQL endpoint in Fabric only supports querying tables, not raw files.</p>\n<p>Delta format:</p>\n<p>Is the native table format in Fabric</p>\n<p>Is fully supported by the SQL endpoint</p>\n<p>Supports ACID transactions</p>\n<p>Supports schema enforcement</p>\n<p>Enables better performance.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:36:row36"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-37-r37",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:37",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a subfolder named Subfolder1 that contains CSV files. You need to convert the CSV files into the delta format that has V-Order optimization enabled. What should you do from Lakehouse explorer?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Use the Load to Tables feature."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Create a new shortcut in the Files section."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Create a new shortcut in the Tables section."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Use the Optimize feature."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Use the &quot;Load to Tables&quot; feature.</p>\n<p>To convert CSV files into Delta format with V-Order optimization, you need to load the files into tables in the Lakehouse.</p>\n<p>&quot;Load to Tables&quot; is a feature in Microsoft Fabric&#x27;s Lakehouse Explorer that allows you to convert files (such as CSV) into managed Delta tables.</p>\n<p>During this process, the data is converted into the Delta format, which supports V-Order optimization for better performance.</p>\n<p>V-Order is an advanced columnar storage format that enhances query performance and compression for Delta tables.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:37:row37"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-38-r38",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:38",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains an unpartitioned table named Table1. You plan to copy data to Table1 and partition the table based on a date column in the source data. You create a Copy activity to copy the data to Table1. You need to specify the partition column in the Destination settings of the Copy activity. What should you do first?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From the Destination tab, set Mode to Append."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "From the Destination tab, select the partition column."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "From the Source tab, select Enable partition discovery."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "From the Destination tabs, set Mode to Overwrite."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>D. From the Destination tabs, set Mode to Overwrite.</p>\n<p>Expand Advanced, in Table action, select Overwrite, and then select Enable partition, under Partition columns, select Add column, and choose the column you want to use as the partition column. You can choose to use a single column or multiple columns as the partition column.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:38:row38"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-39-r39",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:39",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse named Warehouse1. Warehouse1 contains a fact table named FactSales that has one billion rows. You run the following T-SQL statement. CREATE TABLE test.FactSales AS CLONE OF Dbo.FactSales; For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>A replica of dbo.Sales is created in the test schema by copying the metadata only. — Yes / No</li><li>Additional schema changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales. — Yes / No</li><li>Additional schema changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales. — Yes / No</li></ul>\n<p>(The third row is repeated verbatim; it is preserved as printed.)</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "label": "A replica of dbo.Sales is created in the test schema by copying the metadata only.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "s2",
        "label": "Additional schema changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "s3",
        "label": "Additional schema changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. Yes</p>\n<p>A replica of dbo.Sales is created in the test schema by copying the metadata only.</p>\n<p>If metadata-only copying is used, it means the structure (schema) is copied but not the data.</p>\n<p>This is possible with views, synonyms, or schema-only copies (e.g., CREATE TABLE ... AS NO DATA). If the intent is to create an empty table with the same structure, this would be true.</p>\n<p>2.No</p>\n<p>Additional schema changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.</p>\n<p>If test.FactSales was independently created (i.e., copied separately from dbo.FactSales), then any schema changes to dbo.FactSales will NOT automatically reflect in test.FactSales.</p>\n<p>Schema changes would only propagate if test.FactSales was a synonym, view, or linked table.</p>\n<p>Since it&#x27;s likely an independent table, changes to dbo.FactSales do not apply automatically.</p>\n<p>3.No</p>\n<p>Additional schema changes to dbo.FactSales will also apply to test.FactSales.</p>\n<p>This statement is identical to statement 2, so the answer remains the same.</p>\n<p>Changes to dbo.FactSales do not automatically apply to test.FactSales unless explicitly linked (e.g., via a view or a synonym.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:39:row39"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-40-r40",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:40",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have source data in a folder on a local computer. You need to create a solution that will use Fabric to populate a data store. The solution must meet the following requirements: Support the use of dataflows to load and append data to the data store. Ensure that Delta tables are V-Order optimized and compacted automatically. Which type of data store should you use?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "an Azure SQL database"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a warehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "a KQL database"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The Lakehouse and the Delta Lake table format are central to Microsoft Fabric, assuring that tables are optimized for analytics is a key requirement.</p>\n<p>To meet the requirements of supporting dataflows to load and append data to the data store while ensuring that Delta tables are V-Order optimized and compacted automatically, you should use a lakehouse in Fabric as your solution.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:40:row40"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-41-r41",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:41",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse. You are using a Fabric notebook to save a large DataFrame by using the following code. df.write.partitionBy(“year”, “month”, “day”).mode(“overwrite”).parquet(“Files/SalesOrder”) For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>The results will form a hierarchy of folders for each partition key. — Yes / No</li><li>The resulting file partitions can be read in parallel across multiple nodes. — Yes / No</li><li>The resulting file partitions will use file compression. — Yes / No</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "label": "The results will form a hierarchy of folders for each partition key.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "s2",
        "label": "The resulting file partitions can be read in parallel across multiple nodes.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "s3",
        "label": "The resulting file partitions will use file compression.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. &quot;The results will form a hierarchy of folders for each partition key.&quot;</p>\n<p>Yes</p>\n<p>Partitioning creates a folder structure where each folder represents a partition key value.</p>\n<p>For example, in a data lake, partitioned data may be stored as:</p>\n<p>bash</p>\n<p>Copy</p>\n<p>Edit</p>\n<p>/sales/year=2023/month=01/</p>\n<p>/sales/year=2023/month=02/</p>\n<p>This improves query performance by allowing queries to skip unnecessary partitions.</p>\n<p>2. &quot;The resulting file partitions can be read in parallel across multiple nodes.&quot;</p>\n<p>Yes Partitioned files are designed for parallel processing.</p>\n<p>In distributed environments like Spark, Synapse, or Fabric Lakehouse, each partition can be processed independently across multiple compute nodes, improving performance.</p>\n<p>This is especially true for columnar formats like Parquet and Delta tables, which support distributed query execution.</p>\n<p>3.&quot;The resulting file partitions will use file compression.&quot;</p>\n<p>Yes</p>\n<p>File compression is commonly used in partitioned storage to reduce storage costs and improve read performance.</p>\n<p>Formats like Parquet, ORC, and Delta support built-in compression (e.g., Snappy, Gzip, or ZSTD).</p>\n<p>Compressed partitions reduce I/O costs and improve query performance, making them a best practice.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:41:row41"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-42-r42",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:42",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a data flow named Dataflow1 contains a query that returns the data shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>Power Query table with columns VendorID and date columns 2015-01-01 to 2015-01-08. Each date column: Valid 100%, Error 0%, Empty 0%, 2 distinct, 2 unique — except 2015-01-03: 1 distinct, 0 unique. First rows: VendorID 1 and 2. Query settings — Name: Generated-NYC-Taxi-Gre..., Applied steps: Source, Promoted headers, Changed column type.</p>\n<p>You need to transform the data columns into attribute-value pairs, where columns become rows. You select the VendorID column. Which transformation should you select from the context menu of the VendorID column?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power Query (Generated-NYC-Taxi-Green…)</div><ul><li><b>Formula bar:</b> <code>Table.TransformColumnTypes(#&quot;Promoted headers&quot;, {{&quot;VendorID&quot;, Int64.Type}, {&quot;2015-01-01&quot;, Int64.Type}, {&quot;2015-01-02&quot;, …</code></li><li><b>Applied steps:</b> Source → Promoted headers → Changed column type (selected)</li><li>Column profiling is based on the top 1,000 rows. Every column is 100% valid (0% error, 0% empty).</li></ul><div><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>VendorID</th><th>2015-01-01</th><th>2015-01-02</th><th>2015-01-03</th><th>2015-01-04</th><th>2015-01-05</th><th>2015-01-06</th><th>2015-01-07</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>16</td><td>15</td><td>0</td><td>9</td><td>12</td><td>9</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>20</td><td>15</td><td>7</td><td>17</td><td>20</td><td>6</td><td>6</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Group by"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Unpivot columns"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Unpivot other columns"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Split column"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Remove other columns"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>C. Unpivot Other Columns. You need to transform data columns into attribute-value pairs, meaning that columns will be converted into rows. This is achieved using Unpivoting in Power Query.</p>\n<p>&quot;Unpivot Columns&quot; (Option B) → Converts selected columns into rows.</p>\n<p>&quot;Unpivot Other Columns&quot; (Option C) → Converts all columns except the selected column into rows.</p>\n<p>Since you are selecting the VendorID column and want to transform the other columns into attribute-value pairs, you should choose &quot;Unpivot Other Columns.&quot;</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:42:row42"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-43-r43",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:43",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a data pipeline. You need to ensure that the pipeline runs every four hours on Mondays and Fridays. To what should you set Repeat for the schedule?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Daily"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "By the minute"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Weekly"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Hourly"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Answer C : Weekly.</p>\n<p>The only way to do this is to set the schedule to &#x27;&#x27;Weekly&#x27;&#x27;, set the days on Monday and Friday and add manually 6 Time of 4 hour intervals.</p>\n<p>You need the pipeline to run every four hours on Mondays and Fridays.</p>\n<p>Weekly scheduling allows you to specify particular days of the week (e.g., Monday and Friday).</p>\n<p>Within the weekly schedule, you can set the pipeline to repeat every 4 hours.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:43:row43"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-44-r44",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:44",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse. Several times a day, the performance of all warehouse queries degrades. You suspect that Fabric is throttling the compute used by the warehouse. What should you use to identify whether throttling is occurring?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "the Capacity settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "the Monitoring hub"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "dynamic management views (DMVs)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The Microsoft Capacity Metrics app, also known as the metrics app, serves as a monitoring tool within the Microsoft Power BI service. It offers functionalities to track and analyze the resource utilization.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:44:row44"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-45-r45",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:45",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace that uses the default Spark starter pool and runtime version 1.2. You plan to read a CSV file named Sales_raw.csv in a lakehouse, select columns, and save the data as a Delta table to the managed area of the lakehouse. Sales_raw.csv contains 12 columns. You have the following code.</p>\n<p>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<pre><code>from pyspark.sql.functions import year\n\n(spark\n .read\n .format(&quot;csv&quot;)\n .option(&quot;header&quot;, &#x27;true&#x27;)\n .load(&quot;Files/sales_raw.csv&quot;)\n .select(&#x27;SalesOrderNumber&#x27;, &#x27;OrderDate&#x27;, &#x27;CustomerName&#x27;, &#x27;UnitPrice&#x27;)\n .withColumn(&quot;Year&quot;, year(&quot;OrderDate&quot;))\n .write\n .partitionBy(&#x27;Year&#x27;)\n .saveAsTable(&quot;sales&quot;)\n)</code></pre>\n<ul><li>The Spark engine will read only the &#x27;SalesOrderNumber&#x27;, &#x27;OrderDate&#x27;, &#x27;CustomerName&#x27;, &#x27;UnitPrice&#x27; columns from Sales_raw.csv. — Yes / No</li><li>Removing the partition will reduce the execution time of the query. — Yes / No</li><li>Adding inferSchema= &#x27;true&#x27; to the options will increase the execution time of the query. — Yes / No</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "label": "The Spark engine will read only the 'SalesOrderNumber', 'OrderDate', 'CustomerName', 'UnitPrice' columns from Sales_raw.csv.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "s2",
        "label": "Removing the partition will reduce the execution time of the query.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "s3",
        "label": "Adding inferSchema= 'true' to the options will increase the execution time of the query.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>No: The Spark engine will initially read all columns from the CSV file because the .select() transformation is applied after the data has been read into memory. Therefore, all 12 columns from Sales_raw.csv are read before the selection of specific columns is applied.</p>\n<p>•No: Removing the partition might not necessarily reduce the execution time. While there might be some overhead in writing data to partitions, the overall impact on read performance, especially for large datasets, is usually beneficial. The query execution time for saving might be higher due to partitioning, but the read performance improvement usually outweighs this cost.</p>\n<p>•Yes: Adding inferSchema = &#x27;true&#x27; will increase the execution time of the query because Spark will need to read through the entire dataset to determine the data types of each column. This extra pass over the data adds to the initial read time.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:45:row45"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-46-r46",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:46",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse. A user discovers that a report that usually takes two minutes to render has been running for 45 minutes and has still not rendered. You need to identify what is preventing the report query from completing. Which dynamic management view (DMV) should you use?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "sys.dm_exec_requests"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "sys.dm_exec_sessions"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "sys.dm_exec_connections"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "sys.dm_pdw_exec_requests"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The sys.dm_exec_requests dynamic management view provides real-time information about all requests currently executing on the SQL Server. This view helps you understand the status of each request, including whether it is blocked, waiting, or running.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:46:row46"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-47-r47",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:47",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are creating a data flow in Fabric to ingest data from an Azure SQL database by using a T-SQL statement. You need to ensure that any foldable Power Query transformation steps are processed by the Microsoft SQL Server engine. How should you complete the code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<p>Values: EnableFolding / NativeQuery / Optimize / Record / StopFolding / Table / Value</p>\n<pre><code>let\n    Source = Sql.Databases(\n        &quot;server.database.windows.net&quot;\n    ),\n    Database = Source{[Name = &quot;db&quot;]}[Data],\n    Query = [drop target 1].[drop target 2](\n        Database,\n        &quot; SELECT * FROM customer WHERE country IN (&#x27;USA&#x27;, &#x27;UK&#x27;)&quot;,\n        null,\n        [ [drop target 3] = true]\n    )\nin\n    Query</code></pre>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>let\n    Source = Sql.Databases(\n        &quot;server.database.windows.net&quot;\n    ),\n    Database = Source{[Name = &quot;db&quot;]}[Data],\n    Query = [drop target 1].[drop target 2](\n        Database,\n        &quot; SELECT * FROM customer WHERE country IN (&#x27;USA&#x27;, &#x27;UK&#x27;)&quot;,\n        null,\n        [ [drop target 3] = true]\n    )\nin\n    Query</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Target 1",
        "options": [
          "EnableFolding",
          "NativeQuery",
          "Optimize",
          "Record",
          "StopFolding",
          "Table",
          "Value"
        ],
        "answer": "Value"
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Target 2",
        "options": [
          "EnableFolding",
          "NativeQuery",
          "Optimize",
          "Record",
          "StopFolding",
          "Table",
          "Value"
        ],
        "answer": "NativeQuery"
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Target 3",
        "options": [
          "EnableFolding",
          "NativeQuery",
          "Optimize",
          "Record",
          "StopFolding",
          "Table",
          "Value"
        ],
        "answer": "EnableFolding"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Value.</p>\n<p>Value is the function used to store the result of the query.</p>\n<p>NativeQuery.</p>\n<p>NativeQuery allows executing a direct SQL query within Power Query M code. EnableFolding .</p>\n<p>This enables query folding, ensuring that transformations are executed in SQL Server rather than in Power Query.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:47:row47"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-48-r48",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:48",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Readings from 100 IoT devices are appended to a Delta table in Lakehouse1. Each set of readings is approximately 25 KB. Approximately 10 GB of data is received daily. All the table and SparkSession settings are set to the default. You discover that queries are slow to execute. In addition, the lakehouse storage contains data and log files that are no longer used. You need to remove the files that are no longer used and combine small files into larger files with a target size of 1 GB per file. What should you do? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<p>Actions: Set the autoCompact table setting. / Set the optimizeWrite table setting. / Run the VACUUM command on a schedule. / Set the autoCompact SparkSession setting. / Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule. / Set the parallelDelete SparkSession setting.</p>\n<ul><li>Remove the files: [drop target]</li><li>Combine the files: [drop target]</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Remove the files:",
        "options": [
          "Set the autoCompact table setting.",
          "Set the optimizeWrite table setting.",
          "Run the VACUUM command on a schedule.",
          "Set the autoCompact SparkSession setting.",
          "Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule.",
          "Set the parallelDelete SparkSession setting."
        ],
        "answer": "Run the VACUUM command on a schedule."
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Combine the files:",
        "options": [
          "Set the autoCompact table setting.",
          "Set the optimizeWrite table setting.",
          "Run the VACUUM command on a schedule.",
          "Set the autoCompact SparkSession setting.",
          "Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule.",
          "Set the parallelDelete SparkSession setting."
        ],
        "answer": "Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Remove the files that are no longer used:</p>\n<p>Run the VACUUM command on a schedule: The VACUUM command cleans up old files and log files that are no longer needed by the Delta table, helping to free up storage and potentially improve performance by reducing the number of files the query engine needs to consider.</p>\n<p>Combine small files into larger files:</p>\n<p>Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule: The OPTIMIZE command compacts small files into larger ones, improving read performance by reducing the overhead associated with opening many small files. This can be particularly useful when you have a large number of small files due to frequent appends of small data sets.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:48:row48"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-49-r49",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:49",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You need to create a data loading pattern for a Type 1 slowly changing dimension (SCD). Which two actions should you include in the process? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Update rows when the non-key attributes have changed."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Insert new rows when the natural key exists in the dimension table, and the non-key attribute values have changed."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Update the effective end date of rows when the non-key attribute values have changed."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Insert new records when the natural key is a new value in the table."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Update rows when the non-key attributes have changed: In a Type 1 SCD, when a change is detected in any of the non-key attributes of an existing row, the current row is updated with the new values. This type of SCD does not keep any historical data; it simply overwrites the old data with the new data.</p>\n<p>D. Insert new records when the natural key is a new value in the table: When a new record (with a new natural key) is encountered that does not already exist in the dimension table, it is inserted as a new row. This is necessary to ensure that all new entities are captured in the dimension.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:49:row49"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-50-r50",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:50",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 and an Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2 account named storage1. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. You need to create a shortcut to storage1 in Lakehouse1. Which connection and endpoint should you specify? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>Connection: abfs / abfss / https</li><li>Endpoint: blob / dfs / file</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "Connection:",
        "options": [
          "abfs",
          "abfss",
          "https"
        ],
        "answer": "https"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "Endpoint:",
        "options": [
          "blob",
          "dfs",
          "file"
        ],
        "answer": "dfs"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. Connection Type: https</p>\n<p>The selection of https means that the connection is being made using secure HTTPS endpoints rather than using Azure Blob File System (ABFS/ABFSS) protocols.</p>\n<p>https is commonly used for REST API-based access to Azure Storage.</p>\n<p>2. Endpoint Type: dfs</p>\n<p>The selection of dfs (Distributed File System) means that the connection is using Azure Data Lake Storage Gen2.</p>\n<p>dfs endpoints enable Hierarchical Namespace (HNS), which allows for file system-like directory structures and optimized big data analytics.</p>\n<p>This is required for Spark, Azure Synapse, Fabric, and Databricks.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:50:row50"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-51-r51",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:51",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are analyzing customer purchases in a Fabric notebook by using PySpark. You have the following DataFrames: transactions: Contains five columns named transaction_id, customer_id, product_id, amount, and date and has 10 million rows, with each row representing a transaction. customers: Contains customer details in 1,000 rows and three columns named customer_id, name, and country. You need to join the DataFrames on the customer_id column. The solution must minimize data shuffling. You write the following code. from pyspark.sql import functions as F results = Which code should you run to populate the results DataFrame?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "transactions.join(F.broadcast(customers), transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "transactions.join(customers, transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id).distinct()"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "transactions.join(customers, transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "transactions.crossJoin(customers).where(transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. transactions.join(F.broadcast(customers), transactions.customer_id == customers.customer_id)Optimized method to perform a join between a very large table and a smaller one.</p>\n<p>In Apache Spark, broadcasting refers to an optimization technique for join operations. When you join two DataFrames or RDDs and one of them is significantly smaller than the other, Spark can &quot;broadcast&quot; the smaller table to all nodes in the cluster. This approach avoids the need for network shuffles for each row of the larger table, significantly reducing the execution time of the join operation.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:51:row51"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-52-r52",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:52",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Microsoft Power BI report and a semantic model that uses Direct Lake mode. From Power BI Desktop, you open Performance analyzer as shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>Use the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Performance analyzer — Durations (ms):</p>\n<ul><li>Card: 638 total — DAX query 431, Visual display 25, Other 181</li><li>Card (after a filter change): 46</li><li>Card: 9205 total — DAX query 9055, Direct query 8660, Visual display 24, Other 126</li></ul>\n<p>Fields pane shows &quot;Sum of col1&quot;. Drill through: Cross-report Off, Keep all filters On.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>The Direct Lake fallback behavior is set to [dropdown]. — Automatic / DirectLakeOnly / DirectQueryOnly</li><li>The query for the table visual is executed by using [dropdown]. — the composite model / Direct Lake / Direct Query</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Performance analyzer (semantic model in Direct Lake mode)</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Duration (ms)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Recording started (5/15/2023 5:10:49 PM)</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>Refreshed visual</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><b>Card</b></td><td><b>638</b></td></tr><tr><td>&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;DAX query</td><td>431</td></tr><tr><td>&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Visual display</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Other</td><td>181</td></tr><tr><td>Changed a filter</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>Edited a visual</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>Card (collapsed)</td><td>46</td></tr><tr><td>Changed a filter</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td><b>Card</b></td><td><b>9205</b></td></tr><tr><td>&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;DAX query</td><td>9055</td></tr><tr><td>&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Direct query</td><td>8660</td></tr><tr><td>&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Visual display</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Other</td><td>126</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Visualizations pane: Card visual · Field “Sum of col1” · Drill through: Cross-report Off, Keep all filters On.</p></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "The Direct Lake fallback behavior is set to ",
        "options": [
          "Automatic",
          "DirectLakeOnly",
          "DirectQueryOnly"
        ],
        "answer": "Automatic"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "The query for the table visual is executed by using ",
        "options": [
          "the composite model",
          "Direct Lake",
          "Direct Query"
        ],
        "answer": "Direct Lake"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. Direct Lake Fallback Behavior: Automatic.</p>\n<p>Direct Lake Mode enables real-time querying of Delta tables in a Fabric lakehouse without requiring data import or DirectQuery.</p>\n<p>When fallback behavior is set to Automatic, Power BI will automatically switch between Direct Lake Mode and DirectQuery when necessary (e.g., if a feature isn&#x27;t supported in Direct Lake).</p>\n<p>This provides the best balance between performance and flexibility.</p>\n<p>2.Query Execution: Direct Lake.</p>\n<p>Direct Lake Mode allows Power BI to load data directly from Delta tables in a Fabric lakehouse without preloading or sending queries to an external database.</p>\n<p>It provides better performance than DirectQuery by eliminating query overhead and improving response times.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:52:row52"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-53-r53",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:53",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a table named Nyctaxi_raw. Nyctaxi_row contains the following table:</p>\n<p>You create a Fabric notebook and attach it to Lakehouse1. You need to use PySpark code to transform the data. The solution must meet the following requirements: Add a column named pickupDate that will contain only the date portion of pickupDateTime. Filter the DataFrame to include only rows where fareAmount is a positive number that is less than 100. How should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Nyctaxi_raw table:</p>\n<p>| Name | Data type | |---|---| | pickupDateTime | Timestamp | | passengerCount | Integer | | fareAmount | Double | | paymentType | String | | tipAmount | Double |</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<pre><code>df = spark.read.format(&quot;delta&quot;).load(&quot;Tables/nyctaxi_raw&quot;)\n\ndf2 = [dropdown 1] (&quot;pickupDate&quot;, df[&#x27;tpepPickupDateTime&#x27;]\n    [dropdown 2] \\\n    [dropdown 3]</code></pre>\n<ul><li>Dropdown 1: df.columns / df.select / df.withColumn / df.withColumnsRenamed</li><li>Dropdown 2: .alias(&#x27;date&#x27;) / .cast(&#x27;date&#x27;) / .cast(&#x27;pickupDate&#x27;) / .getfield(&#x27;date&#x27;)</li><li>Dropdown 3: .filter(&quot;fareAmount &gt; 0 AND farAmount &lt; 100&quot;) / .filter(col(&quot;fareAmount&quot;).contains(&quot;1..100&quot;)) / .when(df.fareAmount &gt; 0 AND fareAmount &lt; 100) / .where(df.fareAmount.isin([1,100]))</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Table columns</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Data type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>pickupDateTime</td><td>Timestamp</td></tr><tr><td>passengerCount</td><td>Integer</td></tr><tr><td>fareAmount</td><td>Double</td></tr><tr><td>paymentType</td><td>String</td></tr><tr><td>tipAmount</td><td>Double</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><pre>df = spark.read.format(&quot;delta&quot;).load(&quot;Tables/nyctaxi_raw&quot;)\n\ndf2 = [dropdown 1] (&quot;pickupDate&quot;, df[&#x27;tpepPickupDateTime&#x27;]\n    [dropdown 2] \\\n    [dropdown 3]</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "df.columns",
          "df.select",
          "df.withColumn",
          "df.withColumnsRenamed"
        ],
        "answer": "df.withColumn"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          ".alias('date')",
          ".cast('date')",
          ".cast('pickupDate')",
          ".getfield('date')"
        ],
        "answer": ".cast('date')"
      },
      {
        "id": "d3",
        "label": "d3",
        "options": [
          ".filter(\"fareAmount > 0 AND farAmount < 100\")",
          ".filter(col(\"fareAmount\").contains(\"1..100\"))",
          ".when(df.fareAmount > 0 AND fareAmount < 100)",
          ".where(df.fareAmount.isin([1,100]))"
        ],
        "answer": ".filter(\"fareAmount > 0 AND farAmount < 100\")"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. df.withColumn.</p>\n<p>The withColumn function is used to add or replace a column in a DataFrame.</p>\n<p>It is appropriate here because we are creating a new column (pickupDate) based on an existing column (tpepPickupDateTime).</p>\n<p>Alternative options like df.select or df.withColumnsRenamed would not modify a column directly.</p>\n<p>2 .cast(&#x27;date&#x27;)</p>\n<p>The .cast(&#x27;date&#x27;) function is used to convert a column’s data type to date.</p>\n<p>Since tpepPickupDateTime is likely a timestamp, converting it to a date ensures it only contains the date portion.</p>\n<p>Other options like .alias(&#x27;date&#x27;) only rename the column without changing its type.</p>\n<p>3.filter(&quot;fareAmount &gt; 0 AND fareAmount &lt; 100&quot;)</p>\n<p>The .filter() function in PySpark is used to apply SQL-like filtering conditions to DataFrames.</p>\n<p>The selected condition ensures that only rows where fareAmount is greater than 0 and less than 100 are retained.</p>\n<p>Other options like .where(df.fareAmount.isin()) filter for specific values rather than a range.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dp600:53:row53"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dp600-54-r54",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dp600:54",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen. You have a Fabric tenant that contains a new semantic model in OneLake. You use a Fabric notebook to read the data into a Spark DataFrame. You need to evaluate the data to calculate the min, max, mean, and standard deviation values for all the string and numeric columns. Solution: You use the following PySpark expression: df.explain() Does this meet the goal?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The df.explain() method in PySpark is used to print the logical and physical plans of a DataFrame, which helps in understanding how Spark plans to execute the query. It does not compute any statistical values like min, max, mean, or standard deviation.</p>\n<p>To achieve the goal, you should use: df.describe().show()</p>",
    "images": [],
