  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-8-r8",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:8",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a warehouse. Several times a day, the performance of all warehouse queries degrades. You suspect that Fabric is throttling the compute used by the warehouse. What should you use to identify whether throttling is occurring?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "the Capacity settings"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "the Monitoring hub"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "dynamic management views (DMVs)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "the Microsoft Fabric Capacity Metrics app"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The Microsoft Capacity Metrics app, also known as the metrics app, serves as a monitoring tool within the Microsoft Power BI service. It offers functionalities to track and analyze the resource utilization.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:8:row8"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-9-r9",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:9",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You plan to deploy Microsoft Power BI items by using Fabric deployment pipelines. You have a deployment pipeline that contains three stages named Development, Test, and Production. A workspace is assigned to each stage. You need to provide Power BI developers with access to the pipeline. The solution must meet the following requirements: - Ensure that the developers can deploy items to the workspaces for Development and Test. - Prevent the developers from deploying items to the workspace for Production. - Ensure that the developers can view items in Production. - Follow the principle of least privilege. Which three levels of access should you assign to the developers? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Build permission to the production semantic models"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Admin access to the deployment pipeline"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Viewer access to the Development and Test workspaces"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Viewer access to the Production workspace"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces"
      },
      {
        "id": "F",
        "text": "Contributor access to the Production workspace"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "B",
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Admin access to the deployment pipeline : Admin access to the deployment pipeline is necessary to manage the pipeline stages and deployment processes. This allows developers to configure deployments between stages but does not grant direct access to the workspaces themselves.</p>\n<p>Viewer access to the Production workspace : Providing Viewer access to the Production workspace ensures that developers can see the items in the Production stage but cannot make changes or deploy items to the Production workspace. This restriction aligns with the requirement to prevent developers from deploying to the Production workspace.</p>\n<p>Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces : Contributor access to the Development and Test workspaces allows developers to deploy and modify items within these stages. This access is necessary to enable developers to work on and test their items before they move to production.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:9:row9"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-10-r10",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:10",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You need to refresh the Orders table of the Online Sales department. The solution must meet the semantic model requirements. What should you include in the solution?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a Stored procedure activity to retrieve the maximum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a Stored procedure activity to retrieve the minimum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a dataflow to retrieve the minimum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a dataflow to retrieve the maximum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>D. an Azure Data Factory pipeline that executes a dataflow to retrieve the maximum value of the OrderID column in the destination lakehouse. A dataflow can be used to retrieve the max OrderID number (stored in the destination table - OrderID is a sequencial number). This number can be used to set from which row data must be added to the destination table (implementing an incremental load).</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:10:row10"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Contoso"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-11-r11",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:11",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. Workspace1 contains a warehouse named DW1. You share DW1 with User1 and assign User1 the default permissions for DW1. What can User1 do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Build reports by using the default dataset."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Read data from the tables in DW1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Connect to DW1 via the Azure SQL Analytics endpoint."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Read the underlying Parquet files from OneLake."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Sharing with default permissions grants only Read on the item, which is the equivalent of CONNECT: the recipient can connect to the SQL endpoint but cannot query tables, build reports on the default dataset, or read the underlying Parquet files. Those abilities need the additional share options to be selected.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:11:row11"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-12-r12",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:12",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant. You plan to create a Fabric notebook that will use Spark DataFrames to generate Microsoft Power BI visuals. You run the following code. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<pre><code>from powerbiclient import QuickVisualize, get_dataset_config, Report\n\nPBI_visualize = QuickVisualize(get_dataset_config(df))\nPBI_visualize</code></pre>\n<p>Answer Area</p><ul><li>The code embeds an existing Power BI report. — Yes / No</li><li>The code creates a Power BI report. — Yes / No</li><li>The code displays a summary of the DataFrame. — Yes / No</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Notebook code</div><pre><code>from powerbiclient import QuickVisualize, get_dataset_config, Report\n\nPBI_visualize = QuickVisualize(get_dataset_config(df))\nPBI_visualize</code></pre></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "label": "The code embeds an existing Power BI report.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "s2",
        "label": "The code creates a Power BI report.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "s3",
        "label": "The code displays a summary of the DataFrame.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>QuickVisualize builds a new Power BI report from the DataFrame, so the code creates a report but does not embed an existing one. It does not display a summary of the DataFrame; that would need a profiling or summary call.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:12:row12"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-13-r13",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:13",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Which syntax should you use in a notebook to access the Research division data for Productline1?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "spark.read.format(“delta”).load(“Files/ResearchProduct”)"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "spark.sql(“SELECT * FROM Lakehouse1.ResearchProduct ”)"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "spark.sql(“SELECT * FROM Lakehouse1.Tables.ResearchProduct ”)"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "external_table(ResearchProduct)"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>spark.sql(“SELECT * FROM Lakehouse1.ResearchProduct ”)</p>\n<p>The syntax of C and D is correct for KQL databases (incorrect in this use-case). When the shortcut is created, no additional folders have been added to the Tables section, therefore answer A is incorrect. Once created, the line of answer B can be used to access data correctly.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:13:row13"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Contoso"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-14-r14",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:14",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains the workspaces shown in the following table. You have a deployment pipeline named Pipeline1 that deploys items from Workspace_DEV to Workspace_TEST. In Pipeline1, all items that have matching names are paired. You deploy the contents of Workspace_DEV to Workspace_TEST by using Pipeline1. What will the contents of Workspace_TEST be once the deployment is complete?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Workspaces and items</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Workspace_DEV</td><td>Lakehouse1&lt;br&gt;Notebook1&lt;br&gt;Pipeline1&lt;br&gt;SemanticModel1</td></tr><tr><td>Workspace_TEST</td><td>Lakehouse2&lt;br&gt;Notebook2&lt;br&gt;SemanticModel1</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Lakehouse1 Lakehouse2 Notebook1 Notebook2 Pipeline1 SemanticModel1"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Lakehouse1 Notebook1 Pipeline1 SemanticModel1"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Lakehouse2 Notebook2 SemanticModel1"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Lakehouse2 Notebook2 Pipeline1 SemanticModel1"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Items with matching names are paired: SemanticModel1 exists in both workspaces, so it is paired and updated in place. Lakehouse1, Notebook1 and Pipeline1 exist only in Workspace_DEV, so they are deployed as new items into Workspace_TEST. Lakehouse2 and Notebook2 exist only in Workspace_TEST and are left untouched, so Workspace_TEST ends up holding all six items.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:14:row14"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Litware"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-15-r15",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:15",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have an Azure Repos Git repository named Repo1 and a Fabric-enabled Microsoft Power BI Premium capacity. The capacity contains two workspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2. Git integration is enabled at the workspace level. You plan to use Microsoft Power BI Desktop and Workspace1 to make version-controlled changes to a semantic model stored in Repo1. The changes will be built and deployed to Workspace2 by using Azure Pipelines. You need to ensure that report and semantic model definitions are saved as individual text files in a folder hierarchy. The solution must minimize development and maintenance effort. In which file format should you save the changes?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "PBIP"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "PBIDS"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "PBIT"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "PBIX"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>PBIP (Power BI Project) stores the semantic model and report definitions as individual text files in a folder hierarchy, which is what version control with Git and Azure Pipelines builds require. PBIX is a single binary file, PBIT is a template, and PBIDS is a connection file.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:15:row15"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-16-r16",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:16",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Which workspace role assignments should you recommend for ResearchReviewersGroup1 and ResearchReviewersGroup2? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p><ul><li>ResearchReviewersGroup1: Contributor / Member / Viewer</li><li>ResearchReviewersGroup2: Contributor / Member / Viewer</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "ResearchReviewersGroup1:",
        "options": [
          "Contributor",
          "Member",
          "Viewer"
        ],
        "answer": "Viewer"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "ResearchReviewersGroup2:",
        "options": [
          "Contributor",
          "Member",
          "Viewer"
        ],
        "answer": "Contributor"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>ResearchReviewersGroup1: Viewer. For the Research division workspaces, the members of ResearchReviewersGroup1 must be able to read lakehouse and warehouse data and shortcuts by using SQL endpoints. In a lakehouse, a user with the Viewer role can only read data stored in Tables using the SQL analytics endpoint.</p><p>ResearchReviewersGroup2: Contributor. For the Research division workspaces, the members of ResearchReviewersGroup2 must be able to read lakehouse data by using Lakehouse explorer. Members can edit and add content but cannot manage access or settings.</p><p>Member is not correct for either group: it carries more permissions compared to Contributor.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:16:row16"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Contoso"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-17-r17",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:17",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are implementing a medallion architecture in a single Fabric workspace. You have a lakehouse that contains the Bronze and Silver layers and a warehouse that contains the Gold layer. You create the items required to populate the layers as shown in the following table. You need to ensure that the layers are populated daily in sequential order such that Silver is populated only after Bronze is complete, and Gold is populated only after Silver is complete. The solution must minimize development effort and complexity. What should you use to execute each set of items? To answer, drag the appropriate options to the correct items. Each option may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p><p>Triggers: A pipeline Copy activity / A pipeline Dataflow activity / A pipeline Stored procedure activity / A schedule / A Spark job definition / An Invoke pipeline activity</p><ul><li>Orchestration pipeline: [drop target]</li><li>Bronze layer: [drop target]</li><li>Silver layer: [drop target]</li><li>Gold layer: [drop target]</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Medallion layers and tools</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Data integration tool</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bronze</td><td>Pipelines with Copy activities</td></tr><tr><td>Silver</td><td>Dataflows</td></tr><tr><td>Gold</td><td>Stored procedures</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Orchestration pipeline:",
        "options": [
          "A pipeline Copy activity",
          "A pipeline Dataflow activity",
          "A pipeline Stored procedure activity",
          "A schedule",
          "A Spark job definition",
          "An Invoke pipeline activity"
        ],
        "answer": "A schedule"
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Bronze layer:",
        "options": [
          "A pipeline Copy activity",
          "A pipeline Dataflow activity",
          "A pipeline Stored procedure activity",
          "A schedule",
          "A Spark job definition",
          "An Invoke pipeline activity"
        ],
        "answer": "A pipeline Copy activity"
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Silver layer:",
        "options": [
          "A pipeline Copy activity",
          "A pipeline Dataflow activity",
          "A pipeline Stored procedure activity",
          "A schedule",
          "A Spark job definition",
          "An Invoke pipeline activity"
        ],
        "answer": "A pipeline Dataflow activity"
      },
      {
        "id": "t4",
        "label": "Gold layer:",
        "options": [
          "A pipeline Copy activity",
          "A pipeline Dataflow activity",
          "A pipeline Stored procedure activity",
          "A schedule",
          "A Spark job definition",
          "An Invoke pipeline activity"
        ],
        "answer": "A pipeline Stored procedure activity"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The orchestration pipeline runs on a schedule so the layers populate daily in order. Bronze is raw ingestion, so a Copy activity loads the raw data. Silver cleans and transforms, so a Dataflow activity does the transformation. Gold holds business aggregates in the warehouse, so a Stored procedure activity runs the warehouse logic.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:17:row17"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-18-r18",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:18",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Which syntax should you use in a notebook to access the Research division data for Productline1?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "spark.read.format(“delta”).load(“Tables/productline1/ResearchProduct”)"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "spark.sql(“SELECT * FROM Lakehouse1.ResearchProduct ”)"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "external_table(‘Tables/ResearchProduct)"
      },
      {
