  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-50-r50",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:50",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a table named Nyctaxi_raw. Nyctaxi_raw contains the following table: You create a Fabric notebook and attach it to Lakehouse1. You need to use PySpark code to transform the data. The solution must meet the following requirements: - Add a column named pickupDate that will contain only the date portion of pickupDateTime. - Filter the DataFrame to include only rows where fareAmount is a positive number that is less than 100. How should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Nyctaxi_raw table — Name / Data type: pickupDateTime Timestamp, passengerCount Integer, fareAmount Double, paymentType String, tipAmount Double.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p><pre><code>df = spark.read.format(&quot;delta&quot;).load(&quot;Tables/nyctaxi_raw&quot;)\n\ndf2 = [dropdown 1] (&quot;pickupDate&quot;, df[&#x27;pickupDateTime&#x27;]\n    [dropdown 2] \\\n    [dropdown 3]</code></pre><ul><li>Dropdown 1: df.columns / df.select / df.withColumn / df.withColumnsRenamed</li><li>Dropdown 2: .alias(&#x27;date&#x27;) / .cast(&#x27;date&#x27;) / .cast(&#x27;pickupDate&#x27;) / .getfield(&#x27;date&#x27;)</li><li>Dropdown 3: .filter(&quot; fareAmount &gt; 0 AND fareAmount &lt; 100&quot;) / .filter(col(&quot;fareAmount&quot;).contains(&quot;1..100&quot;)) / .when(df.fareAmount &gt; 0 AND fareAmount &lt; 100) / .where(df.fareAmount.isin([1,100]))</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Table columns</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Data type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>pickupDateTime</td><td>Timestamp</td></tr><tr><td>passengerCount</td><td>Integer</td></tr><tr><td>fareAmount</td><td>Double</td></tr><tr><td>paymentType</td><td>String</td></tr><tr><td>tipAmount</td><td>Double</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><pre>df = spark.read.format(&quot;delta&quot;).load(&quot;Tables/nyctaxi_raw&quot;)\n\ndf2 = [dropdown 1] (&quot;pickupDate&quot;, df[&#x27;pickupDateTime&#x27;]\n    [dropdown 2] \\\n    [dropdown 3]</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "df.columns",
          "df.select",
          "df.withColumn",
          "df.withColumnsRenamed"
        ],
        "answer": "df.withColumn"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          ".alias('date')",
          ".cast('date')",
          ".cast('pickupDate')",
          ".getfield('date')"
        ],
        "answer": ".cast('date')"
      },
      {
        "id": "d3",
        "label": "d3",
        "options": [
          ".filter(\" fareAmount > 0 AND fareAmount < 100\")",
          ".filter(col(\"fareAmount\").contains(\"1..100\"))",
          ".when(df.fareAmount > 0 AND fareAmount < 100)",
          ".where(df.fareAmount.isin([1,100]))"
        ],
        "answer": ".filter(\" fareAmount > 0 AND fareAmount < 100\")"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. df.withColumn.</p>\n<p>The withColumn function is used to add or replace a column in a DataFrame.</p>\n<p>It is appropriate here because we are creating a new column (pickupDate) based on an existing column (tpepPickupDateTime).</p>\n<p>Alternative options like df.select or df.withColumnsRenamed would not modify a column directly.</p>\n<p>2 .cast(&#x27;date&#x27;)</p>\n<p>The .cast(&#x27;date&#x27;) function is used to convert a column’s data type to date.</p>\n<p>Since tpepPickupDateTime is likely a timestamp, converting it to a date ensures it only contains the date portion.</p>\n<p>Other options like .alias(&#x27;date&#x27;) only rename the column without changing its type.</p>\n<p>3.filter(&quot;fareAmount &gt; 0 AND fareAmount &lt; 100&quot;)</p>\n<p>The .filter() function in PySpark is used to apply SQL-like filtering conditions to DataFrames.</p>\n<p>The selected condition ensures that only rows where fareAmount is greater than 0 and less than 100 are retained.</p>\n<p>Other options like .where(df.fareAmount.isin()) filter for specific values rather than a range.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:50:row50"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-51-r51",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:51",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a Microsoft Power BI report. You are exploring a new semantic model. You need to display the following column statistics: - Count - Average - Null count - Distinct count - Standard deviation Which Power Query function should you run?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Table.schema"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Table.view"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Table.FuzzyGroup"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Table.Profile"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Table.Profile returns one row per column with Count, Average, Null count, Distinct count, Standard deviation, and minimum and maximum values. Table.Schema returns column metadata only, Table.View is a view helper, and Table.FuzzyGroup groups similar values.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:51:row51"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-52-r52",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:52",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are implementing two dimension tables named Customers and Products in a Fabric warehouse. You need to use slowly changing dimension (SCD) to manage the versioning of data. The solution must meet the requirements shown in the following table. Which type of SCD should you use for each table? To answer, drag the appropriate SCD types to the correct tables. Each SCD type may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p><p>SCD Types: Type 0 / Type 1 / Type 2 / Type 3</p><ul><li>Customers: [drop target]</li><li>Products: [drop target]</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Change handling per table</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Change action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Customers</td><td>Create a new version of the row.</td></tr><tr><td>Products</td><td>Overwrite the existing value in the latest row.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Customers:",
        "options": [
          "Type 0",
          "Type 1",
          "Type 2",
          "Type 3"
        ],
        "answer": "Type 2"
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Products:",
        "options": [
          "Type 0",
          "Type 1",
          "Type 2",
          "Type 3"
        ],
        "answer": "Type 1"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Creating a new version of a row for each change preserves history, which is Type 2. Overwriting the existing value in the latest row keeps only the current value, which is Type 1.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:52:row52"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-53-r53",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:53",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are analyzing the data in a Fabric notebook. You have a Spark DataFrame assigned to a variable named df. You need to use the Chart view in the notebook to explore the data manually. Which function should you run to make the data available in the Chart view?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "displayHTML"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "show"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
