  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-58-r58",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:58",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are creating a data flow in Fabric to ingest data from an Azure SQL database by using a T-SQL statement. You need to ensure that any foldable Power Query transformation steps are processed by the Microsoft SQL Server engine. How should you complete the code? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p><p>Values: EnableFolding / NativeQuery / Optimize / Record / StopFolding / Table / Value</p><pre><code>let\n    Source = Sql.Databases(\n        &quot;server.database.windows.net&quot;\n    ),\n    Database = Source{[Name = &quot;db&quot;]}[Data],\n    Query = [drop target 1].[drop target 2](\n        Database,\n        &quot; SELECT * FROM customer WHERE country IN (&#x27;USA&#x27;, &#x27;UK&#x27;)&quot;,\n        null,\n        [ [drop target 3] = true]\n    )\nin\n    Query</code></pre>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>let\n    Source = Sql.Databases(\n        &quot;server.database.windows.net&quot;\n    ),\n    Database = Source{[Name = &quot;db&quot;]}[Data],\n    Query = [drop target 1].[drop target 2](\n        Database,\n        &quot; SELECT * FROM customer WHERE country IN (&#x27;USA&#x27;, &#x27;UK&#x27;)&quot;,\n        null,\n        [ [drop target 3] = true]\n    )\nin\n    Query</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Target 1",
        "options": [
          "EnableFolding",
          "NativeQuery",
          "Optimize",
          "Record",
          "StopFolding",
          "Table",
          "Value"
        ],
        "answer": "Value"
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Target 2",
        "options": [
          "EnableFolding",
          "NativeQuery",
          "Optimize",
          "Record",
          "StopFolding",
          "Table",
          "Value"
        ],
        "answer": "NativeQuery"
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Target 3",
        "options": [
          "EnableFolding",
          "NativeQuery",
          "Optimize",
          "Record",
          "StopFolding",
          "Table",
          "Value"
        ],
        "answer": "EnableFolding"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Value.</p>\n<p>Value is the function used to store the result of the query.</p>\n<p>NativeQuery.</p>\n<p>NativeQuery allows executing a direct SQL query within Power Query M code. EnableFolding .</p>\n<p>This enables query folding, ensuring that transformations are executed in SQL Server rather than in Power Query.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:58:row58"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-59-r59",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:59",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains two workspaces named Workspace1 and Workspace2 and a user named User1. You need to ensure that User1 can perform the following tasks: - Create a new domain. - Create two subdomains named subdomain1 and subdomain2. - Assign Workspace1 to subdomain1. - Assign Workspace2 to subdomain2. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege. Which role should you assign to User1?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "domain admin"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "domain contributor"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Fabric admin"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "workspace Admin"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Creating a domain is a tenant-level action that only a Fabric admin can perform. Domain admins manage subdomains inside a domain they administer, and a workspace Admin role is scoped to a single workspace.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:59:row59"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-60-r60",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:60",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. In Workspace1, you create a data pipeline named Pipeline1. You have CSV files stored in an Azure Storage account. You need to add an activity to Pipeline1 that will copy data from the CSV files to Lakehouse1. The activity must support Power Query M formula language expressions. Which type of activity should you add?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Dataflow"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Notebook"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Script"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Copy data"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A Dataflow activity runs a dataflow that uses the Power Query M formula language, so it can copy data from the CSV files into the lakehouse with M expressions. A Copy data activity copies data but does not support M expressions.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:60:row60"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-61-r61",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:61",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You need to migrate the Research division data for Productline2. The solution must meet the data preparation requirements. How should you complete the code? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p><p>Answer Area</p><pre><code>df=spark.read.format(&#x27;csv&#x27;).options(header=&quot;true&quot;,inferSchema=&quot;true&quot;).load(&quot;fs1@storage1.dfs.core.windows.net/files/productline2&quot;)\n\ndf.write.mode(&quot;overwrite&quot;).format(&quot;[dropdown 1]&quot;).save(&quot;[dropdown 2]&quot;)</code></pre><ul><li>Dropdown 1: csv / delta / parquet</li><li>Dropdown 2: productline2 / Tables/productline2 / Tables/research/productline2</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>df=spark.read.format(&#x27;csv&#x27;).options(header=&quot;true&quot;,inferSchema=&quot;true&quot;).load(&quot;fs1@storage1.dfs.core.windows.net/files/productline2&quot;)\n\ndf.write.mode(&quot;overwrite&quot;).format(&quot;[dropdown 1]&quot;).save(&quot;[dropdown 2]&quot;)</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "csv",
          "delta",
          "parquet"
        ],
        "answer": "delta"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "productline2",
          "Tables/productline2",
          "Tables/research/productline2"
        ],
        "answer": "Tables/productline2"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Managed tables in a lakehouse are stored in Delta format, so the format must be delta. Writing to Tables/productline2 places the data in the Tables section as a managed table. Writing to productline2 alone would land in the Files section, and Tables/research/productline2 is not the location of the Productline2 data.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:61:row61"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": "Contoso"
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-62-r62",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:62",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 and a user named DBUser. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. DBUser does NOT have access to the tenant. You grant DBUser access to Lakehouse1 as shown in the following exhibit. Use the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p><ul><li>DBUser can read the data in Lakehouse1 by using the [Answer choice]. — default semantic model / OneLake endpoint / Tabular Data Stream (TDS) endpoint</li><li>DBUser can query the data in Lakehouse1 by using [Answer choice]. — a KQL queryset / OneLake file explorer / Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS)</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): “Grant people access” dialog for MyLakehouse1</div><ul><li>Text: “People you share this Lakehouse with can open it and its SQL endpoint and read the default dataset. To allow them to read directly in the Lakehouse, grant additional permissions.”</li><li><b>User:</b> DBUser</li><li><b>Additional permissions:</b> ☐ Read all SQL endpoint data · ☑ Read all Apache Spark · ☐ Build reports on the default semantic model</li><li><b>Notification options:</b> ☑ Notify recipients by email</li></ul></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "DBUser can read the data in Lakehouse1 by using the",
        "options": [
          "default semantic model",
          "OneLake endpoint",
          "Tabular Data Stream (TDS) endpoint"
        ],
        "answer": "OneLake endpoint"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "DBUser can query the data in Lakehouse1 by using",
        "options": [
          "a KQL queryset",
          "OneLake file explorer",
          "Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS)"
        ],
        "answer": "OneLake file explorer"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Only the &quot;Read all Apache Spark&quot; permission is checked, so DBUser can read the lakehouse data through the OneLake endpoint used by Spark and OneLake APIs, and can access it with OneLake file explorer. The SQL endpoint data option is unchecked, so the TDS endpoint and SSMS are not available, and a KQL queryset applies to KQL databases.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:62:row62"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-63-r63",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:63",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a data pipeline named Pipeline1 and a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. You perform the following actions: - Create a workspace named Workspace2. - Create a deployment pipeline named DeployPipeline1 that will deploy items from Workspace1 to Workspace2. - Add a folder named Folder1 to Workspace1. - Move Lakehouse1 to Folder1. - Run DeployPipeline1. Which structure will Workspace2 have when DeployPipeline1 is complete?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Folder1Pipeline1 Folder1Lakehouse1"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Pipeline1 Lakehouse1"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Pipeline1 Folder1Lakehouse1"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Folder1Lakehouse1"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Folders are part of an item’s identity in deployment pipelines: when an item in a folder is deployed, its folder is recreated in the target stage. Lakehouse1 was moved to Folder1, so it arrives as Folder1Lakehouse1. Pipeline1 was never moved to a folder, so it arrives as Pipeline1.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:63:row63"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-64-r64",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:64",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains a Delta table that has one million Parquet files. You need to remove files that were NOT referenced by the table during the past 30 days. The solution must ensure that the transaction log remains consistent, and the ACID properties of the table are maintained. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From OneLake file explorer, delete the files."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Run the OPTIMIZE command and specify the Z-order parameter."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Run the OPTIMIZE command and specify the V-order parameter."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Run the VACUUM command."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>VACUUM removes files that are no longer referenced by a Delta table, including files older than the retention period. OPTIMIZE compacts small files and applies Z-order or V-order; it does not delete unreferenced files. Deleting files from OneLake file explorer bypasses the table’s transaction log.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:64:row64"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-65-r65",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:65",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Readings from 100 IoT devices are appended to a Delta table in Lakehouse1. Each set of readings is approximately 25 KB. Approximately 10 GB of data is received daily. All the table and SparkSession settings are set to the default. You discover that queries are slow to execute. In addition, the lakehouse storage contains data and log files that are no longer used. You need to remove the files that are no longer used and combine small files into larger files with a target size of 1 GB per file. What should you do? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p><p>Actions: Set the autoCompact table setting. / Set the optimizeWrite table setting. / Run the VACUUM command on a schedule. / Set the autoCompact SparkSession setting. / Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule. / Set the parallelDelete SparkSession setting.</p><ul><li>Remove the files: [drop target]</li><li>Combine the files: [drop target]</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Remove the files:",
        "options": [
          "Set the autoCompact table setting.",
          "Set the optimizeWrite table setting.",
          "Run the VACUUM command on a schedule.",
          "Set the autoCompact SparkSession setting.",
          "Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule.",
          "Set the parallelDelete SparkSession setting."
        ],
        "answer": "Run the VACUUM command on a schedule."
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Combine the files:",
        "options": [
          "Set the autoCompact table setting.",
          "Set the optimizeWrite table setting.",
          "Run the VACUUM command on a schedule.",
          "Set the autoCompact SparkSession setting.",
          "Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule.",
          "Set the parallelDelete SparkSession setting."
        ],
        "answer": "Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Remove the files that are no longer used:</p>\n<p>Run the VACUUM command on a schedule: The VACUUM command cleans up old files and log files that are no longer needed by the Delta table, helping to free up storage and potentially improve performance by reducing the number of files the query engine needs to consider.</p>\n<p>Combine small files into larger files:</p>\n<p>Run the OPTIMIZE command on a schedule: The OPTIMIZE command compacts small files into larger ones, improving read performance by reducing the overhead associated with opening many small files. This can be particularly useful when you have a large number of small files due to frequent appends of small data sets.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:65:row65"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-66-r66",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:66",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric notebook that has the Python code and output shown in the following exhibit. Which type of analytics are you performing?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Notebook code</div><pre><code># Look at a histogram of tips by count by using Matplotlib\n\nax1 = sampled_taxi_pd_df[&#x27;tipAmount&#x27;].plot(kind=&#x27;hist&#x27;, bins=25, facecolor=&#x27;lightblue&#x27;...)\nax1.set_title(&#x27;Tip amount distribution&#x27;)\nax1.set_xlabel(&#x27;Tip Amount ($)&#x27;)\nax1.set_ylabel(&#x27;Counts&#x27;)\nplt.suptitle(&#x27;&#x27;)\nplt.show()</code></pre></div><div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Chart output</div><p>A histogram titled <b>“Tip amount distribution”</b> (x-axis: Tip Amount ($), 0–25+; y-axis: Counts). The bars start very tall at $0–1 (about 860 counts), then fall steadily: about 560 at $1–2, 310 at $2–3, 130 at $3–4, 70 at $4–5, and only a few counts beyond $6, with a thin tail out to roughly $26. The distribution is strongly right-skewed.</p></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "descriptive"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "diagnostic"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "prescriptive"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "predictive"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The notebook draws a histogram of tip amounts and the output shows the distribution of the values. Summarizing and visualizing what the data contains is descriptive analytics. Diagnostic analytics would explain why something happened, predictive would forecast, and prescriptive would recommend actions.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:66:row66"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-67-r67",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:67",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are the administrator of a Fabric workspace that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. Lakehouse1 contains the following tables: - Table1: A Delta table created by using a shortcut - Table2: An external table created by using Spark - Table3: A managed table You plan to connect to Lakehouse1 by using its SQL endpoint. What will you be able to do after connecting to Lakehouse1?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Read Table3."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Update the data Table3."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Read Table2."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Update the data in Table1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Read Table3.</p>\n<p>When you connect to a Lakehouse SQL endpoint in Microsoft Fabric, you are connecting to:</p>\n<p>The SQL analytics endpoint (read-only T-SQL surface over lakehouse tables)</p>\n<p>The SQL endpoint has important limitations.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:67:row67"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
