    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-68-r68",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:68",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains customer churn data stored as Parquet files in OneLake. The data contains details about customer demographics and product usage. You create a Fabric notebook to read the data into a Spark DataFrame. You then create column charts in the notebook that show the distribution of retained customers as compared to lost customers based on geography, the number of products purchased, age, and customer tenure. Which type of analytics are you performing?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "diagnostic"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "descriptive"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "prescriptive"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "predictive"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Descriptive analytics provide granular insights based on historical data. This includes tracking past purchases, customer complaints, customer service reviews, and more. The column charts describe what happened in the data: the distribution of retained customers as compared to lost customers.</p><p>Predictive analytics forecasts the behavior of customers based on past data. Prescriptive analytics finds solutions based on insights from descriptive analytics. Diagnostic analytics involves the collection and examination of data pertaining to a particular issue or occurrence in order to comprehend the underlying causes.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:68:row68"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-69-r69",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:69",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant. You are creating a Fabric Data Factory pipeline. You have a stored procedure that returns the number of active customers and their average sales for the current month. You need to add an activity that will execute the stored procedure in a warehouse. The returned values must be available to the downstream activities of the pipeline. Which type of activity should you add?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Get metadata"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Switch"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Lookup"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Append variable"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A Lookup activity executes a query or stored procedure and returns its result so later activities can use it. Get metadata returns information about data rather than query results, Switch branches on a value, and Append variable adds a value to an array variable.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:69:row69"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-dumps-70-r70",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:dumps:70",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a lakehouse. You plan to use a visual query to merge two tables. You need to ensure that the query returns all the rows in both tables. Which type of join should you use?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "inner"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "full outer"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "left outer"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "right anti"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "right outer"
      },
      {
        "id": "F",
        "text": "left anti"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A full outer join returns every row from both tables, matching rows where the keys are equal and keeping unmatched rows from each side. Inner returns only matches, left and right outer keep all rows from one side only, and anti joins return only non-matching rows.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:dumps:70:row70"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-1-r1",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:1",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric semantic model named Model1 that contains a table named Sales. Sales contains one million rows and uses Import storage mode. Model1 includes 50 DAX formulas that reference the Sales table and two DAX measures named Sales Quantity and Sales Quantity LY and 15 other measures. For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "label": "Changing the storage mode of the Sales table to Dual will improve the performance of the formulas.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "s2",
        "label": "Creating a variable in the Sales Quantity LY measure to store the result of the Sales Quantity measure will improve the overall performance of Model1.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "s3",
        "label": "Using the DIVIDE() DAX function in the measures will improve the overall performance of Model1.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Dual storage changes where the Sales table is read from; it does not change how the 50 DAX formulas evaluate, so it does not by itself improve the performance of the formulas. A variable helps only when the same value is calculated more than once in a measure; storing the Sales Quantity result in a variable does not help if it is used once. DIVIDE() guards against division by zero; it is a correctness function, not a performance optimisation.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:1:row1"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-2-r2",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:2",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains a table named TemperatureUnit. TemperatureUnit contains a column named Unit. Unit contains manually added options for Celsius and Fahrenheit. Model1 contains the measures shown in the exhibit. You need to add a dynamic format string to the [TemperatureDisplay] measure to enable users to switch display values between metric (Celsius) and imperial (Fahrenheit). How should you complete the DAX expression? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Measures in Model1</div><pre><code>[TemperatureMetric] = AVERAGE(Weather[Temp])\n\n[TemperatureDisplay] =\nVAR Unit = SELECTEDVALUE(TemperatureUnit[Unit], &quot;Celsius&quot;)\nVAR CelsiusValue = [TemperatureMetric]\nRETURN\n    SWITCH(\n        Unit,\n        &quot;Fahrenheit&quot;, (CelsiusValue * 9/5) + 32,\n        CelsiusValue\n    )</code></pre></div><pre>TempFormatted =\n    SWITCH(\n        [dropdown 1]( [dropdown 2], &quot;Celsius&quot;),\n        &quot;Celsius&quot;, &quot;0.0 °C&quot;,\n        &quot;Fahrenheit&quot;, &quot;0.0 °F&quot;,\n        &quot;0.0&quot;\n    )</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "CALCULATE",
          "LOOKUPVALUE",
          "SELECTEDVALUE"
        ],
        "answer": "SELECTEDVALUE"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "TemperatureMetric[Unit]",
          "Temperature[Unit]",
          "Unit[TemperatureUnit]"
        ],
        "answer": "Temperature[Unit]"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The format string must react to the unit the user picks. SELECTEDVALUE returns the single selected value of TemperatureUnit[Unit] (or &quot;Celsius&quot; when nothing is selected), and the column that holds the unit is Temperature[Unit]. The SWITCH then formats the value as &quot;0.0 °C&quot; or &quot;0.0 °F&quot;.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:2:row2"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-3-r3",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:3",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Microsoft Power BI semantic model that contains a measure named TotalSalesAmount. TotalSalesAmount returns a sales revenue amount that is translated into a selected currency. You need to ensure that the value returned by TotalSalesAmount is formatted to use the correct currency symbol. What should you include in the solution?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "A dynamic format string"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "The WINDOW DAX function"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "A linguistic schema"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "A field parameter"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Scenario: Semantic model contains a measure: TotalSalesAmount. This measure returns sales revenue translated into a selected currency. Requirement: Format the result using the correct currency symbol dynamically. Analysis: Dynamic format string in DAX is specifically designed for applying formats (like currency symbols) dynamically based on slicers or calculation context. WINDOW function # used for windowing calculations, not formatting. Linguistic schema # used for Q&amp;A natural language, not for formatting. Field parameter # used to swap fields/measures dynamically, not for formatting.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:3:row3"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-4-r4",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:4",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains the following tables.</p><p>You are designing a Microsoft Power BI report named Report1 that will deliver near-real-time (NRT) shipping data. Report1 will contain data about many shipped packages. You need to ensure that each table is in the appropriate storage mode to support Report1. The solution must support near-realtime data delivery while maintaining high query performance. Which storage mode should you select for each table? To answer, drag the appropriate storage modes to the correct tables. Each mode may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Model1 tables</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FactPackageEvents</td><td>Contains two columns named PackageID and CarrierID</td></tr><tr><td>DimPackage</td><td>Contains a column named PackageID</td></tr><tr><td>DimCarrier</td><td>Contains a column named CarrierID</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "FactPackageEvents:",
        "options": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Hybrid",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "Hybrid"
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "DimPackage:",
        "options": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Hybrid",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "Dual"
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "DimCarrier:",
        "options": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Hybrid",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "Dual"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Report1 delivers near-real-time shipping data. The fact table must serve both fresh data and cached queries, so it uses Hybrid storage. The two dimensions are small and change rarely, so Dual storage lets them be served from memory or from the underlying tables as needed.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:4:row4"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-5-r5",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:5",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains a fact table with millions of rows of shipment data. You need to enable partitioning to improve the query performance and manageability of Model1. Which two tools can you use to achieve the goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Tabular Editor"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Microsoft SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS)"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "the Microsoft Power BI service"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "DAX Studio"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Partitions are managed through the XMLA endpoint. Tabular Editor and SQL Server Management Studio can both create and manage partitions on a semantic model. Power BI Desktop does not expose partition management, and the Power BI service and DAX Studio are not the tools for this task.</p>Note: the matching question in the reference bank names Power BI Desktop as one of the tools. Power BI Desktop cannot create or manage partitions; partitions are managed through the XMLA endpoint with Tabular Editor or SQL Server Management Studio, so the key shown here is Tabular Editor and SQL Server Management Studio.",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:5:row5"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-6-r6",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:6",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Microsoft Power BI project that contains a file named definition.pbir. definition.pbir contains the following JSON.</p><p>For each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): definition.pbir</div><pre><code>{\n  &quot;version&quot;: &quot;1.0&quot;,\n  &quot;datasetReference&quot;: {\n    &quot;byPath&quot;: {\n      &quot;path&quot;: &quot;../Sales.Dataset&quot;\n    },\n    &quot;byConnection&quot;: null\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "label": "definition.pbir is in the PBIR-Legacy format.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "s2",
        "label": "The semantic model referenced by definition.pbir is located in the Power BI service.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "No"
      },
      {
        "id": "s3",
        "label": "When the related report is opened, Power BI Desktop will open the semantic model in full edit mode.",
        "options": [
          "Yes",
          "No"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Explanation:</p><p>We are analyzing the JSON for definition.pbir in a Power BI Project: { &quot; version &quot; : &quot; 1.0 &quot; , &quot; datasetReference &quot; : { &quot; byPath &quot; : { &quot; path &quot; : &quot; ../Sales.Dataset &quot; }, &quot; byConnection &quot; : null } } Statement 1: &quot; definition.pbir is in the PBIR-Legacy format. &quot; No # The legacy PBIR format references datasets by byConnection (pointing to the Power BI service). Here, it uses byPath, which is the new project format, not legacy. Statement 2: &quot; The semantic model referenced by definition.pbir is located in the Power BI service. &quot; No # The JSON shows byPath: &quot; ../Sales.Dataset &quot; , meaning the semantic model is referenced locally within the project folder (Sales.Dataset), not in the Power BI service. Statement 3: &quot; When the related report is opened, Power BI Desktop will open the semantic model in full edit mode. &quot; Yes # Since the semantic model is referenced by path (local PBIP project files), Power BI Desktop will open the model in full edit mode. If it had been byConnection, it would open in live connect mode to the service instead. Final Answer: PBIR-Legacy format # No Semantic model in Power BI service # No Opens in full edit mode # Yes</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:6:row6"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-7-r7",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:7",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a data warehouse. You need to load rows into a large Type 2 slowly changing dimension (SCD). The solution must minimize resource usage. Which T-SQL statement should you use?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "update and insert"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "MERGE"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "TRUNCATE TABLE and INSERT"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "CREATE TABLE AS SELECT"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>For Type 2 Slowly Changing Dimensions (SCD): MERGE is the standard T-SQL approach, allowing conditional UPDATE for existing rows and INSERT for new history records, in a single statement. UPDATE + INSERT would require multiple steps and higher resource usage. TRUNCATE + INSERT would destroy historical data, violating SCD rules. CREATE TABLE AS SELECT is not suitable for incremental Type 2 handling. Correct answer: B . Reference: Implement SCD Type 2 with MERGE</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:7:row7"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-8-r8",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:8",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named Model1. Model1 contains a fact table named FactSales. FactSales has a relationship to a dimension table named DimDate on a column named OrderDate. To FactSales, you add a second relationship between FactSales and DimDate based on a column named ShippedDate. You need to filter by using ShippedDate in a new report. The solution must NOT affect the function of existing reports. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Disable the ShippedDate relationship between the tables."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Use a relatedtable DAX function."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Enable the ShippedDate relationship."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Use a userelationship DAX function."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Use a crossfilter DAX function."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>The ShippedDate relationship is inactive, so existing reports that use the active OrderDate relationship are unaffected. In the new report you activate the ShippedDate relationship for the measure with USERELATIONSHIP and control the filter direction with CROSSFILTER.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:8:row8"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-9-r9",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:9",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have two Microsoft Power BI queries named Employee and Retired Roles. You need to merge the Employee query with the Retired Roles query. The solution must ensure that rows in the Employee query that match the Retired Roles query are removed. Which column and Join Kind should you use in Power Query Editor? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power Query Merge dialog</div><p>Merge: select a table and matching columns to create a merged table. The <b>Division</b> column is highlighted in both tables.</p><p><b>Employee (top table)</b></p><div><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Division</th><th>Title Detail</th><th>Role</th><th>Department</th><th>Department Number</th><th>Division Number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>198</td><td>Services</td><td>Marketing</td><td>Vice President</td><td>Marketing</td><td>22</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>299</td><td>Services</td><td>Business Development</td><td>Vice President</td><td>Sales</td><td>17</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>169</td><td>Services</td><td>Human Resources</td><td>Vice President</td><td>Human Resources</td><td>13</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>146</td><td>Services</td><td>HSE &amp; Risk Management</td><td>Vice President</td><td>Safety</td><td>16</td><td>14</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Retired Roles (bottom table)</b></p><div><table><thead><tr><th>Division</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Services</td><td>Technical Analyst</td></tr><tr><td>Services</td><td>Technical Support Analyst</td></tr><tr><td>Services</td><td>Senior Engineer</td></tr><tr><td>Services</td><td>Chief Technical Officer</td></tr><tr><td>Services</td><td>Applied Technology Specialist</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Join Kind</b> drop-down options: Left Outer, Right Outer, Full Outer, Inner, Left Anti, Right Anti.</p></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "Column:",
        "options": [
          "ID",
          "Division",
          "Title Detail",
          "Role",
          "Department",
          "Department Number",
          "Division Number"
        ],
        "answer": "Division"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "Join Kind:",
        "options": [
          "Left Outer (all from first, matching from second)",
          "Right Outer (all from second, matching from first)",
          "Full Outer (all rows from both)",
          "Inner (only matching rows)",
          "Left Anti (rows only in first)",
          "Right Anti (rows only in second)"
        ],
        "answer": "Left Anti (rows only in first)"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Note: the marked answer area shows Inner (only matching rows). An inner join keeps only the rows that match, which is the opposite of the stated goal. Left Anti returns the rows in Employee that have no match in Retired Roles, so the matching rows are removed.</p><p>The matching column is Division, which appears in both queries. Left Anti returns only the rows in the first query (Employee) that have no matching row in the second query (Retired Roles), so the employees who have retired are removed from the result.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:9:row9"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-10-r10",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:10",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant named Tenant1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1. You need to add data to Lakehouse1 from a CSV file in an Azure Storage account outside of Fabric. The solution must minimize development effort. What should you use to add the data?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "a Dataflow Gen2"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a shortcut"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "a copy job"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "a pipeline"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Requirement Tenant: Fabric tenant Tenant1 Data target: Lakehouse1 data store: CSV file in an Azure Storage account (outside of Fabric) Requirement: Minimize development effort Step 1: Understand options A). Dataflow Gen2 Used for ETL (transformations, cleansing, ingestion). More effort because you need to build Power Query transformations. Not minimal effort for simply exposing a file. B). Shortcut Fabric supports creating shortcuts to external data sources (OneLake, ADLS Gen2, Azure Blob, Dataverse, etc.). Shortcut directly exposes external files/tables inside the lakehouse without copying or moving data. This is the lowest-effort solution to access external CSV. C). Copy job Pipelines have a copy activity to physically copy data into Fabric. Requires setup and execution. More development effort than a shortcut. D). Pipeline Used for orchestrating complex ETL workflows. Overhead for this scenario. Step 2: Correct Answer Since the goal is simply to access a CSV in external storage with minimal development effort, the right solution is: B). a shortcut</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:10:row10"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-11-r11",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:11",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a single semantic model that has two Microsoft Power BI reports. You have a Microsoft 365 subscription that contains a data loss prevention (DLP) policy named DLP1. You need to apply DLP1 to the items in Workspace1. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Apply a master data endorsement to the semantic model."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Apply a certified endorsement to the semantic model."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Create a workspace identity."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Apply sensitivity labels to the semantic model and reports."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Step 1: Understand the scenario Workspace1 contains a semantic model and two Power BI reports. A Microsoft 365 Data Loss Prevention (DLP) policy (DLP1) already exists. Requirement: Apply DLP1 to the workspace items. In Microsoft Fabric and Power BI, DLP policies are enforced through Microsoft Purview Information Protection (MIP) sensitivity labels. Step 2: Evaluate each option A). Apply a master data endorsement to the semantic model. Endorsements (promoted, certified) help users find and trust datasets but do not enforce DLP policies. Not correct. B). Apply a certified endorsement to the semantic model. Certification improves discoverability and trust, but again, it does not trigger DLP policy enforcement. Not correct. C). Create a workspace identity. Workspace identity (service principal) is used for connecting to external resources with managed identity. It does not relate to DLP policy application. Not correct. D). Apply sensitivity labels to the semantic model and reports. Sensitivity labels (from Microsoft Purview) integrate with Microsoft 365 DLP policies. When sensitivity labels are applied to Power BI datasets, reports, dashboards, and workspaces, DLP1 can enforce protections such as preventing sharing or exporting sensitive data. This is the correct answer. Step 3: Correct Action To apply DLP1 to Workspace1 items, you must: Configure sensitivity labels in Microsoft Purview. Apply those sensitivity labels to the semantic model and reports inside Workspace1. The existing DLP1 policy will then govern data handling for those items.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:11:row11"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-12-r12",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:12",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are creating a report and a semantic model in Microsoft Power BI Desktop. The Value measure has the expression shown in the following exhibit.</p><p>Use the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power BI Desktop: Value measure</div><ul><li><b>Measure name:</b> Value · <b>Home table:</b> Fact · <b>Format:</b> Dynamic · <b>Data category:</b> Uncategorized</li><li><b>Format expression:</b></li></ul><pre><code>SWITCH(SELECTEDVALUE(Metric[Metric]),&quot;# of Customers&quot;, &quot;#&quot;,\n       &quot;Gross Margin %&quot;,&quot;0.00%&quot;,\n       &quot;#,###&quot;)</code></pre><p><b>Report page</b> “Top 5 Products – # of Customers”: a metric slicer with the options # of Customers (selected), Gross Margin, Gross Margin %, Total COGS and Total Revenue; a table of customers per top-5 product (Primus 32, MI-72 17, one unnamed product 16, Gladius 14, Sova 10, total 85); plus charts of customers over time, by industry and by state.</p></div><pre>A [dropdown 1] was added to the Value measure.\n\nThe Value measure can return values formatted as [dropdown 2].</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "dynamic format string",
          "calculation group",
          "data category",
          "synonym"
        ],
        "answer": "dynamic format string"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "percentages only",
          "percentages or decimals",
          "whole numbers only",
          "percentages or whole numbers"
        ],
        "answer": "percentages or whole numbers"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A dynamic format string lets the Value measure change its display format depending on the selected metric. The measure returns either a percentage or a whole number, so the format string must cover both: percentages or whole numbers.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:12:row12"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-13-r13",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:13",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model named model1. The two largest columns in model1 are shown in the following table.</p><p>You need to optimize model 1. The solution must meet the following requirements: * Reduce the model size. * Increase refresh performance when using Import mode. * Ensure that the datetime value for each sales transaction is available in the model. What should you do on each column? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Two largest columns in model1</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Cardinality</th><th>Compressed size</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>TransactionKey</td><td>2,500,350,000</td><td>160 GB</td><td>Contains an incrementing surrogate key for a fact table named SalesTransaction</td></tr><tr><td>SaleDateTime</td><td>6,850,273</td><td>120 GB</td><td>Contains the datetime (to the nearest second) of when a sale occurred</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "TransactionKey:",
        "options": [
          "Remove the column.",
          "Change the data type of the column.",
          "Remove duplicates",
          "Split the column.",
          "Truncate the data."
        ],
        "answer": "Remove the column."
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "SaleDateTime:",
        "options": [
          "Remove the column.",
          "Change the data type of the column.",
          "Remove duplicates",
          "Split the column.",
          "Truncate the data."
        ],
        "answer": "Split the column."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>TransactionKey has a cardinality of 2,500,350,000 and takes 160 GB; it is a key that is not needed for analysis, so it should be removed. SaleDateTime has a cardinality of 6,850,273 and takes 120 GB; splitting it into separate date and time columns lowers the cardinality of each resulting column.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:13:row13"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-14-r14",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:14",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1. You plan to deploy a semantic model named Model1 by using the XMLA endpoint. You need to optimize the deployment of Model1. The solution must minimize how long it takes to deploy Model1. What should you do in Workspace1?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Select Small semantic model storage format."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Select Users can edit data models in the Power BI service."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Set Enable Cache for Shortcuts to On."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Select Large semantic model storage format."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>When using XMLA endpoint for deployment or processing, the semantic model storage format impacts deployment times. Small storage format allows faster metadata operations and shorter deployment times. Large semantic model storage format is only needed for extremely large models ( &gt; 10 GB dataset limit), and it increases deployment overhead. The other options (B: service editing, C: cache for shortcuts) are unrelated to XMLA endpoint deployment performance. Reference: Power BI semantic model storage format</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:14:row14"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-15-r15",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:15",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric warehouse named Warehouse1 that contains a table named TaxiTrips. TaxiTrips contains the following columns. You need to create a query that shows the top three taxi companies based on the total miles traveled. How should you complete the T-SQL statement? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): TaxiTrips columns</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Data type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Company</td><td>Varchar(100)</td><td>Name of each taxi company</td></tr><tr><td>passengerCount</td><td>Integer</td><td>Number of passengers per trip</td></tr><tr><td>tripDistance</td><td>Decimal(10,4)</td><td>Distance traveled in miles during a trip</td></tr><tr><td>pickupDate</td><td>Datetime2</td><td>Date and time a passenger was picked up</td></tr><tr><td>FareAmount</td><td>Decimal(10,4)</td><td>Cost of each trip</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><pre>SELECT [dropdown 1]\n    company AS TaxiCompany,\n    sum(passengerCount) AS TotalPassengers,\n    sum(tripDistance) TotalDistance\nFROM [Warehouse1].[dbo].[TaxiTrips]\n[dropdown 2]\n[dropdown 3] DESC;</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "d1",
        "options": [
          "FETCH NEXT 3 ROWS ONLY",
          "Limit (3)",
          "Top(3)"
        ],
        "answer": "Top(3)"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "d2",
        "options": [
          "GROUP BY company",
          "GROUP BY TaxiCompany",
          "ORDER BY tripDistance"
        ],
        "answer": "GROUP BY company"
      },
      {
        "id": "d3",
        "label": "d3",
        "options": [
          "GROUP BY company",
          "GROUP BY sum(tripDistance)",
          "ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)"
        ],
        "answer": "ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Note: the open-dropdown images show the third option as &quot;ORDER BY tripDistance&quot;, but the final marked answer shows &quot;ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)&quot;. Only &quot;ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)&quot; sorts by the total distance, so the third option is shown here as &quot;ORDER BY sum(tripDistance)&quot;.</p><p>TOP(3) limits the result to three rows. GROUP BY company aggregates the trips per taxi company, and ORDER BY sum(tripDistance) DESC ranks the companies by total distance so the top three are returned.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:15:row15"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-16-r16",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:16",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a Microsoft Power BI report named Report 1. Report1 is slow to render. You suspect that an inefficient DAX query is being executed. You need to identify the slowest DAX query, and then review how long the query spends in the formula engine as compared to the storage engine. Which five actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Step 1",
        "options": [
          "View the Server Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio."
        ],
        "answer": "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording."
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Step 2",
        "options": [
          "View the Server Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio."
        ],
        "answer": "View the Server Timings tab."
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Step 3",
        "options": [
          "View the Server Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio."
        ],
        "answer": "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query."
      },
      {
        "id": "t4",
        "label": "Step 4",
        "options": [
          "View the Server Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio."
        ],
        "answer": "View the Query Timings tab."
      },
      {
        "id": "t5",
        "label": "Step 5",
        "options": [
          "View the Server Timings tab.",
          "From Performance analyzer, capture a recording.",
          "Enable Query Timings and Server Timings. Run the query.",
          "View the Query Timings tab.",
          "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time.",
          "Copy the first query to DAX Studio."
        ],
        "answer": "Sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order by DAX query time."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>First capture a recording in Performance analyzer, then look at the Server Timings tab to see the storage engine queries. Enable Query Timings and Server Timings and run the query, view the Query Timings tab, and sort the Duration (ms) column in descending order so the slowest DAX query is at the top.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:16:row16"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-17-r17",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:17",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a query in Power Query Editor that contains two columns named Order_Date and Shipping_Date. You need to create a column that will calculate the number of days between Order_Date and Shipping_Date for each row. Which Power Query function should you use?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Duration.From"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Date.AddDays"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Duration.Days"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DateTime.LocalNow"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Requirement We have two columns in Power Query Editor: Order_Date Shipping_Date We need to calculate the number of days between the two dates. Step 1: How date differences work in Power Query (M language) Subtracting two date or datetime values in Power Query returns a duration value. Example: [Shipping_Date] - [Order_Date] This produces a duration such as 3.00:00:00 (3 days). Step 2: Converting duration into days To get the number of days as an integer, we use Duration.Days(). Example: Duration.Days([Shipping_Date] - [Order_Date]) This returns 3 if the shipping date is 3 days after the order date. Step 3: Evaluate other options A). Duration.From # Converts a value to a duration type but does not extract the number of days. B). Date.AddDays # Adds days to a given date, not used for calculating the difference. C). Duration.Days # Correct, extracts total days from a duration. D). DateTime.LocalNow # Returns the current system datetime, unrelated to this requirement.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:17:row17"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-18-r18",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:18",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are designing a Fabric semantic model named Model1 for a financial analytics workspace. Model1 includes a DAX measure named Measure1 that returns total sales. You have a table named Currency that contains a column named Format. The Format column defines currency formatting strings for each record as follows: $8,880.00, €8,880.00, or similar. You have a report that uses the Currency column to identify which symbol and format string to display. In the report Measure1 must appear with two decimal places and the appropriate currency symbol based on the selected value in the Currency column. You create the following DAX formula. FORMAT = SELECTEDVALUE( ’ Currency ’ [Format]) You need to ensure that Measure1 appears correctly in visuals. The solution must minimize logic duplication. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Copy the DAX expression from the Format column and profile the expression into the format string expression of Measure."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "For Model1, set the data category of Measure to Country/Region."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "For Model1, set the format type of Measure1 to Currency."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Create a dynamic format string for Measure1 that references the FORMAT measure."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Measure1 must show two decimal places with the currency symbol of the selected Currency row. A dynamic format string on Measure1 that references the FORMAT measure applies the selected format string without duplicating the logic. A static Currency format cannot change by selection, and the data category does not control the format.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:18:row18"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-19-r19",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:19",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Microsoft Fabric tenant that contains a dataflow. You are exploring a new semantic model. From Power Query, you need to view column information as shown in the following exhibit.</p><p>Which three Data view options should you select? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power Query column profile</div><p>Each column also shows a value-distribution histogram and a quality bar (Valid / Error / Empty), as produced by the column-profile options.</p><div><table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Valid</th><th>Error</th><th>Empty</th><th>Distinct</th><th>Unique</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SalesOrderID</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>32</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>OrderDate</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>19</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>CustomerID</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>32</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>LineItem</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>50</td><td>(cut off)</td></tr><tr><td>ProductID</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>142</td><td>(cut off)</td></tr><tr><td>OrderQty</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>19</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Enable column profile"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Show column quality details"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Show column profile in details pane"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Enable details pane"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Show column value distribution"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "C",
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>To view the column profile in Power Query you enable the column profile, show the column profile in the details pane, and enable the details pane. The exhibit shows the profile histograms that these three options produce.</p>Note: the printed answer lists A, B, D. &quot;Show column quality details&quot; is not the option that displays the profile. The column profile is shown by enabling the column profile, showing it in the details pane, and enabling the details pane.",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:19:row19"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-20-r20",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:20",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 and a user named User1. You need to ensure that User1 can perform the following tasks in Workspace1: * Create dashboards. * Publish semantic models. * Create and publish reports. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege. Which workspace role should you assign to User1?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Member"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Admin"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Viewer"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Contributor"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Creating dashboards, publishing semantic models, and creating and publishing reports all require the Contributor role. Viewer cannot create or publish, and Member and Admin grant more than the least privilege requires.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:20:row20"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-21-r21",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:21",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 and an Azure SQL database. You plan to create a dataflow that will read data from the database, and then transform the data by performing an inner join. You need to ignore spaces in the values when performing the inner join The solution must minimize development effort. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Append the queries by using fuzzy matching."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Merge the queries by using fuzzy matching."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Append the queries by using a lookup table."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Merge the queries by using a lookup table."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>You need to perform an inner join and ignore spaces in the values. In Power Query, to handle joins where values may contain spaces or minor mismatches, you use fuzzy matching in the Merge Queries operation. Append queries combines rows, not columns, so it does not apply here. Lookup table is not required since the transformation can be handled natively in Power Query with fuzzy matching. Correct solution: Merge the queries by using fuzzy matching. Reference: Fuzzy merge in Power Query</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:21:row21"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-22-r22",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:22",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric warehouse that contains a table named SalesOrderDetail. SalesOrderDetail contains three columns named OrderQty, ProductID and SalesOrderID. SalesOrderDetail contains one row per combination of SalesOrderID and ProductID. You need to calculate the proportion of the total quantity of each sales order represented by each product within the sales order. Which T-SQL statement should you run?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "SELECT SalesOrderID, ProductID, OrderQty, CAST(1. * OrderQty / SUM(OrderQty) OVER(ORDER BY ProductID) * 100 AS DECIMAL(5,2)) AS PercentbyProductID FROM Sales.SalesOrderDetail;"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "SELECT SalesOrderID, ProductID, OrderQty, CAST(1. * OrderQty / SUM(OrderQty) OVER(PARTITION BY ProductID) * 100 AS DECIMAL(5,2)) AS PercentbyProductID FROM Sales.SalesOrderDetail;"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "SELECT SalesOrderID, ProductID, OrderQty, CAST(1. * OrderQty / SUM(OrderQty) OVER(PARTITION BY SalesOrderID) * 100 AS DECIMAL(5,2)) AS PercentbyProductID FROM Sales.SalesOrderDetail;"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "SELECT SalesOrderID, ProductID, OrderQty, CAST(1. * OrderQty / SUM(OrderQty) OVER(ORDER BY SalesOrderID) * 100 AS DECIMAL(5,2)) AS PercentbyProductID FROM Sales.SalesOrderDetail;"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>We need to calculate the proportion of the total quantity of each sales order represented by each product within the sales order. Step 1: Analyze the requirement Table: SalesOrderDetail Columns: OrderQty, ProductID, SalesOrderID Each row = one product in a sales order. Requirement: For each SalesOrderID, calculate what percentage each product contributes to the total order quantity. This means we must: Calculate the total order quantity per SalesOrderID. Divide each product’s OrderQty by that total. Express it as a percentage. Step 2: Evaluate the options A). Uses OVER(ORDER BY ProductID) Wrong: ORDER BY does not segment data, it only defines sequence. We need grouping by sales order. B). Uses OVER(PARTITION BY ProductID) Wrong: This would calculate percentages per product across all sales orders, not per sales order. C). Uses OVER(PARTITION BY SalesOrderID) Correct: This computes the total OrderQty per SalesOrderID and divides each product’s quantity by that total. Exactly what the requirement asks. D). Uses OVER(ORDER BY SalesOrderID) Wrong: Again, ORDER BY just sequences rows, does not group them. Step 3: Correct Query SELECT SalesOrderID, ProductID, OrderQty, CAST(1. * OrderQty / SUM(OrderQty) OVER(PARTITION BY SalesOrderID) * 100 AS DECIMAL(5,2)) AS PercentByProductID FROM Sales.SalesOrderDetail; Why Option C is Correct PARTITION BY SalesOrderID ensures the denominator is the total quantity for the current order. This produces the correct percentage breakdown of each product inside its sales order.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:22:row22"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-23-r23",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:23",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a semantic model. The model contains 15 tables. You need to programmatically change each column that ends in the word Key to meet the following requirements: * Hide the column. * Set Nullable to False. * Set Summarize By to None * Set Available in MDX to False. * Mark the column as a key column. What should you use?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Microsoft Power BI Desktop"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Tabular Editor"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "ALM Toolkit"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "DAX Studio"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Tabular Editor is an advanced tool for editing Tabular models outside of Power BI Desktop that allows you to script out changes and apply them across multiple columns or tables. To accomplish the task programmatically, you would: * Open the model in Tabular Editor. * Create an Advanced Script using C# to iterate over all tables and their respective columns. * Within the script, check if the column name ends with ’Key’. * For columns that meet the condition, set the properties accordingly: IsHidden = true, IsNullable = false, SummarizeBy = None, IsAvailableInMDX = false. * Additionally, mark the column as a key column. * Save the changes and deploy them back to the Fabric tenant.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:23:row23"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-24-r24",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:24",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have an Azure SQL database named DB1 and a Fabric workspace named Workspace1. Workspace1 contains a lakehouse named LH1 and a Dataflow Gen2 named Dataflow1. Dataflow1 includes a query named Query1 that loads data from DB1. applies transformations to the data, and then filters the data. You discover that Query1 loads all the data before applying the transformations. You need to ensure that Query1 uses query folding. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Remove unused columns from Query1."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Enable incremental refresh for Query1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Move the filter steps before the transformation steps in Query1."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Replace DB1 with a Microsoft SQL Server database."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Query folding pushes the filter to the database so only the filtered rows are loaded. The filter steps must come before the transformation steps that break folding; as written, Query1 loads all the data first and then filters it. Removing unused columns and enabling incremental refresh do not restore folding, and replacing the database does not change the query.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:24:row24"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-25-r25",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:25",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a data warehouse named DW1. DW1 contains a table named DimCustomer. DimCustomer contains the fields shown in the following table.</p><p>You need to identify duplicate email addresses in DimCustomer. The solution must return a maximum of 1,000 records. Which four T-SQL statements should you run in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate statements from the list of statements to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): DimCustomer (sample rows)</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>CustomerKey</th><th>CustomerAltKey</th><th>Title</th><th>FirstName</th><th>LastName</th><th>AddressLine1</th><th>City</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>29546</td><td>christopher1@contoso.com</td><td>Mr.</td><td>Christopher</td><td>Beck</td><td>93-2501, Blackfriars Road</td><td>London</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>29587</td><td>john12@contoso.com</td><td>Mr.</td><td>John</td><td>Brooks</td><td>99-3 Forest Works</td><td>London</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>29612</td><td>richard2@contoso.com</td><td>Mr.</td><td>Richard</td><td>Byham</td><td>Knaves Beech Ind</td><td>High Wycombe</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Step 1",
        "options": [
          "LIMIT 1000",
          "SELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "WHERE COUNT(*) > 1",
          "SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "FROM DimCustomer",
          "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey",
          "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1"
        ],
        "answer": "SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)"
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Step 2",
        "options": [
          "LIMIT 1000",
          "SELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "WHERE COUNT(*) > 1",
          "SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "FROM DimCustomer",
          "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey",
          "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1"
        ],
        "answer": "FROM DimCustomer"
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Step 3",
        "options": [
          "LIMIT 1000",
          "SELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "WHERE COUNT(*) > 1",
          "SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "FROM DimCustomer",
          "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey",
          "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1"
        ],
        "answer": "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey"
      },
      {
        "id": "t4",
        "label": "Step 4",
        "options": [
          "LIMIT 1000",
          "SELECT TOP(1000) CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "WHERE COUNT(*) > 1",
          "SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*)",
          "FROM DimCustomer",
          "GROUP BY CustomerAltKey",
          "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1"
        ],
        "answer": "HAVING COUNT(*) > 1"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A query that finds duplicate CustomerAltKey values reads SELECT CustomerAltKey, COUNT(*) FROM DimCustomer, groups the rows with GROUP BY CustomerAltKey, and keeps only the groups with more than one row with HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:25:row25"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-26-r26",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:26",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Microsoft Power BI Premium Per User (PPU) workspace that contains a semantic model. You have an Azure App Service app named App1 that modifies row-level security (RLS) for the model by using the XMLA endpoint. App1 requires users to sign in by using their Microsoft Entra credentials to access the XMLA endpoint. You need to configure App1 to use a service account to access the model. What should you do first?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Add a managed identity to the workspace."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Add a managed identity to App1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Upgrade the workspace to Premium capacity."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Modify the XMLA Endpoint setting."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Power BI Premium Per User (PPU) already supports XMLA read/write . You don’t need to upgrade to Premium capacity. The workspace itself does not get a managed identity; rather, App1 (Azure App Service) can be configured with a managed identity . Once App1 has a managed identity, you grant it access to the Power BI model in the workspace via XMLA endpoint. Modifying the XMLA endpoint setting is not sufficient; authentication must come from the service (App1). Correct answer: B . Reference: Service principals and managed identities for Power BI XMLA</p><p>Valid DP-600 shared by .com for Helping Passing DP-600 Exam! .com now offer the newest DP-600 exam , the .com DP-600 exam questions have been updated and answers have been corrected get the newest .com DP-600 with Test Engine here: (212 Q&amp;As , 30%OFF Special Discount: )</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:26:row26"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-27-r27",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:27",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric workspace named Workspace1 that contains a lakehouse named Lakehouse1 and a user named User1. You need to ensure that User1 can read all the data in Lakehouse1. The solution must follow the principle of least privilege. Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p><p>B. Select Read all Apache Spark</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Share Lakehouse1 by using item permissions."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Select Read all Apache Spark"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Assign the Contributor role to User1."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Assign the Viewer role to User1."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Assign the Member role to User1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Sharing the lakehouse with item permissions and selecting &quot;Read all Apache Spark&quot; gives User1 read access to all the data in the lakehouse. The Viewer role alone does not grant data read access in a lakehouse, and Contributor and Member grant more than the least privilege requires.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:27:row27"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-28-r28",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:28",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Fabric tenant that contains a workspace named Workspace1 Workspace1 uses the Pro license mode and contains a semantic model named Model1. You have an Azure DevOps organization. You need to enable version control for Workspace1. The solution must ensure that Model 1 is added to the repository. Which three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Step 1",
        "options": [
          "Create a deployment pipeline.",
          "Configure branch policies in Azure DevOps.",
          "Assign Workspace1 to a Fabric capacity.",
          "Connect Workspace1 to a Git provider.",
          "Sync Workspace1 with the repository."
        ],
        "answer": "Assign Workspace1 to a Fabric capacity."
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Step 2",
        "options": [
          "Create a deployment pipeline.",
          "Configure branch policies in Azure DevOps.",
          "Assign Workspace1 to a Fabric capacity.",
          "Connect Workspace1 to a Git provider.",
          "Sync Workspace1 with the repository."
        ],
        "answer": "Connect Workspace1 to a Git provider."
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Step 3",
        "options": [
          "Create a deployment pipeline.",
          "Configure branch policies in Azure DevOps.",
          "Assign Workspace1 to a Fabric capacity.",
          "Connect Workspace1 to a Git provider.",
          "Sync Workspace1 with the repository."
        ],
        "answer": "Sync Workspace1 with the repository."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A workspace must be assigned to a Fabric capacity before it can be connected to Git, and it must be connected to the Git provider before it can be synchronised with the repository.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:extra:28:row28"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-extra-29-r29",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:extra:29",
    "exam": "DP-600",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You create a semantic model by using Microsoft Power BI Desktop. The model contains one security role named SalesRegionManager and the following tables: * Sales * SalesRegion * Sales Ad dress You need to modify the model to ensure that users assigned the SalesRegionManager role cannot see a column named Address in Sales Address. Which three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Step 1",
        "options": [
          "Open the model in Power BI Desktop.",
          "Set Object Level Security to Default for SalesRegionManager.",
          "Set the Hidden property to True.",
          "Open the model in Tabular Editor.",
          "Select the Address column in SalesAddress.",
          "Set Object Level Security to None for SalesRegionManager."
        ],
        "answer": "Open the model in Tabular Editor."
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Step 2",
        "options": [
          "Open the model in Power BI Desktop.",
          "Set Object Level Security to Default for SalesRegionManager.",
          "Set the Hidden property to True.",
          "Open the model in Tabular Editor.",
          "Select the Address column in SalesAddress.",
          "Set Object Level Security to None for SalesRegionManager."
        ],
        "answer": "Select the Address column in SalesAddress."
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Step 3",
        "options": [
          "Open the model in Power BI Desktop.",
          "Set Object Level Security to Default for SalesRegionManager.",
          "Set the Hidden property to True.",
          "Open the model in Tabular Editor.",
          "Select the Address column in SalesAddress.",
          "Set Object Level Security to None for SalesRegionManager."
        ],
        "answer": "Set Object Level Security to None for SalesRegionManager."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Object Level Security cannot be set in Power BI Desktop; it is set in Tabular Editor. Open the model in Tabular Editor, select the Address column in SalesAddress, and set Object Level Security to None for the SalesRegionManager role.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
