  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-35-r35",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:35",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You use Power Query Editor to preview the data shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>You confirm that the data will always start on row 3, and row 3 will always contain the column names. How should you shape the query? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>To configure the query to start on row 3, select [answer choice] Keep top rows / Remove errors / Remove top rows</li><li>To use the values in row 3 as the column names, select [answer choice] Rename / Replace values / Use first row as headers</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power Query preview (Excel sheet loaded without headers)</div><ul><li>All four columns (Column1–Column4, text) are 82% valid, 0% error, 18% empty.</li><li>Column1–3: 10 distinct, 9 unique · Column4: 8 distinct, 5 unique.</li></ul><div><table><thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>Column1</th><th>Column2</th><th>Column3</th><th>Column4</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>metric_order</td><td>metric</td><td>actual</td><td>goal</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>1</td><td>Project Percent Complete</td><td>55</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>2</td><td>On-Time Task Completion</td><td>97</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>3</td><td>Promised Requirements Met</td><td>92</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>5</td><td>Costs</td><td>1,570,250</td><td>1,580,000</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>4</td><td>Team Utilization Rate</td><td>110</td><td>95</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>6</td><td>Customer Satisfaction Index</td><td>78</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>7</td><td>Team Satisfaction Index</td><td>91</td><td>90</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>8</td><td>Post-Deployment Support Hours</td><td>100</td><td>85</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "To configure the query to start on row 3, select [answer choice]",
        "options": [
          "Keep top rows",
          "Remove errors",
          "Remove top rows"
        ],
        "answer": "Remove top rows"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "To use the values in row 3 as the column names, select [answer choice]",
        "options": [
          "Rename",
          "Replace values",
          "Use first row as headers"
        ],
        "answer": "Use first row as headers"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Remove top rows.</p>\n<p>Use First row as headers.</p>\n<p>Remove Top Rows: Use the &quot;Remove Top Rows&quot; function to eliminate the first two rows, bringing row 3 to the top.</p>\n<p>Use First Row as Headers: Apply the &quot;Use First Row as Headers&quot; transformation to promote the current top row (originally row 3) to header status.</p>\n<p>Conclusion:</p>\n<p>By following these steps, you ensure that Power Query correctly interprets the dataset&#x27;s structure, with appropriate headers and data rows, facilitating accurate data analysis in Power BI.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:35:row35"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
