    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-36-r36",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:36",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen. You have a data source that contains a column. The column contains case sensitive data. You have a Power BI semantic model in DirectQuery mode. You connect to the model and discover that it contains undefined values and errors. You need to resolve the issue.</p>\n<p>Solution: You implicitly convert the values into the required type. Does this meet the goal?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Correct answer is B:No.</p>\n<p>The issue stems from the case-sensitive data in the column. Undefined values and errors are likely caused by mismatched case sensitivity between the data source and Power BI semantic model when working in DirectQuery mode.</p>\n<p>Why the Suggested Solution Doesn&#x27;t Meet the Goal:</p>\n<p>Implicit Type Conversion:</p>\n<p>This solution focuses on type conversion but does not address the core issue: case sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Errors are not caused by a data type mismatch; they are due to case sensitivity mismatches in the source data, which is unrelated to type conversion.</p>\n<p>Implicit type conversion will not resolve errors resulting from case sensitivity.</p>\n<p>What Should Be Done:</p>\n<p>To resolve the issue, you need to ensure that the case sensitivity is handled properly. The correct approach would involve:</p>\n<p>Configuring the DirectQuery connection to ensure case-insensitive comparisons.</p>\n<p>Using Power Query transformations to standardize the case of the column data (e.g., converting all text to lowercase or uppercase).</p>\n<p>Verifying the settings in the source system to ensure consistency in case sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Summary:</p>\n<p>The proposed solution (implicitly converting values into the required type) does not address the core issue of case sensitivity in the data source.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is B. No.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:36:row36"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-37-r37",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:37",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution.</p>\n<p>After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen. You have a data source that contains a column. The column contains case sensitive data. You have a Power BI semantic model in DirectQuery mode. You connect to the model and discover that it contains undefined values and errors. You need to resolve the issue. Solution: You change the semantic model mode. Does this meet the goal?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>No is a right answer.</p>\n<p>Why Changing the Semantic Model Mode Doesn&#x27;t Resolve the Issue:</p>\n<p>Switching the semantic model from DirectQuery to Import or other modes won&#x27;t inherently resolve case- sensitivity problems because:</p>\n<p>The underlying data remains case-sensitive.</p>\n<p>Undefined values and errors caused by case mismatches will still persist unless case sensitivity is addressed explicitly.</p>\n<p>The problem is related to data handling rather than the model mode.</p>\n<p>What Should Be Done:</p>\n<p>To resolve the issue, you should:</p>\n<p>Normalize the case of the column values in Power Query (e.g., convert all text to lowercase or uppercase).</p>\n<p>Ensure that the case sensitivity settings in the data source align with those in Power BI.</p>\n<p>Configure DirectQuery settings to handle case sensitivity where applicable.</p>\n<p>Summary:</p>\n<p>Changing the semantic model mode does not solve the issue of case sensitivity in the data. The correct approach would involve addressing case sensitivity explicitly in the data source or during the transformation process.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is B. No.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:37:row37"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-38-r38",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:38",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series</p>\n<p>contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen. You have a data source that contains a column. The column contains case sensitive data. You have a Power BI semantic model in DirectQuery mode. You connect to the model and discover that it contains undefined values and errors. You need to resolve the issue. Solution: You normalize casing in the source query or Power Query Editor. Does this meet the goal?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Correct answer is A:Yes.</p>\n<p>The issue arises because the data source contains case-sensitive data, and the Power BI semantic model in DirectQuery mode encounters errors due to mismatches in case sensitivity. Normalizing the casing in the source query or Power Query Editor resolves this issue by ensuring consistency in how data is interpreted.</p>\n<p>Why Normalizing Casing Resolves the Issue:</p>\n<p>Case Normalization:</p>\n<p>Transforming all text values in the column to either lowercase or uppercase eliminates case sensitivity issues.</p>\n<p>Power BI can then correctly match and interpret the data, preventing undefined values and errors.</p>\n<p>DirectQuery Mode Compatibility:</p>\n<p>By normalizing the casing in the source query or Power Query Editor, the data sent to the semantic model is consistent, and DirectQuery can function correctly.</p>\n<p>How Normalizing Casing Works:</p>\n<p>In Power Query Editor:</p>\n<p>Use transformations like &quot;Transform &gt; Format &gt; Lowercase&quot; or &quot;Uppercase&quot; on the column containing case- sensitive data.</p>\n<p>In the source query (if applicable):</p>\n<p>Use SQL functions like LOWER() or UPPER() in the query to ensure consistent case handling at the data source level.</p>\n<p>Why This Meets the Goal:</p>\n<p>The solution directly addresses the root cause: case sensitivity.</p>\n<p>It ensures consistent data comparison, preventing undefined values and errors in the semantic model.</p>\n<p>​Summary:</p>\n<p>Normalizing casing in the source query or Power Query Editor effectively resolves case sensitivity issues in the data, ensuring that Power BI can interpret and process the data correctly. Therefore, the correct answer is A. Yes.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:38:row38"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-39-r39",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:39",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>Note: This question is part of a series of questions that present the same scenario. Each question in the series contains a unique solution that might meet the stated goals. Some question sets might have more than one correct solution, while others might not have a correct solution. After you answer a question in this section, you will NOT be able to return to it. As a result, these questions will not appear in the review screen. You have a data source that contains a column. The column contains case sensitive data. You have a Power BI semantic model in DirectQuery mode. You connect to the model and discover that it contains undefined values and errors. You need to resolve the issue. Solution: You add an index key and normalize casing in the data source. Does this meet the goal?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "No"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>B. No .</p>\n<p>Undefined values and errors in a DirectQuery model are typically caused by:</p>\n<p>Invalid or inconsistent data types</p>\n<p>Nulls or unexpected values</p>\n<p>Unsupported transformations or calculations pushed to the source</p>\n<p>Adding an index key and normalizing casing:</p>\n<p>May help with joins or consistency</p>\n<p>Does not resolve undefined values or query errors returned by the data source</p>\n<p>What actually addresses the issue:</p>\n<p>Fix data quality problems in the source (null handling, data type corrections)</p>\n<p>Adjust or remove unsupported transformations</p>\n<p>Validate relationships and DAX expressions that translate to source queries</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:39:row39"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
