  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-41-r41",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:41",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You use Power Query Editor to preview a column named Date as shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>You need to change the Date column to contain only the year. The solution must minimize administrative effort. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Date column in Power Query</div><ul><li>Column type: Date · 100% valid, 0% error, 0% empty · <b>16 distinct, 0 unique</b></li><li>First values: 01-01-2014, 01-06-2014, 01-06-2014, 01-06-2014, 01-12-2014, 01-03-2014 …</li></ul></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Split the column by delimiter."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Split the column by number of characters."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Extract the text after the delimiter."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Transform the column to contain only the year."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Transform the column to contain only the year.</p>\n<p>Minimal Administrative Effort:</p>\n<p>By adding a new column, you preserve the original Date column while introducing a new column that contains just the year. This way, no data is lost, and the transformation is straightforward.</p>\n<p>Using Power Query:</p>\n<p>In Power Query, you can Add Column &gt; Date &gt; Year, which is an easy and direct way to extract the year from the date. This is typically a minimal-effort approach because it doesn&#x27;t require complex formulas or additional steps.</p>\n<p>Preserving Original Data:</p>\n<p>Adding a new column ensures that you retain the original date data, which could be useful for other transformations or analysis.</p>\n<p>Conclusion:</p>\n<p>Option D is correct because it provides a simple way to add a column that contains the year, offering a non- invasive solution while keeping the original data intact. This aligns with the requirement for minimal administrative effort.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:41:row41"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-42-r42",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:42",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are designing the data model for a Power BI semantic model. You have the following tables in the star schema.</p>\n<p>Which table is the fact table of the star schema, and which column in the Patient table is the surrogate key of the star schema? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>Fact table: Date / Patient / Test / Test Result</li><li>Surrogate key: Date of birth / Last name / Patient key / Patient source ID</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Tables</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Date</td><td>Contains one row for each day from the last five years. Each row contains attributes for the year, quarter, month, week of the year, and day of the week. Date is the unique identifier of a row.</td></tr><tr><td>Patient</td><td>Contains one row per patient. Each row contains attributes for the patient key, patient source ID, first name, last name, date of birth, gender, address, city, state, and country. Patient key is the unique identifier of a row.</td></tr><tr><td>Test</td><td>Contains one row per test. Each row contains attributes for the test key, test source ID, type, and name. The test key is the unique identifier of a row.</td></tr><tr><td>Test Result</td><td>Contains one row per administered test. Each row contains attributes for the date the test was administered, test key, patient key, result value, and comments.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "Fact table:",
        "options": [
          "Date",
          "Patient",
          "Test",
          "Test Result"
        ],
        "answer": "Test Result"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "Surrogate key:",
        "options": [
          "Date of birth",
          "Last name",
          "Patient key",
          "Patient source ID"
        ],
        "answer": "Patient key"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Test Result.</p>\n<p>Patient key.</p>\n<p>The correct answers, based on the configuration, are:</p>\n<p>Fact Table: Test Result</p>\n<p>Contains the core transactional or measurable data (e.g., test scores, results, dates).</p>\n<p>Surrogate Key: PatientKey</p>\n<p>Links each test record in the fact table to the corresponding patient in the Patient dimension table.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:42:row42"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
