    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-43-r43",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:43",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You use Power BI Desktop to import two tables named Customer and Contacts. The Customer table contains the following columns: •Customer_Name •Customer ID •Website The Contacts table contains the following columns: •Contact ID •Contact Email</p>\n<p>•Contact Name •Customer Name A web-based contact form is used to fill the Contacts table. The data is not sanitized. You need to create a merge for the Customer and Contacts tables. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Disable fuzzy matching."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Enable fuzzy matching."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Set Join Kind to Left Outer."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>enabling fuzzy matching (Option B), Power BI can perform a join that allows for slight variations and discrepancies in the &quot;Customer Name&quot; field between the two tables, effectively handling imperfect data. Fuzzy matching helps ensure that even non-exact matches are found and merged correctly, improving the accuracy of your merged dataset.</p>\n<p>Disabling fuzzy matching would only allow exact matches, which may result in incomplete or inaccurate merges due to the unsanitized data. Setting the Join Kind to Left Outer doesn&#x27;t address the issue of data inconsistencies and would still require exact matches unless fuzzy matching is enabled.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:43:row43"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-44-r44",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:44",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are using Microsoft Power BI Desktop to profile data in Power Query Editor. Table data is displayed as shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>Use the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>Before you can transform the date column to show only the day, you must [answer choice]: change the data type. / use the Parse command on the column. / use the Replace Values command on the column.</li><li>To fix the error displayed for the data.Entries.menuAmount column, you must [answer choice]: change the data type. / rename the column. / use Conditional Formatting.</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power Query preview</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>_id</th><th>date</th><th>data.Entries.menuAmount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>2024-02-07</td><td>01/01/1900</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>2024-02-07</td><td>02/01/1900</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>2024-02-07</td><td>Error</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The <code>data.Entries.menuAmount</code> column shows a quality bar with errors.</p></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "Before you can transform the date column to show only the day, you must [answer choice]:",
        "options": [
          "change the data type.",
          "use the Parse command on the column.",
          "use the Replace Values command on the column."
        ],
        "answer": "change the data type."
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "To fix the error displayed for the data.Entries.menuAmount column, you must [answer choice]:",
        "options": [
          "change the data type.",
          "rename the column.",
          "use Conditional Formatting."
        ],
        "answer": "change the data type."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. Before you can transform the date column to show only the day, you must:</p>\n<p>Change the data type.</p>\n<p>To extract only the day from a date column, you typically need to ensure that the column is in a date or datetime data type. If the column is not already in a date format, you will need to change its data type to a date or datetime format before performing any date-specific transformations.</p>\n<p>2. To fix the error displayed for the data entries, menu Amount column, you must:</p>\n<p>Change the data type.</p>\n<p>If the menu Amount column contains errors, it might be due to an incorrect data type. For example, if the column is supposed to contain numeric values but is set as text, you would need to change the data type to numeric (e.g., integer or decimal) to resolve the errors.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:44:row44"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-45-r45",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:45",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Power BI semantic model named Model1 that contains a table named Sales. Sales contains 10 million records and the following data.</p>\n<p>The related report displays the weekly sales per region. You need to minimize the size of Model1. How should you modify the CustomerID column and the PurchaseDateTime column? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>CustomerID: Change the data type to Binary. / Remove any duplicate values. / Remove the column.</li><li>PurchaseDateTime: Change the data type to Duration. / Remove the column. / Split the column into separate date and time columns.</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Table columns</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Column name</th><th>Data type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PurchaseID</td><td>Text</td><td>Contains a unique ID for each order</td></tr><tr><td>CustomerID</td><td>Text</td><td>Contains a unique ID for each customer</td></tr><tr><td>PurchaseDateTime</td><td>Date/Time/Timezone</td><td>Contains the date and time that each order occurred</td></tr><tr><td>Region</td><td>Text</td><td>Contains the region where each order occurred</td></tr><tr><td>TotalAmount</td><td>Decimal number</td><td>Contains the total cost of each order</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "CustomerID:",
        "options": [
          "Change the data type to Binary.",
          "Remove any duplicate values.",
          "Remove the column."
        ],
        "answer": "Remove the column."
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "PurchaseDateTime:",
        "options": [
          "Change the data type to Duration.",
          "Remove the column.",
          "Split the column into separate date and time columns."
        ],
        "answer": "Split the column into separate date and time columns."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Customer ID : Remove the column.</p>\n<p>The CustomerID field is typically a unique identifier for customers in a dataset.</p>\n<p>Removing this column suggests that the CustomerID is not necessary for the analysis being performed.</p>\n<p>This might be applicable in cases where the dataset is being anonymized, or if the column does not contribute to the required insights.</p>\n<p>PurchaseDateTime :Split the column into separate date and time columns.</p>\n<p>The PurchaseDateTime field likely contains a timestamp, which includes both date and time information.</p>\n<p>Splitting it into separate date and time columns makes it easier to analyze trends based on dates (e.g., daily sales) or time (e.g., peak purchasing hours).</p>\n<p>This transformation is commonly performed in data preprocessing to facilitate time-based analysis.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:45:row45"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-46-r46",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:46",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Microsoft Power BI Desktop report named Report1 that uses an Azure SQL database as a data source. A user named User1 plans to create a report by using the same data source as Report1. You need to simplify the connection to the data source for User1. Which type of file should you create?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "PBIDS"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "XLSX"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "PBIT"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "PBIX"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. PBIDS.</p>\n<p>A .pbids (Power BI data source) file is specifically designed to simplify connections to a data source. If the user double-clicks the .pbids file, Power BI Desktop opens and automatically connects them to the specified data source—making it easy for someone else (User1) to reuse the same Azure SQL connection as in Report1.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:46:row46"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-47-r47",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:47",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Power BI semantic model. You discover that the semantic model contains values that display as errors. You need to use data profiling features in Power Query to preview the data and identify the issues. What should you select to gain insight into the number of errors in the model, and what should you select to resolve the errors? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>To gain insight, select: Column distribution / Column quality / Formula Bar / Monospaced</li><li>To resolve the errors, select: Keep Errors / Remove Duplicates / Remove Empty / Replace Errors</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "To gain insight, select:",
        "options": [
          "Column distribution",
          "Column quality",
          "Formula Bar",
          "Monospaced"
        ],
        "answer": "Column quality"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "To resolve the errors, select:",
        "options": [
          "Keep Errors",
          "Remove Duplicates",
          "Remove Empty",
          "Replace Errors"
        ],
        "answer": "Remove Empty"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. To gain insight, Select: Column quality.</p>\n<p>Column quality provides useful information about valid, error, and empty values in a column.</p>\n<p>This helps assess the data&#x27;s reliability and cleanliness before analysis.</p>\n<p>It is particularly useful when dealing with datasets that may have missing or incorrect values.</p>\n<p>2. To resolve the errors, Select: Remove Empty.</p>\n<p>Remove Empty helps eliminate blank or missing values from the dataset.</p>\n<p>Empty values can cause issues in data analysis, affecting calculations and visualizations.</p>\n<p>Removing empty values ensures cleaner and more accurate data.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:47:row47"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-48-r48",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:48",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You plan to create a Power BI semantic model named Model1 that will contain data from an Azure SQL database named DB1. Model1 must show updated data within two minutes of the data being updated in DB1. You need to select a connectivity mode for the connection to DB1. What should you choose?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "DirectQuery"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "live connection"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "import"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Direct Query.</p>\n<p>Direct Query Mode – Queries are sent to the data source in real-time without importing data.</p>\n<p>DirectQuery mode ensures that Power BI always retrieves the latest data directly from DB1 every time a user interacts with a report.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:48:row48"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-49-r49",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:49",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You need to create a semantic model in Power BI Desktop. The solution must meet the following requirements: •The model must contain a table named Orders that has one row per order. Each row will contain the total amount per order. •The orders must be filtered to the selected CustomerID value. •Users must select the CustomerID value from a list. •The list of customers must come from an OData source. Which three objects should you create in Power Query Editor? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "an Orders query that has a filter on CustomerID"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "a Customers query that has a filter on CustomerID"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "an Orders query that has a single column containing a list of customers"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "a Customers query that has a single column containing a list of customer IDs"
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "a parameter for CustomerID that uses a query to populate the suggested values"
      },
      {
        "id": "F",
        "text": "a parameter for CustomerID that uses manually entered values to populate the suggested values"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "D",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. An Orders query that has a filter on CustomerID: This query will fetch the orders and apply a filter based on the selected CustomerID to ensure the orders are filtered to the selected CustomerID value.</p>\n<p>D. A Customers query that has a single column containing a list of customer IDs: This query will fetch the list of customers from the OData source and provide a list of customer IDs that users can select from.</p>\n<p>E. A parameter for CustomerID that uses a query to populate the suggested values: This parameter will be used to dynamically filter the Orders query based on the selected CustomerID value. It will use the Customers query to populate the suggested values for the CustomerID parameter.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:49:row49"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-50-r50",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:50",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You use Power BI Desktop to create a report named RPT1 that loads data from a Microsoft Excel file named File1. File1 is located in a network share. File1 is moved to a different folder. When you open RPT1 in Power BI Desktop, you discover that the report displays outdated data. You need to ensure that the data in File1 refreshes properly. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Change the data source."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Clear the permissions of File1."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Export a PBIDS file."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Modify the permissions for File1."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Change the data source.</p>\n<p>Since File1 has been moved to a different folder, the path to the file has changed. Power BI Desktop relies on static file paths to locate data sources like Excel, CSV, or text files. When the original file is no longer in the expected location, Power BI cannot find it during refresh, and it may use cached/outdated data.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:50:row50"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-51-r51",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:51",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a query that contains customer data in Power Query Editor as shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>Use the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>Some values in the [answer choice] column are repeated. Customer ID / Customer Key / Customer Name</li><li>To standardize the values in the Phone column to 10 digits and exclude country calling codes, [answer choice]. extract the text after the &quot;)&quot; delimiter / extract the last 12 characters / split the column at the right-most occurrence of &quot;)&quot;</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power Query: Customers table (column quality, distribution and profile enabled)</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Valid</th><th>Error</th><th>Empty</th><th>Distinct</th><th>Unique</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Customer Key</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>18484</td><td>18484</td></tr><tr><td>Customer ID</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>18484</td><td>18484</td></tr><tr><td>Customer Name</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>18400</td><td>18317</td></tr><tr><td>Birth Date</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>6139</td><td>1666</td></tr><tr><td><b>Phone</b> (selected)</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>8890</td><td>8175</td></tr><tr><td>Address</td><td>100%</td><td>0%</td><td>0%</td><td>12802</td><td>7606</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Sample rows:</b></p><div><table><thead><tr><th>Customer Key</th><th>Customer ID</th><th>Customer Name</th><th>Birth Date</th><th>Phone</th><th>Address</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>11010</td><td>AW00011010</td><td>Jacquelyn Suarez</td><td>8/5/1969</td><td>1 (11) 500 555-0169</td><td>7800 Corrinne Court</td></tr><tr><td>11011</td><td>AW00011011</td><td>Curtis Lu</td><td>5/3/1969</td><td>1 (11) 500 555-0117</td><td>1224 Shoenic</td></tr><tr><td>11012</td><td>AW00011012</td><td>Lauren Walker</td><td>1/14/1979</td><td>717-555-0164</td><td>4785 Scott Street</td></tr><tr><td>11013</td><td>AW00011013</td><td>Ian Jenkins</td><td>8/3/1979</td><td>817-555-0185</td><td>7902 Hudson Ave.</td></tr><tr><td>11014</td><td>AW00011014</td><td>Sydney Bennett</td><td>11/6/1973</td><td>431-555-0156</td><td>9011 Tank Drive</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Column statistics for Phone:</b> Count 18484 · Error 0 · Empty 0 · Distinct 8890 · Unique 8175 · Empty string 0 · Min “1 (11) 5…” · Max “999-555…”. <b>Text length distribution:</b> two bars, for lengths 12 and 19.</p></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "Some values in the [answer choice] column are repeated.",
        "options": [
          "Customer ID",
          "Customer Key",
          "Customer Name"
        ],
        "answer": "Customer Name"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "To standardize the values in the Phone column to 10 digits and exclude country calling codes, [answer choice].",
        "options": [
          "extract the text after the \")\" delimiter",
          "extract the last 12 characters",
          "split the column at the right-most occurrence of \")\""
        ],
        "answer": "extract the last 12 characters"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Customer Name.</p>\n<p>Extract the last 12 characters.</p>\n<p>Customer Name because it has a greater number of distinct vs unique values in the column. This implies that there are repeating values in it. -Second statement requires 10 digits in the phone number. If choose &quot;extract the text after the &quot;)&quot; delimiter&quot;, it will leave a leading whitespace for certain rows since there is a space after the &quot;)&quot; in certain rows and this counts as a digit which would make it 11 digits which does NOT meet the requirement.BUT, if we use the other option &quot;Extract the last 12 characters&quot;, then it will NOT include the leading whitespace and correctly satisfy the 10 digit requirement as well as not including whitespace which can throw off calculations, sort orders, etc.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:51:row51"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-52-r52",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:52",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Power BI report named Report that contains the following tables.</p>\n<p>For the last three days, refresh in Power BI has failed, and you receive the following error message: “You have reached the maximum allowable memory allocation for your tier. Consider upgrading to a tier with more available memory.” You need to resolve the error and ensure that query response times are minimized for end users. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Tables</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Product</td><td>10 rows with a primary key named ProductId</td></tr><tr><td>Sales person</td><td>Five rows with a primary key named SalesPersonId</td></tr><tr><td>Calendar</td><td>Standard calendar table without time columns that is generated from a calculated table</td></tr><tr><td>Customer</td><td>25 rows with a primary key named CustomerId</td></tr><tr><td>Invoice</td><td>12 million rows from the last three months with many-to-one relationships to Product, Sales Person, Calendar, and Customer</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Merge the Sales person and invoice tables based on the SalesPersonId column."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Change the invoice table to a DirectQuery table and read the data live from the source, retaining only the dimension tables as import tables."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Reduce the size of the invoice table by summarizing sales by Product, Sales person, Calendar, and Customer."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Change the invoice table to a composite table that contains historical data as a DirectQuery table and hot data as an import table, and then partition the table."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>D. Change the invoice table to a composite table that contains historical data as a DirectQuery table and hot data as an import table, and then partition the table.</p>\n<p>Combines Import (fast performance) with DirectQuery (real-time, but slower).</p>\n<p>Hot data (recent, frequently queried) is kept in Import mode for performance.</p>\n<p>Historical data (older, less frequently queried) is kept in DirectQuery to reduce memory usage.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:52:row52"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-53-r53",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:53",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a table named DIMCountries that contains a column named Country as shown in the following table.</p>\n<p>You plan to use DIMCountries as a dimension in a report. You need to ensure that the Country column in DIMCountries lists each country only once. Which two actions should you perform on the Country column? Each correct answer presents part of the solution. NOTE: Each correct answer is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Country column</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Country</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Afghanistan</td></tr><tr><td>afghanistan</td></tr><tr><td>Albania</td></tr><tr><td>Algeria</td></tr><tr><td>American Samoa</td></tr><tr><td>(blank)</td></tr><tr><td>Andorra</td></tr><tr><td>andorra</td></tr><tr><td>Antarctica</td></tr><tr><td>Argentina</td></tr><tr><td>(blank)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Change Data category to Country."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Remove duplicate values."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Apply a clean transform."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Replace the empty fields."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Apply a Capitalize Each Word transform."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "B",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>B. Remove duplicate values.</p>\n<p>E. Apply a Capitalize Each Word transform.</p>\n<p>Remove duplicate values is essential to ensure that each country appears only once in the dimension.</p>\n<p>Capitalize Each Word standardizes the text (for example, usa, USA, Usa → Usa), which helps prevent</p>\n<p>duplicates caused by inconsistent casing before or during deduplication.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:53:row53"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-54-r54",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:54",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You plan to use Power Query Editor to get data from an API. The API returns a JSON response. You have a username and password for the API. Which data source and authentication method should you use to connect to the API? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>Data source: Dataflows / JSON / OData / Web</li><li>Authentication: Anonymous / Basic / Organizational account / Web API</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "Data source:",
        "options": [
          "Dataflows",
          "JSON",
          "OData",
          "Web"
        ],
        "answer": "Web"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "Authentication:",
        "options": [
          "Anonymous",
          "Basic",
          "Organizational account",
          "Web API"
        ],
        "answer": "Basic"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Data source: Web</p>\n<p>Power Query uses the Web connector to fetch data from APIs (which typically return JSON or XML).</p>\n<p>Authentication: Basic.</p>\n<p>Since you have a username and password, the most common authentication for APIs that require these is Basic Authentication (username and password sent encoded in the HTTP header).If the API uses some other auth method (like OAuth), that would differ, but given username/password, Basic Auth is the standard.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:54:row54"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-55-r55",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:55",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You use Power Query Editor to create a query named Products. Products return the results shown in the following table.</p>\n<p>You need to shape the data to display a single combination of ProductCategory and ProductID on each row. How should you configure the ProductIDList column? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>Split column: By delimiter / By positions / By number of characters</li><li>Advanced options: Set Split into to Columns. / Set Split into to Rows. / Set Quote character to None. / Set Number of columns to split into to 5.</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Product categories</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>ProductCategory</th><th>ProductIDList</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bikes</td><td>1,2,3,5,7</td></tr><tr><td>Components</td><td>4,6,8,10</td></tr><tr><td>Clothing</td><td>9,11,12,13,14</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "Split column:",
        "options": [
          "By delimiter",
          "By positions",
          "By number of characters"
        ],
        "answer": "By delimiter"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "Advanced options:",
        "options": [
          "Set Split into to Columns.",
          "Set Split into to Rows.",
          "Set Quote character to None.",
          "Set Number of columns to split into to 5."
        ],
        "answer": "Set Split into to Rows."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Split column: By delimiter.</p>\n<p>This option tells Power Query how to split the data.</p>\n<p>A delimiter is a character that separates values, such as:</p>\n<p>Comma ,</p>\n<p>Space &quot; &quot;</p>\n<p>Dash -</p>\n<p>Pipe |</p>\n<p>Advanced option: Set Split into to Rows.</p>\n<p>This controls how the split results are arranged.</p>\n<p>Two possibilities:</p>\n<p>Split into Columns (default)</p>\n<p>Split into Rows (selected in your image)</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:55:row55"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-56-r56",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:56",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You plan to create a Power BI semantic model named Model1 that will contain data from an Azure SQL database. Model1 will have the following tables.</p>\n<p>You create DimDate and DimProduct by using import storage mode. You need to configure the storage mode for each remaining table. The solution must meet the following requirements: •The data in FactSales must be available within five minutes of being available in the source data. •The query response time for reports connected to the model must be minimized. Which storage mode should you configure for each table? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>DimCustomer: DirectQuery / Dual / Import</li><li>FactSales: DirectQuery / Dual / Import</li><li>FactMarketingEmail: DirectQuery / Dual / Import</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "DimCustomer:",
        "options": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "Dual"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "FactSales:",
        "options": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "DirectQuery"
      },
      {
        "id": "d3",
        "label": "FactMarketingEmail:",
        "options": [
          "DirectQuery",
          "Dual",
          "Import"
        ],
        "answer": "Import"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1. DimCustomer: Dual</p>\n<p>While the image shows Import, the &quot;ideal&quot; answer for a dimension table in a composite model that joins to both Import and DirectQuery fact tables is often Dual.</p>\n<p>Dual mode allows a table to behave as Import when joined with an Import table (avoiding slow cross-source joins) and as DirectQuery when joined with a DirectQuery table (avoiding the performance penalty of a &quot;limited relationship&quot;).</p>\n<p>Why the image might select Import: If FactSales is the only DirectQuery table and there is no strict requirement for real-time customer data, keeping dimensions in Import is common for simplicity and fast slicer performance.</p>\n<p>2. FactSales: DirectQuery</p>\n<p>The Reason: Large transaction tables that require real-time reporting or are too massive to fit into memory are assigned DirectQuery. This leaves the data in the source (like SQL Server or Snowflake) and only queries what is needed when a visual is rendered.</p>\n<p>The Trade-off: Slower visual rendering compared to Import, but no need for a data refresh to see the latest sales.</p>\n<p>3. FactMarketingEmail: Import</p>\n<p>The Reason: This suggests this specific fact table is either smaller in size or does not require up-to-the-minute updates. By using Import, Power BI loads the data into the VertiPaq engine (in-memory).</p>\n<p>The Benefit: Blazing-fast performance for calculations and aggregations related to marketing metrics.</p>\n<p>Note: the marked answer image for this question shows Import for DimCustomer; the explanation gives Dual, which is used as the key here.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:56:row56"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-57-r57",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:57",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You use Power BI Desktop to import two tables named Customer and Contacts. The Customer table contains the following columns: •Customer_Name •Customer ID •Website The Contacts table contains the following columns: •Contact ID •Contact Email •Contact Name •Customer Name A web-based contact form is used to fill the Contacts table. The data is not sanitized. You need to create a merge for the Customer and Contacts tables. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Set Join Kind to Full Outer."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Disable fuzzy matching."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Set Join Kind to Left Outer."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Enable fuzzy matching."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Technical Justification for Choosing Option D</p>\n<p>Requirement Context: Merge Customer and Contacts tables in Power BI Desktop, considering the Contacts</p>\n<p>table&#x27;s data is not sanitized (potentially leading to variations in matching fields).</p>\n<p>Key Consideration: The match between the two tables is based on names (Customer_Name in Customer table and Customer Name in Contacts table), which are prone to variations (typos, different formats, etc.) due to unsanitized data from a web form.</p>\n<p>Option Analysis:</p>\n<p>A. Set Join Kind to Full Outer:</p>\n<p>Inappropriateness Reason: The join type (Full Outer) does not address the primary concern of handling potential variations in matching names. A Full Outer Join would return all records from both tables, regardless of matches, which doesn&#x27;t solve the data integration issue at hand. Relevance to Sanity of Data: Irrelevant to the issue of unsanitized data causing matching challenges.</p>\n<p>B. Disable Fuzzy Matching:</p>\n<p>Inappropriateness Reason: Disabling fuzzy matching would exacerbate the problem. With potentially varied spellings or formats in the Customer Name field, disabling this feature would reduce the likelihood of successful matches. Impact on Data Integrity: Negatively impacts by potentially leading to under-reporting of contacts due to failed matches.</p>\n<p>C. Set Join Kind to Left Outer:</p>\n<p>Inappropriateness Reason: Similar to A, the join type (Left Outer) doesn’t tackle the core issue of name variations. A Left Outer Join would return all records from the Customer table and matching records from the Contacts table, but doesn’t ensure more accurate name matching. Relevance to Data Sanitization: Does not address the lack of data sanitization.</p>\n<p>D. Enable Fuzzy Matching:</p>\n<p>Correctness Reason: Enabling fuzzy matching is the most appropriate action. It allows Power BI to identify and match records based on similarities rather than exact string matches, effectively countering the issues posed by unsanitized data with potential variations in Customer Name / Customer_Name fields. Benefit: Enhances the accuracy of the merge by accounting for potential typos or formatting differences in names, directly addressing the data sanitization issue.</p>\n<p>Conclusion: Given the unsanitized nature of the Contacts table&#x27;s data and the reliance on name matching for the merge, enabling fuzzy matching (Option D) is the best approach to ensure a more accurate and comprehensive integration of the Customer and Contacts tables.</p>\n<p>1. Microsoft Power BI Documentation - Fuzzy Matching: query/what-is-power-query#fuzzy-matching 2. Power BI Desktop - Merge Queries (with Join Types Explanation): us/power-bi/guided-learning/power-bi-learn-data-prepare-transform#merge-queries</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:57:row57"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-58-r58",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:58",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Power BI semantic model that imports data from a Microsoft SQL Server database and an Oracle database. The semantic model is deployed to the Power BI service. Parameters are already used for the SQL Server data source. You are planning to make change to the semantic model that will ensure consistent behavior across all the data sources.</p>\n<p>You need to enable parameterization for the Oracle data source. The solution must minimize changes to the existing model. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From Power BI Desktop, modify the Oracle data source to use a parameter, and then republish the model to the Power BI service."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "From the Power BI service, modify the parameters of the SQL Server data source."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "From the Power BI service, create a new semantic model that uses the existing model as a data source, and then parameterize the new model."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "From the Power BI service, set a parameter value for the model."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Technical Justification for Correct Answer: A</p>\n<p>Why A is the Best Option:</p>\n<p>Direct Modification and Minimal Changes: Option A involves directly modifying the Oracle data source in Power BI Desktop to use a parameter, which is the most straightforward approach to enable parameterization for that specific data source. This method ensures minimal changes to the existing model, as only the Oracle data source settings are altered. Republishing Maintains Consistency: Republishing the updated model to the Power BI service after making the changes ensures that the semantic model in the service reflects the new parameterization for the Oracle data source, maintaining consistency across all data sources (SQL Server and Oracle). Alignment with Best Practices: Modifying the data source directly in Power BI Desktop before republishing aligns with best practices for managing and updating Power BI models, as it allows for local testing before deployment to the service.</p>\n<p>Why Other Options are Less Suitable:</p>\n<p>B. Modifying SQL Server Parameters:</p>\n<p>Off-Target: This option does not address the requirement to enable parameterization for the Oracle data source. Potential for Unnecessary Changes: Altering existing parameters for SQL Server could introduce unintended behavior or require additional testing for no gain towards the Oracle objective. C. Creating a New Semantic Model:</p>\n<p>Excessive Complexity: Introduces unnecessary complexity by adding a layer of indirection (using the existing model as a data source for a new model). Maintenance Overhead: Doubles the maintenance effort, as changes would need to be reflected in both the original and the new model. D. Setting a Parameter Value in the Power BI Service:</p>\n<p>Lacks Enablement of Parameterization: This option only sets a value for an existing parameter but does not enable parameterization for the Oracle data source if it&#x27;s not already set up. Misinterpretation of the Requirement: Fails to address the core need to configure the Oracle data source for parameterization.</p>\n<p>Correct Answer: A. From Power BI Desktop, modify the Oracle data source to use a parameter, and then republish the model to the Power BI service.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:58:row58"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-59-r59",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:59",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You are creating a report in Power BI Desktop. You load a data extract that includes a free text field named coll. You need to analyze the frequency distribution of the string lengths in col1. The solution must not affect the size of the model. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "In the report, add a DAX calculated column that calculates the length of col1"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "In the report, add a DAX function that calculates the average length of col1"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "From Power Query Editor, add a column that calculates the length of col1"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "From Power Query Editor, change the distribution for the Column profile to group by length for col1"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A will affect the size of the model as would C.</p>\n<p>B doesn&#x27;t give you enough information about the distribution (just the average)</p>\n<p>D is the right answer.</p>\n<p>1. Power Query Editor -&gt; View -&gt; Enable Column Profile</p>\n<p>2. Select three dots (top left corner) in the profile pane appear at the bottom of the Query Editor window.</p>\n<p>3. Group By -&gt; Text length</p>\n<p>Using Column Profiling in Power Query Editor allows you to analyze the frequency distribution of string lengths in col1 without adding new columns or increasing the size of the model. This meets the requirements efficiently, as the analysis is performed in-memory and not persisted in the model.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:59:row59"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-60-r60",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:60",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a collection of reports for the HR department of your company. The datasets use row-level security (RLS). The company has multiple sales regions. Each sales region has an HR manager. You need to ensure that the HR managers can interact with the data from their region only. The HR managers must be prevented from changing the layout of the reports. How should you provision access to the reports for the HR managers?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Publish the reports in an app and grant the HR managers access permission."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Create a new workspace, copy the datasets and reports, and add the HR managers as members of the workspace."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Publish the reports to a different workspace other than the one hosting the datasets."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Add the HR managers as members of the existing workspace that hosts the reports and the datasets."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "A"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Publish the reports in an app and grant the HR managers access permission.</p>\n<p>Power BI apps are the recommended way to share content with end users.</p>\n<p>HR managers get read-only access, so they cannot change the report layout.</p>\n<p>Row-level security (RLS) is enforced automatically, ensuring each HR manager sees only data for their own sales region.</p>\n<p>Access can be managed easily using Azure AD groups per region.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:60:row60"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-61-r61",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:61",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You need to provide a user with the ability to add members to a workspace. The solution must use the principle of least privilege. Which role should you assign to the user?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Viewer"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Admin"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Contributor"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Member"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Member role allows adding members or other with lower permissions to the workspace.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:61:row61"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-62-r62",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:62",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Power BI query named Sales that imports the columns shown in the following table.</p>\n<p>Users only use the date part of the Sales_Date field. Only rows with a Status of Finished are used in analysis. You need to reduce the load times of the query without affecting the analysis. Which two actions achieve this goal? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Sales data columns</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Description</th><th>Sample value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ID</td><td>A unique value that represents a sale</td><td>10253</td></tr><tr><td>Sale_Date</td><td>Sales date. A column to extract the date of the sale</td><td>2021-11-23T09:53:00</td></tr><tr><td>Customer_ID</td><td>Represents a unique customer ID number</td><td>13158</td></tr><tr><td>Delivery_Time</td><td>Elapsed delivery time in hours. Can contain null values</td><td>51.52</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Sales status. Contains only the following two values: Finished and Canceled</td><td>Finished</td></tr><tr><td>Canceled_Date</td><td>Cancellation date and time. Can contain null values</td><td>2021-11-24T14:11:23</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Remove the rows in which Sales[Status] has a value of Canceled."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Remove Sales[Sales_Date]."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Change the data type of Sale[Delivery_Time] to Integer."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Split Sales[Sale_Date] into separate date and time columns."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "Remove Sales[Canceled Date]."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "E"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A. Remove the rows in which Sales[Status] has a value of Canceled</p>\n<p>E. Remove Sales[Canceled Date]</p>\n<p>A reduces the number of rows loaded by filtering out data that is never used in analysis (only Finished rows are analyzed). Fewer rows = faster load times.</p>\n<p>E removes an unused column, which reduces model size and improves query load performance.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:62:row62"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-63-r63",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:63",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You build a report to analyze customer transactions from a database that contains the tables shown in the following table.</p>\n<p>You import the tables. Which relationship should you use to link the tables?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Tables</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Table name</th><th>Column name</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Customer</td><td>CustomerID (primary key)&lt;br&gt;Name&lt;br&gt;State&lt;br&gt;Email</td></tr><tr><td>Transaction</td><td>TransactionID (primary key)&lt;br&gt;CustomerID (foreign key)&lt;br&gt;Date&lt;br&gt;Amount</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "one-to-many from Transaction to Customer"
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "one-to-one between Customer and Transaction"
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "many-to-many between Customer and Transaction"
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "one-to-many from Customer to Transaction"
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "D"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>D. one-to-many from Customer to Transaction.</p>\n<p>A Customer can have multiple Transactions</p>\n<p>Each Transaction belongs to one Customer</p>\n<p>This is the classic one-to-many relationship:</p>\n<p>One Customer → Many Transactions</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:63:row63"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-64-r64",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:64",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a custom connector that returns ID, From, To, Subject, Body, and Has Attachments for every email sent during the past year. More than 10 million records are returned. You build a report analyzing the internal networks of employees based on whom they send emails to. You need to prevent report recipients from reading the analyzed emails. The solution must minimize the model size. What should you do?</p>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "From Model view, set the Subject and Body columns to Hidden."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Remove the Subject and Body columns during the import."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "Implement row-level security (RLS) so that the report recipients can only see results based on the emails they sent."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "single",
    "answers": [
      "B"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>&quot;prevent report recipients from reading the analyzed emails&quot;</p>\n<p>The Subject and the Body are not needed in the report. Dropping them resolves the security problem and minimizes the model.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:64:row64"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-65-r65",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:65",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You create a Power BI dataset that contains the table shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>You need to make the table available as an organizational data type in Microsoft Excel. How should you configure the properties of the table? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point. Hot Area:</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>Row label: Cost Center / Headcount / ID / Name</li><li>Key column: Cost Center / Headcount / ID / Name</li><li>Is featured table: No / Yes</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Business Unit table (model view)</div><p>Fields: <b>Cost Center, Headcount, ID, Name</b>.</p></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "Row label:",
        "options": [
          "Cost Center",
          "Headcount",
          "ID",
          "Name"
        ],
        "answer": "Name"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "Key column:",
        "options": [
          "Cost Center",
          "Headcount",
          "ID",
          "Name"
        ],
        "answer": "ID"
      },
      {
        "id": "d3",
        "label": "Is featured table:",
        "options": [
          "No",
          "Yes"
        ],
        "answer": "Yes"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Box 1:Row label: Name</p>\n<p>excel#:~:text=Power%20BI%20Organizational%20Data%20Types%20in%20Excel%20allow%20you</p>\n<p>%20to,company%2C%20to%20name%20a%20few.</p>\n<p>Box 2: ID -</p>\n<p>The Key column field value provides the unique ID for the row. This value enables Excel to link a cell to a specific row in the table.</p>\n<p>Box 3: Yes -</p>\n<p>In the Data Types Gallery in Excel, your users can find data from featured tables in your Power BI datasets.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:65:row65"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-66-r66",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:66",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have the Power BI model shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>A manager can represent only a single country. You need to use row-level security (RLS) to meet the following requirements:</p>\n<ul><li>The managers must only see the data of their respective country.</li><li>The number of RLS roles must be minimized.</li></ul>\n<p>Which two actions should you perform? Each correct answer presents a complete solution. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point.</p>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power BI model</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Country</td><td>Country_Id, Country_Name, Manager, Manager_Email</td></tr><tr><td>Calendar</td><td>Date, Month Name, Year</td></tr><tr><td>Purchase</td><td>Country_Id, Purchase Date, Purchase ID, Total Value (Σ)</td></tr><tr><td>Purchase Detail</td><td>Item Name, Purchase ID, Quantity, Unit Price</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Relationships</b> (1 → *): Country → Purchase (single filter direction, from Country) · Calendar → Purchase (single filter direction, from Calendar) · Purchase → Purchase Detail (<b>both</b> filter directions).</p></div>",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Create a single role that filters Country[Manager_Email] by using the USERNAME DAX function."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Create a single role that filters Country[Manager_Email] by using the USEROBJECTID DAX function."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "For the relationship between Purchase Detail and Purchase, select Apply security filter in both directions."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "Create one role for each country."
      },
      {
        "id": "E",
        "text": "For the relationship between Purchase and Purchase Detail, change the Cross filter direction to Single."
      }
    ],
    "controls": [],
    "mode": "multi",
    "answers": [
      "A",
      "C"
    ],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>A: You can take advantage of the DAX functions username() or userprincipalname() within your dataset. You can use them within expressions in Power BI</p>\n<p>Desktop. When you publish your model, it will be used within the Power BI service.</p>\n<p>Note: To define security roles, follow these steps.</p>\n<p>Import data into your Power BI Desktop report, or configure a DirectQuery connection.</p>\n<p>1. From the Modeling tab, select Manage Roles.</p>\n<p>2. From the Manage roles window, select Create.</p>\n<p>3. Under Roles, provide a name for the role.</p>\n<p>4. Under Tables, select the table to which you want to apply a DAX rule.</p>\n<p>5. In the Table filter DAX expression box, enter the DAX expressions. This expression returns a value of true or</p>\n<p>false. For example: [Entity ID] = Value.</p>\n<p>6. After you&#x27;ve created the DAX expression, select the checkmark above the expression box to validate the expression.</p>\n<p>Note: You can use username() within this expression.</p>\n<p>7. Select Save.</p>\n<p>C: By default, row-level security filtering uses single-directional filters, whether the relationships are set to single direction or bi-directional. You can manually enable bi-directional cross-filtering with row-level security by selecting the relationship and checking the Apply security filter in both directions checkbox. Select this option when you&#x27;ve also implemented dynamic row-level security at the server level, where row-level security is based on username or login ID.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:66:row66"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-67-r67",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:67",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Power BI imported dataset that contains the data model shown in the following exhibit.</p>\n<p>Use the drop-down menus to select the answer choice that completes each statement based on the information presented in the graphic. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point. Hot Area:</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<ul><li>Changing the [answer choice] setting of the relationships will improve report query performance. Cardinality / Cross filter direction / Assume Referential Integrity</li><li>The data model is organized into a [answer choice]. star schema / snowflake schema / denormalized table</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Power BI model</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Table</th><th>Fields shown</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employee</td><td>Age, AgeGroupID, BadHires, BU, date, EmpID, EthnicGroup, FP, Gender, …</td></tr><tr><td>SeparationReason</td><td>SeparationTypeID</td></tr><tr><td>Ethnicity</td><td>Ethnic Group</td></tr><tr><td>Date</td><td>Date, MonthEndDate, MonthStartDate</td></tr><tr><td>FP</td><td>FP</td></tr><tr><td>PayType</td><td>PayTypeID</td></tr><tr><td>Gender</td><td>ID</td></tr><tr><td>BU</td><td>BU</td></tr><tr><td>AgeGroup</td><td>AgeGroupID</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Relationships:</b> each of SeparationReason, Ethnicity, Date, FP, PayType, Gender, BU and AgeGroup has a one-to-many relationship (1 → *) to Employee, and every relationship shows the <b>double-arrow</b> (cross-filter direction: Both).</p></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "d1",
        "label": "Changing the [answer choice] setting of the relationships will improve report query performance.",
        "options": [
          "Cardinality",
          "Cross filter direction",
          "Assume Referential Integrity"
        ],
        "answer": "Cross filter direction"
      },
      {
        "id": "d2",
        "label": "The data model is organized into a [answer choice].",
        "options": [
          "star schema",
          "snowflake schema",
          "denormalized table"
        ],
        "answer": "star schema"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>Box 1: cross filter direction -</p>\n<p>As the answer correctly states &quot;Assume Referential Integrity&quot; only works for direct query connections.</p>\n<p>Box 2: Star schema -</p>\n<p>Star schema is a mature modeling approach widely adopted by relational data warehouses. It requires modelers to classify their model tables as either dimension or fact.</p>\n<p>Generally, dimension tables contain a relatively small number of rows. Fact tables, on the other hand, can contain a very large number of rows and continue to grow over time.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<p>Reference:</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:67:row67"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-68-r68",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:68",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You have a Power BI model that contains a table named Sales and a related date table. Sales contains a measure named Total Sales. You need to create a measure that calculates the total sales from the equivalent month of the previous year. How should you complete the calculation? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area. NOTE: Each correct selection is worth one point. Hot Area:</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<p>Values: CALCULATE / EVALUATE / SUM / SUMX / DATESMTD / PARALLELPERIOD / SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR / TOTALMTD / [Date] / &#x27;Date&#x27; [Date] / &#x27;Date&#x27; [Month]</p>\n<pre><code>Sales Previous Year = [drop target 1] ( [Total Sales], [drop target 2] ( [drop target 3] ) )</code></pre>",
    "contextHtml": "<pre>Sales Previous Year = [drop target 1] ( [Total Sales], [drop target 2] ( [drop target 3] ) )</pre>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Target 1",
        "options": [
          "CALCULATE",
          "EVALUATE",
          "SUM",
          "SUMX",
          "DATESMTD",
          "PARALLELPERIOD",
          "SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR",
          "TOTALMTD",
          "[Date]",
          "'Date' [Date]",
          "'Date' [Month]"
        ],
        "answer": "CALCULATE"
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Target 2",
        "options": [
          "CALCULATE",
          "EVALUATE",
          "SUM",
          "SUMX",
          "DATESMTD",
          "PARALLELPERIOD",
          "SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR",
          "TOTALMTD",
          "[Date]",
          "'Date' [Date]",
          "'Date' [Month]"
        ],
        "answer": "SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR"
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Target 3",
        "options": [
          "CALCULATE",
          "EVALUATE",
          "SUM",
          "SUMX",
          "DATESMTD",
          "PARALLELPERIOD",
          "SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR",
          "TOTALMTD",
          "[Date]",
          "'Date' [Date]",
          "'Date' [Month]"
        ],
        "answer": "'Date' [Date]"
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>CALCULATE</p>\n<p>SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR</p>\n<p>&#x27;DATE&#x27;[DATE]</p>\n<p>Box 1: CALCULATE -</p>\n<p>Box 2: SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR</p>\n<p>accepts a data column, Month will usually be either text (Jan) or Integer (1). so: CALCULATE([Total Sales], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR(&#x27;Date&#x27;[Date]))</p>\n<p>Box 3: &#x27;DATE&#x27; [DATE]</p>\n<p>us/dax/sameperiodlastyear-function-dax</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:68:row68"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-69-r69",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:69",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You plan to create a report that will display sales data from the last year for multiple regions. You need to restrict access to individual rows of the data on a per region-basis by using roles. Which four actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order. Select and Place:</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<p>Values: Publish the report. / Assign users to the role. / Add a filter to the report. / Create a role definition. / Import the data to Power BI Desktop.</p>\n<ul><li>Step 1</li><li>Step 2</li><li>Step 3</li><li>Step 4</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Step 1",
        "options": [
          "Publish the report.",
          "Assign users to the role.",
          "Add a filter to the report.",
          "Create a role definition.",
          "Import the data to Power BI Desktop."
        ],
        "answer": "Import the data to Power BI Desktop."
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Step 2",
        "options": [
          "Publish the report.",
          "Assign users to the role.",
          "Add a filter to the report.",
          "Create a role definition.",
          "Import the data to Power BI Desktop."
        ],
        "answer": "Create a role definition."
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Step 3",
        "options": [
          "Publish the report.",
          "Assign users to the role.",
          "Add a filter to the report.",
          "Create a role definition.",
          "Import the data to Power BI Desktop."
        ],
        "answer": "Publish the report."
      },
      {
        "id": "t4",
        "label": "Step 4",
        "options": [
          "Publish the report.",
          "Assign users to the role.",
          "Add a filter to the report.",
          "Create a role definition.",
          "Import the data to Power BI Desktop."
        ],
        "answer": "Assign users to the role."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>With respect, you can not assign users to a role until AFTER the report has been published to the Power BI Service. Those posting that you create the role and then assign users to the role BEFORE publishing are incorrect. Roles are created in Power BI Desktop. Desktop does not have any way to assign users to the roles. They are empty when created. Role assignment happens in the service.</p>\n<p>Publish the report to the Power BI service. Go to your Workspace, using the Dataset, select the More Options menu(...) and click Security. This is where the Roles are populated.</p>\n<ul><li>Import your data into Power BI Desktop</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Create the role definition (on the Modeling tab)</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Publish the report to the Power BI service</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Assign users to the role</li></ul>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
    "sourceRecords": [
      "study-hub:pl300:69:row69"
    ],
    "review": "Source-supplied answer; not independently validated",
    "revealImages": [],
    "caseLabel": ""
  },
  {
    "id": "new-hub-pl300-70-r70",
    "sourceId": "study-hub:pl300:70",
    "exam": "PL-300",
    "stemHtml": "<p>You create a data model in Power BI. Report developers and users provide feedback that the data model is too complex. The model contains the following tables.</p>\n<p>The model has the following relationships:</p>\n<ul><li>There is a one-to-one relationship between Sales_Region and Region_Manager.</li><li>There are more records in Manager than in Region_Manager, but every record in Region_Manager has a</li></ul>\n<p>corresponding record in Manager.</p>\n<ul><li>There are more records in Sales_Manager than in Sales_Region, but every record in Sales_Region has a</li></ul>\n<p>corresponding record in Sales_Manager. You need to denormalize the model into a single table. Only managers who are associated to a sales region must be included in the reports. Which three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order. NOTE: More than one order of answer choices is correct. You will receive credit for any of the correct orders you select. Select and Place:</p>\n<p>Answer Area</p>\n<p>Values: Merge [Region_Manager] and [Manager] by using an inner join. / Merge [Sales_Manager] and [Sales_Region] by using a left join. / Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join. / Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join as a new query named [Sales_Region_and_Manager]. / Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using a right join as a new query named [Sales_Region_and_Region_Manager]. / Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using an inner join.</p>\n<ul><li>Step 1</li><li>Step 2</li><li>Step 3</li></ul>",
    "contextHtml": "<div><div>Exhibit (transcribed): Tables</div><div><table><thead><tr><th>Table name</th><th>Column name</th><th>Data type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sales_Region</td><td>region_id</td><td>Integer</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>name</td><td>Varchar</td></tr><tr><td>Region_Manager</td><td>region_id</td><td>Integer</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>manager_id</td><td>Integer</td></tr><tr><td>Sales_Manager</td><td>sales_manager_id</td><td>Integer</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>name</td><td>Varchar</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>region_id</td><td>Integer</td></tr><tr><td>Manager</td><td>manager_id</td><td>Integer</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>name</td><td>Varchar</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>",
    "options": [],
    "controls": [
      {
        "id": "t1",
        "label": "Step 1",
        "options": [
          "Merge [Region_Manager] and [Manager] by using an inner join.",
          "Merge [Sales_Manager] and [Sales_Region] by using a left join.",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join.",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join as a new query named [Sales_Region_and_Manager].",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using a right join as a new query named [Sales_Region_and_Region_Manager].",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using an inner join."
        ],
        "answer": "Merge [Region_Manager] and [Manager] by using an inner join."
      },
      {
        "id": "t2",
        "label": "Step 2",
        "options": [
          "Merge [Region_Manager] and [Manager] by using an inner join.",
          "Merge [Sales_Manager] and [Sales_Region] by using a left join.",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join.",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join as a new query named [Sales_Region_and_Manager].",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using a right join as a new query named [Sales_Region_and_Region_Manager].",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using an inner join."
        ],
        "answer": "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join."
      },
      {
        "id": "t3",
        "label": "Step 3",
        "options": [
          "Merge [Region_Manager] and [Manager] by using an inner join.",
          "Merge [Sales_Manager] and [Sales_Region] by using a left join.",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join.",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join as a new query named [Sales_Region_and_Manager].",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using a right join as a new query named [Sales_Region_and_Region_Manager].",
          "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using an inner join."
        ],
        "answer": "Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using an inner join."
      }
    ],
    "mode": "controls",
    "answers": [],
    "explanationHtml": "<p>1.Merge [Region_Manager] and [Manager] by using an inner join.</p>\n<p>3.Merge [Sales_Region] and [Sales_Manager] by using an inner join.</p>\n<p>6.Merge [Sales_Region] and [Region_Manager] by using an inner join.</p>",
    "images": [],
    "refs": [],
    "blockers": [],
